window.HIDAYA_DATA.push({"id":"anglais-en01","domaine":"anglais","unite":1,"theme":"Unit 1 — Our new things","tag":"EN01","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 1 - Words (school things)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Name at least 16 school objects in English (pen, pencil, rubber, ruler, sharpener, book, notebook, bag, desk, chair, board, chalk, map, poster, dictionary, schoolbag).","Pronounce each new word with correct word stress and match it to its written form.","Use <em>a / an</em> correctly before singular school objects.","Ask and answer \"What's this?\" – \"It's a ruler.\" in pairs.","Build a personal bilingual glossary (English – Arabic – French) of school things."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Warm-up situation.</strong> It is the first Monday of September in a middle school in Agadir. Youssef opens his new schoolbag on the desk and takes out his things one by one: a pen, two pencils, a rubber, a ruler, a sharpener and three notebooks. His friend Salma looks inside and asks: \"What's this?\" Youssef answers: \"It's a sharpener.\" The teacher writes the two sentences on the board. In this lesson we learn the names of the things we use every day, so that we can talk about our own bag and our own classroom in English.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher empties a real schoolbag on the desk and holds up each object. Pupils listen and repeat three times: <em>pen · pencil · rubber · ruler · sharpener · book · notebook · bag</em>. Then the teacher points at classroom furniture and equipment: <em>desk · chair · board · chalk · map · poster · window · door</em>. Pupils copy a two-column table: <strong>Things in my bag</strong> / <strong>Things in the classroom</strong>, and classify the sixteen words. Objects that are hard to bring can be drawn quickly on the board — a rectangle with lines is a <em>notebook</em>, a long thin rectangle with numbers is a <em>ruler</em>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — a noun.</strong> A word that names a person, a place or a thing is a <strong>noun</strong>. <em>Pen, chair, school, Salma, Agadir</em> are nouns. In English, common nouns take a small letter (<em>pen</em>), but names of people, towns, countries, days and months take a <strong>capital letter</strong> (<em>Salma, Agadir, Morocco, Monday, September</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — a / an.</strong> Before a singular noun we use <strong>a</strong> when the word begins with a consonant sound (<em>a pen, a ruler, a book, a bag</em>) and <strong>an</strong> when it begins with a vowel sound (<em>an apple, an eraser, an English book, an orange bag</em>). We look at the <strong>sound</strong>, not only the letter.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the plural -s.</strong> Most nouns add <strong>-s</strong> in the plural: <em>pen → pens, book → books, desk → desks</em>. Nouns ending in <em>-s, -sh, -ch, -x</em> add <strong>-es</strong>: <em>box → boxes, brush → brushes</em>. Nouns ending in consonant + <em>-y</em> change to <em>-ies</em>: <em>dictionary → dictionaries</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — how to learn a new word in four steps.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Listen</strong> to the word and say it aloud twice (<em>sharpener /ˈʃɑːpənə/</em>).</li><li><strong>See</strong> the object or a drawing of it, so the word is linked to a picture, not to a translation only.</li><li><strong>Write</strong> the word in your glossary with its Arabic gloss and one short sentence: <em>a sharpener — مِبْراة — I've got a sharpener.</em></li><li><strong>Use</strong> it the same day in a real question: \"Amine, have you got a sharpener, please?\"</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> The teacher holds up a small pink object used to remove pencil marks and asks: \"What's this?\" We look for the noun (<em>rubber</em>), we check the first sound (/r/ is a consonant sound → <em>a</em>), and we answer with the full pattern: <strong>\"It's a rubber.\"</strong> If the object is far from us, we say: <strong>\"That's a rubber.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Salma shows two thin books with squared paper. There are two of them, so we need the plural. <em>Notebook</em> + <strong>-s</strong> = <em>notebooks</em>, and <em>a</em> disappears with plurals: <strong>\"They're notebooks.\"</strong> With a number we say: <strong>\"They're two notebooks.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes <em>an ruler</em> on the board. We test the first sound of <em>ruler</em>: /r/, a consonant sound, so <strong>a ruler</strong> is correct. Then the pupil writes <em>a English book</em>: the first sound of <em>English</em> is /ɪ/, a vowel sound, so the correct form is <strong>an English book</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> Pupils work in pairs (A and B) with their real school things on the desk. A closes his/her eyes, B puts one object in A's hands; A touches it and guesses: \"It's a … ?\" B confirms: \"Yes, it is.\" / \"No, it isn't. It's a …\". Each pair does eight exchanges, then two pairs perform in front of the row. The teacher circulates with a checklist and ticks <em>a/an</em> accuracy and pronunciation.</p>\n<p><strong>Cultural note.</strong> In Morocco, families buy school things in the <em>souk</em> or in small neighbourhood shops at the end of August: a pen costs about 2 dirhams, a notebook 4 to 6 dirhams, a schoolbag 60 to 150 dirhams. Pupils will use these prices again in Unit 4 with numbers 20–100.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th><th>French gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a pen</td><td>/pen/</td><td>قَلَم جاف</td><td>un stylo</td></tr><tr><td>a pencil</td><td>/ˈpensl/</td><td>قَلَم رَصاص</td><td>un crayon</td></tr><tr><td>a rubber</td><td>/ˈrʌbə/</td><td>مِمْحاة</td><td>une gomme</td></tr><tr><td>a ruler</td><td>/ˈruːlə/</td><td>مِسْطَرة</td><td>une règle</td></tr><tr><td>a sharpener</td><td>/ˈʃɑːpənə/</td><td>مِبْراة</td><td>un taille-crayon</td></tr><tr><td>a book</td><td>/bʊk/</td><td>كِتاب</td><td>un livre</td></tr><tr><td>a notebook</td><td>/ˈnəʊtbʊk/</td><td>دَفْتَر</td><td>un cahier</td></tr><tr><td>a schoolbag</td><td>/ˈskuːlbæɡ/</td><td>مِحْفَظة</td><td>un cartable</td></tr><tr><td>a desk</td><td>/desk/</td><td>طاوِلة</td><td>une table</td></tr><tr><td>a chair</td><td>/tʃeə/</td><td>كُرْسي</td><td>une chaise</td></tr><tr><td>a board</td><td>/bɔːd/</td><td>سَبورة</td><td>un tableau</td></tr><tr><td>chalk</td><td>/tʃɔːk/</td><td>طَبْشور</td><td>la craie</td></tr><tr><td>a map</td><td>/mæp/</td><td>خَريطة</td><td>une carte</td></tr><tr><td>a poster</td><td>/ˈpəʊstə/</td><td>مُلْصَق</td><td>une affiche</td></tr><tr><td>a dictionary</td><td>/ˈdɪkʃənri/</td><td>مُعْجَم</td><td>un dictionnaire</td></tr><tr><td>a window / a door</td><td>/ˈwɪndəʊ/ /dɔː/</td><td>نافِذة / باب</td><td>une fenêtre / une porte</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Notation / pattern</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What's this? — It's a pen.</td><td>asking about one near object</td></tr><tr><td>What are these? — They're pens.</td><td>asking about several near objects</td></tr><tr><td>a + consonant sound / an + vowel sound</td><td>article choice</td></tr><tr><td>noun + -s / -es / -ies</td><td>plural formation</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Writing <em>an ruler</em> or <em>a English book</em>: the choice depends on the first <strong>sound</strong>, so <em>a ruler</em> and <em>an English book</em>. ⚠️ Keeping the article in the plural (<em>They're a pens</em>): plurals take no <em>a/an</em> — <em>They're pens</em>. ⚠️ Confusing <em>rubber</em> (مِمْحاة) with <em>ruler</em> (مِسْطَرة) because the two words begin with the same letter. ⚠️ Pronouncing the silent <em>l</em> in <em>chalk</em> /tʃɔːk/ and the <em>k</em> in <em>desk</em> too weakly; and stressing <em>pen-CIL</em> instead of <em>PEN-cil</em>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Write the letters in the right order to find school things: (a) NPE (b) LRUER (c) KOBO (d) HRCAI (e) LKHAC.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Complete with <strong>a</strong> or <strong>an</strong>: (a) … pencil (b) … eraser (c) … schoolbag (d) … English dictionary (e) … chair (f) … orange rubber.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Write the plural: (a) pen (b) desk (c) box (d) dictionary (e) notebook (f) brush.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Match the word with the place: pen · board · chalk · notebook · map — <strong>in my bag</strong> / <strong>in the classroom</strong>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Look at the underlined word and answer <em>near</em> or <em>far</em>: (a) <strong>This</strong> is my desk. (b) <strong>That</strong> is the map. (c) <strong>These</strong> are my books.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Complete the dialogue: Salma: \"What's … ?\" Youssef: \"… a sharpener.\" Salma: \"And what are … ?\" Youssef: \"They're … .\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Write one sentence for each picture-word using <em>It's</em> or <em>They're</em>: (a) one map (b) three chairs (c) one dictionary (d) two posters.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Correct the mistake in each sentence: (a) It's an ruler. (b) They're a pens. (c) I have got two notebook. (d) What's these?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Put the words in order to make questions: (a) this / what / is / ? (b) these / are / what / ? (c) got / you / a / have / rubber / ?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write the Arabic gloss of: rubber, sharpener, chalk, poster, desk.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Youssef's schoolbag contains: 2 pens, 1 pencil, 1 ruler, 3 notebooks, 1 dictionary. Write five sentences describing the bag, using <em>There is</em> / <em>There are</em> (example: <em>There is one ruler.</em>).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>In your notebook, draw a plan of your classroom in Agadir and label eight things in English. Then write three sentences: <em>This is my desk. That is the board. These are our chairs.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"a pen","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/pen/</span> un stylo — قَلَم جاف"},{"r":"a pencil","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈpensl/</span> un crayon — قَلَم رَصاص"},{"r":"a rubber","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈrʌbə/</span> une gomme — مِمْحاة"},{"r":"a ruler","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈruːlə/</span> une règle — مِسْطَرة"},{"r":"a sharpener","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈʃɑːpənə/</span> un taille-crayon — مِبْراة"},{"r":"a book","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/bʊk/</span> un livre — كِتاب"},{"r":"a notebook","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈnəʊtbʊk/</span> un cahier — دَفْتَر"},{"r":"a schoolbag","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈskuːlbæɡ/</span> un cartable — مِحْفَظة"},{"r":"a desk","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/desk/</span> une table — طاوِلة"},{"r":"a chair","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/tʃeə/</span> une chaise — كُرْسي"},{"r":"a board","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/bɔːd/</span> un tableau — سَبورة"},{"r":"chalk","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/tʃɔːk/</span> la craie — طَبْشور"},{"r":"a map","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/mæp/</span> une carte — خَريطة"},{"r":"a poster","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈpəʊstə/</span> une affiche — مُلْصَق"},{"r":"a dictionary","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈdɪkʃənri/</span> un dictionnaire — مُعْجَم"},{"r":"a window / a door","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈwɪndəʊ/ /dɔː/</span> une fenêtre / une porte — نافِذة / باب"},{"r":"What's this? — It's a pen.","v":"asking about one near object"},{"r":"What are these? — They're pens.","v":"asking about several near objects"},{"r":"a + consonant sound / an + vowel sound","v":"article choice"},{"r":"noun + -s / -es / -ies","v":"plural formation"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (8 marks).</strong> Write the English name of these ten school things from their Arabic glosses: قَلَم جاف · مِسْطَرة · مِبْراة · دَفْتَر · مِحْفَظة · كُرْسي · سَبورة · طَبْشور · خَريطة · مُعْجَم.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>a</em>, <em>an</em> or nothing (—): (a) I've got … English book. (b) There is … poster on the wall. (c) They're … notebooks. (d) That's … orange sharpener. (e) This is … chair. (f) These are … maps.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (6 marks).</strong> Write four sentences about your own schoolbag using <em>There is</em> / <em>There are</em> and a number in each sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (0.8 each): pen · ruler · sharpener · notebook · schoolbag · chair · board · chalk · map · dictionary.</li><li>Task 2 (1 each): (a) an (b) a (c) — (d) an (e) a (f) —.</li><li>Task 3 (1.5 each): correct choice of <em>There is</em> (singular) / <em>There are</em> (plural) 0.5 · correct noun and plural form 0.5 · capital letter and full stop 0.5. Example of a full-mark answer: <em>There is one ruler. There are two pens. There are four notebooks. There is one dictionary.</em></li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en02","domaine":"anglais","unite":2,"theme":"Unit 1 — Our new things","tag":"EN02","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 1 - Grammar (this / that / these / those)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Distinguish <em>this / that</em> (singular) from <em>these / those</em> (plural).","Link the choice of demonstrative to distance: near = <em>this/these</em>, far = <em>that/those</em>.","Ask and answer \"What's this?\" / \"What are those?\" with the correct short answer.","Produce the contracted forms <em>What's = What is</em>, <em>It's = It is</em>, <em>They're = They are</em>.","Describe six objects in the classroom using the four demonstratives."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> Salma is standing at her desk in Agadir. She touches her pencil case and says: \"<strong>This</strong> is my pencil case.\" Then she points at the teacher's table at the other end of the room: \"<strong>That</strong> is the teacher's desk.\" She shows the three notebooks in her hands: \"<strong>These</strong> are my notebooks.\" Finally she points at the posters on the far wall: \"<strong>Those</strong> are our posters.\" Four words, one difference: <strong>how far</strong> the object is, and <strong>how many</strong> objects there are.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher puts a pen on a pupil's desk and a bag on the far window sill, then says the four sentences while walking. Pupils complete a grid on the board:</p>\n<p>``<code> NEAR (here)        FAR (over there) ONE  (singular)   this               that MANY (plural)     these              those </code>``</p>\n<p>Pupils then test the grid: the teacher says \"Point to <em>that</em> map\" and pupils must point at the far map, not the near one. Errors are visible immediately, which is exactly what we want at this stage.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — demonstratives.</strong> <strong>this</strong> = one thing near me · <strong>that</strong> = one thing far from me · <strong>these</strong> = several things near me · <strong>those</strong> = several things far from me. They come before the verb <em>be</em> in a statement (<em>This is a pen</em>) and after <em>what</em> in a question (<em>What is this?</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — agreement.</strong> Singular demonstratives go with <strong>is</strong> and a singular noun: <em>This is a book.</em> Plural demonstratives go with <strong>are</strong> and a plural noun: <em>These are books.</em> Never mix them: <em>These is books</em> is wrong.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — contractions.</strong> In speech and in informal writing we shorten: <em>What is → What's</em> · <em>It is → It's</em> · <em>They are → They're</em> · <em>That is → That's</em>. There is <strong>no</strong> contraction for <em>this is</em> (<em>this's</em> does not exist) and none for <em>these are / those are</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — choosing the right demonstrative.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Count</strong> the objects: one → <em>this/that</em>; more than one → <em>these/those</em>.</li><li><strong>Measure the distance</strong>: can I touch it? → <em>this/these</em>; is it away from me? → <em>that/those</em>.</li><li><strong>Choose the verb</strong>: singular → <em>is</em>; plural → <em>are</em>.</li><li><strong>Check the noun</strong>: singular noun with <em>a/an</em>, plural noun with <em>-s</em> and no article.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Three rubbers are on my own desk. Count = 3 → plural. Distance = on my desk → near. So: <strong>\"These are rubbers.\"</strong> The question that produces this answer is: <strong>\"What are these?\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> One map is hanging on the wall behind the teacher. Count = 1 → singular. Distance = far → <em>that</em>. So: <strong>\"That is a map.\"</strong>, contracted <strong>\"That's a map.\"</strong> Short answer to \"Is that a poster?\" → \"No, it isn't. It's a map.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes: <em>Those is my books.</em> Step 1: <em>books</em> is plural → <em>those</em> is correct. Step 3: plural needs <strong>are</strong>. Correction: <strong>\"Those are my books.\"</strong> Note that with a possessive (<em>my, your, our</em>) we do not use <em>a</em>: <em>those are my books</em>, not <em>those are my a books</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Near or far?\" Pupil A stands, points at or touches an object, and says a sentence with a deliberate mistake or a correct one. Pupil B answers \"Correct\" or \"Wrong — say it again\", and corrects. Then they swap. Eight turns each. The teacher gives each pair a slip with four objects to use (a near singular, a near plural, a far singular, a far plural) so that all four forms are practised.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension for the Moroccan context.</strong> The same structures work outside the classroom: at the souk in Fez a customer points at a tray of dates and says \"How much are <strong>those</strong> dates?\", and the seller lifts one bag: \"<strong>This</strong> bag is 20 dirhams.\" Pupils write two souk sentences to prepare Unit 4.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Distance</th><th>Number</th><th>Verb</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>this</td><td>near</td><td>singular</td><td>is</td><td>This is my pen.</td></tr><tr><td>that</td><td>far</td><td>singular</td><td>is</td><td>That's the board.</td></tr><tr><td>these</td><td>near</td><td>plural</td><td>are</td><td>These are my books.</td></tr><tr><td>those</td><td>far</td><td>plural</td><td>are</td><td>Those are the windows.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Contraction</th><th>Full form</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What's</td><td>What is</td><td>/wɒts/</td></tr><tr><td>It's</td><td>It is</td><td>not <em>its</em> (possessive)</td></tr><tr><td>They're</td><td>They are</td><td>/ðeə/</td></tr><tr><td>That's</td><td>That is</td><td>no contraction for <em>this is</em></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Expected answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What's this / that?</td><td>It's a (an) + singular noun.</td></tr><tr><td>What are these / those?</td><td>They're + plural noun.</td></tr><tr><td>Is this a ruler?</td><td>Yes, it is. / No, it isn't.</td></tr><tr><td>Are those maps?</td><td>Yes, they are. / No, they aren't.</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>These is my books</em> — plural demonstratives always take <strong>are</strong>. ⚠️ Answering \"What's this?\" with \"This is a pen\" is possible but the natural answer replaces the demonstrative with the pronoun: <strong>\"It's a pen.\"</strong> ⚠️ Writing <em>this'</em> or <em>this's</em>: there is no contraction for <em>this is</em>. ⚠️ Confusing <em>these</em> /ðiːz/ (near, plural) and <em>this</em> /ðɪs/ (near, singular) because of the vowel length; lengthen the /iː/ clearly.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Choose <em>this</em> or <em>these</em>: (a) … is a pencil. (b) … are my rulers. (c) … is our board. (d) … are two maps.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Choose <em>that</em> or <em>those</em>: (a) … is the door. (b) … are the windows. (c) … is a poster. (d) … are the teacher's books.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Complete with <em>is</em> or <em>are</em>: (a) This … a chair. (b) Those … chairs. (c) These … notebooks. (d) That … chalk.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Write the contracted form: (a) What is this? (b) It is a bag. (c) They are maps. (d) That is my desk.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Near or far? (a) These are my pens. (b) Those are your books. (c) This is a rubber. (d) That is the map.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Ask the question for each answer: (a) It's a sharpener. (b) They're posters. (c) Yes, it is. (d) No, they aren't.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Make the sentences plural: (a) This is a book. (b) That is a chair. (c) This is my notebook.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Make the sentences singular: (a) These are pens. (b) Those are windows. (c) These are our dictionaries.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Correct the mistakes: (a) These is my bag. (b) That are the doors. (c) What are this? (d) Those is chalk.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Complete the dialogue in the classroom: A: \"What's … ?\" B: \"… a dictionary.\" A: \"And what are … over there?\" B: \"… maps.\"</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write six sentences about your classroom, two with <em>this</em>, one with <em>that</em>, two with <em>these</em> and one with <em>those</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a six-line dialogue at the souk in Fez between a customer and a seller. Use <em>this, that, these, those</em> at least once each, plus prices in dirhams.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (8 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>this, that, these</em> or <em>those</em>: (a) … is my sharpener (in my hand). (b) … are the posters (on the far wall). (c) … is the door (far). (d) … are my two rulers (on my desk). (e) … is a dictionary (in my bag). (f) … are the teacher's books (on the teacher's desk, far). (g) … are our chairs (here). (h) … is the board (far).</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Write the question and the short answer: (a) It's a pen. (b) They're maps. (c) (Is this a rubber? → no).</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (6 marks).</strong> Write four sentences about the things in your classroom in Agadir, using each demonstrative once.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) This (b) Those (c) That (d) These (e) This (f) Those (g) These (h) That.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) What's this/that? — It's a pen. (b) What are these/those? — They're maps. (c) Is this a rubber? — No, it isn't.</li><li>Task 3 (1.5 each): correct demonstrative 0.5 · correct verb agreement 0.5 · spelling, capital letter and full stop 0.5.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en03","domaine":"anglais","unite":3,"theme":"Unit 1 — Our new things","tag":"EN03","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 1 - Phonics (alphabet: initial sounds)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Say the 26 letter <strong>names</strong> and distinguish them from the 26 letter <strong>sounds</strong>.","Identify the initial sound of a spoken word and write the matching letter.","Produce the sounds that do not exist in Arabic or French: /p/, /v/, /ɡ/, /tʃ/, /ð/.","Sort classroom words into sound families by their first sound.","Spell his/her own first name aloud, letter by letter."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> The teacher calls the register in an Agadir classroom: \"B-A-D-R\", \"S-A-L-M-A\". Then she says three words slowly: <em>pen · book · map</em>, and asks: \"What is the <strong>first sound</strong>?\" A pupil answers \"P\". Two ideas are now on the table: a letter has a <strong>name</strong> (the <em>pee</em> we say when we spell) and a <strong>sound</strong> (the /p/ we hear at the beginning of <em>pen</em>). This lesson trains the ear to hear the first sound, and the hand to write the matching letter.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher says twelve familiar words one by one — <em>pen, book, map, ruler, desk, chalk, notebook, table, window, girl, van, chair</em> — and pupils raise the letter card of the first sound. Then the class builds an initial-sound alphabet with words they already know:</p>\n<p>``<code> A apple   B book    C chair   D desk    E egg     F flag G girl    H hand    I ink     J jam     K key     L lamp M map     N notebook O orange P pen     Q queen   R ruler S school  T table   U umbrella V van    W window  X box(-x) Y yes     Z zip </code>``</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — letter name vs letter sound.</strong> The <strong>name</strong> of a letter is what we say when we spell a word: <em>B</em> is called /biː/. The <strong>sound</strong> of a letter is what we hear inside a word: in <em>book</em>, <em>b</em> sounds /b/. Twenty-six letters produce more than forty sounds in English, which is why we learn sounds, not only letters.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — vowels and consonants.</strong> The five <strong>vowel</strong> letters are <strong>a, e, i, o, u</strong> (and sometimes <em>y</em>). All the others are <strong>consonants</strong>. Every English syllable contains one vowel sound: <em>pen</em> = /p/ + /e/ + /n/.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Careful — sounds that are new for Moroccan learners.</strong> /p/ (pen) is often replaced by /b/; /v/ (van) by /f/; /ɡ/ (girl) by /ʒ/ or /ɡ/ from French; /tʃ/ (chair) is easy because it exists in Moroccan Arabic; /ð/ (this) does exist in Arabic (ذ) and can be transferred deliberately.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — hearing the first sound.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Listen</strong> to the whole word without repeating it (<em>ruler</em>).</li><li><strong>Say</strong> only the beginning, cut after the first sound (<em>rrr…</em>).</li><li><strong>Feel</strong> where it is made: lips together = /p/ or /b/; teeth on lip = /f/ or /v/; tongue behind teeth = /t/, /d/, /n/.</li><li><strong>Write</strong> the letter, then check with a second word that starts the same way (<em>ruler, rubber</em>).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> <em>pen</em> /pen/. Lips are closed then released with a small puff of air, and the voice does not vibrate → /p/, letter <strong>p</strong>. Contrast with <em>Ben</em> /ben/, where the voice vibrates → /b/. Test: hold a strip of paper in front of your mouth; it moves for <em>pen</em>, hardly at all for <em>Ben</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> <em>chalk</em> /tʃɔːk/. The first sound is not /k/ and not /ʃ/, but the pair /tʃ/ written with <strong>two letters, ch</strong>. This is a <strong>digraph</strong> — two letters, one sound — and it will be studied fully in EN08.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> <em>umbrella</em> /ʌmˈbrelə/ begins with a vowel sound /ʌ/, which is why we say <em>an umbrella</em>. This links phonics back to the <em>a / an</em> rule of EN01: the article depends on the <strong>initial sound</strong>, and that is exactly what this lesson teaches us to hear.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Sound bingo\". Each pupil draws a 3×3 grid and writes nine letters of his/her choice. Pupil A reads a word from the unit list; both pupils cross the letter of the initial sound if they have it. First to complete a line says \"Bingo!\" and must then say the nine words that gave the letters. The teacher keeps the master word list so that every letter appears.</p>\n<p><strong>Spelling my name.</strong> Each pupil spells his/her first name aloud (<em>S-A-L-M-A</em>) while the partner writes it. Names are then checked on the board with the reminder that first names take a <strong>capital letter</strong>. This activity prepares EN05 (capitalisation).</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Name (IPA)</th><th>Sound</th><th>Key word</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a</td><td>/eɪ/</td><td>/æ/</td><td>apple</td></tr><tr><td>b</td><td>/biː/</td><td>/b/</td><td>book</td></tr><tr><td>c</td><td>/siː/</td><td>/k/</td><td>chair (as /k/ in <em>cat</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>d</td><td>/diː/</td><td>/d/</td><td>desk</td></tr><tr><td>e</td><td>/iː/</td><td>/e/</td><td>egg</td></tr><tr><td>f</td><td>/ef/</td><td>/f/</td><td>flag</td></tr><tr><td>g</td><td>/dʒiː/</td><td>/ɡ/</td><td>girl</td></tr><tr><td>h</td><td>/eɪtʃ/</td><td>/h/</td><td>hand</td></tr><tr><td>i</td><td>/aɪ/</td><td>/ɪ/</td><td>ink</td></tr><tr><td>j</td><td>/dʒeɪ/</td><td>/dʒ/</td><td>jam</td></tr><tr><td>k</td><td>/keɪ/</td><td>/k/</td><td>key</td></tr><tr><td>l</td><td>/el/</td><td>/l/</td><td>lamp</td></tr><tr><td>m</td><td>/em/</td><td>/m/</td><td>map</td></tr><tr><td>n</td><td>/en/</td><td>/n/</td><td>notebook</td></tr><tr><td>o</td><td>/əʊ/</td><td>/ɒ/</td><td>orange</td></tr><tr><td>p</td><td>/piː/</td><td>/p/</td><td>pen</td></tr><tr><td>q</td><td>/kjuː/</td><td>/kw/</td><td>queen</td></tr><tr><td>r</td><td>/ɑː/</td><td>/r/</td><td>ruler</td></tr><tr><td>s</td><td>/es/</td><td>/s/</td><td>school</td></tr><tr><td>t</td><td>/tiː/</td><td>/t/</td><td>table</td></tr><tr><td>u</td><td>/juː/</td><td>/ʌ/</td><td>umbrella</td></tr><tr><td>v</td><td>/viː/</td><td>/v/</td><td>van</td></tr><tr><td>w</td><td>/ˈdʌbljuː/</td><td>/w/</td><td>window</td></tr><tr><td>x</td><td>/eks/</td><td>/ks/</td><td>box</td></tr><tr><td>y</td><td>/waɪ/</td><td>/j/</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>z</td><td>/zed/</td><td>/z/</td><td>zip</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>phoneme</td><td>the smallest sound unit of a language</td></tr><tr><td>initial sound</td><td>the first sound we hear in a word</td></tr><tr><td>vowel / consonant</td><td>a, e, i, o, u / the other letters</td></tr><tr><td>digraph</td><td>two letters that make one sound (<em>ch, sh, th</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>voiced / voiceless</td><td>with vibration (/b/, /v/) / without (/p/, /f/)</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Replacing /p/ with /b/: <em>pen</em> pronounced <em>ben</em>, <em>pencil</em> pronounced <em>bencil</em>. Use the paper-strip test. ⚠️ Replacing /v/ with /f/: <em>van</em> pronounced <em>fan</em>. Put the top teeth on the lower lip <strong>and</strong> switch the voice on. ⚠️ Confusing the letter <strong>name</strong> with the letter <strong>sound</strong>: answering \"bee\" instead of /b/ when asked for the first sound of <em>book</em>. ⚠️ Adding a vowel before an initial consonant cluster (<em>iskool</em> for <em>school</em>): start the /s/ alone, then glide into /k/.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Write the missing letters of the alphabet: a, b, …, d, e, …, g, …, i, j, …, l, m, …, o, p, …, r, s, …, u, v, …, x, y, … .</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Write the first letter: (a) …uler (b) …esk (c) …indow (d) …otebook (e) …ap (f) …en.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Vowel or consonant? a · m · e · r · u · t · o.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Circle the word that begins with a <strong>vowel sound</strong>: (a) pen / apple (b) umbrella / ruler (c) desk / egg (d) orange / map.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Match the letter with its key word: p · v · g · j · z — jam · van · zip · pen · girl.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Sort by initial sound /p/ or /b/: pen, book, pencil, board, bag, poster.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Sort by initial sound /f/ or /v/: van, flag, five, fan, very, four.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Write <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> using the initial <strong>sound</strong>: (a) … umbrella (b) … pen (c) … egg (d) … ruler (e) … apple (f) … window.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Spell these words aloud, then write the letter names: (a) MAP (b) CHAIR (c) DESK.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Find one classroom word for each of these initial sounds: /b/, /tʃ/, /m/, /r/, /d/, /w/.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Read this list aloud and group the words in three sound families by initial sound: pen, book, bag, pencil, board, poster, map, mother, Monday.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write your full name and your town, then write the letter names you would say to spell them (example: Fez = F-E-Z, /ef/ /iː/ /zed/).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"phoneme","v":"the smallest sound unit of a language"},{"r":"initial sound","v":"the first sound we hear in a word"},{"r":"vowel / consonant","v":"a, e, i, o, u / the other letters"},{"r":"digraph","v":"two letters that make one sound (<em>ch, sh, th</em>)"},{"r":"voiced / voiceless","v":"with vibration (/b/, /v/) / without (/p/, /f/)"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Write the first letter of each word: …hair, …indow, …ictionary, …oster, …ubber, …otebook.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (8 marks).</strong> Sort these eight words in the table /p/ – /b/ – /f/ – /v/: pen, book, five, van, poster, bag, four, very.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (6 marks).</strong> Write <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> and explain in three words why: (a) … apple (b) … pencil (c) … umbrella (d) … desk (e) … egg (f) … map.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): c, w, d, p, r, n.</li><li>Task 2 (1 each): /p/ pen, poster · /b/ book, bag · /f/ five, four · /v/ van, very.</li><li>Task 3 (1 each, 0.5 for the article + 0.5 for the reason): (a) an — vowel sound (b) a — consonant sound (c) an — vowel sound (d) a — consonant sound (e) an — vowel sound (f) a — consonant sound.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en04","domaine":"anglais","unite":4,"theme":"Unit 1 — Our new things","tag":"EN04","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 1 - Reading (a description)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Read a 120–250-word A1 description aloud with correct initial sounds and full stops.","Find explicit information (names, numbers, objects, places) in the text.","Answer true/false and wh-questions on the text in complete short sentences.","Guess the meaning of two unknown words from the picture and the context.","Re-use five words from the text to describe his/her own classroom orally."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> The teacher writes the title on the board — <em>My new classroom</em> — and asks two questions in simple English: \"Where is your classroom?\" and \"What is in it?\" Pupils answer with the words of EN01. Then the teacher pre-teaches only four items which are needed for the text and which pupils cannot guess: <em>new, next to, on the wall, timetable</em>. Everything else in the text is deliberately built from EN01–EN03 vocabulary.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading text (original, written for this database).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>My new classroom</strong> Hello! My name is Salma Benali. I am twelve years old. I live in Agadir, in the south of Morocco. This is my first year in middle school. My school is near the beach, and my classroom is on the first floor. My classroom is big and clean. There are twenty desks and forty chairs. My desk is next to the window, so I can see the palm trees in the schoolyard. My friend Hind sits next to me. She is very kind. On the wall, there is a big map of Morocco and a green poster of the alphabet. The board is black and the chalk is white. Behind the teacher's desk, there is a small cupboard for the dictionaries. In my schoolbag I have got two pens, one pencil, a rubber, a ruler, a sharpener and four notebooks. My English notebook is new. It is blue. Our timetable is on the door. We have got English on Monday and on Thursday. English is my favourite subject. Our teacher is Mr Alaoui. He is from Fez. He says: \"Good morning, class!\" and we answer: \"Good morning, sir!\" I like my new classroom very much.</p></blockquote>\n<p>(174 words.)</p>\n<p><strong>While reading — three readings, three purposes.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Silent reading (2 min): global understanding.</strong> Pupils read and answer one single question: \"Who is speaking, and where?\" (Salma, in Agadir.) This trains reading for gist without translating word by word.</li><li><strong>Guided reading aloud (5 min): pronunciation.</strong> The teacher reads one paragraph, then rows repeat, then two volunteers read. Focus on the words practised in EN03: <em>pen, poster, board, window, notebook</em>, and on stopping at every full stop.</li><li><strong>Detailed reading (10 min): scanning.</strong> Pupils underline in pencil every <strong>number</strong> in the text (twelve, twenty, forty, two, one, four) and every <strong>place</strong> (Agadir, Morocco, first floor, next to the window, on the wall, on the door, Fez).</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method — answering a comprehension question in four steps.</strong> (1) Read the question and underline the <strong>question word</strong> (<em>who, where, what, how many</em>). (2) Find the <strong>key word</strong> of the question in the text (<em>timetable, map, desk</em>). (3) Read the sentence before and after it. (4) Answer with a <strong>short complete sentence</strong>, re-using the words of the text: <em>Where is Salma's desk? — It is next to the window.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Question: <em>How many chairs are there in the classroom?</em> Question word = <em>how many</em> → a number is expected. Key word = <em>chairs</em>. The text says \"There are twenty desks and forty chairs.\" Answer: <strong>\"There are forty chairs.\"</strong> A one-word answer (\"forty\") is understood but the target answer at A1 is the short complete sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Question: <em>True or false — Salma's English notebook is old.</em> Key word = <em>English notebook</em>. The text says \"My English notebook is new. It is blue.\" Answer: <strong>False.</strong> Justification is part of the answer: <em>\"False: it is new.\"</em></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — guessing a word.</strong> <em>Cupboard</em> is unknown. Context: \"Behind the teacher's desk, there is a small cupboard <strong>for the dictionaries</strong>.\" A thing that holds dictionaries and stands behind the desk is a piece of furniture, a خِزانة. We guess from the function, not from a dictionary.</p>\n<p><strong>After reading — speaking (pair work).</strong> Pupil A is a journalist for the school magazine, pupil B is Salma. A asks five questions (name, age, town, favourite subject, what is on the wall); B answers with the book closed. Then they swap and B describes his/her <strong>own</strong> classroom in four sentences beginning <em>There is / There are</em>. The best two descriptions are read to the class.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>new</td><td>/njuː/</td><td>جَديد</td><td>opposite: old</td></tr><tr><td>clean</td><td>/kliːn/</td><td>نَظيف</td><td>opposite: dirty</td></tr><tr><td>next to</td><td>/ˈnekst tuː/</td><td>بِجانِب</td><td>position</td></tr><tr><td>on the wall</td><td>/wɔːl/</td><td>على الحائِط</td><td>position</td></tr><tr><td>a timetable</td><td>/ˈtaɪmteɪbl/</td><td>جَدْوَل الحِصَص</td><td>used again in EN22</td></tr><tr><td>a cupboard</td><td>/ˈkʌbəd/</td><td>خِزانة</td><td>silent <em>p</em></td></tr><tr><td>favourite</td><td>/ˈfeɪvərɪt/</td><td>مُفَضَّل</td><td>+ subject</td></tr><tr><td>a schoolyard</td><td>/ˈskuːljɑːd/</td><td>ساحَة المَدْرَسة</td><td>compound noun</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Reading skill</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>skimming</td><td>reading fast for the general idea</td></tr><tr><td>scanning</td><td>looking for one precise piece of information</td></tr><tr><td>guessing from context</td><td>using the sentence around an unknown word</td></tr><tr><td>justifying</td><td>giving the sentence from the text that proves the answer</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Useful structures from the text</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>There is / There are + noun</td><td>describing a place</td></tr><tr><td>My desk is next to the window.</td><td>position</td></tr><tr><td>We have got English on Monday.</td><td>timetable (EN22)</td></tr><tr><td>It is blue. / She is kind.</td><td>describing with adjectives</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Translating word by word instead of scanning for the key word: reading time is lost and the answer is often missed. ⚠️ Answering <em>true/false</em> without justification; the marking grid in Moroccan tests usually asks for the sentence from the text. ⚠️ Copying a whole paragraph as an answer; the answer must be the shortest complete sentence that answers the question. ⚠️ Using <em>There is</em> with a plural (<em>There is forty chairs</em>): plural → <strong>There are</strong>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>True or false, and justify with a sentence from the text: (a) Salma is eleven. (b) Her school is near the beach. (c) The board is green. (d) Mr Alaoui is from Agadir.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Answer: (a) Where does Salma live? (b) How many desks are there? (c) Who sits next to Salma? (d) What is her favourite subject?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Find in the text one word for: a piece of furniture for dictionaries · the paper with the lessons of the week · the yard of the school.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Complete from the text: \"My desk is … the window.\" / \"On the wall, there is a big … of Morocco.\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Write the numbers you find in the text in figures.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Complete with <em>There is</em> or <em>There are</em>: (a) … twenty desks. (b) … a big map on the wall. (c) … forty chairs. (d) … a small cupboard.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Match the adjective with the noun as in the text: big · green · black · new · blue — map, poster, board, notebook, year.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Write the question for each answer: (a) She is twelve. (b) It is on the first floor. (c) He is from Fez. (d) On Monday and on Thursday.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Correct: (a) There is forty chairs. (b) Salma live in Agadir. (c) Her notebook are blue. (d) English is her favorite subject on monday.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Put these events of the text in order: the timetable · the schoolbag · the wall · Salma's name · the classroom.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write five questions you would ask Salma if she came to your class, then write her probable answers using the text.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a description of your own classroom (5–7 sentences, 60–90 words). Use <em>There is / There are</em>, two position words, two adjectives, and the name of your town.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"new","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/njuː/</span> opposite: old — جَديد"},{"r":"clean","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/kliːn/</span> opposite: dirty — نَظيف"},{"r":"next to","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈnekst tuː/</span> position — بِجانِب"},{"r":"on the wall","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/wɔːl/</span> position — على الحائِط"},{"r":"a timetable","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈtaɪmteɪbl/</span> used again in EN22 — جَدْوَل الحِصَص"},{"r":"a cupboard","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈkʌbəd/</span> silent <em>p</em> — خِزانة"},{"r":"favourite","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈfeɪvərɪt/</span> + subject — مُفَضَّل"},{"r":"a schoolyard","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈskuːljɑːd/</span> compound noun — ساحَة المَدْرَسة"},{"r":"There is / There are + noun","v":"describing a place"},{"r":"My desk is next to the window.","v":"position"},{"r":"We have got English on Monday.","v":"timetable (EN22)"},{"r":"It is blue. / She is kind.","v":"describing with adjectives"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (8 marks).</strong> Read the text of the lesson again and answer in complete sentences: (a) How old is Salma? (b) Where is her school? (c) What is on the door? (d) How many notebooks has she got?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> True or false + justification: (a) Salma's desk is next to the door. (b) There is a poster of the alphabet on the wall. (c) Mr Alaoui says \"Good morning, class!\".</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (6 marks).</strong> Write four sentences about your classroom with <em>There is / There are</em> and two position words.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (2 each): (a) She is twelve years old. (b) It is near the beach (in Agadir). (c) The timetable is on the door. (d) She has got four notebooks.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) False: \"My desk is next to the window.\" (b) True: \"there is … a green poster of the alphabet\". (c) True: 'He says: \"Good morning, class!\"'. — 1 mark for true/false, 1 for the justification.</li><li>Task 3 (1.5 each): correct <em>There is/are</em> 0.5 · correct vocabulary 0.5 · capital letter, full stop and position word 0.5.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en05","domaine":"anglais","unite":5,"theme":"Unit 1 — Our new things","tag":"EN05","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 1 - Writing (capitalisation, writing about my classroom)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["State and apply the six capitalisation rules of English.","Punctuate a simple sentence with a capital letter and a full stop.","Order words correctly in an English affirmative sentence (subject + verb + complement).","Write a guided paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) describing his/her classroom.","Check his/her own text with a four-point checklist before handing it in."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> A pupil writes on the board: <em>my name is amine. i live in fez. i study english on monday.</em> The class reads it and something feels wrong: no capital letters. The teacher rewrites: <em>My name is Amine. I live in Fez. I study English on Monday.</em> Five capital letters have appeared. Which ones, and why? In Arabic there are no capital letters, so this is a genuinely new skill that must be taught explicitly, not assumed.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> Pupils compare the two versions on the board and build the rule list themselves. The teacher classifies their findings in a table (see Key points). Then they apply it immediately to their own identity: first name, family name, town, country, school, favourite subject, one day of the week.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — beginning of a sentence.</strong> The first letter of every sentence takes a capital: <em>This is my classroom.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — the pronoun I.</strong> The word <strong>I</strong> is always a capital, anywhere in the sentence: <em>Salma and I are friends.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — names of people and places.</strong> First names, family names, towns, countries, rivers, mountains: <em>Amine El Idrissi, Agadir, Fez, Morocco, the Atlas.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — days, months, nationalities, languages.</strong> <em>Monday, Thursday, September, Ramadan, Moroccan, Arabic, English, French.</em> (Note: unlike French, languages and nationalities take a capital in English.)</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 5 — titles before a name.</strong> <em>Mr Alaoui, Mrs Benali, Miss Hind, Doctor Karim.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 6 — school subjects that are proper nouns.</strong> <em>English, Arabic, French</em> take a capital because they are languages; <em>maths, history, science</em> take a small letter inside a sentence.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the full stop.</strong> Every affirmative or negative sentence ends with a <strong>full stop</strong> (.). A question ends with a <strong>question mark</strong> (?) — studied in EN20.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — writing a paragraph about my classroom.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Plan</strong> with a spidergram: centre = <em>my classroom</em>; branches = <em>where it is · size and colour · furniture (numbers) · walls · my desk · my feeling about it</em>.</li><li><strong>Write one sentence per branch</strong>, subject first: <em>My classroom is on the first floor.</em></li><li><strong>Link</strong> the sentences with <em>and</em>, <em>but</em>, <em>so</em>: <em>It is big and clean. There are twenty desks, so we are not many.</em></li><li><strong>Check</strong> with the four-point checklist: capital letter at the start of each sentence · capital letters for names, days, languages · full stop at the end · correct verb (<em>is / are / have got</em>).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1 — correcting a text.</strong> Given: <em>my classroom is in agadir. we have got english on monday. our teacher is mr alaoui.</em> Corrections: <em>My</em> (rule 1), <em>Agadir</em> (rule 3), <em>We</em> (rule 1), <em>English</em> (rule 4), <em>Monday</em> (rule 4), <em>Our</em> (rule 1), <em>Mr Alaoui</em> (rules 3 and 5). Result: <strong>My classroom is in Agadir. We have got English on Monday. Our teacher is Mr Alaoui.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2 — word order.</strong> Given the words <em>classroom · is · my · big</em>: English puts the <strong>subject first</strong>, then the verb, then the rest: <em>My classroom is big.</em> The adjective <em>big</em> never takes an <em>-s</em> and never moves after the noun with <em>be</em> omitted.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — a full model paragraph (68 words).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>My classroom.</strong> My name is Amine. I am twelve years old and I am a pupil in Fez. My classroom is on the ground floor. It is big and clean. There are eighteen desks and thirty-six chairs. My desk is next to the window. On the wall, there is a map of Morocco and a poster of the alphabet. We have got English on Tuesday and on Friday. I like my classroom very much.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The class counts the capital letters (Amine, Fez, Morocco, Tuesday, Friday, English, I, and every sentence opening) and the full stops (nine).</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (peer correction).</strong> Pupils exchange notebooks and use the four-point checklist as a marking grid: they put a small circle around every missing capital letter and a cross where a full stop is missing, without writing the correction. The author corrects his/her own text. This \"circle, don't correct\" routine works with any class size and makes the checklist automatic.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Capitalisation rule</th><th>Examples</th><th>Arabic-speaker note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>start of a sentence</td><td><strong>T</strong>his is my desk.</td><td>new: Arabic has no capitals</td></tr><tr><td>the pronoun <em>I</em></td><td>Hind and <strong>I</strong> are friends.</td><td>never <em>i</em></td></tr><tr><td>people's names</td><td><strong>S</strong>alma <strong>B</strong>enali, <strong>M</strong>r <strong>A</strong>laoui</td><td>family name also capital</td></tr><tr><td>places</td><td><strong>A</strong>gadir, <strong>F</strong>ez, <strong>M</strong>orocco, the <strong>A</strong>tlas</td><td>including <em>the Sahara</em></td></tr><tr><td>days and months</td><td><strong>M</strong>onday, <strong>S</strong>eptember, <strong>R</strong>amadan</td><td>different from French usage</td></tr><tr><td>languages and nationalities</td><td><strong>E</strong>nglish, <strong>A</strong>rabic, <strong>M</strong>oroccan</td><td>different from French usage</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Punctuation</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>full stop</td><td>.</td><td>end of a statement</td></tr><tr><td>question mark</td><td>?</td><td>end of a question (EN20)</td></tr><tr><td>comma</td><td>,</td><td>list of three or more items (EN45)</td></tr><tr><td>apostrophe</td><td>'</td><td>contractions: <em>It's, They're</em></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Paragraph checklist</th><th>Yes/No</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Every sentence starts with a capital letter.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Names, days, languages have capitals.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Every sentence ends with a full stop.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Verbs <em>is / are / have got</em> agree with the subject.</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Writing <em>i</em> instead of <strong>I</strong> — the most frequent capitalisation error of Moroccan beginners. ⚠️ Writing <em>english</em>, <em>monday</em>, <em>moroccan</em> with small letters by analogy with French <em>anglais, lundi, marocain</em>. ⚠️ Forgetting the full stop at the end of the last sentence of a paragraph. ⚠️ Putting the adjective after the noun (<em>a classroom big</em>) under the influence of Arabic and French word order: in English it is <strong>a big classroom</strong>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Rewrite with capital letters: (a) my name is salma. (b) i live in agadir. (c) we study english and arabic. (d) our teacher is mr alaoui.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Add the missing full stops: <em>This is my classroom It is big There are twenty desks I like it</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Circle the words that need a capital letter: pen · fez · monday · desk · english · morocco · chair · september.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Put the words in order: (a) is / classroom / big / my (b) desks / are / there / twenty (c) window / my / is / next to / desk / the.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Choose <em>is</em> or <em>are</em>: (a) My classroom … clean. (b) There … forty chairs. (c) The board … black. (d) The posters … green.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Rewrite this text correctly (capitals + full stops): <em>my school is in fez i am in the first year we have got english on tuesday our classroom is on the first floor</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Complete about yourself: My name is … . I am … years old. I live in … . My school is in … . We have got English on … .</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Join the sentences with <em>and</em>, <em>but</em> or <em>so</em>: (a) The classroom is big. It is clean. (b) The board is old. It is good. (c) There are twenty desks. We are not many.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Correct the five mistakes: <em>my classroom is big and clean there are 20 desk i like english very much</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four sentences about your school using one place, one number, one adjective and one day of the week.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) entitled \"My classroom\". Use <em>There is / There are</em>, two adjectives, one position word, one day of the week and one opinion sentence.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Exchange notebooks with your partner. Use the four-point checklist and circle (do not correct) every missing capital letter or full stop. Then write your own corrected version.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Rewrite this text with capital letters and full stops: <em>my name is hind i am twelve i live in fez i study english and arabic our teacher is mrs benali</em></p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Put the words in order and punctuate: (a) big / is / classroom / our (b) desks / twenty / are / there (c) on / we / english / monday / got / have.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) about your classroom. Use <em>There is / There are</em>, two adjectives, one position word and one day of the week.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 per corrected element): My name is Hind. I am twelve. I live in Fez. I study English and Arabic. Our teacher is Mrs Benali.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) Our classroom is big. (b) There are twenty desks. (c) We have got English on Monday.</li><li>Task 3 (8): task achievement 3 · grammar and vocabulary 2 · capitals, full stops and spelling 2 · handwriting and layout 1.</li></ul>\n<p>## Unit 2 — They're happy now!</p>\n<p>Unit 2 moves from objects to people and emotions. Learners name feelings, use the plural forms of the verb <em>be</em> (<em>we're, you're, they're</em>) in statements, negatives and questions, revise the digraphs <em>ch, sh, th</em>, read a short original poem and write about their own feelings. Anchor context: a family in <strong>Fez</strong> during <strong>Ramadan</strong>, and a school football match in <strong>Agadir</strong>.</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en06","domaine":"anglais","unite":6,"theme":"Unit 2 — They're happy now!","tag":"EN06","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 2 - Words (feelings)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Name at least twelve feelings in English (happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, angry, afraid, cold, hot, bored, excited, worried).","Combine <em>be</em> + feeling adjective in the first, second and third person.","Ask \"How are you?\" / \"How is he?\" and answer with a feeling adjective.","Grade a feeling with <em>very</em>, <em>a little</em>, <em>not very</em>.","Match a facial expression or a situation to the right adjective."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> It is the first day of Ramadan in Fez. At five in the afternoon, Karim is <strong>thirsty</strong> and a little <strong>tired</strong>. His little sister Lina is <strong>hungry</strong> and <strong>bored</strong>. At seven o'clock the family sits around the table with dates, milk and harira; ten minutes later everybody is <strong>happy</strong> and nobody is tired. One family, one hour, six different feelings: to talk about them in English we need a small set of adjectives and one verb, <strong>be</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher draws six simple faces on the board (a smile, a frown, closed eyes, a hand on the stomach, a hand on the throat, crossed eyebrows) and says the adjectives while pointing: <em>happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, angry</em>. Pupils repeat and copy the faces with the labels. Then the teacher mimes six more: <em>afraid, cold, hot, bored, excited, worried</em>. Finally the class plays \"Show me\": the teacher says an adjective, pupils show it with their faces and hands. In one minute the whole class has produced twelve words.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — be + adjective.</strong> Feelings are expressed with the verb <strong>be</strong> followed by an adjective: <em>I <strong>am</strong> happy. She <strong>is</strong> tired. They <strong>are</strong> hungry.</em> The adjective never takes <em>-s</em> and never changes for gender: <em>he is happy</em>, <em>she is happy</em>, <em>they are happy</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Careful — \"I have hunger\" does not exist.</strong> Arabic and French say \"I have hunger / j'ai faim\", but English says <strong>I am hungry</strong>. The same applies to thirst (<em>I am thirsty</em>), cold (<em>I am cold</em>), heat (<em>I am hot</em>) and fear (<em>I am afraid</em>). This is one of the most frequent errors of Moroccan learners.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — grading a feeling.</strong> We put the adverb before the adjective: <strong>very</strong> tired (much) · <strong>a little / a bit</strong> tired (small) · <strong>not very</strong> tired (weak) · <strong>really</strong> happy (strong). <em>I am very hungry. She is a little worried. We are not very tired.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — asking about feelings.</strong> <em>How are you? — I'm fine, thank you. / I'm tired.</em> · <em>How is your brother? — He's happy.</em> · <em>Why are you sad? — Because my team lost.</em> The answer to <em>How …?</em> is a feeling; the answer to <em>Why …?</em> begins with <strong>because</strong>.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — choosing the right feeling word.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Find the cause</strong>: no food → <em>hungry</em>; no water → <em>thirsty</em>; no sleep → <em>tired</em>; nothing to do → <em>bored</em>.</li><li><strong>Find the person</strong> and the matching form of <em>be</em>: <em>I am · you are · he/she/it is · we are · you are · they are</em>.</li><li><strong>Add the adverb</strong> if needed: <em>very, a little, not very</em>.</li><li><strong>Say it in one short sentence</strong> and check that the adjective has no <em>-s</em>.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Lina has no food and it is 6 p.m. Cause = no food → <em>hungry</em>. Person = she → <em>is</em>. Degree = strong → <em>very</em>. Sentence: <strong>\"She is very hungry.\"</strong> Contracted: <strong>\"She's very hungry.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> After the school football match in Agadir, eleven players have run for ninety minutes. Cause = running a long time → <em>tired</em>. Person = they → <em>are</em>. Sentence: <strong>\"They are tired.\"</strong> They also won, so: <strong>\"They are happy and tired.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes <em>I have hot.</em> Two problems: the verb (<em>have</em> → <em>am</em>) and the sense (hot is a state of the person, not an object). Correction: <strong>\"I am hot.\"</strong> Compare with <strong>\"The tea is hot.\"</strong> — the same adjective describes a person or a thing, always with <em>be</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Feelings dominoes.\" Each pair gets eight small situation cards written by the teacher on scrap paper: <em>no breakfast · 40 degrees in Marrakech · a maths test tomorrow · a goal for your team · a long walk · no water in the bottle · a boring film · a big dog in the street</em>. Pupil A reads a card, pupil B answers with a full sentence: \"You are hungry.\" Then B asks \"And how are you?\" and A answers about himself/herself truthfully. Sixteen exchanges per pair in six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Values and citizenship link.</strong> The unit title says <em>They're happy now!</em>: the class discusses in very simple English what makes a classmate happy (help with homework, a friendly word, sharing a snack in Ramadan) and writes three \"kindness sentences\" on the wall poster: <em>When I help my friend, he is happy.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th><th>Opposite / near word</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>happy</td><td>/ˈhæpi/</td><td>سَعيد</td><td>sad</td></tr><tr><td>sad</td><td>/sæd/</td><td>حَزين</td><td>happy</td></tr><tr><td>tired</td><td>/ˈtaɪəd/</td><td>مُتْعَب</td><td>full of energy</td></tr><tr><td>hungry</td><td>/ˈhʌŋɡri/</td><td>جائِع</td><td>full</td></tr><tr><td>thirsty</td><td>/ˈθɜːsti/</td><td>عَطْشان</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>angry</td><td>/ˈæŋɡri/</td><td>غاضِب</td><td>calm</td></tr><tr><td>afraid</td><td>/əˈfreɪd/</td><td>خائِف</td><td>brave</td></tr><tr><td>cold</td><td>/kəʊld/</td><td>يَشْعُر بِالبَرْد</td><td>hot</td></tr><tr><td>hot</td><td>/hɒt/</td><td>يَشْعُر بِالحَرّ</td><td>cold</td></tr><tr><td>bored</td><td>/bɔːd/</td><td>يَشْعُر بِالمَلَل</td><td>excited</td></tr><tr><td>excited</td><td>/ɪkˈsaɪtɪd/</td><td>مُتَحَمِّس</td><td>bored</td></tr><tr><td>worried</td><td>/ˈwʌrid/</td><td>قَلِق</td><td>calm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>subject + be + adjective</td><td>They are happy.</td></tr><tr><td>subject + be + very/a little + adjective</td><td>I am very tired.</td></tr><tr><td>How + be + subject?</td><td>How is Lina? — She's hungry.</td></tr><tr><td>Why + be + subject + adjective?</td><td>Why are you sad? — Because I lost my pen.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Forms of <em>be</em></th><th>Full</th><th>Short</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st sing.</td><td>I am</td><td>I'm</td></tr><tr><td>2nd</td><td>you are</td><td>you're</td></tr><tr><td>3rd sing.</td><td>he/she/it is</td><td>he's/she's/it's</td></tr><tr><td>1st pl.</td><td>we are</td><td>we're</td></tr><tr><td>3rd pl.</td><td>they are</td><td>they're</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>I have hungry / I have hunger</em> instead of <strong>I am hungry</strong> (interference from Arabic and French). ⚠️ Adding <em>-s</em> to the adjective in the plural: <em>They are happys</em>. Adjectives are invariable. ⚠️ Confusing <em>bored</em> (the person feels nothing to do) and <em>boring</em> (the film causes the feeling): <em>I am bored because the film is boring.</em> ⚠️ Pronouncing <em>thirsty</em> with /s/ or /t/ instead of /θ/; the <em>th</em> sound is practised in EN08.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Match the situation with the feeling: (a) no breakfast (b) 42 degrees in Marrakech (c) a long walk (d) a goal for your team — tired · hot · hungry · happy.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Complete with <em>am</em>, <em>is</em> or <em>are</em>: (a) I … thirsty. (b) She … sad. (c) They … tired. (d) We … happy. (e) You … angry. (f) Karim … hungry.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Write the opposite: (a) happy (b) hot (c) bored (d) hungry (use <em>full</em>).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Write the short form: (a) I am tired. (b) She is happy. (c) They are hungry. (d) We are cold.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Write the Arabic gloss: happy · thirsty · angry · afraid · worried.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Correct: (a) I have hungry. (b) They are happys. (c) She is boring because the lesson is bored. (d) He have thirsty.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Add <em>very</em>, <em>a little</em> or <em>not very</em>: (a) After the match, they are … tired. (b) It is 6 p.m. in Ramadan: Lina is … hungry. (c) I slept nine hours: I am … tired.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Ask the question: (a) I'm fine, thank you. (b) She's tired. (c) Because my team lost.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete the dialogue: A: \"How … you?\" B: \"I'm … , thanks. And you?\" A: \"I'm … because it's very hot.\" B: \"Have some water!\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write one sentence for each person, using a different feeling: I · my mother · my two brothers · my teacher.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write six sentences describing the feelings of your family at 6 p.m. during Ramadan in Fez. Use <em>very</em> or <em>a little</em> three times.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a short dialogue (6 lines) after a football match in Agadir. One player is happy, one is sad. Use <em>How are you?</em>, <em>Why …?</em> and <em>because</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"happy","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈhæpi/</span> sad — سَعيد"},{"r":"sad","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/sæd/</span> happy — حَزين"},{"r":"tired","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈtaɪəd/</span> full of energy — مُتْعَب"},{"r":"hungry","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈhʌŋɡri/</span> full — جائِع"},{"r":"thirsty","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈθɜːsti/</span> — — عَطْشان"},{"r":"angry","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈæŋɡri/</span> calm — غاضِب"},{"r":"afraid","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/əˈfreɪd/</span> brave — خائِف"},{"r":"cold","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/kəʊld/</span> hot — يَشْعُر بِالبَرْد"},{"r":"hot","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/hɒt/</span> cold — يَشْعُر بِالحَرّ"},{"r":"bored","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/bɔːd/</span> excited — يَشْعُر بِالمَلَل"},{"r":"excited","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ɪkˈsaɪtɪd/</span> bored — مُتَحَمِّس"},{"r":"worried","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈwʌrid/</span> calm — قَلِق"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (8 marks).</strong> Complete with the right form of <em>be</em> and a feeling adjective from the list (happy, tired, hungry, thirsty, cold, angry, bored, excited): (a) After the match, the players … . (b) It is 7 p.m. in Ramadan and Lina … . (c) In winter in Ifrane, we … . (d) The film is boring, so I … .</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Correct the mistakes: (a) I have thirsty. (b) They are sads. (c) How you are?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (6 marks).</strong> Write four sentences about the feelings of four different people in your family, with <em>very</em> or <em>a little</em> in two of them.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (2 each): (a) are tired (b) is hungry (c) are cold (d) am bored — 1 for the <em>be</em> form, 1 for a logical adjective.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) I <strong>am</strong> thirsty. (b) They are <strong>sad</strong>. (c) How <strong>are you</strong>?</li><li>Task 3 (1.5 each): correct <em>be</em> form 0.5 · appropriate adjective 0.5 · adverb, capital letter and full stop 0.5.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en07","domaine":"anglais","unite":7,"theme":"Unit 2 — They're happy now!","tag":"EN07","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 2 - Grammar (we're / they're / are they …?)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Conjugate the verb <em>be</em> in the present in all six persons, full and short forms.","Build negative sentences with <em>not</em> and its contractions (<em>isn't, aren't</em>).","Build yes/no questions by inverting subject and verb, and give short answers.","Ask wh-questions with <em>be</em> (<em>How, Where, Why, Who</em>).","Sustain a six-turn pair dialogue about feelings using all three sentence types."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> In the schoolyard in Agadir, the teacher meets two pupils after the football final. \"<strong>Are</strong> you happy?\" — \"Yes, we <strong>are</strong>! We <strong>aren't</strong> tired. <strong>They aren't</strong> happy: they lost.\" Three forms of the same verb appear in four seconds: a question, an affirmative and a negative. The verb <em>be</em> is the most frequent verb in English, and this lesson organises everything we know about it into one table.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher writes six sentences on the board in mixed order and asks pupils to sort them into three columns — <strong>(+) affirmative</strong>, <strong>(−) negative</strong>, <strong>(?) question</strong>:</p>\n<p>``<code> (+)                     (-)                        (?) I am happy.             I am not sad.              Am I late? You are tired.          You aren't hungry.         Are you tired? He is thirsty.          He isn't cold.             Is he thirsty? We are ready.           We aren't afraid.          Are we early? They are excited.       They aren't bored.         Are they excited? </code>``</p>\n<p>Pupils discover the two mechanisms alone: the negative **adds <em>not</em> after the verb<strong>, the question </strong>puts the verb before the subject**.</p>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — the present of <em>be</em>.** <em>I am · you are · he/she/it is · we are · you are · they are.</em> Short forms: <em>I'm, you're, he's, she's, it's, we're, you're, they're</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the negative.</strong> Add <strong>not</strong> after the form of <em>be</em>: <em>I am not · you are not · he is not</em>. Contractions: <em>I'm not · you aren't (you're not) · he isn't (he's not) · we aren't · they aren't</em>. There is no contraction <em>amn't</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the yes/no question.</strong> <strong>Invert</strong> the verb and the subject: <em>You are happy.</em> → <strong>Are you happy?</strong> No auxiliary <em>do</em> is used with <em>be</em>. The short answers are: <em>Yes, I am. / No, I'm not. · Yes, he is. / No, he isn't. · Yes, they are. / No, they aren't.</em> Never <em>Yes, he's.</em> — the affirmative short answer is never contracted.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — wh-questions with <em>be</em>.** Question word + <em>be</em> + subject: <em>How are you? · Where is Karim? · Why are they sad? · Who is your teacher? · What is your name? · How old are you?</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — turning a statement into a question and a negative.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>**Find the form of <em>be</em>** in the sentence: <em>They <strong>are</strong> happy.</em></li><li><strong>For the negative</strong>, insert <em>not</em> right after it and contract if you wish: <em>They <strong>aren't</strong> happy.</em></li><li><strong>For the question</strong>, move <em>be</em> in front of the subject and add a question mark: <em><strong>Are</strong> they happy?</em></li><li><strong>For the short answer</strong>, repeat subject + <em>be</em> only: <em>Yes, they are. / No, they aren't.</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Statement: <em>We are ready for the test.</em> Negative: <em>We <strong>aren't</strong> ready for the test.</em> Question: <em><strong>Are</strong> we ready for the test?</em> Short answers: <em>Yes, we are. / No, we aren't.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> A pupil writes <em>Do you are happy?</em> The verb <em>be</em> needs no auxiliary: the correct question is <em><strong>Are</strong> you happy?</em> Compare with a normal verb, where <em>do</em> is needed (studied in EN27): <em>Do you play football?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> Answer the question <em>Why are they sad?</em> about the losing team. The answer starts with <strong>because</strong> and repeats a full clause: <em>\"<strong>Because</strong> they lost the final.\"</em> A short answer <em>Yes/No</em> is impossible with a wh-question — this is a very frequent test error.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Find someone who is …\". Each pupil writes four questions with <em>Are you …?</em> using four different adjectives. Then, staying seated, they ask their two shoulder partners and the two pupils in front, note the answers in a small table (name / question / Yes or No), and report one result to the class: <em>\"Yasmine is hungry, but Omar isn't.\"</em> This produces about sixteen authentic questions per pupil in eight minutes with no movement in the room.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>(+)</th><th>(−)</th><th>(?)</th><th>Short answers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>I'm happy.</td><td>I'm not happy.</td><td>Am I happy?</td><td>Yes, I am. / No, I'm not.</td></tr><tr><td>you</td><td>You're tired.</td><td>You aren't tired.</td><td>Are you tired?</td><td>Yes, you are. / No, you aren't.</td></tr><tr><td>he/she/it</td><td>She's cold.</td><td>She isn't cold.</td><td>Is she cold?</td><td>Yes, she is. / No, she isn't.</td></tr><tr><td>we</td><td>We're ready.</td><td>We aren't ready.</td><td>Are we ready?</td><td>Yes, we are. / No, we aren't.</td></tr><tr><td>they</td><td>They're bored.</td><td>They aren't bored.</td><td>Are they bored?</td><td>Yes, they are. / No, they aren't.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Question word</th><th>Asks about</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What</td><td>a thing / a name</td><td>What's your name?</td></tr><tr><td>Who</td><td>a person</td><td>Who is your teacher?</td></tr><tr><td>Where</td><td>a place</td><td>Where are you from?</td></tr><tr><td>How</td><td>manner / feeling</td><td>How are you?</td></tr><tr><td>How old</td><td>age</td><td>How old is your sister?</td></tr><tr><td>Why</td><td>reason</td><td>Why are you happy? — Because …</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Remember</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>no <em>do</em> with <em>be</em></td><td><em>Are you happy?</em> not <em>Do you are happy?</em></td></tr><tr><td>no contraction in a positive short answer</td><td><em>Yes, I am.</em> not <em>Yes, I'm.</em></td></tr><tr><td>no <em>amn't</em></td><td>use <em>I'm not</em></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>Do you are happy?</em> — the verb <em>be</em> is its own auxiliary; simply invert. ⚠️ Omitting <em>be</em> altogether (<em>They happy</em>), because Arabic nominal sentences need no copula. ⚠️ Answering <em>Yes, they're.</em> — positive short answers use the full form: <em>Yes, they are.</em> ⚠️ Answering a <em>Why</em> question with <em>Yes</em> or <em>No</em> instead of a clause with <em>because</em>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Complete with <em>am</em>, <em>is</em>, <em>are</em>: (a) We … in the first year. (b) They … from Fez. (c) I … twelve. (d) You … my friend. (e) It … hot today. (f) Hind and Salma … happy.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Write the short form: (a) We are ready. (b) They are not tired. (c) He is not cold. (d) I am not sad.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Make negative: (a) They are bored. (b) She is hungry. (c) We are late. (d) I am worried.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Make questions: (a) You are tired. (b) They are happy. (c) He is thirsty. (d) We are ready.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Give both short answers for: (a) Are you hungry? (b) Is she tired? (c) Are they from Agadir?</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Correct: (a) Do you are happy? (b) They happy now. (c) Yes, they're. (d) She no is tired.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Complete with a question word: (a) … are you? — I'm fine. (b) … is your teacher? — Mr Alaoui. (c) … are you from? — From Fez. (d) … old are you? — Twelve. (e) … are they sad? — Because they lost.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Write the question for each answer: (a) Yes, we are. (b) No, he isn't. (c) Because it's very hot. (d) They're in the schoolyard.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete the dialogue: A: \"… you tired?\" B: \"No, I … . I'm hungry.\" A: \"… your brothers hungry too?\" B: \"Yes, they … .\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write three sentences about your class: one affirmative, one negative, one question, all with <em>be</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a six-turn dialogue between two pupils after the school match in Agadir. Use one yes/no question, one wh-question, one negative and two short answers.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write six sentences about your family using <em>we're</em>, <em>they're</em>, <em>he isn't</em>, <em>she isn't</em>, <em>I'm</em> and <em>I'm not</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with the right form of <em>be</em>: (a) We … in Agadir. (b) They … not hungry. (c) … you tired? (d) My sister … twelve. (e) I … not sad. (f) … they at school?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Rewrite in the negative and then as a question: (a) They are happy. (b) She is thirsty. (c) We are ready.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a six-line dialogue between you and a friend after a football match. Use one yes/no question, one <em>Why</em> question, one negative sentence and two short answers.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) are (b) are (c) Are (d) is (e) am (f) Are.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each, 1 per transformation): (a) They aren't happy. / Are they happy? (b) She isn't thirsty. / Is she thirsty? (c) We aren't ready. / Are we ready?</li><li>Task 3 (8): required forms 4 (1 each) · communicative coherence 2 · spelling, capitals and punctuation 2.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en08","domaine":"anglais","unite":8,"theme":"Unit 2 — They're happy now!","tag":"EN08","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 2 - Phonics (review of digraphs: ch, sh, th)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Define a digraph and identify <em>ch, sh, th</em> in written words.","Produce the sounds /tʃ/, /ʃ/, /θ/ and /ð/ accurately.","Distinguish voiceless /θ/ (<em>think</em>) from voiced /ð/ (<em>this</em>).","Sort at least eighteen words by digraph sound.","Read aloud a short tongue-twister text containing the three digraphs."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> The teacher says four words and asks the class which two letters they can hear together: <em>chair · shop · think · this</em>. Pupils know the letters <em>c, h, s, t</em> separately, but here two letters give <strong>one new sound</strong>. In EN03 we met <em>chalk</em> /tʃɔːk/ and noted that <em>ch</em> is a special case: this lesson deals with the three special pairs that appear in dozens of everyday words.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> Pupils build three columns on the board from words they already know:</p>\n<p>``<code> ch = /tʃ/          sh = /ʃ/           th = /θ/ or /ð/ chair              shop               three   /θ/ chalk              shirt              thirsty /θ/ cheese             shoe               think   /θ/ children           fish               thank   /θ/ teacher            wash               this    /ð/ lunch              Marrakesh          that    /ð/ mother  /ð/ brother /ð/ </code>``</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — a digraph.</strong> Two letters written side by side that produce <strong>one</strong> sound are a <strong>digraph</strong>. <em>ch, sh, th, ph, wh</em> are digraphs. The two letters are never separated when we read: <em>fi-sh</em>, not <em>fis-h</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — /tʃ/ written <em>ch</em>.** The sound of <em>chair, cheese, children, teacher, lunch, watch</em>. It exists in Moroccan Arabic (as in \"تشيرة\"-type borrowings and in the name <em>Marrakech</em> pronounced with /ʃ/), so it is usually easy. Careful: in a few words of Greek origin <em>ch</em> = /k/ (<em>school, chemistry</em>) and in French loanwords <em>ch</em> = /ʃ/ (<em>machine, Marrakesh</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — /ʃ/ written <em>sh</em>.** The sound of <em>shop, shirt, shoe, fish, wash, English (-sh)</em>. It is the Arabic ش, so production is easy; the difficulty is spelling, because learners write <em>ch</em> for this sound under French influence (<em>chirt</em> for <em>shirt</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — <em>th</em> has two sounds.<strong> </strong>Voiceless /θ/** in <em>three, think, thank, thirsty, mouth, month</em> — the tongue tip is between the teeth and there is no vibration (Arabic ث). <strong>Voiced /ð/</strong> in <em>this, that, these, those, mother, brother, the, they</em> — same tongue position, but the voice vibrates (Arabic ذ). Both sounds exist in standard Arabic, so Moroccan learners can produce them by transfer; the trap is replacing them with /s/, /z/, /t/ or /d/ under French influence.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — reading a word with a digraph.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Spot the pair</strong> of letters (<em>ch, sh, th</em>) and cover the rest of the word.</li><li><strong>Say the digraph sound alone</strong> (/tʃ/, /ʃ/, /θ/ or /ð/).</li><li><strong>Add the vowel</strong> and finish the word (/tʃ/ + /eə/ = <em>chair</em>).</li><li><strong>Test /θ/ vs /ð/</strong> with your hand on your throat: vibration = /ð/ (<em>this</em>), no vibration = /θ/ (<em>think</em>).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> <em>thirsty</em>. Digraph = <em>th</em>. Is it voiced? Put your hand on your throat: no vibration → /θ/. Result: /ˈθɜːsti/. Compare <em>thirteen</em> /θɜːˈtiːn/ — same start.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> <em>these</em>. Digraph = <em>th</em> + long vowel. Hand on throat: vibration → /ð/. Result: /ðiːz/. Grammar words (<em>the, this, that, these, those, they, them, then, there</em>) almost always take the <strong>voiced</strong> /ð/.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> <em>school</em>. The word contains <em>ch</em>, but here the sound is /k/: /skuːl/. The class notes this in a small \"exceptions\" box, with <em>chemistry</em> /ˈkemɪstri/ and <em>Christmas</em> /ˈkrɪsməs/.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Sound sorting race.\" The teacher reads eighteen words in random order; each pair writes them in three columns (/tʃ/, /ʃ/, /θ-ð/). The first pair with eighteen correct entries wins. Then pupils read the tongue-twister aloud to each other, three times, faster each time:</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>Three children with three chairs and three shirts wash the fish in the shop. This shirt is thin, that shirt is thick, and my brother thinks they are the same.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p>The partner counts the mistakes; roles are swapped. Finally two volunteers perform for the class.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Digraph</th><th>Sound (IPA)</th><th>Voiced?</th><th>Key words</th><th>Arabic equivalent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ch</td><td>/tʃ/</td><td>voiceless</td><td>chair, cheese, children, teacher, lunch, watch</td><td>تش</td></tr><tr><td>ch (exception)</td><td>/k/</td><td>voiceless</td><td>school, chemistry, Christmas</td><td>ك</td></tr><tr><td>sh</td><td>/ʃ/</td><td>voiceless</td><td>shop, shirt, shoe, fish, wash, English</td><td>ش</td></tr><tr><td>th</td><td>/θ/</td><td>voiceless</td><td>three, think, thank, thirsty, month, mouth</td><td>ث</td></tr><tr><td>th</td><td>/ð/</td><td>voiced</td><td>this, that, these, those, the, they, mother, brother</td><td>ذ</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Useful new words</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a shirt</td><td>/ʃɜːt/</td><td>قَميص</td></tr><tr><td>a shop</td><td>/ʃɒp/</td><td>دُكّان</td></tr><tr><td>cheese</td><td>/tʃiːz/</td><td>جُبْن</td></tr><tr><td>children</td><td>/ˈtʃɪldrən/</td><td>أَطْفال</td></tr><tr><td>to think</td><td>/θɪŋk/</td><td>يُفَكِّر</td></tr><tr><td>a mouth</td><td>/maʊθ/</td><td>فَم</td></tr><tr><td>a brother</td><td>/ˈbrʌðə/</td><td>أَخ</td></tr><tr><td>thin / thick</td><td>/θɪn/ /θɪk/</td><td>رَقيق / سَميك</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Writing <em>ch</em> for the /ʃ/ sound (<em>chop</em> for <em>shop</em>, <em>chirt</em> for <em>shirt</em>) under the influence of French spelling. ⚠️ Replacing /θ/ with /s/ or /t/ (<em>sink</em> for <em>think</em>, <em>tank</em> for <em>thank</em>) and /ð/ with /z/ or /d/ (<em>zis</em> for <em>this</em>). ⚠️ Separating the two letters of the digraph when reading (<em>fis-h</em>, <em>t-hink</em>). ⚠️ Forgetting that <em>ch</em> = /k/ in <em>school</em> and <em>chemistry</em>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Underline the digraph: chair · fish · think · shop · mother · lunch.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Sort into <em>ch</em>, <em>sh</em>, <em>th</em>: cheese, shirt, three, teacher, wash, brother.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Write the missing digraph: (a) …air (b) …op (c) …ree (d) tea…er (e) fi… (f) …is.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>/θ/ or /ð/? (a) think (b) this (c) thank (d) mother (e) month (f) they.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Which word has <em>ch</em> = /k/? chair · school · cheese · children.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Correct the spelling: (a) chirt (b) chop (for a place where you buy things) (c) tink (d) zis.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Find two words of your own for each sound: /tʃ/, /ʃ/, /θ/, /ð/.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Read aloud and mark the digraph sound above each word: <em>This teacher thinks the shirt is thin.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete with words from the lesson: My … (أخ) is in the … (دكان). He wants … (جبن) and a … (قميص).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Say whether the words rhyme: (a) think / sink (b) this / fish (c) chair / share.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write your own tongue-twister of two lines using at least four words with <em>ch</em>, <em>sh</em> or <em>th</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Read the lesson tongue-twister three times and note your time; then write five words from it in a table with their digraph and IPA sound.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Write the missing digraph: (a) …eese (b) …oe (c) …ank (d) mo…er (e) lun… (f) wa… .</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> /θ/ or /ð/? three · this · month · brother · thirsty · they.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write four sentences, each containing at least two words with <em>ch</em>, <em>sh</em> or <em>th</em> (example: <em>My brother thinks this shirt is thin.</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) ch (b) sh (c) th (d) th (e) ch (f) sh.</li><li>Task 2 (1 each): /θ/ three, month, thirsty · /ð/ this, brother, they.</li><li>Task 3 (2 each): two digraph words 1 · correct spelling of the digraphs 0.5 · capital letter and full stop 0.5.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en09","domaine":"anglais","unite":9,"theme":"Unit 2 — They're happy now!","tag":"EN09","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 2 - Reading (a poem)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Read an original A1 poem aloud with rhythm and correct digraph sounds.","Identify the rhyming pairs and the number of lines and stanzas.","Find the feelings expressed in the poem and the reason for each.","Answer comprehension questions in short complete sentences.","Write two new rhyming lines in the same pattern."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> The teacher asks: \"What is a poem?\" and gives the three ideas needed at A1: a poem is <strong>short</strong>, it is written in <strong>lines</strong>, and the ends of the lines often <strong>rhyme</strong> (the same sound, like <em>sad</em> and <em>bad</em>). Pupils recall a short Arabic nursery rhyme they know and clap its rhythm; then they will clap the rhythm of the English poem.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading text (original poem written for this database).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Monday Morning, Friday Afternoon</strong> On Monday morning I am tired, My schoolbag's heavy, my shoes are new. My brother's happy, my sister's bored, And the sky in Fez is grey, not blue. At nine o'clock we are in class, Three maps, one board, and chalk that's white. Our teacher smiles: \"Good morning, class!\" And suddenly the room is bright. On Friday afternoon at two We aren't sad and we aren't slow. The ball is round, the team is you, The goal is near, the wind is low. We win! We shout! We are not tired. My brother's happy, my sister's too. The sky in Fez is not so grey, And Monday morning feels far away. (137 words with the title.)</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>While reading — three passes.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Listening and clapping (5 min).</strong> The teacher reads the poem twice; pupils clap once per stressed syllable in the first stanza. They discover that English poetry has a <strong>beat</strong>, and that a line usually carries four strong beats.</li><li><strong>Rhyme hunt (8 min).</strong> Pupils underline the last word of each line and pair the sounds: <em>tired / bored</em> (no rhyme), <em>new / blue</em> (rhyme /uː/), <em>class / that's</em> (near), <em>white / bright</em> (rhyme /aɪt/), <em>two / you</em> (/uː/), <em>slow / low</em> (/əʊ/), <em>tired / grey / away</em>. They conclude that stanzas 1–3 rhyme in an <strong>A B C B</strong> pattern.</li><li><strong>Meaning (10 min).</strong> Pupils fill in a two-column table: <strong>feeling</strong> / <strong>reason</strong> — <em>I am tired (Monday morning) · my brother is happy (no reason given) · my sister is bored (Monday) · we aren't sad (Friday, football) · we are not tired (we win) · my sister is happy too (we win)</em>.</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method — reading a poem in four steps.</strong> (1) Read it <strong>silently</strong> for the general picture: who speaks, where, when. (2) Read it <strong>aloud</strong> and mark the beats. (3) Find the <strong>rhymes</strong> at the end of the lines. (4) Look for the <strong>feelings</strong> and their <strong>causes</strong>, then say in one sentence what the poem is about.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Question: <em>How does the speaker feel on Monday morning?</em> Key words = <em>Monday morning</em>, line 1. The text says \"On Monday morning I am tired\". Answer: <strong>\"He/She is tired.\"</strong> The A1 answer keeps the adjective from the poem.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Question: <em>Why aren't they tired on Friday afternoon?</em> Key words = <em>Friday, tired</em>. Lines 9–13 mention the ball, the team, the goal, and \"We win!\". Answer: <strong>\"Because they play football and they win.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — rhyme.</strong> Which word rhymes with <em>new</em>? Candidates in the poem: <em>blue, two, you</em>. All three end in /uː/. A rhyme is a matter of <strong>sound</strong>, not spelling: <em>new, blue, two, you</em> have four different spellings and one sound.</p>\n<p><strong>After reading — speaking and creating.</strong> In pairs, pupils choose a stanza, learn it by heart in four minutes (the teacher rubs out one word every thirty seconds until the board is empty — the \"vanishing poem\" technique), and recite it to each other. Then each pupil writes <strong>two new lines</strong> on the model \"On Saturday morning I am …, / My … is … , my … is … .\" The best six lines are copied on a class poster entitled <em>Our feelings poem</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Poem word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>heavy</td><td>/ˈhevi/</td><td>ثَقيل</td></tr><tr><td>grey</td><td>/ɡreɪ/</td><td>رَمادي</td></tr><tr><td>bright</td><td>/braɪt/</td><td>مُشْرِق</td></tr><tr><td>round</td><td>/raʊnd/</td><td>مُسْتَدير</td></tr><tr><td>a goal</td><td>/ɡəʊl/</td><td>هَدَف</td></tr><tr><td>the wind</td><td>/wɪnd/</td><td>الرّيح</td></tr><tr><td>to shout</td><td>/ʃaʊt/</td><td>يَصيح</td></tr><tr><td>slow</td><td>/sləʊ/</td><td>بَطيء</td></tr><tr><td>far away</td><td>/ˌfɑːr əˈweɪ/</td><td>بَعيد</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Poetry term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example from the poem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>line</td><td>one row of the poem</td><td>\"On Monday morning I am tired,\"</td></tr><tr><td>stanza</td><td>a group of lines</td><td>the poem has four stanzas of four lines</td></tr><tr><td>rhyme</td><td>same sound at the end of lines</td><td>new / blue / two / you</td></tr><tr><td>beat (rhythm)</td><td>the strong syllables</td><td><em>ON</em> Mon-day <em>MOR</em>-ning <em>I</em> am <em>TIRED</em></td></tr><tr><td>repetition</td><td>a word or structure said again</td><td>\"We win! We shout!\"</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Structures re-used</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>be + adjective</td><td>I am tired · the sky is grey</td></tr><tr><td>aren't / are not</td><td>We aren't sad.</td></tr><tr><td>possessive 's</td><td>my schoolbag's heavy = my schoolbag is heavy</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Believing that rhyme = same spelling: <em>new</em> and <em>sew</em> are spelled alike at the end but do not rhyme; <em>new</em> and <em>blue</em> rhyme with different spellings. ⚠️ Reading a poem word by word without stopping at the end of the line, which destroys the rhythm. ⚠️ Confusing the two uses of <em>'s</em>: in \"my schoolbag<strong>'s</strong> heavy\" it is <em>is</em>; in \"my brother<strong>'s</strong> book\" it is possession. ⚠️ Answering \"Why …?\" with a feeling only (<em>tired</em>) instead of a clause with <em>because</em>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>How many stanzas and how many lines are there in the poem?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Find the rhyming word in the poem: (a) new (b) white (c) slow (d) two.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>True or false + justification: (a) The speaker is happy on Monday morning. (b) The sky in Fez is blue on Monday. (c) The team wins on Friday.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Complete from the poem: \"My schoolbag's … , my shoes are … .\" / \"The ball is … , the team is … .\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Find in the poem three words with the digraph <em>sh</em> or <em>th</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Answer in complete sentences: (a) Where does the speaker live? (b) How does the sister feel on Monday? (c) Why are they happy on Friday? (d) What time are they in class?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Rewrite in full form: (a) My schoolbag's heavy. (b) My brother's happy. (c) chalk that's white.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Match the feeling with its cause according to the poem: tired · bored · happy · not tired — Monday morning · school on Monday · the win · the football game.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Say whether these pairs rhyme: (a) grey / away (b) blue / new (c) class / white (d) low / slow.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four negative sentences taken from the poem's ideas, using <em>aren't</em> or <em>isn't</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a four-line stanza about Saturday morning, with an A B C B rhyme, using two feeling adjectives.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Learn the last stanza by heart and write it from memory; then explain in two sentences why \"Monday morning feels far away\".</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (8 marks).</strong> Answer in complete sentences: (a) How does the speaker feel on Monday morning? (b) What colour is the sky in Fez on Monday? (c) What happens on Friday afternoon? (d) How does the sister feel at the end?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Find the rhyming pairs: new · bright · low · white · blue · slow.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (6 marks).</strong> Write four lines of your own poem about your feelings on a school day. Use at least two feeling adjectives and one rhyme pair.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (2 each): (a) He/She is tired. (b) It is grey. (c) They play football and they win. (d) She is happy.</li><li>Task 2 (2 per pair): new/blue · bright/white · low/slow.</li><li>Task 3 (6): four lines 2 · two feeling adjectives 2 · one correct rhyme 1 · spelling and capitals 1.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en10","domaine":"anglais","unite":10,"theme":"Unit 2 — They're happy now!","tag":"EN10","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 2 - Writing (long and short forms, writing about my feelings)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Convert long forms of <em>be</em> into short forms and vice versa.","Place the apostrophe correctly in <em>I'm, you're, he's, isn't, aren't</em>.","Choose long forms for formal writing and short forms for friendly writing.","Write a guided paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) about his/her feelings during a week.","Self-check the text with a five-point checklist."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> Two messages are written on the board. Message 1: <em>Dear Sir, I am not at school today. I am ill. My mother is with me.</em> Message 2: <em>Hi Anas! I'm not at school today — I'm ill! My mother's with me. See you!</em> The content is the same, the forms are different: <strong>long forms</strong> in the note to the teacher, <strong>short forms</strong> in the message to a friend. Both are correct English; the choice depends on <strong>who reads</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> Pupils build the conversion table themselves. The teacher writes the long forms and pupils supply the short ones, saying where the apostrophe goes and which letter it replaces:</p>\n<p>``<code> I am        -> I'm        (a) you are     -> you're     (a) he is       -> he's       (i) she is      -> she's      (i) it is       -> it's       (i) we are      -> we're      (a) they are    -> they're    (a) is not      -> isn't      (o) are not     -> aren't     (o) am not      -> I'm not    (no <em>amn't</em>) </code>``</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the apostrophe replaces the missing letter(s).</strong> <em>I am → I'<strong>m</strong></em> (the <em>a</em> of <em>am</em> disappears); <em>he is → he'<strong>s</strong></em> (the <em>i</em> of <em>is</em>); <em>are not → are<strong>n't</strong></em> (the <em>o</em> of <em>not</em>). The apostrophe goes exactly where the letter was removed, never between the words.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — when to use each form.</strong> <strong>Short forms</strong>: speaking, dialogues, friendly letters and messages, poems. <strong>Long forms</strong>: exam answers when the sentence must be complete, formal notes, and always in a <strong>positive short answer</strong> (<em>Yes, I am.</em>, never <em>Yes, I'm.</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Careful — it's / its and they're / their / there.</strong> <em>It's</em> = <em>it is</em> (<em>It's hot today.</em>) but <em>its</em> = possession (<em>The dog and its bowl.</em>). <em>They're</em> = <em>they are</em>; <em>their</em> = possession (<em>their books</em>); <em>there</em> = place or <em>there is/are</em>. This is the most common written mistake at every level, so learn the three words in one sentence: <em>They're in there with their books.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — writing a paragraph about my feelings.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Choose the frame</strong>: one day of the week per sentence, or one situation per sentence.</li><li><strong>Write the sentence</strong>: subject + <em>be</em> + (adverb) + feeling adjective + <em>because</em> + reason. <em>On Monday I'm tired because I get up at six.</em></li><li><strong>Alternate the forms</strong>: use short forms for a friendly text, long forms for the first and last sentences to give them weight.</li><li><strong>Link</strong> with <em>and, but, so, because</em>.</li><li><strong>Check</strong> five things: capital letters · full stops · a <em>be</em> form in every sentence · apostrophes in the right place · no adjective with <em>-s</em>.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1 — long to short.</strong> <em>We are not hungry because it is early.</em> → <em>We <strong>aren't</strong> hungry because <strong>it's</strong> early.</em> Two apostrophes, two removed letters (<em>o</em> of <em>not</em>, <em>i</em> of <em>is</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2 — short to long.</strong> <em>They're happy but she isn't.</em> → <em>They <strong>are</strong> happy but she <strong>is not</strong>.</em> In a test, when the instruction says \"write the full form\", the answer must contain no apostrophe at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — a model paragraph (74 words).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>My week.</strong> My name is Karim and I'm twelve years old. On Monday I'm tired because we have got five hours of lessons. On Tuesday I'm happy because we have got English. On Wednesday afternoon my friends and I aren't at school, so we play football in the street. On Thursday I'm a little worried because we have got a maths test. On Friday I'm excited: it's couscous day at home! I am never bored at school.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The class counts the short forms (<em>I'm</em> × 4, <em>aren't</em>, <em>it's</em>) and the long forms (<em>I am</em> in the first and last sentences), and identifies four <em>because</em> clauses.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (peer editing).</strong> Partners exchange notebooks and use the five-point checklist. They must find at least one apostrophe placed wrongly or missing, one capital letter and one full stop, and write the three points at the bottom of the page as \"two stars and a wish\". Authors then rewrite their final version.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Long form</th><th>Short form</th><th>Letter removed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I am</td><td>I'm</td><td>a</td></tr><tr><td>you are</td><td>you're</td><td>a</td></tr><tr><td>he is / she is / it is</td><td>he's / she's / it's</td><td>i</td></tr><tr><td>we are</td><td>we're</td><td>a</td></tr><tr><td>they are</td><td>they're</td><td>a</td></tr><tr><td>is not</td><td>isn't</td><td>o</td></tr><tr><td>are not</td><td>aren't</td><td>o</td></tr><tr><td>am not</td><td>I'm not (no <em>amn't</em>)</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Confusable pair</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>it's / its</td><td>it is / possession</td><td>It's hot. / Its colour is blue.</td></tr><tr><td>they're / their / there</td><td>they are / possession / place</td><td>They're there with their bags.</td></tr><tr><td>you're / your</td><td>you are / possession</td><td>You're late. / Your bag is here.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Register</th><th>Choose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>speaking, message to a friend, dialogue, poem</td><td>short forms</td></tr><tr><td>note to the teacher, test answer, positive short answer</td><td>long forms</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Five-point self-check</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>capital letters (sentence, names, days)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>full stop at every sentence end</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>a form of <em>be</em> in every sentence</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>apostrophe in the right place</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>adjectives without <em>-s</em></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Putting the apostrophe between the two words: <em>are'nt</em>, <em>is'nt</em>, <em>I a'm</em>. It replaces the missing letter: <em>aren't, isn't, I'm</em>. ⚠️ Writing <em>there</em> for <em>they're</em> or <em>your</em> for <em>you're</em>. ⚠️ Contracting a positive short answer: <em>Yes, I'm.</em> → <strong>Yes, I am.</strong> ⚠️ Writing <em>amn't</em>: the only possible contraction in the first person negative is <em>I'm not</em>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Write the short form: (a) I am happy. (b) She is tired. (c) They are hungry. (d) We are not bored. (e) He is not cold.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Write the long form: (a) You're late. (b) It's hot. (c) They aren't sad. (d) I'm not thirsty.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Put the apostrophe in the right place: (a) Im tired. (b) shes happy. (c) they arent ready. (d) it isnt cold.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Choose: (a) (It's / Its) very hot today. (b) (They're / Their) books are new. (c) (You're / Your) my friend.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Short or long form? Say which register: (a) a message to a friend (b) a note to the headmaster (c) a positive short answer.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Correct: (a) Yes, I'm. (b) I amn't tired. (c) They are'nt happy. (d) Their happy now.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Rewrite the paragraph with short forms: <em>I am Karim. I am twelve. My sister is bored and my brothers are not at home. It is Friday.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Rewrite with long forms: <em>We're in the first year. We aren't tired. It's a good school. I'm happy here.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete with a feeling and a reason: (a) On Monday I'm … because … . (b) On Friday we aren't … because … .</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four sentences about your feelings on four days of the week, using two short forms and two long forms.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) entitled \"My week and my feelings\". Use four feeling adjectives, three <em>because</em> clauses, at least three short forms and one long form.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write the same content twice in three sentences each: version A as a message to a friend (short forms), version B as a note to your teacher (long forms).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Write the short form: (a) I am worried. (b) She is not tired. (c) They are excited. (d) We are not bored. (e) He is happy. (f) It is cold.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Correct the mistakes: (a) Yes, they're. (b) I amn't hungry. (c) Their very happy today.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) about your feelings during the week. Use four feeling adjectives, two <em>because</em> clauses and at least three short forms.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) I'm worried. (b) She isn't tired. (c) They're excited. (d) We aren't bored. (e) He's happy. (f) It's cold.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) Yes, they <strong>are</strong>. (b) I'<strong>m not</strong> hungry. (c) <strong>They're</strong> very happy today.</li><li>Task 3 (8): task achievement 2 · four feeling adjectives 2 · two <em>because</em> clauses 2 · forms, apostrophes, capitals and full stops 2.</li></ul>\n<p>## Unit 3 — I can ride a bike!</p>\n<p>Unit 3 introduces ability. Learners name outdoor activities, use the modal <em>can / can't</em> in the three sentence types, revise short-vowel CVC words, read information on a web page and write about what they can and cannot do. Anchor context: a sports club in <strong>Agadir</strong>, the beach, the Atlas mountains, and a school bicycle in <strong>Fez</strong>.</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en11","domaine":"anglais","unite":11,"theme":"Unit 3 — I can ride a bike!","tag":"EN11","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 3 - Words (outdoor activities)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Name at least fourteen outdoor activities in English.","Combine the right verb with the right activity (<em>play</em> football, <em>ride</em> a bike, <em>go</em> swimming).","Use the three verb families <em>play / ride / go</em> correctly.","Ask \"What do you do after school?\" and answer with two activities.","Classify activities by place (beach, mountain, street, schoolyard)."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> Saturday morning in Agadir. On the beach, Sofia and her cousins <strong>swim</strong> and <strong>play volleyball</strong>. In the street behind the school, Anas <strong>rides a bike</strong> and his brother <strong>skates</strong>. In the Atlas mountains near Ifrane, an uncle <strong>walks</strong> and <strong>climbs</strong>. Every activity needs a <strong>verb</strong>, and in English the verb is not chosen freely: we say <em>play football</em> but <em>ride a bike</em> and <em>go swimming</em>. This lesson learns the activity words and their verbs together, because a verb and its noun form one block in the memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher mimes fourteen activities one after the other; pupils guess in Arabic, then repeat the English: <em>play football · play volleyball · play basketball · play tennis · ride a bike · ride a horse · swim · run · walk · climb · skate · skip · fly a kite · fish</em>. Pupils then classify them in a three-column table according to the verb used, and discover the pattern themselves.</p>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — <em>play</em> + ball games and games with rules.** <em>play football, play volleyball, play basketball, play tennis, play chess, play cards</em>. No article: <em>play football</em>, not <em>play the football</em>. (With musical instruments we do use <em>the</em>: <em>play the guitar</em>.)</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — <em>ride</em> + something you sit on.** <em>ride a bike, ride a horse, ride a motorbike, ride a camel</em>. Here the article <strong>a</strong> is needed.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — <em>go</em> + activity ending in <em>-ing</em>.** <em>go swimming, go running, go walking, go fishing, go climbing, go skating, go shopping</em>. This structure names an outing: <em>On Sunday we go swimming.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — simple verbs alone.</strong> Some activities are just one verb: <em>swim, run, walk, climb, skate, skip, jump, fish</em>. So the same idea has two possible forms: <em>I swim</em> (I do the action) and <em>I go swimming</em> (I go out to do it). Both are correct at A1.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — choosing the verb.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Is it a ball game or a game with rules?</strong> → <em>play</em> + no article (<em>play basketball</em>).</li><li><strong>Do you sit on it?</strong> → <em>ride</em> + <em>a</em> (<em>ride a bike</em>).</li><li><strong>Is it an outing?</strong> → <em>go</em> + verb-<em>ing</em> (<em>go fishing</em>).</li><li><strong>Otherwise</strong> use the simple verb alone (<em>I run every morning</em>).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Anas has a bicycle. He sits on it → <em>ride</em>, with an article: <strong>\"Anas rides a bike.\"</strong> In the third person singular the verb takes <strong>-s</strong> (studied fully in EN27 and EN42); at this stage learners meet it as a fixed pattern: <em>he rides, she swims, he plays</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> On Sunday morning the family leaves the house to swim at the beach in Agadir. It is an outing → <em>go</em> + <em>-ing</em>: <strong>\"On Sunday we go swimming.\"</strong> If we simply describe the action in the water we say: <strong>\"We swim in the sea.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes <em>I play the football with my friends.</em> Football is a ball game, so no article: <strong>\"I play football with my friends.\"</strong> Compare: <em>I play the guitar</em> (instrument, article needed).</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Activity survey.\" Each pupil draws a table with four columns: <em>activity · me · partner 1 · partner 2</em>. He/she writes six activities of his/her choice, then asks the two nearest classmates: \"Do you play volleyball?\" — \"Yes, I do. / No, I don't.\" (The auxiliary <em>do</em> is used here as a fixed formula; it is taught systematically in EN27.) Each pupil then reports one result to the class: <em>\"Salma plays volleyball, but Hind doesn't.\"</em></p>\n<p><strong>Moroccan context.</strong> Football is the first activity in every Moroccan schoolyard, and neighbourhood tournaments are organised in the street in summer. On the coast (Agadir, Essaouira) swimming and surfing are common; in the Atlas, walking and climbing; in the countryside, riding a horse or a mule and flying kites over the fields. Pupils choose the three activities that exist in their own town and keep them for the writing lesson EN15.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th><th>Verb family</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>play football</td><td>/ˈfʊtbɔːl/</td><td>يَلْعَب كُرَة القَدَم</td><td>play</td></tr><tr><td>play volleyball</td><td>/ˈvɒlibɔːl/</td><td>الكُرَة الطّائِرة</td><td>play</td></tr><tr><td>play basketball</td><td>/ˈbɑːskɪtbɔːl/</td><td>كُرَة السَّلّة</td><td>play</td></tr><tr><td>play tennis</td><td>/ˈtenɪs/</td><td>التِّنِس</td><td>play</td></tr><tr><td>ride a bike</td><td>/raɪd ə baɪk/</td><td>يَرْكَب الدَّرّاجة</td><td>ride</td></tr><tr><td>ride a horse</td><td>/hɔːs/</td><td>يَرْكَب الحِصان</td><td>ride</td></tr><tr><td>swim / go swimming</td><td>/swɪm/</td><td>يَسْبَح</td><td>simple / go</td></tr><tr><td>run</td><td>/rʌn/</td><td>يَجْري</td><td>simple</td></tr><tr><td>walk</td><td>/wɔːk/</td><td>يَمْشي</td><td>simple / go</td></tr><tr><td>climb</td><td>/klaɪm/</td><td>يَتَسَلَّق</td><td>simple / go</td></tr><tr><td>skate</td><td>/skeɪt/</td><td>يَتَزَلَّج</td><td>simple / go</td></tr><tr><td>skip</td><td>/skɪp/</td><td>يَنُطّ بِالحَبْل</td><td>simple</td></tr><tr><td>fly a kite</td><td>/flaɪ ə kaɪt/</td><td>يُطَيِّر طائِرة وَرَقية</td><td>fly</td></tr><tr><td>fish / go fishing</td><td>/fɪʃ/</td><td>يَصْطاد السَّمَك</td><td>simple / go</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>play + game (no article)</td><td>I play football.</td></tr><tr><td>ride + a + vehicle/animal</td><td>She rides a bike.</td></tr><tr><td>go + verb-ing</td><td>We go swimming on Sunday.</td></tr><tr><td>simple verb</td><td>They run in the schoolyard.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Place words</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>on the beach · in the sea</td><td>Agadir, Essaouira</td></tr><tr><td>in the mountains</td><td>the Atlas, Ifrane</td></tr><tr><td>in the street · in the schoolyard</td><td>everywhere</td></tr><tr><td>in the park · in the countryside</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>I play the football</em> — no article with ball games; the article stays with instruments (<em>play the guitar</em>). ⚠️ <em>I go to swimming</em> — the structure is <em>go swimming</em>, with no <em>to</em>. ⚠️ <em>I ride bike</em> — <em>ride</em> needs the article: <em>ride <strong>a</strong> bike</em>. ⚠️ Confusing <em>climb</em> /klaɪm/ (silent <em>b</em>) and <em>climate</em>; and pronouncing the <em>l</em> in <em>walk</em> /wɔːk/.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Complete with <em>play</em>, <em>ride</em> or <em>go</em>: (a) … football (b) … a bike (c) … swimming (d) … tennis (e) … a horse (f) … fishing.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Match the activity with the place: swim · climb · play football · fly a kite — in the schoolyard · in the sea · in the mountains · in the countryside.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Write the Arabic gloss: ride a bike · go swimming · climb · skip · fly a kite.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>True or false: (a) We say \"play the volleyball\". (b) We say \"go fishing\". (c) We say \"ride a horse\".</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Write three activities you can do on the beach in Agadir.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Correct: (a) I play the basketball. (b) She go to swimming. (c) He rides bike. (d) They plays tennis.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Complete the sentences about your town: (a) In my street, children … . (b) In the schoolyard, we … . (c) On Sunday, my family … .</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Ask the question: (a) I play football after school. (b) Yes, I do. (c) In the schoolyard.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Classify these activities in the three verb families: tennis, a horse, swimming, basketball, a bike, climbing.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Complete the dialogue: A: \"What … you do after school?\" B: \"I … football and I … a bike. And you?\" A: \"I … swimming on Saturday.\"</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write six sentences about a Sunday with your family, using <em>play</em>, <em>ride</em>, <em>go + -ing</em> and two place words.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Conduct the activity survey with two classmates (six activities), then write four sentences reporting the results (example: <em>Omar plays football, but Hind doesn't.</em>).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"play football","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈfʊtbɔːl/</span> play — يَلْعَب كُرَة القَدَم"},{"r":"play volleyball","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈvɒlibɔːl/</span> play — الكُرَة الطّائِرة"},{"r":"play basketball","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈbɑːskɪtbɔːl/</span> play — كُرَة السَّلّة"},{"r":"play tennis","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈtenɪs/</span> play — التِّنِس"},{"r":"ride a bike","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/raɪd ə baɪk/</span> ride — يَرْكَب الدَّرّاجة"},{"r":"ride a horse","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/hɔːs/</span> ride — يَرْكَب الحِصان"},{"r":"swim / go swimming","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/swɪm/</span> simple / go — يَسْبَح"},{"r":"run","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/rʌn/</span> simple — يَجْري"},{"r":"walk","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/wɔːk/</span> simple / go — يَمْشي"},{"r":"climb","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/klaɪm/</span> simple / go — يَتَسَلَّق"},{"r":"skate","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/skeɪt/</span> simple / go — يَتَزَلَّج"},{"r":"skip","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/skɪp/</span> simple — يَنُطّ بِالحَبْل"},{"r":"fly a kite","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/flaɪ ə kaɪt/</span> fly — يُطَيِّر طائِرة وَرَقية"},{"r":"fish / go fishing","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/fɪʃ/</span> simple / go — يَصْطاد السَّمَك"},{"r":"on the beach · in the sea","v":"Agadir, Essaouira"},{"r":"in the mountains","v":"the Atlas, Ifrane"},{"r":"in the street · in the schoolyard","v":"everywhere"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>play</em>, <em>ride</em>, <em>go</em> or nothing: (a) I … volleyball. (b) She … a bike. (c) We … fishing. (d) They … in the schoolyard (run). (e) He … a horse. (f) I … swimming.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Correct the mistakes: (a) We play the tennis. (b) He go to climbing. (c) She rides bike.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write five sentences about the activities of your family and friends on Sunday, with at least two place words.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) play (b) rides (c) go (d) run (e) rides (f) go.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) We play tennis. (b) He <strong>goes</strong> climbing. (c) She rides <strong>a</strong> bike.</li><li>Task 3 (8): five sentences 3 · correct verb families 2 · two place words 2 · capitals and full stops 1.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en12","domaine":"anglais","unite":12,"theme":"Unit 3 — I can ride a bike!","tag":"EN12","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 3 - Grammar (she can / can't … / can he …?)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Express ability with <em>can</em> + base verb in all persons.","Build the negative <em>cannot / can't</em> and the question <em>Can you …?</em>","Give short answers <em>Yes, I can. / No, I can't.</em>","Distinguish ability (<em>can swim</em>) from permission and requests (<em>Can I go out, please?</em>).","Interview a classmate about five abilities and report the results."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> At the school sports club in Agadir the coach makes a list. He asks each pupil one question: \"<strong>Can</strong> you swim?\" — \"Yes, I <strong>can</strong>.\" / \"No, I <strong>can't</strong>.\" Anas answers: \"I <strong>can</strong> ride a bike and I <strong>can</strong> run fast, but I <strong>can't</strong> swim.\" One little word, <em>can</em>, expresses everything about what we are able to do. And it behaves differently from every other verb we know.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher writes six sentences on the board and asks the class to find the four rules of <em>can</em> by comparison:</p>\n<p>``<code> I can swim.              She can swim.            (no -s in the 3rd person!) I can't swim.            She can't swim.          (negative: can + not) Can you swim?            Can she swim?            (question: can before the subject) Yes, I can. / No, I can't.                        (short answer: can alone) </code>``</p>\n<p>Pupils notice: (1) <em>can</em> never changes; (2) the verb after it has **no <em>to</em><strong> and </strong>no <em>-s</em>**; (3) the negative adds <em>not</em>; (4) the question inverts, with no <em>do</em>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — form.</strong> <em>can</em> is a <strong>modal verb</strong>. It is the same for all persons: <em>I can · you can · he/she/it can · we can · they can</em>. It is followed by the <strong>base form</strong> of the verb, without <em>to</em>: <em>I can swim</em> (never <em>I can to swim</em>, never <em>she cans swim</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — negative.</strong> <em>cannot</em> = <em>can not</em>, usually written in one word, and contracted to <strong>can't</strong> /kɑːnt/. <em>He can't ride a horse.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — question.</strong> Put <em>can</em> in front of the subject: <em>Can you swim? Can she ride a bike? Can they play tennis?</em> No auxiliary <em>do</em>. Wh-questions: <em>What can you do? Where can we swim? Who can climb?</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — short answers.</strong> <em>Yes, I can. / No, I can't.</em> — the main verb is never repeated: not <em>Yes, I can swim</em> as a short answer (though it is a correct full answer).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Meanings of <em>can</em>.<strong> (1) </strong>Ability**: <em>I can swim.</em> (2) <strong>Possibility</strong>: <em>We can play in the schoolyard at ten.</em> (3) <strong>Permission / request</strong>: <em>Can I open the window, please?</em> — <em>Can you help me, please?</em> At A1 the three uses are met, but ability is the focus of this unit.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — building the three forms.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Choose the subject</strong> (any person, <em>can</em> does not change).</li><li>**Add <em>can</em><strong> (affirmative), </strong><em>can't</em><strong> (negative), or </strong>put <em>can</em> first** (question).</li><li><strong>Add the base verb</strong> with no <em>to</em> and no <em>-s</em>.</li><li><strong>Complete</strong> with the activity and, if useful, an adverb: <em>well, very well, a little, fast</em>.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Subject = <em>my sister</em>; activity = <em>play volleyball</em>; she is able to. → <strong>\"My sister can play volleyball.\"</strong> With an adverb of degree: <strong>\"My sister can play volleyball very well.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Same subject, but she is not able to swim. → <strong>\"My sister can't swim.\"</strong> The question the coach asks: <strong>\"Can your sister swim?\"</strong> Short answer: <strong>\"No, she can't.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes <em>She cans to ride a bike.</em> Two errors: <em>can</em> never takes <em>-s</em>, and no <em>to</em> follows it. Correction: <strong>\"She can ride a bike.\"</strong> Compare with a normal verb: <em>She likes <strong>to</strong> ride a bike.</em> — where <em>to</em> is possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Ability interview.\" Each pupil writes five questions with <em>Can you …?</em> about activities from EN11. He/she interviews the shoulder partner and ticks Yes/No in a grid, then writes three report sentences: <em>\"Yasmine can ride a bike, but she can't swim.\"</em> Two pairs then perform a live interview at the front. The teacher records on the board the class score for each activity (e.g. <em>swim: 18 can, 27 can't</em>), which gives an authentic reason to use <em>can</em> in the third person plural: <strong>\"Eighteen pupils can swim.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Values link.</strong> The class discusses in simple English what everybody in the school should be able to do: <em>We can help our friends. We can keep our classroom clean. We can speak English in class.</em> Three sentences go on the wall poster.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>affirmative</td><td>subject + can + base verb</td><td>They can climb.</td></tr><tr><td>negative</td><td>subject + can't + base verb</td><td>He can't swim.</td></tr><tr><td>yes/no question</td><td>Can + subject + base verb?</td><td>Can you ride a bike?</td></tr><tr><td>wh-question</td><td>What/Where/Who + can + subject + base verb?</td><td>What can you do?</td></tr><tr><td>short answers</td><td>Yes, … can. / No, … can't.</td><td>Yes, she can.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Never</th><th>Always</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>she cans</em></td><td><em>she can</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>can to swim</em></td><td><em>can swim</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Do you can swim?</em></td><td><em>Can you swim?</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Yes, I can swim.</em> (as short answer)</td><td><em>Yes, I can.</em></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Useful adverbs</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>well / very well</td><td>ability level</td><td>I can swim very well.</td></tr><tr><td>a little / a bit</td><td>small ability</td><td>I can swim a little.</td></tr><tr><td>fast / slowly</td><td>speed</td><td>He can run fast.</td></tr><tr><td>not … at all</td><td>zero ability</td><td>I can't swim at all.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Three meanings of <em>can</em></th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ability</td><td>I can play the guitar.</td></tr><tr><td>possibility</td><td>We can play at four o'clock.</td></tr><tr><td>permission / request</td><td>Can I go out, please? · Can you help me?</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>She cans swim</em> — modal verbs never take <em>-s</em> in the third person singular. ⚠️ <em>I can to swim</em> — no <em>to</em> after <em>can</em>. ⚠️ <em>Do you can swim?</em> — <em>can</em> is its own auxiliary: <em>Can you swim?</em> ⚠️ Pronouncing <em>can't</em> like <em>can</em>: in the negative the vowel is long /kɑːnt/ and the /t/ must be heard, otherwise the meaning is reversed.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Complete with <em>can</em> or <em>can't</em> according to the picture-idea: (a) A fish … swim. (b) A cat … fly. (c) A bird … fly. (d) A baby … ride a bike.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Make negative: (a) I can swim. (b) She can climb. (c) They can play tennis. (d) He can run fast.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Make questions: (a) You can ride a horse. (b) She can skate. (c) They can play volleyball.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Give the two short answers for: (a) Can you swim? (b) Can he climb? (c) Can they play chess?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Correct: (a) She cans swim. (b) I can to run. (c) Do you can skate?</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Complete with an adverb (<em>very well, a little, fast, at all</em>): (a) I can swim … . (b) He can't swim … . (c) She can run … . (d) They can play tennis … .</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Write the wh-question: (a) I can play football and volleyball. (b) We can swim in the sea. (c) Hind can climb.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Ability or permission? (a) Can I open the door, please? (b) I can ride a bike. (c) Can you help me? (d) She can play the guitar.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete the interview: A: \"… you ride a bike?\" B: \"Yes, I … .\" A: \"… you swim?\" B: \"No, I … . But my brother … swim very well.\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four sentences about your own abilities: two with <em>can</em>, two with <em>can't</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Interview your partner with five <em>Can you …?</em> questions, then write four report sentences using <em>can</em> and <em>can't</em> in the third person.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a short paragraph (5–6 sentences) presenting yourself to the school sports club in Agadir: what you can do well, what you can do a little, and what you can't do yet.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"ability","v":"I can play the guitar."},{"r":"possibility","v":"We can play at four o'clock."},{"r":"permission / request","v":"Can I go out, please? · Can you help me?"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>can</em> or <em>can't</em>: (a) A fish … swim. (b) A dog … fly. (c) My baby brother … ride a bike. (d) Birds … fly. (e) I … speak Arabic. (f) A cat … play the guitar.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Write the question and the two short answers: (a) (you / swim) (b) (she / climb) (c) (they / play tennis).</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a paragraph of five sentences about what you and your family can and can't do. Use <em>can</em> three times, <em>can't</em> twice and one adverb (<em>very well, a little, fast</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) can (b) can't (c) can't (d) can (e) can (f) can't.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) Can you swim? — Yes, I can. / No, I can't. (b) Can she climb? — Yes, she can. / No, she can't. (c) Can they play tennis? — Yes, they can. / No, they can't.</li><li>Task 3 (8): five sentences 2 · <em>can</em> × 3 correct 2 · <em>can't</em> × 2 correct 2 · adverb 1 · capitals and full stops 1.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en13","domaine":"anglais","unite":13,"theme":"Unit 3 — I can ride a bike!","tag":"EN13","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 3 - Phonics (review of CVC words: a e i o u)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Define a CVC word and segment it into three sounds.","Produce the five short vowels /æ/, /e/, /ɪ/, /ɒ/, /ʌ/.","Blend three sounds into a word and segment a word into three sounds.","Discriminate minimal pairs (<em>cat/cut</em>, <em>pen/pin</em>, <em>hat/hot</em>).","Read and spell twenty CVC words accurately."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> The teacher writes three letters on the board: <strong>c – a – t</strong>. She says each sound separately, /k/ /æ/ /t/, then joins them faster and faster until the class hears <em>cat</em>. Then she changes only the middle letter: <strong>c – u – t</strong> → <em>cut</em>. One letter, one sound, a completely different word. Reading English begins here: hearing the three sounds inside a short word and blending them.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> Pupils build five word families on the board, one per short vowel:</p>\n<p>``<code> a /æ/    cat  hat  map  bag  man  fan  sad e /e/    pen  ten  red  bed  leg  net  hen i /ɪ/    pin  six  big  fish sit  win  lip o /ɒ/    dog  box  hot  top  pot  mop  sock u /ʌ/    cup  bus  sun  run  cut  mud  nut </code>``</p>\n<p>Then the teacher says a word and pupils show one, two or three fingers for the number of sounds; then they say which vowel they heard.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — a CVC word.</strong> A <strong>CVC</strong> word has three sounds in the order <strong>consonant – vowel – consonant</strong>: <em>cat</em> = /k/ + /æ/ + /t/, <em>pen</em> = /p/ + /e/ + /n/, <em>sun</em> = /s/ + /ʌ/ + /n/. Some words have four letters but are still CVC in sound, because a digraph counts as one sound: <em>fish</em> = /f/ + /ɪ/ + /ʃ/, <em>sock</em> = /s/ + /ɒ/ + /k/.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the short vowels.</strong> In a CVC word the single vowel letter is usually <strong>short</strong>: <strong>a</strong> = /æ/ (<em>cat</em>), <strong>e</strong> = /e/ (<em>pen</em>), <strong>i</strong> = /ɪ/ (<em>pin</em>), <strong>o</strong> = /ɒ/ (<em>dog</em>), <strong>u</strong> = /ʌ/ (<em>cup</em>). This is the most useful reading rule of English for beginners.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — blending and segmenting.</strong> <strong>Blending</strong> = joining sounds to read a word (/b/ /ɪ/ /ɡ/ → <em>big</em>). <strong>Segmenting</strong> = cutting a word into sounds to spell it (<em>bus</em> → /b/ /ʌ/ /s/ → b-u-s). A good reader does both, in both directions.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Careful — Arabic short vowels.</strong> Arabic has three short vowels (fatha, kasra, damma), English has five in the CVC pattern. The two new ones for Moroccan learners are /e/ (<em>pen</em>) versus /æ/ (<em>pan</em>), and /ʌ/ (<em>cup</em>) versus /ɒ/ (<em>cop</em>). These pairs must be drilled with the mouth open to different degrees.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — reading an unknown CVC word.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Point</strong> to each letter and say its <strong>sound</strong> (not its name): /m/ /ʌ/ /d/.</li><li><strong>Blend</strong> slowly, then at normal speed: <em>m-u-d, mud</em>.</li><li><strong>Check the meaning</strong>: does the word exist? Is it in my glossary?</li><li><strong>Write it</strong> and read it again to fix the spelling.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Read <em>hat</em>. Sounds: /h/ /æ/ /t/. Blend: <em>hat</em>. Change the vowel: /h/ /ɒ/ /t/ = <em>hot</em>; /h/ /ɪ/ /t/ = <em>hit</em>; /h/ /ʌ/ /t/ = <em>hut</em>. One family, four words, one difference: the middle sound.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Spell <em>fish</em>. Segment: /f/ /ɪ/ /ʃ/ — three sounds, but the last one is written with the digraph <em>sh</em>, so four letters: <strong>f-i-sh</strong>. This is why we count <strong>sounds</strong>, not letters.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil reads <em>pen</em> as /piːn/, using the letter name /iː/ instead of the sound /e/. Remedy: go back to the key word of the family (<em>bed</em>), say /e/ alone with the mouth half open, then blend /p/ /e/ /n/.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Vowel swap.\" Pupil A says a CVC word from the list; pupil B must change the vowel and produce a real word (<em>cat → cut</em>, <em>pen → pin</em>, <em>dog → dig</em>). If the new word does not exist, the turn passes. Then they play \"sound dictation\": A says three sounds slowly, B writes the word. Ten items each. Finally each pair reads aloud this original CVC text and counts the words with each vowel:</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>A big dog sits on the mat. The sun is hot. A man has a red cap and a fat cat. The cat is in the box with a nut and a cup. The dog can run and the cat can jump. Ten pupils sit on the bus with a bag, a pen and a map. It is fun!</em></p></blockquote>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Vowel letter</th><th>Short sound</th><th>Key word</th><th>Six more words</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a</td><td>/æ/</td><td>cat</td><td>hat, map, bag, man, fan, sad</td></tr><tr><td>e</td><td>/e/</td><td>pen</td><td>ten, red, bed, leg, net, hen</td></tr><tr><td>i</td><td>/ɪ/</td><td>pin</td><td>six, big, fish, sit, win, lip</td></tr><tr><td>o</td><td>/ɒ/</td><td>dog</td><td>box, hot, top, pot, sock, mop</td></tr><tr><td>u</td><td>/ʌ/</td><td>cup</td><td>bus, sun, run, cut, mud, nut</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CVC word</td><td>consonant + vowel + consonant, three sounds</td></tr><tr><td>blending</td><td>joining sounds to read (/b/ /ʌ/ /s/ → bus)</td></tr><tr><td>segmenting</td><td>cutting a word to spell it (bus → /b/ /ʌ/ /s/)</td></tr><tr><td>minimal pair</td><td>two words differing by one sound (cat / cut)</td></tr><tr><td>short vowel</td><td>the sound of a single vowel letter in a CVC word</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Minimal pairs to drill</th><th>Sounds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>cat / cut</td><td>/æ/ – /ʌ/</td></tr><tr><td>pen / pin</td><td>/e/ – /ɪ/</td></tr><tr><td>hat / hot</td><td>/æ/ – /ɒ/</td></tr><tr><td>cup / cop</td><td>/ʌ/ – /ɒ/</td></tr><tr><td>bag / big</td><td>/æ/ – /ɪ/</td></tr><tr><td>ten / tin</td><td>/e/ – /ɪ/</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Using the letter <strong>name</strong> instead of the sound: reading <em>pen</em> as /piːn/ or <em>cat</em> as /keɪt/. ⚠️ Confusing /e/ and /ɪ/ (<em>pen / pin</em>) and /æ/ and /ʌ/ (<em>cat / cut</em>): the mouth opening must change. ⚠️ Counting letters instead of sounds: saying that <em>fish</em> has four sounds. ⚠️ Adding a vowel between the two final consonants (<em>mil-ik</em> for <em>milk</em>): consonant clusters are studied in EN18.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>How many sounds? (a) cat (b) fish (c) pen (d) sock (e) bus.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Write the short vowel sound: (a) hat (b) bed (c) big (d) dog (e) cup.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Blend and write the word: (a) /b/ /ɪ/ /ɡ/ (b) /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ (c) /m/ /æ/ /p/ (d) /t/ /e/ /n/.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Segment into sounds: (a) bus (b) pin (c) top (d) fan.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Sort by vowel: cat, pen, dog, cup, six, bag, box, run, bed, fish.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Change only the vowel to make a new word: (a) cat → c…t (b) pen → p…n (c) dog → d…g (d) bag → b…g.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Which word do you hear? Choose: (a) cat / cut (b) pen / pin (c) hat / hot (d) bag / big.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Find three CVC words in this sentence: <em>A big dog sits on the mat with a red cap.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Correct the spelling of these misheard words: (a) pin (for a writing tool) (b) cot (for a drinking object) (c) bad (for a place to sleep).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Read the CVC text of the lesson and count the words with /æ/ and with /ʌ/.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write your own five-line CVC text (one line per vowel), using at least four CVC words per line.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Make a table of the five vowels with four CVC words each from your own memory, and write the IPA symbol at the top of each column.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"CVC word","v":"consonant + vowel + consonant, three sounds"},{"r":"blending","v":"joining sounds to read (/b/ /ʌ/ /s/ → bus)"},{"r":"segmenting","v":"cutting a word to spell it (bus → /b/ /ʌ/ /s/)"},{"r":"minimal pair","v":"two words differing by one sound (cat / cut)"},{"r":"short vowel","v":"the sound of a single vowel letter in a CVC word"},{"r":"cat / cut","v":"/æ/ – /ʌ/"},{"r":"pen / pin","v":"/e/ – /ɪ/"},{"r":"hat / hot","v":"/æ/ – /ɒ/"},{"r":"cup / cop","v":"/ʌ/ – /ɒ/"},{"r":"bag / big","v":"/æ/ – /ɪ/"},{"r":"ten / tin","v":"/e/ – /ɪ/"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Write the short vowel sound of each word: cat · pen · fish · box · cup · bag.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Blend and write the words: (a) /d/ /ɒ/ /ɡ/ (b) /r/ /ʌ/ /n/ (c) /s/ /ɪ/ /t/ (d) /h/ /æ/ /t/ (e) /b/ /e/ /d/ (f) /m/ /ɒ/ /p/.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write eight CVC words of your own, at least one per vowel, and segment two of them into sounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): /æ/, /e/, /ɪ/, /ɒ/, /ʌ/, /æ/.</li><li>Task 2 (1 each): dog, run, sit, hat, bed, mop.</li><li>Task 3 (8): eight real CVC words 5 (0.625 each, rounded to the nearest half) · five vowels represented 1 · two correct segmentations 2.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en14","domaine":"anglais","unite":14,"theme":"Unit 3 — I can ride a bike!","tag":"EN14","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 3 - Reading (information on a web page)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Identify the parts of a web page (title, menu, sections, contact information).","Scan a web page for a precise piece of information (day, time, price, activity).","Answer true/false, matching and wh-questions on the text.","Use <em>can / can't</em> to talk about what is possible at the club.","Write two questions he/she would send to the club by e-mail."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> The teacher draws the skeleton of a web page on the board: a <strong>title bar</strong> at the top, a <strong>menu</strong> (Home · Activities · Timetable · Contact), a <strong>body</strong> with short paragraphs and lists, and a <strong>footer</strong> with the address and telephone number. Pupils learn that a web page is <strong>not</strong> read from the first word to the last: we look for the section we need and we read only that part. Four words are pre-taught: <em>free, member, timetable, price</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading text (original web page written for this database).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>AGADIR YOUNG SPORTS CLUB — www.example.ma/aysc</strong> <strong>Home | Activities | Timetable | Contact</strong> <strong>Welcome!</strong> We are a small club for pupils from 11 to 15. Our club is near the beach, in Talborjt, Agadir. You can come with your family and visit us on Saturday morning. <strong>Activities.</strong> You can swim in our pool, play volleyball on the sand, play football on the small pitch and ride a bike with the club group. You can also learn first aid. You can't ride a horse here, and you can't play tennis: we haven't got a tennis court. <strong>Timetable.</strong> Swimming: Monday and Wednesday, from 5 p.m. to 6 p.m. Volleyball: Tuesday, from 5 p.m. to 6.30 p.m. Football: Friday, from 4 p.m. to 6 p.m. Bike group: Sunday morning, from 9 a.m. to 11 a.m. <strong>Price.</strong> 30 dirhams a month for one activity, 50 dirhams for two activities. Swimming is free in September. Members can borrow a ball or a bike. <strong>Contact.</strong> 12, Rue des Oranges, Talborjt, Agadir. Telephone: 05 28 00 00 00. Ask for Mr Bouzid. (172 words.)</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>While reading — three tasks, three skills.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Page map (3 min).</strong> Pupils copy the five section titles in order and say what kind of information each one contains. This is the specific skill of reading a web page: <strong>using the structure before reading the words</strong>.</li><li><strong>Scanning race (7 min).</strong> The teacher asks six questions and pupils must find the answer in under twenty seconds each: <em>When is football? · How much is one activity? · Where is the club? · Who do you ask for? · What is free in September? · What can't you do at the club?</em></li><li><strong>Detailed reading (8 min).</strong> Pupils underline every activity and, next to it, the day and the time, and build a small timetable table.</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method — scanning a web page in four steps.</strong> (1) Read the <strong>question</strong> first. (2) Decide <strong>which section</strong> contains the answer (<em>price → Price</em>, <em>day → Timetable</em>). (3) Move your eyes down that section only, looking for a <strong>number, a name or a capital letter</strong>. (4) Copy the information and write the answer as a short sentence.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Question: <em>How much does one activity cost?</em> Section = <em>Price</em>. Key = a number followed by <em>dirhams</em>. The text says \"30 dirhams a month for one activity\". Answer: <strong>\"It costs 30 dirhams a month.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Question: <em>Can you play tennis at the club?</em> Section = <em>Activities</em>. The text says \"you can't play tennis: we haven't got a tennis court\". Answer: <strong>\"No, you can't. There isn't a tennis court.\"</strong> Note how the answer re-uses <em>can't</em> from EN12.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — a trap.</strong> Question: <em>Is swimming free?</em> A hasty reader answers \"Yes\". The text says \"Swimming is free <strong>in September</strong>\". The complete answer is: <strong>\"It is free in September only; after that it costs 30 dirhams a month.\"</strong> Reading the whole sentence, not the first three words, is the lesson here.</p>\n<p><strong>After reading — speaking and writing.</strong> In pairs, pupil A is a pupil who wants to join the club and pupil B is Mr Bouzid. A asks five questions (activities, day, time, price, address); B answers using the page. Then each pupil writes two questions that are <strong>not</strong> answered by the page (<em>Can I come with my sister? Can girls play football? Is there a bus to the club?</em>) — the beginning of the writing skill \"asking for information\", which returns in Unit 9.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Web-page word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a menu</td><td>/ˈmenjuː/</td><td>قائِمة</td></tr><tr><td>home (page)</td><td>/həʊm/</td><td>الصَّفْحة الرَّئيسية</td></tr><tr><td>a timetable</td><td>/ˈtaɪmteɪbl/</td><td>جَدْوَل الحِصَص</td></tr><tr><td>a price</td><td>/praɪs/</td><td>ثَمَن</td></tr><tr><td>free</td><td>/friː/</td><td>مَجّاني</td></tr><tr><td>a member</td><td>/ˈmembə/</td><td>عُضْو</td></tr><tr><td>to borrow</td><td>/ˈbɒrəʊ/</td><td>يَسْتَعير</td></tr><tr><td>a pitch / a court / a pool</td><td>/pɪtʃ/ /kɔːt/ /puːl/</td><td>مَلْعَب / مَلْعَب مُضَرَّب / مَسْبَح</td></tr><tr><td>first aid</td><td>/ˌfɜːst ˈeɪd/</td><td>الإِسْعافات الأَوَّلية</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Reading a web page</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>title bar</td><td>the name of the site</td></tr><tr><td>menu</td><td>the list of pages</td></tr><tr><td>section</td><td>one block with its own heading</td></tr><tr><td>footer / contact</td><td>address, telephone</td></tr><tr><td>scanning</td><td>reading only the section you need</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Time expressions</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>from 5 p.m. to 6 p.m.</td><td>duration</td></tr><tr><td>on Monday / on Sunday morning</td><td>day</td></tr><tr><td>30 dirhams a month</td><td>price per period</td></tr><tr><td>in September</td><td>month</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Reading the whole page word by word instead of going to the right section: the answer takes four times longer to find. ⚠️ Stopping in the middle of a sentence: <em>\"Swimming is free\"</em> is not what the page says. ⚠️ Confusing <em>a.m.</em> (morning) and <em>p.m.</em> (afternoon and evening). ⚠️ Answering with a number alone (<em>30</em>) instead of a short sentence with the unit (<em>It costs 30 dirhams a month.</em>).</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Write the five section titles of the page in order.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>True or false + justification: (a) The club is in Marrakech. (b) You can swim on Monday. (c) You can play tennis. (d) Swimming is free in September.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Answer: (a) When is football? (b) How much are two activities? (c) Where is the club? (d) Who do you ask for?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Complete the timetable: swimming … · volleyball … · football … · bike group … .</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Find in the page a word for: مَجّاني · عُضْو · جَدْوَل الحِصَص.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Complete with <em>can</em> or <em>can't</em> according to the page: (a) You … swim in the pool. (b) You … ride a horse. (c) Members … borrow a ball. (d) You … play tennis.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Which section do you read to find: (a) the telephone number (b) the price (c) the days (d) the list of sports?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Write the question for each answer: (a) 30 dirhams a month. (b) In Talborjt, Agadir. (c) On Sunday morning. (d) Mr Bouzid.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Correct: (a) The club is for pupils from 11 to 15 years. (b) Football is in Friday. (c) You can to swim on Monday. (d) The bike group is at 9 p.m.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>How much do you pay for a year with two activities? Show your calculation.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write five sentences presenting a sports club in your own town, with the name, the place, two activities, one day and the price in dirhams.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a short e-mail (4–5 sentences) to Mr Bouzid: introduce yourself, say what you can do, ask two questions that the page does not answer.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (8 marks).</strong> Answer in complete sentences: (a) Where is the Agadir Young Sports Club? (b) When is volleyball? (c) How much are two activities a month? (d) What can members borrow?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> True or false + justification: (a) The club is for pupils from 11 to 15. (b) You can ride a horse at the club. (c) The bike group is on Sunday afternoon.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (6 marks).</strong> Write four sentences about a club in your town using <em>can</em> twice and <em>can't</em> once, plus a price in dirhams.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (2 each): (a) It is in Talborjt, Agadir (12, Rue des Oranges). (b) It is on Tuesday, from 5 p.m. to 6.30 p.m. (c) They cost 50 dirhams a month. (d) They can borrow a ball or a bike.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) True: \"for pupils from 11 to 15\". (b) False: \"you can't ride a horse here\". (c) False: \"Sunday morning, from 9 a.m. to 11 a.m.\" — 1 for the answer, 1 for the justification.</li><li>Task 3 (1.5 each): correct <em>can/can't</em> 0.5 · relevant content and price 0.5 · capitals and full stops 0.5.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en15","domaine":"anglais","unite":15,"theme":"Unit 3 — I can ride a bike!","tag":"EN15","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 3 - Writing (using a / an, writing about what I can and can't do)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Apply the <em>a / an</em> rule based on the initial <strong>sound</strong>, not the letter.","Use <em>a / an</em> with adjective + noun groups (<em>an old bike, a new ball</em>).","Recognise when no article is needed (plurals, uncountables, ball games).","Write a guided paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) about abilities.","Proof-read his/her own text with a six-point checklist."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> Two pupils write on the board: <em>I can ride a bike. I can play an football.</em> The class hears immediately that the second sentence is wrong twice: <em>football</em> is a ball game (no article at all) and, even with a noun, <em>an</em> would be wrong before /f/. The little words <em>a</em> and <em>an</em> seem tiny, but they appear in almost every English sentence, and Arabic has no equivalent, which makes them a permanent source of mistakes.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher writes eight noun groups on the board and pupils sort them into three columns — <em>a</em> · <em>an</em> · no article: <em>bike · umbrella · football · orange ball · old bike · water · English book · volleyball</em>. Then they state the rule themselves by listening to the <strong>first sound</strong> of each group.</p>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule 1 — <em>a</em> before a consonant sound.** <em>a bike, a ball, a horse, a new bike, a young pupil.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule 2 — <em>an</em> before a vowel sound.** <em>an umbrella, an apple, an orange ball, an English book, an old bike, an hour</em> (the <em>h</em> is silent, so the first sound is /aʊ/).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — the sound, not the letter.</strong> <em>a university</em> (/juː/ is a consonant-like sound), <em>a European pupil</em>, <em>an hour</em>, <em>an honest man</em>. These few words must be learnt as they are.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — the article goes before the whole group.</strong> If there is an adjective, the article agrees with the <strong>adjective's</strong> first sound: <em>a bike</em> → <em>an old bike</em>; <em>an apple</em> → <em>a red apple</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 5 — no article.</strong> With <strong>plurals</strong> (<em>I can ride bikes</em>), with <strong>uncountables</strong> (<em>water, chalk, milk, bread</em>), with <strong>ball games</strong> (<em>play football</em>), with <strong>school subjects</strong> (<em>I like English</em>), and with <strong>languages</strong> (<em>I speak Arabic</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — writing my abilities paragraph.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>List</strong> four things you can do and two you cannot do, using the activities of EN11.</li><li><strong>Write</strong> each one as a sentence: <em>I can + verb + (a/an) + noun</em> or <em>I can't + verb …</em>.</li><li><strong>Add a degree adverb</strong> to two sentences: <em>very well, well, a little, fast</em>.</li><li><strong>Add a reason or a wish</strong> with <em>because</em> or <em>but</em>: <em>I can't swim, but I want to learn.</em></li><li><strong>Open and close</strong> the paragraph: <em>My name is … and I am twelve. / I want to learn to swim this year.</em></li><li><strong>Check six points</strong>: capital letters · full stops · <em>can</em> with no <em>to</em> and no <em>-s</em> · <em>a/an</em> by sound · no article with games and plurals · adjectives before nouns.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> I own a bicycle that is not new. Noun group = <em>old bike</em>; first sound of <em>old</em> = /əʊ/, a vowel sound → <strong>an old bike</strong>. Sentence: <strong>\"I can ride an old bike.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> I am able to play the ball game called football, and also to use a musical instrument. Ball game → no article: <em>I can play football.</em> Instrument → <em>the</em>: <em>I can play the guitar.</em> Full sentence: <strong>\"I can play football and I can play the guitar a little.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — model paragraph (79 words).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>What I can do.</strong> My name is Sofia and I am twelve years old. I live in Agadir, near the beach. I can swim very well because my father is a swimming teacher. I can ride an old bike in the street and I can play volleyball on the sand. I can also make a cake with my mother. I can't ride a horse and I can't play tennis, because there isn't a tennis court in my quarter. I want to learn tennis this year.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The class counts the articles (<em>a swimming teacher, an old bike, a cake, a horse, a tennis court</em>) and checks each one against the initial sound; they also note <em>play volleyball</em> and <em>play tennis</em> with <strong>no</strong> article.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (peer proof-reading).</strong> Partners exchange notebooks and hunt only for <strong>article errors</strong>: they underline every <em>a</em> and <em>an</em> and write a small tick or cross above it. This narrow focus makes peer correction feasible and fast, and the author then rewrites the sentences that carry a cross.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Article</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>consonant sound</td><td>a</td><td>a bike, a ball, a horse, a new bag</td></tr><tr><td>vowel sound</td><td>an</td><td>an apple, an umbrella, an old bike, an English book, an hour</td></tr><tr><td>letter ≠ sound</td><td>a / an by sound</td><td>a university, a European friend, an hour, an honest man</td></tr><tr><td>adjective + noun</td><td>article by the adjective</td><td>a red apple, an orange ball</td></tr><tr><td>plural</td><td>no article</td><td>I can ride bikes.</td></tr><tr><td>uncountable</td><td>no article</td><td>I can drink water.</td></tr><tr><td>ball games</td><td>no article</td><td>I can play football.</td></tr><tr><td>languages / subjects</td><td>no article</td><td>I can speak Arabic. I like English.</td></tr><tr><td>musical instruments</td><td>the</td><td>I can play the guitar.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Six-point checklist</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>capital letters (sentence, names, days, languages)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>full stop at each sentence end</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>can</em> + base verb (no <em>to</em>, no <em>-s</em>)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>a / an</em> chosen by the initial sound</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>no article with games, plurals, uncountables</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>adjective before the noun</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>an ruler / a apple</em> — always test the initial <strong>sound</strong>. ⚠️ <em>I can play a football</em> — ball games take no article. ⚠️ Forgetting the article completely (<em>I can ride bike</em>), because Arabic has no indefinite article. ⚠️ <em>a old bike</em> instead of <strong>an old bike</strong>: with an adjective, the article follows the adjective's first sound.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Complete with <em>a</em> or <em>an</em>: (a) … bike (b) … apple (c) … horse (d) … umbrella (e) … ball (f) … English book.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p><em>a</em>, <em>an</em> or nothing? (a) I can play … football. (b) I can ride … old bike. (c) I can drink … water. (d) I can speak … Arabic. (e) I can play … guitar (with <em>the</em>). (f) I can see … orange.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Add the adjective and change the article if needed: (a) a bike (old) (b) an apple (red) (c) a ball (orange).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Write two sentences with <em>can</em> and two with <em>can't</em> about yourself.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Correct: (a) an ruler (b) a umbrella (c) a old bike (d) an football.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Complete the paragraph: <em>I can ride … bike. I can play … volleyball. I can't play … tennis, because there isn't … tennis court near my house.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Add a degree adverb to each sentence: (a) I can swim … . (b) He can run … . (c) She can play the guitar … .</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Join with <em>but</em> or <em>because</em>: (a) I can't swim. I want to learn. (b) I can ride a bike. My father gave me one.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Correct the five mistakes: <em>i can play a football and i can ride bike. i can't to swim.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four sentences about the abilities of two members of your family, with one article in each sentence.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) entitled \"What I can and can't do\". Use <em>can</em> three times, <em>can't</em> twice, two degree adverbs, at least three articles and one <em>because</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a five-sentence application to the Agadir Young Sports Club saying what you can do, what you can't do yet, and which activity you want to choose.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>a</em>, <em>an</em> or nothing: (a) I can ride … bike. (b) I can play … football. (c) I can eat … apple. (d) I can drink … water. (e) I can read … English book. (f) I can see … umbrella.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Correct the mistakes: (a) I can play a volleyball. (b) She can ride a old bike. (c) He can to swim.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) about what you can and can't do. Use <em>can</em> three times, <em>can't</em> twice, two articles and one <em>because</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) a (b) — (c) an (d) — (e) an (f) an.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) I can play volleyball. (b) She can ride <strong>an</strong> old bike. (c) He can <strong>swim</strong>.</li><li>Task 3 (8): task achievement 2 · <em>can/can't</em> forms 2 · articles 2 · <em>because</em> and adverbs 1 · capitals, full stops and spelling 1.</li></ul>\n<p>## Unit 4 — Have you got a milkshake?</p>\n<p>Unit 4 takes the class to the café and the souk. Learners name food and drinks and count from 20 to 100, use <em>have got / has got</em> in the three sentence types, practise the consonant blends <em>gr, br, fr</em>, read a caption story and write with question marks and full stops. Anchor context: a <strong>souk in Fez</strong>, a snack bar in <strong>Agadir</strong>, prices in <strong>dirhams</strong>, and the family table in <strong>Ramadan</strong>.</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en16","domaine":"anglais","unite":16,"theme":"Unit 4 — Have you got a milkshake?","tag":"EN16","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 4 - Words (food - numbers 20-100)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Name at least sixteen items of food and drink in English.","Say and write the numbers from 20 to 100 (tens and compound numbers).","Distinguish countable food (<em>an apple, two eggs</em>) from uncountable food (<em>bread, milk</em>).","Ask and give a price in dirhams: \"How much is it?\" — \"It's 45 dirhams.\"","Order a snack in a role-play at a snack bar in Agadir."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> Saturday morning at the souk in Fez. Mrs Benali buys <strong>bread</strong>, <strong>eggs</strong>, <strong>milk</strong>, <strong>cheese</strong>, <strong>oranges</strong>, <strong>tomatoes</strong> and <strong>olives</strong>. The seller says: \"That's <strong>forty-five</strong> dirhams, please.\" Her son Karim wants a <strong>milkshake</strong> at the snack bar next door: it costs <strong>twelve</strong> dirhams. To do the shopping in English we need two things: the names of the food, and the numbers up to a hundred.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity — food.</strong> The teacher shows empty packets or draws items on the board in two groups. Pupils repeat and copy: <strong>countable food</strong> — <em>an apple, an orange, an egg, a banana, a tomato, a cake, a sandwich, a biscuit</em>; <strong>uncountable food</strong> — <em>bread, milk, water, cheese, rice, meat, fish, tea, juice, sugar, butter, honey</em>. Then they classify five Moroccan items: <em>harira</em> (soup, uncountable), <em>msemen</em> (countable), <em>dates</em> (countable, plural), <em>olives</em> (countable, plural), <em>mint tea</em> (uncountable).</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity — numbers.</strong> The class builds the tens on the board and finds the pattern:</p>\n<p>``<code> 20 twenty     30 thirty     40 forty      50 fifty 60 sixty      70 seventy    80 eighty     90 ninety     100 a hundred 21 twenty-one   35 thirty-five   48 forty-eight   99 ninety-nine </code>``</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the tens.</strong> From 20 to 90 the tens end in <strong>-ty</strong>: <em>twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, sixty, seventy, eighty, ninety</em>. Note the spelling traps: <strong>forty</strong> (no <em>u</em>, unlike <em>four</em>), <strong>fifty</strong> (not <em>fivety</em>), <strong>eighty</strong> (one <em>t</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — compound numbers.</strong> Between two tens we write a <strong>hyphen</strong>: <em>twenty-one, thirty-five, forty-eight, ninety-nine</em>. 100 = <em>a hundred</em> or <em>one hundred</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — thirteen or thirty?</strong> The teens are stressed on the <strong>second</strong> syllable and end in /iːn/: <em>thir-TEEN</em> /θɜːˈtiːn/. The tens are stressed on the <strong>first</strong> syllable and end in /i/: <em>THIR-ty</em> /ˈθɜːti/. Prices in a shop depend on hearing this difference correctly.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — countable and uncountable.</strong> <strong>Countable</strong> nouns can be counted and have a plural: <em>an apple → three apples</em>. <strong>Uncountable</strong> nouns cannot: we never say <em>two breads</em> or <em>a milk</em>; we say <em>some bread, some milk</em>, or we use a container: <em>a bottle of water, a glass of milk, a kilo of sugar, a loaf of bread, a cup of tea</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — asking prices.</strong> <em>How much is it? — It's 45 dirhams. · How much are the oranges? — They're 12 dirhams a kilo.</em> The currency word stays singular in figures-plus-word writing (<em>45 DH</em>), and <em>dirhams</em> takes an <em>-s</em> when written in full after a number greater than one.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — ordering food.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Greet</strong>: <em>Hello! / Good morning!</em></li><li><strong>Ask</strong> for what you want: <em>Have you got a milkshake, please?</em> or <em>I'd like a sandwich, please.</em></li><li><strong>Ask the price</strong>: <em>How much is it?</em></li><li><strong>Pay and thank</strong>: <em>Here you are. / Thank you. / You're welcome.</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Karim wants a banana milkshake and a cheese sandwich. Both are countable and singular → <em>a</em>. Order: <strong>\"Good morning! Have you got a banana milkshake, please? And a cheese sandwich.\"</strong> Price question: <strong>\"How much is it?\"</strong> Answer: <strong>\"It's twenty-two dirhams.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Mrs Benali buys two kilos of tomatoes at 8 dirhams a kilo and a kilo of olives at 30 dirhams. Total: 2 × 8 + 30 = <strong>46 dirhams</strong> = <em>forty-six dirhams</em>. Note the hyphen in the written form.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes <em>I want two breads and a milk.</em> Both nouns are uncountable. Correction: <strong>\"I want two loaves of bread and a glass of milk.\"</strong> or, more simply at A1: <strong>\"I want some bread and some milk.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Snack bar role-play.\" One pupil is the seller with a price list written in his/her notebook (sandwich 15 DH · milkshake 12 DH · juice 8 DH · cake 6 DH · water 4 DH · tea 5 DH), the other is the customer with 40 dirhams. The customer must order three items, ask the price, and check the change. Then they swap. Every pair must produce at least one number between 20 and 100 (the total).</p>\n<p><strong>Numbers game.</strong> \"Bingo 20–100\": each pupil writes nine numbers between 20 and 100 in a 3×3 grid; the teacher reads numbers at random; the first to complete a line reads his/her nine numbers aloud.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Countable food</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>an apple</td><td>/ˈæpl/</td><td>تُفّاحة</td></tr><tr><td>an orange</td><td>/ˈɒrɪndʒ/</td><td>بُرْتُقالة</td></tr><tr><td>an egg</td><td>/eɡ/</td><td>بَيْضة</td></tr><tr><td>a banana</td><td>/bəˈnɑːnə/</td><td>مَوْزة</td></tr><tr><td>a tomato</td><td>/təˈmɑːtəʊ/</td><td>طَماطِم</td></tr><tr><td>a sandwich</td><td>/ˈsænwɪdʒ/</td><td>شَطيرة</td></tr><tr><td>a cake</td><td>/keɪk/</td><td>كَعْكة</td></tr><tr><td>a biscuit</td><td>/ˈbɪskɪt/</td><td>بِسْكْويت</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Uncountable food</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th><th>Container word</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>bread</td><td>/bred/</td><td>خُبْز</td><td>a loaf of bread</td></tr><tr><td>milk</td><td>/mɪlk/</td><td>حَليب</td><td>a glass of milk</td></tr><tr><td>water</td><td>/ˈwɔːtə/</td><td>ماء</td><td>a bottle of water</td></tr><tr><td>cheese</td><td>/tʃiːz/</td><td>جُبْن</td><td>a piece of cheese</td></tr><tr><td>rice</td><td>/raɪs/</td><td>أَرُزّ</td><td>a kilo of rice</td></tr><tr><td>meat</td><td>/miːt/</td><td>لَحْم</td><td>a kilo of meat</td></tr><tr><td>tea</td><td>/tiː/</td><td>شاي</td><td>a cup of tea</td></tr><tr><td>juice</td><td>/dʒuːs/</td><td>عَصير</td><td>a glass of juice</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Numbers</th><th>Written form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>20 · 30 · 40 · 50</td><td>twenty · thirty · forty · fifty</td></tr><tr><td>60 · 70 · 80 · 90 · 100</td><td>sixty · seventy · eighty · ninety · a hundred</td></tr><tr><td>21 · 35 · 48 · 99</td><td>twenty-one · thirty-five · forty-eight · ninety-nine</td></tr><tr><td>teens vs tens</td><td>thir-TEEN /iːn/ vs THIR-ty /i/</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Shopping phrases</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Have you got …, please?</td><td>asking for an item</td></tr><tr><td>I'd like …, please.</td><td>ordering</td></tr><tr><td>How much is it? / How much are they?</td><td>asking the price</td></tr><tr><td>It's 45 dirhams.</td><td>giving the price</td></tr><tr><td>Here you are. / Thank you.</td><td>paying and thanking</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>two breads / a milk</em> — uncountable nouns take no plural and no <em>a</em>: <em>some bread, a glass of milk</em>. ⚠️ Writing <em>fourty</em> or <em>fivety</em>: the correct spellings are <strong>forty</strong> and <strong>fifty</strong>. ⚠️ Confusing <em>thirteen</em> and <em>thirty</em>, <em>fifteen</em> and <em>fifty</em>: listen to the stress and the final sound. ⚠️ Forgetting the hyphen in compound numbers: <em>twenty five</em> → <strong>twenty-five</strong>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Write the numbers in words: 20 · 35 · 40 · 48 · 60 · 99.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Write in figures: seventy-two · fifty · eighty-five · a hundred.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Countable or uncountable? apple · bread · egg · milk · banana · rice.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Complete with <em>a</em>, <em>an</em> or <em>some</em>: (a) … apple (b) … bread (c) … egg (d) … water (e) … banana (f) … cheese.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Match with the container: milk · water · tea · rice — a bottle of · a glass of · a cup of · a kilo of.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Correct: (a) I want two breads. (b) He has got a milk. (c) It's fourty dirhams. (d) twenty five dirhams.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>13 or 30? Write the word you hear from the stress pattern: (a) THIR-ty (b) fif-TEEN (c) SIX-ty (d) thir-TEEN.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Calculate and write the answer in words: (a) 2 kilos of tomatoes at 8 DH + 1 kilo of olives at 30 DH. (b) 3 milkshakes at 12 DH.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete the dialogue: Customer: \"Good morning! … you got a cheese sandwich, please?\" Seller: \"Yes, we have.\" Customer: \"How … is it?\" Seller: \"It's … dirhams.\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write the shopping list of your family for one day, with six items and one price each.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a ten-line dialogue at a snack bar in Agadir. The customer orders three items and pays with 50 dirhams; the seller gives the change.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write six sentences about the food on your table during Ramadan in Fez, using three countable and three uncountable nouns with the right words.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Write in words: 24 · 40 · 57 · 68 · 90 · 100.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>a</em>, <em>an</em>, <em>some</em> or a container word: (a) … egg (b) … milk (c) … bread (d) … apple (e) … water (f) … tea.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a dialogue of six lines at a snack bar: order two items, ask the price, pay with 50 dirhams and get the change. Write all the numbers in words.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): twenty-four · forty · fifty-seven · sixty-eight · ninety · a hundred.</li><li>Task 2 (1 each): (a) an (b) some / a glass of (c) some / a loaf of (d) an (e) some / a bottle of (f) some / a cup of.</li><li>Task 3 (8): two orders 2 · price question 1 · correct total 2 · correct change 1 · numbers written in words 1 · greetings and punctuation 1.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en17","domaine":"anglais","unite":17,"theme":"Unit 4 — Have you got a milkshake?","tag":"EN17","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 4 - Grammar (I have / haven't / he has / hasn't)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Conjugate <em>have got / has got</em> in all persons, full and short forms.","Build negatives with <em>haven't got / hasn't got</em>.","Build questions <em>Have you got …? / Has he got …?</em> and give short answers.","Use <em>have got</em> for possession and <em>there is / there are</em> for existence.","Ask a classmate about what he/she has got in the schoolbag or at home."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> At the snack bar in Agadir a customer asks: \"<strong>Have</strong> you <strong>got</strong> a milkshake?\" The seller answers: \"Yes, we <strong>have</strong>. But we <strong>haven't got</strong> any juice today.\" At the next table two pupils compare their bags: \"I'<strong>ve got</strong> two pens. My brother <strong>hasn't got</strong> a pen; he'<strong>s got</strong> a pencil.\" All these sentences use the same structure: <strong>have/has + got</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher builds the paradigm on the board with the pupils' own possessions:</p>\n<p>``<code> (+) I've got a pen.          He's got a pen. (-) I haven't got a pen.     He hasn't got a pen. (?) Have you got a pen?      Has he got a pen? Yes, I have. / No, I haven't.   Yes, he has. / No, he hasn't. </code>``</p>\n<p>Pupils notice three things: <em>have</em> becomes <strong>has</strong> in the third person singular; the negative adds <strong>not</strong> to <em>have/has</em>; the question puts <em>have/has</em> first, with **no <em>do</em>**.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — forms.</strong> <em>I have got · you have got · he/she/it <strong>has</strong> got · we have got · they have got.</em> Short forms: <em>I've got, you've got, he's got, she's got, it's got, we've got, they've got</em>. Careful: <em>he's</em> can mean <em>he is</em> <strong>or</strong> <em>he has</em> — the word <em>got</em> after it tells you which.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — negative.</strong> <em>have not got → haven't got · has not got → hasn't got</em>: <em>We haven't got any juice. She hasn't got a bike.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — question and short answers.</strong> <em>Have you got a milkshake? — Yes, I have. / No, I haven't.</em> · <em>Has he got a sandwich? — Yes, he has. / No, he hasn't.</em> The word <em>got</em> is not repeated in the short answer.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — <em>some</em> and <em>any</em>.<strong> In affirmative sentences we use </strong>some** with uncountables and plurals: <em>I've got some bread.</em> In negatives and questions we use <strong>any</strong>: <em>I haven't got any bread. Have you got any bread?</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — <em>have got</em> or <em>there is</em>?** <em>have got</em> expresses <strong>possession</strong> by a person: <em>Karim has got a bike.</em> <em>There is / There are</em> expresses <strong>existence</strong> in a place: <em>There is a bike in the schoolyard.</em> Both are needed to describe a house or a shop.</p></blockquote>\n<p>**Step-by-step method — using <em>have got</em>.**</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Choose the subject</strong> and the matching form: <em>he/she/it → has</em>, everybody else → <em>have</em>.</li><li>**Add <em>got</em>**.</li><li><strong>Add the object</strong>: <em>a/an</em> + singular, <em>some/any</em> + uncountable or plural.</li><li><strong>Transform</strong> if needed: <em>not</em> after <em>have/has</em> for the negative; <em>have/has</em> to the front for the question.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Subject = <em>my sister</em>; object = <em>a new schoolbag</em>. Third person singular → <em>has</em>. Sentence: <strong>\"My sister has got a new schoolbag.\"</strong> Short form: <strong>\"My sister's got a new schoolbag.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Negative with an uncountable noun: the shop does not sell juice today. → <strong>\"They haven't got any juice.\"</strong> Question form: <strong>\"Have they got any juice?\"</strong> Short answer: <strong>\"No, they haven't.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes <em>Do you have got a pen?</em> Two auxiliaries compete. Two correct possibilities: <strong>\"Have you got a pen?\"</strong> (British <em>have got</em>) or <strong>\"Do you have a pen?\"</strong> (the simple verb <em>have</em>, studied in EN27). Mixing them is the error.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Bag detectives.\" Pupil A writes five secret items on a slip (a red pen, a dictionary, an apple, a bottle of water, a comb). Pupil B must guess them with a maximum of ten questions: \"Have you got a dictionary?\" A answers only \"Yes, I have.\" or \"No, I haven't.\" Then they swap. Every pair produces around twenty questions and twenty short answers in eight minutes, all correctly formed because the frame is fixed.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension — the family fridge.</strong> Each pupil writes four sentences about what there is and is not in the kitchen at home: <em>We've got some bread and some olives. We haven't got any cheese today.</em> This links Unit 4 vocabulary with the new structure and prepares the writing lesson EN20.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>(+)</th><th>(−)</th><th>(?)</th><th>Short answers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I / you / we / they</td><td>I've got a pen.</td><td>I haven't got a pen.</td><td>Have you got a pen?</td><td>Yes, I have. / No, I haven't.</td></tr><tr><td>he / she / it</td><td>She's got a pen.</td><td>She hasn't got a pen.</td><td>Has she got a pen?</td><td>Yes, she has. / No, she hasn't.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Structure</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>possession (a person)</td><td>have/has got</td><td>Karim has got a bike.</td></tr><tr><td>existence (a place)</td><td>there is / there are</td><td>There is a bike in the yard.</td></tr><tr><td>affirmative quantity</td><td>some</td><td>I've got some bread.</td></tr><tr><td>negative / question quantity</td><td>any</td><td>I haven't got any bread. Have you got any bread?</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Careful</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>he's got</em></td><td>= he <strong>has</strong> got (not <em>he is</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>no <em>do</em> with <em>have got</em></td><td><em>Have you got …?</em> not <em>Do you have got …?</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>got</em> not repeated</td><td><em>Yes, I have.</em> not <em>Yes, I have got.</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>has</em> only for he/she/it</td><td><em>They have got</em>, never <em>they has got</em></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>Do you have got …?</em> — choose one auxiliary: <em>Have you got …?</em> or <em>Do you have …?</em> ⚠️ <em>He have got</em> — the third person singular is <strong>has got</strong>. ⚠️ <em>I haven't got some bread</em> — in a negative use <strong>any</strong>. ⚠️ Confusing possession and existence: <em>There is a bike</em> (in a place) vs <em>He has got a bike</em> (it is his).</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Complete with <em>have got</em> or <em>has got</em>: (a) I … a pen. (b) She … a bike. (c) They … two cats. (d) My brother … a ball. (e) We … some bread. (f) You … a dictionary.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Write the short form: (a) I have got a pen. (b) He has got a bike. (c) We have got some milk. (d) She has got a cake.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Make negative: (a) I've got a ruler. (b) He's got a bike. (c) They've got some juice.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Make questions: (a) You've got a milkshake. (b) She's got a sandwich. (c) They've got some water.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Give the two short answers: (a) Have you got a pen? (b) Has he got a bike? (c) Have they got any bread?</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p><em>some</em> or <em>any</em>? (a) I've got … cheese. (b) We haven't got … juice. (c) Have you got … milk? (d) She's got … biscuits.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Correct: (a) Do you have got a pen? (b) He have got a bike. (c) Yes, I have got. (d) They hasn't got any bread.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p><em>have got</em> or <em>there is/are</em>? (a) … a map on the wall. (b) My sister … a new bag. (c) … forty chairs in my classroom. (d) We … some olives at home.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete the dialogue: Customer: \"… you got a milkshake, please?\" Seller: \"Yes, we … . But we … got any juice.\" Customer: \"… you got some water?\" Seller: \"Yes, we … .\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four sentences about what you have got and haven't got in your schoolbag.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Play \"Bag detectives\" with your partner, then write five questions you asked and the five short answers you received.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write six sentences about the food in your kitchen, using <em>have got</em>, <em>haven't got</em>, <em>some</em>, <em>any</em> and <em>there is / there are</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>have got</em>, <em>has got</em>, <em>haven't got</em> or <em>hasn't got</em>: (a) I … a bike (+). (b) She … a dictionary (−). (c) They … some biscuits (+). (d) My brother … any money (−). (e) We … a big kitchen (+). (f) He … a ball (+).</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Write the question and the two short answers: (a) (you / a pen) (b) (he / a bike) (c) (they / any juice).</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write six sentences about your kitchen at home using <em>have got</em>, <em>haven't got</em>, <em>some</em>, <em>any</em> and <em>there is / there are</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) have got (b) hasn't got (c) have got (d) hasn't got (e) have got (f) has got.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) Have you got a pen? — Yes, I have. / No, I haven't. (b) Has he got a bike? — Yes, he has. / No, he hasn't. (c) Have they got any juice? — Yes, they have. / No, they haven't.</li><li>Task 3 (8): six sentences 3 · <em>have/has got</em> correct 2 · <em>some/any</em> correct 1.5 · <em>there is/are</em> correct 1.5.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en18","domaine":"anglais","unite":18,"theme":"Unit 4 — Have you got a milkshake?","tag":"EN18","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 4 - Phonics (consonant blends: gr, br, fr)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Define a consonant blend and distinguish it from a digraph.","Produce the initial blends /ɡr/, /br/, /fr/ without inserting a vowel.","Read and spell at least eighteen words beginning with <em>gr, br, fr</em>.","Discriminate minimal pairs (<em>grow/glow</em>, <em>brown/frown</em>, <em>free/three</em>).","Read aloud a short original text loaded with the three blends."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> The teacher writes three words on the board: <em>green · bread · friend</em>. Pupils try to read them and many produce <em>girin, bired, firend</em> — they insert a small vowel between the two consonants, because Arabic syllables rarely begin with two consonants. This lesson trains the mouth to say two consonants <strong>together</strong>, with no vowel between them: it is one of the most useful pronunciation skills for a Moroccan learner of English.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> Pupils build three word families from vocabulary they already know or can guess:</p>\n<p>``<code> gr /ɡr/    green   grey    grapes   great   grandmother  grass   group br /br/    bread   brother brown    bring   break        bridge  brush fr /fr/    friend  from    fresh    free    Friday       fruit   fridge </code>``</p>\n<p>The teacher says each word twice: once slowly with the two consonants clearly joined, once at normal speed. Pupils repeat with a hand on the mouth to check that no vowel escapes between the sounds.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — a consonant blend.</strong> A <strong>blend</strong> (or cluster) is two or three consonants pronounced one after the other, each keeping <strong>its own sound</strong>: <em>gr</em> = /ɡ/ + /r/, <em>br</em> = /b/ + /r/, <em>fr</em> = /f/ + /r/. This is different from a <strong>digraph</strong> (EN08), where two letters make <strong>one</strong> sound (<em>sh</em> = /ʃ/).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — no vowel inside the blend.</strong> <em>green</em> is /ɡriːn/ in one movement, never /ɡɪˈriːn/. The trick is to start the second sound before finishing the first: say /r/ while the tongue is still leaving /ɡ/.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the English /r/.</strong> In <em>gr, br, fr</em> the /r/ is not rolled as in Arabic ر or French. The tongue tip goes <strong>up and back</strong> without touching the roof of the mouth. Say /ɜː/ then curl the tongue: that is the English /r/.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Careful — /fr/ and /θr/.</strong> <em>free</em> /friː/ and <em>three</em> /θriː/ differ only in the first sound: teeth on the lip for /f/, tongue between the teeth for /θ/. Both blends are common (<em>from, fresh, fruit</em> vs <em>three, throw, through</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — reading a word with a blend.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Cover</strong> the whole word except the first two letters.</li><li><strong>Say the two sounds separately</strong>, then join them faster and faster: /ɡ/ … /r/ … /ɡr/.</li><li><strong>Add the vowel</strong> and finish: /ɡr/ + /iː/ + /n/ = <em>green</em>.</li><li><strong>Check</strong> that no extra vowel appeared: record yourself mentally, or put a finger under the chin — the jaw should not drop twice.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> <em>bread</em> /bred/. Blend = /br/. Vowel = /e/ (short, as in <em>bed</em>, EN13). Final = /d/. Total: three sounds after the blend is treated as one movement. Watch the spelling: <em>ea</em> here gives the short /e/, an exception worth memorising with <em>bread, breakfast, head</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> <em>fridge</em> /frɪdʒ/. Blend = /fr/. Vowel = /ɪ/ (as in <em>pin</em>). Ending = <em>dge</em> = /dʒ/. Note the useful pair <em>fridge</em> (the machine) and <em>bridge</em> (over the river): only the first sound changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil reads <em>grandmother</em> as <em>girand-mother</em>. Remedy: isolate <em>gr</em> (/ɡr/ said five times, faster each time), then add <em>-and</em> (<em>grand</em>), then the second word (<em>mother</em>), then join the whole compound. Building the word from the blend outwards, not from the whole word inwards, is the effective order.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Blend race and dictation.\" Pupil A reads six words from the <em>gr</em> list while B writes them; then B reads six <em>br</em> words and A writes; finally both read the <em>fr</em> list together. Each pupil then reads this original text aloud twice while the partner counts the blend words (there are eighteen):</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>On Friday, my grandmother goes to the green grocer's in the great grey street. She brings fresh bread, brown grapes and a big bag of fruit from the souk. My brother breaks a glass in the kitchen, so I bring a brush. The fridge is free of milk, so my friend brings a bottle from the shop across the bridge. We eat bread with grapes on the grass in front of the house. It is a great Friday!</em></p></blockquote>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Blend</th><th>IPA</th><th>Words</th><th>Arabic gloss of two of them</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>gr</td><td>/ɡr/</td><td>green, grey, grapes, great, grandmother, grass, group, grocer</td><td>green = أَخْضَر · grapes = عِنَب</td></tr><tr><td>br</td><td>/br/</td><td>bread, brother, brown, bring, break, bridge, brush, breakfast</td><td>bread = خُبْز · bridge = جِسْر</td></tr><tr><td>fr</td><td>/fr/</td><td>friend, from, fresh, free, Friday, fruit, fridge, front</td><td>friend = صَديق · fruit = فاكِهة</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>consonant blend</td><td>two or three consonants, each with its own sound</td></tr><tr><td>digraph</td><td>two letters, one sound (<em>sh, ch, th</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>cluster reduction</td><td>the error of dropping one consonant (<em>bed</em> for <em>bread</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>vowel insertion</td><td>the error of adding a vowel (<em>bired</em> for <em>bread</em>)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Minimal pairs</th><th>Contrast</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>grow / glow</td><td>/ɡr/ – /ɡl/</td></tr><tr><td>brown / frown</td><td>/br/ – /fr/</td></tr><tr><td>free / three</td><td>/fr/ – /θr/</td></tr><tr><td>bread / bed</td><td>blend vs single consonant</td></tr><tr><td>fridge / bridge</td><td>/fr/ – /br/</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Inserting a vowel: <em>bired</em> for <em>bread</em>, <em>girin</em> for <em>green</em>, <em>firend</em> for <em>friend</em>. ⚠️ Dropping one consonant: <em>bed</em> for <em>bread</em>, <em>ree</em> for <em>free</em>. ⚠️ Rolling the /r/ as in Arabic or French instead of curling the tongue back. ⚠️ Reading <em>ea</em> in <em>bread</em> as /iː/: here it is the short /e/, like in <em>breakfast</em> and <em>head</em>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Underline the blend: green · bread · friend · brother · fruit · grass.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Sort into <em>gr</em>, <em>br</em>, <em>fr</em>: grey, bridge, Friday, grapes, brush, fresh.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Write the missing blend: (a) …een (b) …ead (c) …iend (d) …apes (e) …idge (f) …esh.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Blend or digraph? gr · sh · br · th · fr · ch.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Write the Arabic gloss: bread · green · friend · fruit · brother.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Correct the misspelt words: (a) bired (b) girin (c) firend (d) fesh.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Which word do you hear? (a) bread / bed (b) free / three (c) brown / frown (d) fridge / bridge.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Complete with a word from the lesson: My … (صديق) brings … (خبز) and … (عنب) on … (الجمعة).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>How many sounds in each word? (a) green (b) bread (c) free (d) brush.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Read the lesson text aloud and count the words with <em>gr</em>, <em>br</em> and <em>fr</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write four sentences about your Friday, using at least six words with <em>gr</em>, <em>br</em> or <em>fr</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Build a three-column table with six words per blend from your own memory, and mark the short vowel of each word.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"consonant blend","v":"two or three consonants, each with its own sound"},{"r":"digraph","v":"two letters, one sound (<em>sh, ch, th</em>)"},{"r":"cluster reduction","v":"the error of dropping one consonant (<em>bed</em> for <em>bread</em>)"},{"r":"vowel insertion","v":"the error of adding a vowel (<em>bired</em> for <em>bread</em>)"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Write the missing blend: (a) …ass (b) …other (c) …idge (over a river) (d) …uit (e) …een (f) …iday.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Sort into <em>gr</em>, <em>br</em>, <em>fr</em>: grapes, bread, fresh, great, brush, from.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write four sentences about a Friday at home, each containing at least two words with <em>gr</em>, <em>br</em> or <em>fr</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) gr (b) br (c) br (d) fr (e) gr (f) Fr.</li><li>Task 2 (1 each): <em>gr</em> grapes, great · <em>br</em> bread, brush · <em>fr</em> fresh, from.</li><li>Task 3 (2 each): two blend words 1 · correct spelling of the blends 0.5 · capital letter and full stop 0.5.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en19","domaine":"anglais","unite":19,"theme":"Unit 4 — Have you got a milkshake?","tag":"EN19","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 4 - Reading (a caption story)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Explain what a caption story is and how pictures and text work together.","Put the captions of a story in the right order.","Answer comprehension questions about characters, place, objects and prices.","Identify the sequencing words <em>first, then, after that, finally</em>.","Write two new captions to continue the story."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> The teacher explains the format: a <strong>caption story</strong> is a story told in a few pictures, each with one or two sentences under it — the <strong>caption</strong>. In a comic the words are inside bubbles; in a caption story they are under the picture. Since we cannot print pictures here, each caption begins with a short <strong>description of the picture in words</strong>, and pupils must draw the six pictures themselves in their exercise books as they read: reading and drawing together is an excellent A1 comprehension check.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading text (original caption story written for this database).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A milkshake for Lina — a story in six pictures</strong> <strong>Picture 1</strong> <em>(A boy in front of a small snack bar, with a schoolbag on his back.)</em> This is Karim. He is twelve. It is four o'clock and school is over. Karim is thirsty and he has got thirty dirhams in his pocket. <strong>Picture 2</strong> <em>(The boy talking to a man behind a counter with bottles and glasses.)</em> \"Good afternoon! Have you got a banana milkshake, please?\" — \"Yes, we have. It's twelve dirhams.\" — \"Two milkshakes, please. One for me and one for my sister.\" <strong>Picture 3</strong> <em>(The man looking into a fridge, and the boy waiting.)</em> But there is a problem. The man says: \"I'm sorry. I haven't got any bananas today. I've got oranges and apples.\" <strong>Picture 4</strong> <em>(The boy thinking, with a finger on his head.)</em> Karim thinks. His sister Lina doesn't like oranges, but she likes apples. \"OK,\" says Karim. \"Two apple milkshakes, please.\" <strong>Picture 5</strong> <em>(The man giving two glasses; the boy giving money.)</em> The man makes the two milkshakes. \"That's twenty-four dirhams.\" Karim gives him thirty dirhams and the man gives him six dirhams back. <strong>Picture 6</strong> <em>(The boy and a little girl in a street, both with a glass.)</em> At home, Lina is very happy. \"Thank you, Karim! Apple is my favourite!\" Karim smiles. Now they aren't thirsty, and they've got six dirhams for tomorrow. (207 words.)</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>While reading — three tasks.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Picture drawing (6 min).</strong> Pupils divide a page into six boxes and draw a very simple sketch for each caption (stick figures are enough). This forces them to understand each caption completely.</li><li><strong>Sequencing (5 min).</strong> The teacher gives the six captions in the wrong order on the board (only the first four words of each) and pupils reorder them, using the clues: the time (<em>four o'clock</em>), the problem (<em>but</em>), the solution (<em>OK</em>), the payment (<em>that's twenty-four</em>), the end (<em>at home</em>).</li><li><strong>Detailed reading (10 min).</strong> Pupils fill in a table: <strong>character · what he/she wants · what he/she has got · what he/she says</strong>.</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method — reading a caption story.</strong> (1) Look at the <strong>picture</strong> first (or read the description) and guess the situation. (2) Read the <strong>caption</strong> and check your guess. (3) Follow the <strong>story line</strong>: who wants what, what the problem is, how it is solved. (4) Note the <strong>sequencing words</strong>: <em>first, then, after that, finally</em> — they show the order even when the pictures are mixed.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Question: <em>How much money has Karim got at the beginning?</em> Picture 1, key word <em>dirhams</em>: \"he has got thirty dirhams in his pocket\". Answer: <strong>\"He has got thirty dirhams.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Question: <em>Why doesn't Karim buy banana milkshakes?</em> The cause is in picture 3: \"I haven't got any bananas today.\" Answer: <strong>\"Because the man hasn't got any bananas.\"</strong> The <em>why</em> question is answered with <em>because</em> and the negative <em>hasn't got any</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — arithmetic in the story.</strong> How much change does Karim get? Two milkshakes at 12 DH = 24 DH; he pays 30 DH; 30 − 24 = <strong>6 DH</strong>. The text confirms: \"the man gives him six dirhams back\". Stories with prices let learners check their comprehension with a calculation, which is a very reliable A1 assessment technique.</p>\n<p><strong>After reading — speaking and writing.</strong> In pairs, pupils act out pictures 2 to 5 (three turns each), using the exact sentences of the story, then changing the fruit and the prices. Then each pupil writes <strong>two new captions</strong> (pictures 7 and 8): what happens the next day with the six dirhams. The best six continuations are read aloud.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Story word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a caption</td><td>/ˈkæpʃn/</td><td>تَعْليق</td></tr><tr><td>a counter</td><td>/ˈkaʊntə/</td><td>مِنْضَدة</td></tr><tr><td>a pocket</td><td>/ˈpɒkɪt/</td><td>جَيْب</td></tr><tr><td>a problem</td><td>/ˈprɒbləm/</td><td>مُشْكِل</td></tr><tr><td>change (money back)</td><td>/tʃeɪndʒ/</td><td>الباقي</td></tr><tr><td>favourite</td><td>/ˈfeɪvərɪt/</td><td>مُفَضَّل</td></tr><tr><td>to smile</td><td>/smaɪl/</td><td>يَبْتَسِم</td></tr><tr><td>school is over</td><td>/ˈəʊvə/</td><td>اِنْتَهَت الدِّراسة</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Sequencing words</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>first</td><td>the first event</td></tr><tr><td>then / after that</td><td>the following events</td></tr><tr><td>but</td><td>the problem</td></tr><tr><td>finally / at the end</td><td>the last event</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Structures re-used</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>have got / haven't got any</td><td>Karim has got thirty dirhams. / I haven't got any bananas.</td></tr><tr><td>be + feeling</td><td>He is thirsty. Lina is very happy.</td></tr><tr><td>prices</td><td>It's twelve dirhams. That's twenty-four dirhams.</td></tr><tr><td>likes / doesn't like</td><td>She doesn't like oranges, but she likes apples.</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Reading only the captions without looking at the pictures: half the meaning is in the images. ⚠️ Answering <em>why</em> questions without <em>because</em>. ⚠️ Mixing up who says what in a dialogue caption: the speaker changes at every dash (—). ⚠️ Forgetting to check the arithmetic: prices in a story are always coherent and give a free comprehension check.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Answer: (a) How old is Karim? (b) What time is it in picture 1? (c) How much money has he got? (d) Who is Lina?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>True or false + justification: (a) The man has got bananas. (b) Karim buys two milkshakes. (c) Lina likes oranges. (d) Karim gets four dirhams change.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Put in order: <em>Karim pays · Karim is thirsty · the man hasn't got any bananas · Lina is happy · Karim asks for a banana milkshake.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Find in the story a word for: تَعْليق · جَيْب · الباقي · مُشْكِل.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>How much is one milkshake? And two?</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Answer with <em>because</em>: (a) Why is Karim thirsty? (b) Why doesn't he buy banana milkshakes? (c) Why is Lina happy?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Complete with <em>have got</em>, <em>has got</em> or <em>haven't got</em>: (a) Karim … thirty dirhams. (b) The man … any bananas. (c) At the end, they … six dirhams.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Who says it? (a) \"Have you got a banana milkshake, please?\" (b) \"I'm sorry. I haven't got any bananas today.\" (c) \"Thank you, Karim!\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Do the arithmetic: (a) 2 × 12 DH = ? (b) 30 − 24 = ? (c) If a milkshake were 15 DH, how much for two, and how much change from 30 DH?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Add the sequencing words: … Karim is thirsty. … he goes to the snack bar. … the man hasn't got any bananas. … Lina is happy.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write two new captions (pictures 7 and 8) telling what Karim and Lina do the next day with their six dirhams.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write your own caption story in four captions about buying fruit at the souk in Fez, with two prices and one problem.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"first","v":"the first event"},{"r":"then / after that","v":"the following events"},{"r":"but","v":"the problem"},{"r":"finally / at the end","v":"the last event"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (8 marks).</strong> Answer in complete sentences: (a) Where is Karim in picture 2? (b) What has the man got in the fridge? (c) How much are two apple milkshakes? (d) How much change does Karim get?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> True or false + justification: (a) Karim has got twenty dirhams. (b) Lina's favourite is apple. (c) The man has got bananas today.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (6 marks).</strong> Write two captions of your own (three sentences in total) telling what Karim and Lina buy the next day.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (2 each): (a) He is at the snack bar / in front of the counter. (b) He has got oranges and apples. (c) They are twenty-four dirhams. (d) He gets six dirhams.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) False: \"he has got thirty dirhams\". (b) True: \"Apple is my favourite!\". (c) False: \"I haven't got any bananas today.\" — 1 for the answer, 1 for the justification.</li><li>Task 3 (6): two captions 2 · coherence with the story 2 · grammar, capitals and full stops 2.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en20","domaine":"anglais","unite":20,"theme":"Unit 4 — Have you got a milkshake?","tag":"EN20","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 4 - Writing (question marks and full stops, writing about my food)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Choose between a full stop and a question mark according to the sentence type.","Punctuate a short dialogue with dashes or quotation marks.","Write yes/no and wh-questions with correct word order and punctuation.","Write a guided paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) about his/her food.","Proof-read a text for punctuation with a five-point checklist."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> Three sentences are on the board without punctuation: <em>have you got a milkshake · it is twelve dirhams · how much is it</em>. The class reads them aloud and hears immediately that two of them <strong>go up</strong> at the end (questions) and one <strong>goes down</strong> (statement). Punctuation writes down what the voice does: a <strong>question mark</strong> for the rising voice, a <strong>full stop</strong> for the falling voice.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> Pupils sort ten sentences into two columns, <strong>statement (.)</strong> and <strong>question (?)</strong>, and then find the two signals of a question: either the sentence <strong>begins with a question word</strong> (<em>what, where, when, who, how, how much</em>), or the <strong>verb comes before the subject</strong> (<em>Have you got …? Are you …? Can you …?</em>).</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the full stop (.).</strong> Ends a statement, affirmative or negative: <em>I've got two pens. She hasn't got any juice.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the question mark (?).</strong> Ends every direct question: <em>Have you got a milkshake? How much is it? Where is the shop?</em> Only <strong>one</strong> mark is needed, never <em>?.</em> and never <em>??</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the two question patterns.</strong> (1) <strong>Yes/no question</strong>: verb or auxiliary first — <em>Are you hungry? Have you got bread? Can you swim?</em> (2) <strong>Wh-question</strong>: question word + verb + subject — <em>What have you got? How much is it? Where are you from?</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — writing a dialogue.</strong> Each speaker starts a <strong>new line</strong>. Use a dash (—) or quotation marks: — <em>Have you got a milkshake?</em> / — <em>Yes, we have.</em> Inside quotation marks the punctuation stays inside: <em>\"How much is it?\"</em> The comma separates the speech from the verb: <em>He says, \"Two milkshakes, please.\"</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the exclamation mark (!).</strong> Used for a strong feeling or a short exclamation: <em>Thank you! That's great!</em> At A1, use it sparingly: one per paragraph is enough.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — writing my food paragraph.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Plan</strong> four ideas: breakfast · lunch · a favourite food · a food I don't like.</li><li><strong>Write</strong> each idea as a statement with <em>have got</em>, <em>there is/are</em>, or <em>like</em>: <em>For breakfast I've got bread, olive oil and mint tea.</em></li><li><strong>Add one question</strong> to the reader at the end: <em>And you? What have you got for breakfast?</em></li><li><strong>Punctuate</strong>: full stop after every statement, question mark after the question, capital letter after every stop.</li><li><strong>Check five points</strong>: full stops · question marks · capital letters · word order in the question · <em>some/any</em>.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Turn <em>you have got bread</em> into a question and punctuate: the auxiliary moves to the front and a question mark closes the sentence: <strong>\"Have you got any bread?\"</strong> Note <em>any</em> in the question.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Punctuate this exchange: <em>how much is it it is twelve dirhams</em> → two sentences, one question and one statement: <strong>\"How much is it?\" — \"It's twelve dirhams.\"</strong> Each speaker on a new line, capital letters restored.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — model paragraph (77 words).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>My food.</strong> My name is Lina and I am twelve. For breakfast I've got bread, olive oil and a glass of mint tea. At school I've got a cheese sandwich and an apple in my bag. My favourite food is couscous on Friday, with vegetables and chicken. I haven't got a sweet tooth, so I don't eat many cakes. On Sunday my mother makes msemen with honey. And you? What have you got for breakfast?</p></blockquote>\n<p>The class counts the full stops (six) and the question marks (two), checks the capital letters after each stop, and notes the correct word order in the final question.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (punctuation hunt).</strong> Partners exchange notebooks and hunt for punctuation only: they add a small caret (^) where a full stop or a question mark is missing and circle any capital letter that is missing after a stop. The author then rewrites the sentences concerned. Narrow, mechanical peer correction of this kind is fast and reliable even in a very large class.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Name</th><th>Use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>.</td><td>full stop</td><td>end of a statement</td><td>I've got a pen.</td></tr><tr><td>?</td><td>question mark</td><td>end of a question</td><td>Have you got a pen?</td></tr><tr><td>!</td><td>exclamation mark</td><td>strong feeling</td><td>Thank you!</td></tr><tr><td>,</td><td>comma</td><td>list, or before speech</td><td>bread, cheese and olives</td></tr><tr><td>\" \" or —</td><td>quotation marks / dash</td><td>direct speech</td><td>\"How much is it?\"</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Question type</th><th>Word order</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>yes/no with <em>be</em></td><td>be + subject</td><td>Are you hungry?</td></tr><tr><td>yes/no with <em>have got</em></td><td>have/has + subject + got</td><td>Has she got a bike?</td></tr><tr><td>yes/no with <em>can</em></td><td>can + subject + verb</td><td>Can you swim?</td></tr><tr><td>wh-question</td><td>wh + verb + subject</td><td>What have you got?</td></tr><tr><td>price question</td><td>How much + be + subject</td><td>How much is it?</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Five-point punctuation check</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a full stop at the end of every statement</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>a question mark at the end of every question</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>a capital letter after every full stop</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>the verb before the subject in yes/no questions</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>some</em> in affirmatives, <em>any</em> in questions and negatives</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Ending a question with a full stop (<em>Have you got bread.</em>) or a statement with a question mark. ⚠️ Writing a question with statement word order (<em>You have got bread?</em>): in written English the verb must come first. ⚠️ Forgetting the capital letter after a full stop, especially in a long paragraph. ⚠️ Using <em>??</em> or <em>?!</em>: one mark is enough at A1.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Add a full stop or a question mark: (a) I've got two pens (b) Have you got a ruler (c) How much is it (d) She hasn't got any juice.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Statement or question? (a) Are you hungry (b) It's forty dirhams (c) What have you got (d) They haven't got any bread.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Add the missing capital letters and stops: <em>i've got a sandwich it is a cheese sandwich have you got one</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Write the question: (a) Yes, I have. (b) It's twelve dirhams. (c) Yes, she can.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Correct the punctuation: (a) Have you got bread. (b) I've got a pen? (c) How much is it??</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Rewrite as correct written questions: (a) You have got a milkshake? (b) She can swim? (c) It is how much?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Punctuate this dialogue: <em>good afternoon have you got a milkshake yes we have it is twelve dirhams</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Complete with <em>some</em> or <em>any</em> and punctuate: (a) I've got … bread (b) Have you got … juice (c) We haven't got … cheese.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Write four questions to ask a seller at the souk, with correct word order and punctuation.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four statements about your breakfast, each with a full stop and a capital letter.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) entitled \"My food\". Use <em>have got</em> twice, <em>some</em> and <em>any</em>, one price in dirhams, and finish with a question to the reader.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a ten-line dialogue at the souk in Fez with correct dialogue punctuation: four questions, four answers and two prices.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Add a full stop or a question mark and the missing capital letters: (a) have you got a sandwich (b) it is fifteen dirhams (c) how much are the grapes (d) i haven't got any juice (e) she has got a bike (f) can you swim.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Rewrite as correct written questions: (a) You have got bread? (b) It is how much? (c) She can play tennis?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences (60–90 words) about your food. Use <em>have got</em> twice, <em>some</em> and <em>any</em>, one price in dirhams, and end with a question to the reader.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) Have you got a sandwich? (b) It is fifteen dirhams. (c) How much are the grapes? (d) I haven't got any juice. (e) She has got a bike. (f) Can you swim?</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) Have you got any bread? (b) How much is it? (c) Can she play tennis?</li><li>Task 3 (8): task achievement 2 · <em>have got</em> and <em>some/any</em> 2 · price 1 · final question 1 · punctuation and capitals 2.</li></ul>\n<p>## Unit 5 — We've got English!</p>\n<p>Unit 5 goes back to the classroom. Learners name the school subjects, ask <em>what</em> and <em>when</em> questions with <em>have got</em>, practise the blends <em>dr, tr, cr</em>, read a description on a web page and write with capital letters. Anchor context: the <strong>timetable of a 1AC class in Agadir</strong>, the school week from <strong>Monday to Saturday</strong>, and a school website presenting the class.</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en21","domaine":"anglais","unite":21,"theme":"Unit 5 — We've got English!","tag":"EN21","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 5 - Words (school subjects)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Name the twelve school subjects of the Moroccan 1AC timetable in English.","Say the days of the week and the times of the school day.","Read a timetable and say what subject is on at a given time.","Say which subject is his/her favourite and why, with <em>because</em>.","Ask a classmate about his/her timetable in a pair-work interview."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> On the wall of every Moroccan classroom there is a timetable — <em>emploi du temps</em>. Salma, a 1AC pupil in Agadir, wants to send her timetable to an English pen-friend. She knows the subjects in Arabic and French, but not in English. This lesson gives her the words she needs: the subjects, the days, and the times.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher writes the class's real timetable on the board and elicits the English names one by one, using cognates as a bridge from French where they exist (<em>mathematics ≈ mathématiques</em>, <em>history ≈ histoire</em>, <em>geography ≈ géographie</em>, <em>physics ≈ physique</em>):</p>\n<p>``<code> Arabic / French            English الرياضيات / maths          maths (mathematics) العربية                    Arabic الفرنسية                   French الإنجليزية                 English التاريخ والجغرافيا         history and geography التربية الإسلامية          Islamic education العلوم / الفيزياء          science / physics and chemistry علوم الحياة والأرض         life and earth sciences التربية البدنية            PE (physical education) التربية التشكيلية          art التربية الموسيقية          music المعلوميات                 computer science / ICT </code>``</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — subjects take a capital letter when they are languages or nationalities.</strong> <em>English, Arabic, French</em> always take a <strong>capital</strong> because they come from the names of peoples and countries. Other subjects take a small letter in mid-sentence: <em>maths, history, geography, science, art, music</em>. This is the point developed in EN25.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — no article with a subject.</strong> <em>I like maths.</em> not <em>I like the maths.</em> · <em>We've got English on Monday.</em> not <em>the English</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — abbreviations.</strong> <em>maths</em> (British) = <em>mathematics</em>; <em>PE</em> = <em>physical education</em>; <em>ICT</em> = <em>information and communication technology</em>. In British English <em>maths</em> keeps the <em>-s</em> and is used with a <strong>singular</strong> verb: <em>Maths is difficult.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — days and prepositions.</strong> Days take a <strong>capital letter</strong> and the preposition <strong>on</strong>: <em>on Monday, on Tuesday, on Saturday</em>. For times we use <strong>at</strong>: <em>at eight o'clock, at half past two</em>. For parts of the day: <strong>in</strong> the morning, <strong>in</strong> the afternoon, but <strong>at</strong> night.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>The school week and the times.</strong> The Moroccan school week runs from <strong>Monday</strong> to <strong>Saturday</strong>, with lessons in the morning and the afternoon and a free afternoon in the middle of the week for many classes. Useful expressions: <em>first period, break (recreation), lunch time, the last period, timetable</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — reading a timetable.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Find the day</strong> in the top row.</li><li><strong>Find the time</strong> in the left column.</li><li><strong>Read the cell</strong> where the two meet.</li><li><strong>Make the sentence</strong>: <em>On Monday at nine o'clock we've got maths.</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Timetable cell: Tuesday, 10:00 → English. Sentence: <strong>\"On Tuesday at ten o'clock we've got English.\"</strong> Note <em>on</em> + day, <em>at</em> + time, no article before the subject.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Favourite subject with a reason: <strong>\"My favourite subject is English because it is useful and the lessons are fun.\"</strong> The reason clause with <em>because</em> raises the answer from A1 minimum to A1 solid and is systematically rewarded in exams.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes <em>I have the mathematics in the Monday.</em> Three errors: article before the subject, article before the day, wrong preposition. Correct: <strong>\"I've got maths on Monday.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Timetable interview.\" Pupil A asks five questions and fills in a grid, then they swap: <em>What is your favourite subject? Why? How many hours of English have we got a week? What have we got on Monday at nine? When have we got PE? What is your first period on Saturday?</em> Each pupil then reports two facts about the partner to the class: <em>Youssef's favourite subject is science because he likes experiments.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>School subject</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>maths</td><td>/mæθs/</td><td>الرِّياضِيّات</td></tr><tr><td>English</td><td>/ˈɪŋɡlɪʃ/</td><td>الإنْجْليزية</td></tr><tr><td>Arabic</td><td>/ˈærəbɪk/</td><td>العَرَبية</td></tr><tr><td>French</td><td>/frentʃ/</td><td>الفَرَنْسية</td></tr><tr><td>history</td><td>/ˈhɪstri/</td><td>التّاريخ</td></tr><tr><td>geography</td><td>/dʒiˈɒɡrəfi/</td><td>الجُغْرافيا</td></tr><tr><td>science</td><td>/ˈsaɪəns/</td><td>العُلوم</td></tr><tr><td>Islamic education</td><td>/ɪzˈlæmɪk/</td><td>التَّرْبية الإسْلامية</td></tr><tr><td>art</td><td>/ɑːt/</td><td>التَّرْبية التَّشْكيلية</td></tr><tr><td>music</td><td>/ˈmjuːzɪk/</td><td>المُوسيقى</td></tr><tr><td>PE</td><td>/ˌpiː ˈiː/</td><td>التَّرْبية البَدَنية</td></tr><tr><td>computer science</td><td>/kəmˈpjuːtə/</td><td>المَعْلوميّات</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monday</td><td>/ˈmʌndeɪ/</td><td>الاِثْنَيْن</td></tr><tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>/ˈtjuːzdeɪ/</td><td>الثُّلاثاء</td></tr><tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>/ˈwenzdeɪ/</td><td>الأَرْبِعاء</td></tr><tr><td>Thursday</td><td>/ˈθɜːzdeɪ/</td><td>الخَميس</td></tr><tr><td>Friday</td><td>/ˈfraɪdeɪ/</td><td>الجُمْعة</td></tr><tr><td>Saturday</td><td>/ˈsætədeɪ/</td><td>السَّبْت</td></tr><tr><td>Sunday</td><td>/ˈsʌndeɪ/</td><td>الأَحَد</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>School words</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a timetable</td><td>جَدْوَل الحِصَص</td></tr><tr><td>a period / a lesson</td><td>حِصّة</td></tr><tr><td>a break</td><td>اِسْتِراحة</td></tr><tr><td>a subject</td><td>مادّة</td></tr><tr><td>homework</td><td>تَمارين مَنْزِلية</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Prepositions</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>on + day</td><td>on Monday</td></tr><tr><td>at + time</td><td>at nine o'clock</td></tr><tr><td>in + part of the day</td><td>in the morning</td></tr><tr><td>no article + subject</td><td>I like maths.</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>the maths, the English</em> — no article before a school subject. ⚠️ <em>in Monday</em> — the preposition with a day is <strong>on</strong>. ⚠️ Small letter for <em>english, arabic, french</em> — languages always take a capital. ⚠️ <em>Maths are difficult</em> — <em>maths</em> takes a singular verb: <em>Maths <strong>is</strong> difficult.</em></p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Write in English: الرياضيات · التاريخ · العلوم · الموسيقى · التربية البدنية · المعلوميات.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Write the seven days in order, with capital letters.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Capital letter or not? (a) english (b) maths (c) arabic (d) history (e) french (f) music.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Complete with <em>on</em>, <em>at</em> or <em>in</em>: (a) … Monday (b) … nine o'clock (c) … the morning (d) … Saturday (e) … half past two (f) … the afternoon.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Correct: (a) I like the maths. (b) We've got English in Tuesday. (c) Maths are easy.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Look at your own timetable and write four sentences: <em>On … at … we've got …</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Answer: (a) What is your favourite subject? (b) Why? (c) How many hours of English have you got a week?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Match the subject and the activity: maths · PE · art · science — we draw · we run · we count · we do experiments.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete: (a) In … we learn about kings and dates. (b) In … we learn about maps and countries. (c) In … we use computers.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four sentences with <em>because</em>: <em>I like … because …</em></p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Interview your partner with five questions about his/her timetable, then write four sentences reporting the answers.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write your Monday timetable in English (five periods with times) and add two sentences about your favourite and least favourite subject with reasons.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"a timetable","v":"جَدْوَل الحِصَص"},{"r":"a period / a lesson","v":"حِصّة"},{"r":"a break","v":"اِسْتِراحة"},{"r":"a subject","v":"مادّة"},{"r":"homework","v":"تَمارين مَنْزِلية"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Write in English: العلوم · الجغرافيا · التربية البدنية · الموسيقى · الرياضيات · العربية.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>on</em>, <em>at</em> or <em>in</em>, and correct the capital letters: (a) We've got english … monday. (b) The lesson is … ten o'clock. (c) We've got PE … the afternoon.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write six sentences about your timetable: four with <em>On … at … we've got …</em>, and two about your favourite and least favourite subject with <em>because</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): science · geography · PE · music · maths · Arabic.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) We've got <strong>English on Monday</strong>. (b) … <strong>at</strong> ten o'clock. (c) … <strong>in</strong> the afternoon.</li><li>Task 3 (8): four timetable sentences 4 · prepositions and capitals 2 · two sentences with <em>because</em> 2.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en22","domaine":"anglais","unite":22,"theme":"Unit 5 — We've got English!","tag":"EN22","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 5 - Grammar (what / when have we got ...?)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Build wh-questions with <em>what</em> and <em>when</em> + <em>have/has got</em>.","Choose the right question word among <em>what, when, where, who, how many, what time</em>.","Answer wh-questions with a full sentence, not a short <em>yes/no</em> answer.","Use <em>on + day</em> and <em>at + time</em> in the answers.","Conduct a five-question timetable interview and report the answers."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> Salma writes to her pen-friend in Manchester: \"<strong>What</strong> have you got on Monday morning?\" He answers: \"We've got maths and history.\" Then he asks: \"And <strong>when</strong> have you got English?\" Salma answers: \"We've got English on Tuesday and Thursday, at ten o'clock.\" Two question words, two kinds of information: <strong>what</strong> asks for a <strong>thing</strong>, <strong>when</strong> asks for a <strong>time</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher puts a table on the board and pupils complete the missing questions from the answers:</p>\n<p>``<code> ?  ______________________  →  We've got English on Tuesday. ?  ______________________  →  We've got maths and art. ?  ______________________  →  We've got four hours of English a week. ?  ______________________  →  Our English teacher is Mr Alami. </code>``</p>\n<p>Working backwards from the answer is the fastest way to make pupils feel which question word is needed.</p>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — the wh-question with <em>have got</em>.<strong> Word order: </strong>question word + have/has + subject + got + …?** <em>What have we got on Monday? · When have you got English? · What has she got at nine o'clock?</em> The auxiliary comes <strong>before</strong> the subject and <em>got</em> comes <strong>after</strong> it. There is no <em>do</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the main question words.</strong> <strong>What</strong> = a thing (<em>What have we got now?</em>) · <strong>When</strong> = a time or a day (<em>When have we got PE?</em>) · <strong>What time</strong> = a precise hour (<em>What time have we got English?</em>) · <strong>Where</strong> = a place (<em>Where is the science room?</em>) · <strong>Who</strong> = a person (<em>Who is your maths teacher?</em>) · <strong>How many</strong> = a number (<em>How many hours of English have we got?</em>) · <strong>Why</strong> = a reason, answered with <em>because</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — answering a wh-question.</strong> A wh-question cannot be answered by <em>yes</em> or <em>no</em>. It needs the information asked for, ideally in a full sentence: <em>When have we got art? — <strong>We've got art on Wednesday.</strong></em> Compare with the yes/no question of EN17: <em>Have we got art on Wednesday? — Yes, we have.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — <em>how many</em> + plural.** <em>How many</em> is followed by a <strong>plural</strong> noun: <em>How many hours have we got? How many pupils are there in your class? — There are forty-two.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — building the question.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Decide what you do not know</strong>: a thing → <em>what</em>; a day or time → <em>when</em> / <em>what time</em>; a number → <em>how many</em>; a person → <em>who</em>; a place → <em>where</em>.</li><li><strong>Put the question word first.</strong></li><li>**Add <em>have</em> or <em>has</em>** (<em>has</em> for he/she/it).</li><li>**Add the subject, then <em>got</em>.**</li><li><strong>Add the rest</strong> (day, time) and a <strong>question mark</strong>.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> I want to know the subject at 11 o'clock on Thursday. Unknown = a thing → <em>what</em>. Question: <strong>\"What have we got at eleven o'clock on Thursday?\"</strong> Answer: <strong>\"We've got geography.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> I want to know the day of the music lesson. Unknown = a day → <em>when</em>. Question: <strong>\"When have we got music?\"</strong> Answer: <strong>\"We've got music on Saturday morning.\"</strong> Note <em>on</em> before the day.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil writes <em>When we have got English?</em> The auxiliary has not moved. Correct: <strong>\"When have we got English?\"</strong> Another writes <em>What do you have got on Monday?</em>: two auxiliaries. Correct: <strong>\"What have you got on Monday?\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Two timetables.\" Pupil A has the timetable of class 1AC/1 and pupil B has the timetable of class 1AC/2 (each writes a five-line version invented or copied from a neighbouring class). They must find <strong>three differences</strong> by asking wh-questions only: <em>What have you got on Monday at eight? When have you got PE? How many hours of maths have you got?</em> The information gap makes the questions genuinely necessary, which is the core principle of communicative practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension.</strong> Each pair writes the three differences as full sentences: <em>We've got English on Tuesday but they've got English on Wednesday.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Question word</th><th>Asks for</th><th>Example</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>what</td><td>a thing</td><td>What have we got now?</td><td>We've got maths.</td></tr><tr><td>when</td><td>a day / a time</td><td>When have we got PE?</td><td>On Thursday.</td></tr><tr><td>what time</td><td>a precise hour</td><td>What time have we got English?</td><td>At ten o'clock.</td></tr><tr><td>where</td><td>a place</td><td>Where is the art room?</td><td>Next to the library.</td></tr><tr><td>who</td><td>a person</td><td>Who is your English teacher?</td><td>Mr Alami.</td></tr><tr><td>how many</td><td>a number (+ plural)</td><td>How many hours have we got?</td><td>Four hours.</td></tr><tr><td>why</td><td>a reason</td><td>Why do you like English?</td><td>Because it is useful.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Word order</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>wh + have/has + subject + got + …?</td><td>What have we got on Monday?</td></tr><tr><td>no <em>do</em></td><td>not <em>What do you have got …?</em></td></tr><tr><td>auxiliary before the subject</td><td>not <em>When we have got …?</em></td></tr><tr><td>question mark at the end</td><td>What have we got?</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Answers</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>full sentence preferred</td><td>We've got English on Tuesday.</td></tr><tr><td><em>on</em> + day</td><td>on Monday, on Saturday</td></tr><tr><td><em>at</em> + time</td><td>at nine o'clock, at half past ten</td></tr><tr><td>never <em>yes/no</em> to a wh-question</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>When we have got English?</em> — the auxiliary must come before the subject: <em>When <strong>have we</strong> got English?</em> ⚠️ <em>What do you have got …?</em> — one auxiliary only: <em>What have you got …?</em> ⚠️ Answering a wh-question with <em>yes</em> or <em>no</em>. ⚠️ <em>How many hour</em> — <em>how many</em> takes a plural noun: <em>how many hours</em>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p><em>What</em> or <em>when</em>? (a) … have we got on Monday? (b) … have we got English? (c) … has she got at nine o'clock? (d) … have you got PE?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Put in order: (a) got / what / we / have / on Tuesday / ? (b) English / when / you / have / got / ? (c) got / has / what / he / at ten / ?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Choose the question word: (a) … is your maths teacher? (b) … hours of English have you got? (c) … is the science room? (d) … do you like English?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Answer in a full sentence about your own timetable: (a) What have you got on Monday at eight? (b) When have you got art?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Correct: (a) When we have got music? (b) What do you have got on Friday? (c) How many hour have you got?</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Write the question for each answer: (a) We've got maths. (b) On Thursday. (c) At eleven o'clock. (d) Four hours a week.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Complete with <em>on</em> or <em>at</em>: (a) We've got PE … Saturday. (b) The lesson starts … eight o'clock. (c) We've got English … Tuesday … ten.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Yes/no question or wh-question? Then answer: (a) Have you got English on Monday? (b) When have you got English?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Write four questions to ask a pupil from another class about his/her timetable.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write four questions and four answers about your own timetable.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Play \"Two timetables\" with your partner and write the three differences you found as full sentences.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a ten-line email to a pen-friend in Manchester: ask four questions about his/her timetable and describe two days of yours.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"wh + have/has + subject + got + …?","v":"What have we got on Monday?"},{"r":"no <em>do</em>","v":"not <em>What do you have got …?</em>"},{"r":"auxiliary before the subject","v":"not <em>When we have got …?</em>"},{"r":"question mark at the end","v":"What have we got?"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Complete with <em>what</em>, <em>when</em>, <em>what time</em>, <em>who</em>, <em>how many</em> or <em>where</em>: (a) … have we got on Monday? (b) … have we got English? (c) … is your art teacher? (d) … hours of maths have you got? (e) … is the music room? (f) … does the lesson start?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Put in the right order and punctuate: (a) got / we / have / what / on Saturday (b) English / when / we / got / have (c) at nine / what / she / got / has</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write four questions and four full answers about your timetable, using <em>what</em>, <em>when</em>, <em>what time</em> and <em>how many</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) What (b) When (c) Who (d) How many (e) Where (f) What time.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) What have we got on Saturday? (b) When have we got English? (c) What has she got at nine?</li><li>Task 3 (8): four correct question forms 4 · four full answers 2 · prepositions <em>on/at</em> 1 · question marks 1.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en23","domaine":"anglais","unite":23,"theme":"Unit 5 — We've got English!","tag":"EN23","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 5 - Phonics (consonant blends: dr, tr, cr)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Produce the initial blends /dr/, /tr/, /kr/ with no inserted vowel.","Read and spell at least eighteen words beginning with <em>dr, tr, cr</em>.","Discriminate the minimal pairs <em>drive/dive</em>, <em>train/rain</em>, <em>cry/try</em>.","Explain why <em>tr</em> and <em>dr</em> often sound like /tʃr/ and /dʒr/ in natural speech.","Read aloud an original text loaded with the three blends."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> Three words on the board: <em>draw · train · crayon</em>. Pupils have met all three objects in class (they draw, they take the train to Marrakesh, they use crayons in art). Reading them is another matter: the temptation is to say <em>diraw, tirain, kirayon</em>. This lesson completes the blend work of EN18 with the second family of <em>r</em>-blends.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> The three word families are built on the board:</p>\n<p>``<code> dr /dr/    draw   dress   drink   drive   dream   drum    dry     driver tr /tr/    train  tree    try     travel  truck   trousers true   translate cr /kr/    crayon cross   cry     cream   crowd   crocodile  create  crisps </code>``</p>\n<p>Pupils repeat each word three times: slowly, at normal speed, then in a short phrase (<em>I draw a tree. I drink cream. I cross the street.</em>).</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — the blend is one movement.</strong> /dr/, /tr/, /kr/ = two sounds pronounced in a single jaw movement. Never insert a vowel: <em>train</em> is /treɪn/, never /tɪˈreɪn/.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Note — natural pronunciation of <em>tr</em> and <em>dr</em>.** In natural English speech <em>tr</em> often sounds close to /tʃr/ (<em>train</em> ≈ \"chrain\") and <em>dr</em> close to /dʒr/ (<em>dream</em> ≈ \"jream\"). This is normal and helps learners avoid inserting a vowel; imitating it makes the blend easier, not harder.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule — /kr/ is spelt <em>cr</em>, not <em>kr</em>.** In English the /k/ before <em>r</em> is written with a <strong>c</strong>: <em>crayon, cross, cry, cream</em>. The letter <em>k</em> before <em>r</em> is very rare.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Careful — minimal pairs.</strong> <em>drive</em> /draɪv/ vs <em>dive</em> /daɪv/ · <em>train</em> /treɪn/ vs <em>rain</em> /reɪn/ · <em>cry</em> /kraɪ/ vs <em>try</em> /traɪ/ · <em>cross</em> /krɒs/ vs <em>class</em> /klɑːs/. Dropping the first or second consonant changes the word completely, so the blend carries meaning.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Isolate the blend</strong> and repeat it five times, faster each time: /dr/ /dr/ /dr/.</li><li><strong>Add the vowel</strong>: /dr/ + /ɪ/ + /ŋk/ = <em>drink</em>.</li><li><strong>Say the word in a phrase</strong> so the blend survives at natural speed: <em>I drink tea.</em></li><li><strong>Check</strong> that the jaw drops only once and that no vowel appeared inside the blend.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> <em>trousers</em> /ˈtraʊzəz/. Blend /tr/ + the diphthong /aʊ/ (as in <em>how</em>) + /zəz/. It is a plural-only noun: <em>a pair of trousers</em>, never <em>a trouser</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> <em>crayon</em> /ˈkreɪɒn/. Blend /kr/ spelt <em>cr</em>. Useful in art lessons and easy to link with French <em>crayon</em>, though the English meaning is a wax colouring stick, not a pencil (<em>a pencil</em> = قَلَم رَصاص).</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3.</strong> A pupil reads <em>drink</em> as <em>dirink</em>. Remedy: start from the second sound. Say /rɪŋk/ first (<em>rink</em>), then add /d/ in front without pausing: /d/+/rɪŋk/ = <em>drink</em>. Building backwards from the second consonant is often more effective than building forwards.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (communicative task).</strong> \"Blend dictation and tongue twister.\" Pupil A dictates six <em>dr</em> words, pupil B six <em>tr</em> words, then both write the six <em>cr</em> words together. Then each pupil reads this original text twice while the partner counts the blend words (there are twenty):</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>On Tuesday we travel by train to Marrakesh. My little brother draws a crocodile with a red crayon, and my sister tries to draw a truck. The driver drinks cream and coffee, then he drives across the dry desert road. Do not cry, says my mother: try again! We cross a green tree-lined street, we drink cold water, and we dream of the drums in the big crowd on the square.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p>Finally the class says the tongue twister three times, faster each time: <em>Try to drive the truck across the dry crowd of trees.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Blend</th><th>IPA</th><th>Words</th><th>Arabic gloss of two of them</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>dr</td><td>/dr/</td><td>draw, dress, drink, drive, dream, drum, dry, driver</td><td>to draw = يَرْسُم · to drink = يَشْرَب</td></tr><tr><td>tr</td><td>/tr/</td><td>train, tree, try, travel, truck, trousers, true, translate</td><td>train = قِطار · tree = شَجَرة</td></tr><tr><td>cr</td><td>/kr/</td><td>crayon, cross, cry, cream, crowd, crocodile, create, crisps</td><td>to cry = يَبْكي · crowd = حَشْد</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Minimal pairs</th><th>Contrast</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>drive / dive</td><td>/dr/ – /d/</td></tr><tr><td>train / rain</td><td>/tr/ – /r/</td></tr><tr><td>cry / try</td><td>/kr/ – /tr/</td></tr><tr><td>cross / class</td><td>/kr/ – /kl/</td></tr><tr><td>dream / cream</td><td>/dr/ – /kr/</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Spelling and usage notes</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>/kr/ is written <em>cr</em></td><td>crayon, cross, cream</td></tr><tr><td><em>trousers</em></td><td>plural only: a pair of trousers</td></tr><tr><td><em>tr</em> ≈ /tʃr/, <em>dr</em> ≈ /dʒr/</td><td>natural speech, helps avoid vowel insertion</td></tr><tr><td>blend ≠ digraph</td><td><em>dr</em> = two sounds, <em>sh</em> = one sound</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Vowel insertion: <em>diraw, tirain, kirayon</em> for <em>draw, train, crayon</em>. ⚠️ Dropping a consonant: <em>rain</em> for <em>train</em>, <em>dive</em> for <em>drive</em>. ⚠️ Writing <em>krayon</em> or <em>kross</em>: the /k/ before <em>r</em> is spelt <strong>c</strong>. ⚠️ Saying <em>a trouser</em>: the word is plural, <em>trousers</em> or <em>a pair of trousers</em>.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Underline the blend: draw · train · crayon · drink · tree · cross.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Sort into <em>dr</em>, <em>tr</em>, <em>cr</em>: dress, truck, cream, drive, try, cry.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Write the missing blend: (a) …aw (b) …ain (c) …ayon (d) …ink (e) …ee (f) …oss.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Write the Arabic gloss: to draw · train · to drink · tree · to cry.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Blend or digraph? dr · ch · tr · th · cr · sh.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Correct: (a) diraw (b) tirain (c) krayon (d) a trouser.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Which word? (a) train / rain (b) drive / dive (c) cry / try (d) cream / dream.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>How many sounds? (a) draw (b) tree (c) cry (d) drink.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Complete with a blend word: I … (أرسم) a … (شجرة) with a red … (قلم شمعي) on … (الثلاثاء).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Read the lesson text aloud and count the <em>dr</em>, <em>tr</em> and <em>cr</em> words.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write four sentences about a journey by train, using at least six words with <em>dr</em>, <em>tr</em> or <em>cr</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Make a six-column table with the six blends of EN18 and EN23 (<em>gr, br, fr, dr, tr, cr</em>) and write three words in each column.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Write the missing blend: (a) …ess (b) …uck (c) …eam (d) …ive (e) …ee (f) …y (to weep).</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Sort into <em>dr</em>, <em>tr</em>, <em>cr</em>: drum, travel, crowd, dream, trousers, cross.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write four sentences about a journey by train to Marrakesh, each with at least two words containing <em>dr</em>, <em>tr</em> or <em>cr</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): (a) dr (b) tr (c) cr or dr (<em>cream</em>, <em>dream</em>) (d) dr (e) tr (f) cr.</li><li>Task 2 (1 each): <em>dr</em> drum, dream · <em>tr</em> travel, trousers · <em>cr</em> crowd, cross.</li><li>Task 3 (2 each): two blend words 1 · correct spelling of the blends 0.5 · capital letter and full stop 0.5.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en24","domaine":"anglais","unite":24,"theme":"Unit 5 — We've got English!","tag":"EN24","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 5 - Reading (a description on a web page)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["Identify the parts of a web page: title, heading, paragraph, menu, link.","Scan a school web page for specific information (numbers, names, days).","Answer wh-questions and true/false items with justification.","Recognise the difference between <em>have got</em> (possession) and <em>there is/are</em> (existence) in a real text.","Write a short web-page paragraph presenting his/her own class."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> A <strong>web page</strong> is not read like a story: it is <strong>scanned</strong>. The eye jumps to the headings, the numbers and the names, because the reader is usually looking for one piece of information. The teacher shows the parts on the board: the <strong>title</strong> at the top, the <strong>menu</strong> with links (Home · Our school · Classes · Contact), the <strong>headings</strong> in bold, the short <strong>paragraphs</strong>, and sometimes a <strong>caption</strong> under a photo. Pupils should learn one habit: read the headings first, then go to the paragraph that answers the question.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading text (original web page written for this database).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>AL AMAL MIDDLE SCHOOL — AGADIR</strong> Home · Our school · Classes · Timetable · Contact <strong>Welcome to our school!</strong> Al Amal Middle School is in Agadir, near the big market. We've got twenty-four classes and nine hundred pupils. There are three floors, a library, a computer room and a big schoolyard with two football goals. <strong>Class 1AC/3 — our English class</strong> We are forty-two pupils in class 1AC/3: twenty-two girls and twenty boys. Our English teacher is Mr Alami. We've got four hours of English a week: two hours on Tuesday, one hour on Thursday and one hour on Saturday morning. <strong>What we do in English</strong> In our English lessons we read short stories, we play word games and we sing songs. We have got a small English club on Wednesday afternoon. There are twelve pupils in the club. We write letters to a school in Manchester, in England. <strong>Our favourite subjects</strong> Our favourite subject is English, because the lessons are fun. Salma likes science and Youssef likes PE. Nobody likes homework! <strong>Contact</strong> Email the school office from Monday to Saturday, from eight o'clock to four o'clock. (176 words.)</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>While reading — three tasks.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Scanning race (4 min).</strong> The teacher asks six questions and pupils must find each answer in under fifteen seconds: <em>How many pupils are there in the school? How many in class 1AC/3? Who is the English teacher? How many hours of English a week? When is the English club? Where is the pen-friend school?</em> Speed is the skill being trained here, not accuracy of grammar.</li><li><strong>Structure identification (4 min).</strong> Pupils label the parts: title, menu, four headings, paragraphs.</li><li><strong>Detailed reading (10 min).</strong> Pupils answer the wh-questions and the true/false items, always quoting the sentence that proves the answer.</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method — scanning a web page.</strong> (1) Read the <strong>headings</strong> only. (2) Decide which heading contains the answer. (3) Read <strong>that paragraph</strong> and look for the <strong>number, name or day</strong> you need. (4) Copy the sentence that proves your answer. A justified answer is always worth more marks than a bare one.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Question: <em>How many hours of English has the class got a week?</em> The heading \"Class 1AC/3\" contains timetable information. Sentence: \"We've got four hours of English a week.\" Answer: <strong>\"They have got four hours of English a week.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> Question: <em>Are there thirty pupils in class 1AC/3?</em> Justification: \"We are forty-two pupils in class 1AC/3.\" Answer: <strong>\"False: they are forty-two pupils.\"</strong> Note the two components: the verdict and the quotation.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — grammar in context.</strong> The text mixes the two structures of the unit: <em>We've got twenty-four classes</em> (<strong>possession</strong> by the school) and <em>There are three floors</em> (<strong>existence</strong> in the place). Pupils underline three examples of each and explain the choice; seeing the contrast in an authentic-looking text fixes it far better than isolated drills.</p>\n<p><strong>After reading — speaking and writing.</strong> In pairs, pupils ask each other five wh-questions about the page (recycling EN22). Then each pupil writes a paragraph of 60–90 words for the school website presenting his/her own class: number of pupils, teacher's name, hours of English, one activity, and the class's favourite subject with <em>because</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Web-page word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a web page</td><td>/ˈweb peɪdʒ/</td><td>صَفْحة وِيب</td></tr><tr><td>a heading</td><td>/ˈhedɪŋ/</td><td>عُنْوان فَرْعي</td></tr><tr><td>a menu</td><td>/ˈmenjuː/</td><td>قائِمة</td></tr><tr><td>a link</td><td>/lɪŋk/</td><td>رابِط</td></tr><tr><td>to welcome</td><td>/ˈwelkəm/</td><td>يُرَحِّب</td></tr><tr><td>a floor</td><td>/flɔː/</td><td>طابِق</td></tr><tr><td>a library</td><td>/ˈlaɪbrəri/</td><td>مَكْتَبة</td></tr><tr><td>a club</td><td>/klʌb/</td><td>نادٍ</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Structure in the text</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>We've got twenty-four classes.</td><td>possession (the school owns)</td></tr><tr><td>There are three floors.</td><td>existence (in the place)</td></tr><tr><td>Our English teacher is Mr Alami.</td><td>identification</td></tr><tr><td>Salma likes science.</td><td>preference</td></tr><tr><td>because the lessons are fun</td><td>reason</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Numbers in the text</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>twenty-four classes</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>nine hundred pupils</td><td>900</td></tr><tr><td>forty-two pupils</td><td>42</td></tr><tr><td>four hours a week</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>twelve pupils in the club</td><td>12</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ Reading a web page from the first word to the last: read the headings first, then scan. ⚠️ Answering true/false without the quotation that proves it: half the marks are in the justification. ⚠️ Confusing <em>We've got twenty-four classes</em> (possession) and <em>There are three floors</em> (existence). ⚠️ Copying a whole paragraph instead of the one sentence that answers the question.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Answer: (a) Where is the school? (b) How many pupils are there in the school? (c) Who is the English teacher? (d) How many pupils are there in class 1AC/3?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>True or false + justification: (a) The school has got ten classes. (b) There is a library. (c) The English club is on Monday. (d) The class writes to a school in England.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Find in the page: عُنْوان فَرْعي · رابِط · طابِق · مَكْتَبة.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>How many hours of English on Tuesday? On Thursday? On Saturday?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Name the five parts of the web page.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Answer with <em>because</em>: (a) Why is English the class's favourite subject? (b) Why is the school easy to find?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p><em>have got</em> or <em>there is/are</em>? Copy three examples of each from the text.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Write the numbers of the text in words: 24 · 900 · 42 · 12.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Write four wh-questions about the page and their answers.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Complete: The class has got … hours of English a week, on …, … and … .</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a paragraph of 60–90 words for your school website presenting your own class: number of pupils, teacher, hours of English, one activity, favourite subject with <em>because</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write the \"Our school\" section of your school's web page in 80–100 words, with one heading and at least three numbers, using <em>have got</em> twice and <em>there is/are</em> twice.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (8 marks).</strong> Answer in complete sentences: (a) Where is Al Amal Middle School? (b) How many classes has it got? (c) How many hours of English has class 1AC/3 got a week? (d) When is the English club?</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (4 marks).</strong> True or false + justification: (a) There are nine hundred pupils in the school. (b) The class writes to a school in France.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a paragraph of 60–90 words for your school website presenting your class: number of pupils, teacher, hours of English, one activity, and your favourite subject with <em>because</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (2 each): (a) It is in Agadir, near the big market. (b) It has got twenty-four classes. (c) It has got four hours a week. (d) It is on Wednesday afternoon.</li><li>Task 2 (2 each): (a) True: \"nine hundred pupils\". (b) False: \"a school in Manchester, in England.\" — 1 verdict, 1 quotation.</li><li>Task 3 (8): content points 3 · <em>have got</em> / <em>there is-are</em> 2 · <em>because</em> 1 · spelling, capitals and full stops 2.</li></ul>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en25","domaine":"anglais","unite":25,"theme":"Unit 5 — We've got English!","tag":"EN25","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 5 - Writing (capital letters, writing about my school subjects)","duree":"1 h","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["List the seven contexts that require a capital letter in English.","Apply the capital rules to days, months, languages, nationalities and names.","Correct a text in which all the capitals are missing.","Write a paragraph of 60–90 words about his/her school subjects, correctly capitalised.","Proof-read with a six-point checklist covering capitals and punctuation."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Entry situation.</strong> A pupil writes on the board: <em>on monday we have got english and arabic with mr alami.</em> The class counts the mistakes: five capitals are missing (<em>On, Monday, English, Arabic, Mr Alami</em>). English uses capital letters in places where Arabic has no capitals at all and where French capitalises less (French writes <em>le lundi</em>, <em>l'anglais</em>, <em>le français</em> in lower case). This is therefore a rule to learn explicitly, not by intuition.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery activity.</strong> Pupils classify twelve correctly written examples into groups and name the rule for each group: <em>I, Monday, January, English, Arabic, Morocco, Moroccan, Agadir, Mr Alami, Al Amal Middle School, Ramadan, Al Massae</em> (a newspaper title).</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — the first word of a sentence.</strong> <em>We've got maths today. On Monday we've got English.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule 2 — the pronoun <em>I</em>.** Always a capital, anywhere in the sentence: <em>My sister and I are pupils.</em> This is unique to English.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — days and months.</strong> <em>Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday · January, February, March, April, May, June, July, August, September, October, November, December.</em> French does not capitalise them; English always does.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — languages, nationalities and countries.</strong> <em>English, Arabic, French, Amazigh · Moroccan, English, Spanish · Morocco, England, Spain.</em> So the school subject <em>English</em> keeps its capital, while <em>maths</em>, <em>history</em>, <em>science</em>, <em>art</em> and <em>music</em> do not.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 5 — names of people and places.</strong> <em>Karim, Salma, Mr Alami, Mrs Rachidi · Agadir, Fez, Casablanca, Manchester · Al Amal Middle School, Hassan II Avenue.</em> Titles before a name are capitalised: <em>Mr, Mrs, Miss, Dr</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 6 — festivals, months of the religious calendar and special days.</strong> <em>Ramadan, Eid, Independence Day, Green March Day.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 7 — titles.</strong> The important words of a book, film, newspaper or web-page title: <em>What's Up</em>, <em>Al Massae</em>, <em>Our School Web Page</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Careful — what does NOT take a capital.</strong> School subjects that are not languages (<em>maths, history, geography, science, art, music</em>), seasons in most style guides (<em>spring, summer, autumn, winter</em>), and common nouns (<em>a teacher, a school, a pupil</em>) unless they are part of a name (<em>Al Amal Middle School</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step method — writing my subjects paragraph.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Plan</strong> five ideas: my class and school · my subjects · my favourite subject and why · a subject I find difficult · my English lessons.</li><li><strong>Write</strong> the five sentences with <em>have got</em>, <em>like</em>, <em>there is/are</em> and <em>because</em>.</li><li><strong>Capitalise</strong>: every sentence start, every <em>I</em>, every day, every language, every name.</li><li><strong>Punctuate</strong>: full stop after each statement, question mark if you ask the reader something.</li><li><strong>Check six points</strong>: sentence-start capitals · <em>I</em> · days · languages · names · full stops.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example 1.</strong> Correct <em>on tuesday i have got english, maths and arabic with mrs rachidi.</em> → <strong>\"On Tuesday I have got English, maths and Arabic with Mrs Rachidi.\"</strong> Six corrections: <em>On</em> (sentence start), <em>Tuesday</em> (day), <em>I</em> (pronoun), <em>English</em> and <em>Arabic</em> (languages), <em>Mrs Rachidi</em> (name and title). <em>maths</em> correctly stays in lower case — the trap of the exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 2.</strong> <em>my favourite subject is english because i like the songs.</em> → <strong>\"My favourite subject is English because I like the songs.\"</strong> Note that <em>because</em> does not start a new sentence here, so no capital after it.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — model paragraph (81 words).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>My school subjects.</strong> My name is Youssef and I am in class 1AC/3 at Al Amal Middle School in Agadir. We've got twelve subjects this year. My favourite subject is English, because the lessons are fun and I like the songs. I've got four hours of English a week: on Tuesday, on Thursday and on Saturday. Maths is difficult for me, but my teacher Mr Alami says: \"Try again!\" In Ramadan we've got shorter lessons. And you? What is your favourite subject?</p></blockquote>\n<p>The class counts the capitals (fourteen) and checks each against the seven rules, then counts the full stops and the one question mark.</p>\n<p><strong>Pair work (capital hunt).</strong> Partners exchange notebooks and check <strong>only</strong> the capitals, using the six-point checklist, marking a small circle around each letter that should be a capital. The author then rewrites the sentences concerned. As in EN20, a narrow, mechanical peer-correction task is fast, accurate and manageable even in a very large class.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Capital?</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>first word of a sentence</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>W</strong>e've got maths.</td></tr><tr><td>the pronoun <em>I</em></td><td>yes, always</td><td>my sister and <strong>I</strong></td></tr><tr><td>days and months</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>M</strong>onday, <strong>S</strong>eptember</td></tr><tr><td>languages and nationalities</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>E</strong>nglish, <strong>A</strong>rabic, <strong>M</strong>oroccan</td></tr><tr><td>countries and cities</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>M</strong>orocco, <strong>A</strong>gadir, <strong>F</strong>ez</td></tr><tr><td>names and titles</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>M</strong>r <strong>A</strong>lami, <strong>S</strong>alma</td></tr><tr><td>festivals</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>R</strong>amadan, <strong>E</strong>id</td></tr><tr><td>titles of books/pages</td><td>yes (main words)</td><td><strong>W</strong>hat's <strong>U</strong>p</td></tr><tr><td>non-language subjects</td><td>no</td><td>maths, history, art, music</td></tr><tr><td>seasons</td><td>no</td><td>spring, summer</td></tr><tr><td>common nouns</td><td>no</td><td>a teacher, a school</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Six-point proof-reading checklist</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>capital at the start of every sentence</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>I</em> always capital</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>days and months capital</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>languages, nationalities, countries capital</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>names of people and places capital</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>full stop or question mark at the end of every sentence</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Useful sentence frames</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>My favourite subject is … because …</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I've got … hours of … a week.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>… is difficult for me, but …</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>We've got … on Monday and Thursday.</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<p>⚠️ <em>english, arabic, french</em> in lower case — languages always take a capital. ⚠️ <em>monday, september</em> in lower case, by transfer from French <em>lundi, septembre</em>. ⚠️ <em>i</em> in lower case: the English pronoun <em>I</em> is always a capital. ⚠️ Over-capitalising: <em>Maths, History, Art</em> in mid-sentence do <strong>not</strong> need a capital.</p>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Capital or not? (a) monday (b) maths (c) english (d) history (e) agadir (f) ramadan.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Rewrite with capitals: (a) on tuesday we have got french. (b) my name is salma. (c) i live in fez.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Find the rule for each capital: <em>On Monday I have got English with Mr Alami in Agadir.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Correct only the wrong capitals: (a) I like Maths. (b) We've got english on monday. (c) My teacher is mr alami.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Write the seven days and three months with capitals.</p>\n<p><em>Level 2 — Practice (Exercices 6–10)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Rewrite the text with capitals and full stops: <em>my name is karim i am in class 1ac/3 in agadir on tuesday we have got english and arabic my teacher is mrs rachidi</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Write four sentences about your subjects, each containing at least two capitals.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Which words need a capital? <em>ramadan · school · spring · morocco · teacher · amazigh</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Correct: (a) my favourite subject is History. (b) i have got Maths on friday. (c) we speak arabic and french in morocco.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write three sentences with <em>I</em>, one day and one language in each.</p>\n<p><em>Level 3 — Going further (Exercices 11–12)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a paragraph of 60–90 words entitled \"My school subjects\": your class and school, your subjects, your favourite with <em>because</em>, a difficult subject, and a final question to the reader. Apply the six-point check.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write a short web-page section (80–100 words) presenting your class for your school website, then underline every capital letter and write the rule number (1–7) above it.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: 20 marks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Task 1 (6 marks).</strong> Capital or not? (a) tuesday (b) science (c) arabic (d) fez (e) music (f) eid.</p>\n<p><strong>Task 2 (6 marks).</strong> Rewrite with capitals and full stops: <em>my name is youssef i am in 1ac/3 in agadir on saturday we have got english and pe my teacher is mr alami</em></p>\n<p><strong>Task 3 (8 marks).</strong> Write a paragraph of 60–90 words entitled \"My school subjects\": your class and school, your favourite subject with <em>because</em>, one difficult subject, your hours of English, and a final question to the reader.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer key and marking.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Task 1 (1 each): capitals: Tuesday, Arabic, Fez, Eid · lower case: science, music.</li><li>Task 2 (6): My name is Youssef. I am in 1AC/3 in Agadir. On Saturday we have got English and PE. My teacher is Mr Alami. — capitals 4 · full stops 2.</li><li>Task 3 (8): content points 3 · capitals 2 · <em>because</em> 1 · punctuation 1 · final question 1.</li></ul>\n<p>## Unit 6 — Let's play after school!</p>\n<p>Unit 6 opens the second half of the <em>What's Up</em> 1AC programme. Learners move from the verb <em>be</em> and <em>have got</em> to their first <strong>full present simple</strong> with <em>I / you / we / they</em>, so that they can talk about what they really do after class in Casablanca, Ifrane or Zagora. The five lessons follow the official order of the unit: Words, Grammar, Phonics, Reading, Writing.</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<em>Devoir à faire sur le cahier — correction avec le professeur.</em>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en26","domaine":"anglais","unite":26,"theme":"Unit 6 — Let's play after school!","tag":"EN26","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 6 - Words (after-school activities)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Name</strong> at least twelve after-school activities in English (<em>play football, visit my grandparents, help my mother, do my homework…</em>).","<strong>Pronounce</strong> the new items with correct stress and read them aloud from flashcards.","<strong>Match</strong> each activity to a place (<em>club, house, street, playground, library</em>) and to a day of the week.","<strong>Ask and answer</strong> in pairs: <em>What do you do after school? — I play football with my friends.</em>","<strong>Classify</strong> the activities into three groups: <em>sport / home / study</em>.","<strong>Write</strong> three true sentences about his or her own afternoon."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée / Warm-up situation.</strong> It is 4 p.m. in a middle school in Salé. The bell rings. Yassine takes his football and runs to the playground. Salma goes home and helps her mother in the kitchen. Their friend Amine walks to the neighbourhood library because he has an English test on Friday. Three pupils, three afternoons: to talk about them in English we need a <strong>word bank of after-school activities</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte / Discovery activity.</strong> The teacher writes four pictures-words on the board (a ball, a book, a broom, a bicycle) and asks: <em>What can you see?</em> Learners answer with the words they already know from EN01 and EN11 (<em>ball, book, bike</em>). The teacher then adds a verb in front of each noun: <strong>play</strong> football, <strong>do</strong> my homework, <strong>help</strong> my mother, <strong>ride</strong> my bike. The class discovers the golden rule of this unit: in English an activity is almost always <strong>verb + noun</strong>, not a single word.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — an activity phrase</strong> An <em>activity phrase</em> is a <strong>verb</strong> followed by a <strong>noun</strong> or a <strong>place</strong>: <em>play basketball</em>, <em>watch TV</em>, <em>visit my cousins</em>, <em>go to the club</em>. In English you cannot say <em>I football</em>. You must say <em>I <strong>play</strong> football</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The four \"big verbs\" of Unit 6</strong> <strong>play</strong> + sport / game / instrument → <em>play football, play cards, play the guitar</em> <strong>do</strong> + homework / housework / sport in general → <em>do my homework, do karate</em> <strong>go</strong> + to + place → <em>go to the club, go to the mosque, go to the market</em> <strong>watch / help / visit / ride / read</strong> + noun → <em>watch TV, help my father, visit my grandmother, ride my bike, read a book</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas / Step by step.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Listen and repeat.</strong> The teacher says the phrase twice; the class repeats twice; then rows repeat one by one. The stress falls on the noun: <em>play FOOTball</em>, <em>do my HOMEwork</em>.</li><li><strong>Look at the spelling.</strong> Learners copy the phrase and underline the verb in red and the noun in blue. This colour code prevents the classic mistake <em>I am play football</em>.</li><li><strong>Add a detail.</strong> Every phrase can grow: <em>play football</em> → <em>play football <strong>with my friends</strong></em> → <em>play football with my friends <strong>in the street</strong></em>.</li><li><strong>Say it about yourself.</strong> The learner chooses three phrases that are true for him or her and says them aloud.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Picture: a girl with a schoolbag in front of a building full of books. Question: <em>What does she do after school?</em> — Wrong answer: <em>She library.</em> — Correct answer: <em>She <strong>goes to the library</strong>.</em> We need the verb <em>go</em> + <em>to</em> + place.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Yassine's afternoon in three sentences: <em>After school, I play football with my friends. Then I do my homework. In the evening, I watch TV with my family.</em> Notice the three time expressions: <em>after school</em>, <em>then</em>, <em>in the evening</em>. They organise the afternoon like the three parts of a paragraph.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Classification task. Put these six phrases in the right column: <em>play basketball / read a book / help my mother / do my homework / ride my bike / clean my room</em>.</p>\n<p>``<code> SPORT & PLAY        | STUDY              | HOME play basketball     | read a book        | help my mother ride my bike        | do my homework     | clean my room </code>``</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain / Moroccan context.</strong> Many 1AC learners in Morocco attend a <strong>دار الشباب (youth centre)</strong>, help in a family shop, or go to the <em>hammam</em> with their family on Friday. All of these can be said in simple English: <em>I go to the youth centre. I help in my father's shop. I go to the market with my mother.</em> Prices are in dirhams: <em>A ball costs 60 dirhams.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> Learner A: <em>What do you do after school?</em> Learner B answers with two phrases, then asks the same question. Each pair then reports one sentence to the class: <em>Karim plays football and helps his mother.</em> (The <em>-s</em> of the third person is only introduced in Unit 7; here the teacher accepts it as a \"gift\" but does not test it.)</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>play football</td><td>/pleɪ ˈfʊtbɔːl/</td><td>يلعب كرة القدم</td><td>I play football on Saturday.</td></tr><tr><td>play basketball</td><td>/pleɪ ˈbɑːskɪtbɔːl/</td><td>يلعب كرة السلة</td><td>We play basketball at school.</td></tr><tr><td>do my homework</td><td>/duː maɪ ˈhəʊmwɜːk/</td><td>يقوم بواجباته المنزلية</td><td>I do my homework at 6 o'clock.</td></tr><tr><td>watch TV</td><td>/wɒtʃ ˌtiːˈviː/</td><td>يشاهد التلفاز</td><td>I watch TV in the evening.</td></tr><tr><td>ride my bike</td><td>/raɪd maɪ baɪk/</td><td>يركب دراجته</td><td>I ride my bike in the park.</td></tr><tr><td>visit my grandparents</td><td>/ˈvɪzɪt maɪ ˈɡrænpeərənts/</td><td>يزور جده وجدته</td><td>I visit my grandparents on Friday.</td></tr><tr><td>help my mother / father</td><td>/help maɪ ˈmʌðə/</td><td>يساعد أمه / أباه</td><td>I help my mother at home.</td></tr><tr><td>read a book</td><td>/riːd ə bʊk/</td><td>يقرأ كتابا</td><td>I read a book before bed.</td></tr><tr><td>go to the club</td><td>/ɡəʊ tə ðə klʌb/</td><td>يذهب إلى النادي</td><td>I go to the club after school.</td></tr><tr><td>go to the library</td><td>/ˈlaɪbrəri/</td><td>يذهب إلى المكتبة</td><td>I go to the library on Wednesday.</td></tr><tr><td>clean my room</td><td>/kliːn maɪ ruːm/</td><td>ينظف غرفته</td><td>I clean my room on Sunday.</td></tr><tr><td>listen to music</td><td>/ˈlɪsn tə ˈmjuːzɪk/</td><td>يستمع إلى الموسيقى</td><td>I listen to music at home.</td></tr><tr><td>play the guitar</td><td>/pleɪ ðə ɡɪˈtɑː/</td><td>يعزف على الغيتار</td><td>My brother plays the guitar.</td></tr><tr><td>walk in the garden</td><td>/wɔːk/</td><td>يتمشى في الحديقة</td><td>We walk in the garden.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Time expressions</strong>: <em>after school, in the afternoon, in the evening, every day, on Monday, at the weekend, then, and</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Missing verb.</strong> ✗ <em>I football after school.</em> → ✓ <em>I <strong>play</strong> football after school.</em></li><li>⚠️ **Verb <em>be</em> + verb.** ✗ <em>I am play basketball.</em> → ✓ <em>I play basketball.</em> (There is no <em>am</em> in a present simple sentence with an action verb.)</li><li>⚠️ **Missing <em>to</em> after <em>go</em>.** ✗ <em>I go the club.</em> → ✓ <em>I go <strong>to</strong> the club.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Wrong big verb.</strong> ✗ <em>I do football / I play my homework.</em> → ✓ <em>I <strong>play</strong> football / I <strong>do</strong> my homework.</em></li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Match the verb with the noun. 1. play … 2. do … 3. watch … 4. ride … 5. visit … a. my bike b. my homework c. football d. my grandparents e. TV</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Complete with <em>play</em>, <em>do</em>, <em>go</em> or <em>watch</em>.**</p>\n<ol><li>I ____ my homework in the evening. 2. We ____ basketball at school. 3. They ____ to the library on Wednesday. 4. Amina ____ TV with her sister. 5. I ____ the guitar.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Find the word that does not belong.</p>\n<ol><li>football / basketball / homework / volleyball</li><li>library / club / market / bike</li><li>mother / father / grandparents / room</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Write T or F.</p>\n<ol><li>I play football after school. 2. I clean my room every day. 3. I read a book in the evening. 4. I go to the club on Saturday.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>after / I / football / school / play</li><li>homework / do / my / I / at / 6 o'clock</li><li>library / go / I / the / to</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>these phrases into SPORT / STUDY / HOME: <em>ride my bike, do my homework, help my father, play volleyball, read a book, clean my room</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>**Exercice 7 — Add <em>to</em> where it is necessary.**</p>\n<ol><li>I go ____ the market. 2. I watch ____ TV. 3. We go ____ the youth centre. 4. She listens ____ music.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<ol><li>I ____ my mother in the kitchen. 2. My friends ____ to the club. 3. We ____ music on my phone. 4. I ____ a book about Morocco.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>A: What do you ____ after school? B: I ____ basketball with my friends. And you? A: I ____ my homework, then I ____ TV.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>about your afternoon, using <em>after school</em>, <em>then</em> and <em>in the evening</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Match the word with its transcription: 1. bike 2. book 3. music 4. club — a. /bʊk/ b. /klʌb/ c. /baɪk/ d. /ˈmjuːzɪk/</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write four questions you can ask a classmate about his or her afternoon (use <em>What…?</em>, <em>Where…?</em>, <em>When…?</em>, <em>Do you…?</em>).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>\"My afternoon\". Use at least five activity phrases and two time expressions.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Look at this afternoon plan and write one sentence for each day. ``<code> Monday: library | Tuesday: football | Wednesday: help father in the shop Thursday: homework + TV | Friday: visit grandparents </code>``</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"play football","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/pleɪ ˈfʊtbɔːl/</span> I play football on Saturday. — يلعب كرة القدم"},{"r":"play basketball","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/pleɪ ˈbɑːskɪtbɔːl/</span> We play basketball at school. — يلعب كرة السلة"},{"r":"do my homework","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/duː maɪ ˈhəʊmwɜːk/</span> I do my homework at 6 o'clock. — يقوم بواجباته المنزلية"},{"r":"watch TV","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/wɒtʃ ˌtiːˈviː/</span> I watch TV in the evening. — يشاهد التلفاز"},{"r":"ride my bike","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/raɪd maɪ baɪk/</span> I ride my bike in the park. — يركب دراجته"},{"r":"visit my grandparents","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈvɪzɪt maɪ ˈɡrænpeərənts/</span> I visit my grandparents on Friday. — يزور جده وجدته"},{"r":"help my mother / father","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/help maɪ ˈmʌðə/</span> I help my mother at home. — يساعد أمه / أباه"},{"r":"read a book","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/riːd ə bʊk/</span> I read a book before bed. — يقرأ كتابا"},{"r":"go to the club","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ɡəʊ tə ðə klʌb/</span> I go to the club after school. — يذهب إلى النادي"},{"r":"go to the library","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈlaɪbrəri/</span> I go to the library on Wednesday. — يذهب إلى المكتبة"},{"r":"clean my room","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/kliːn maɪ ruːm/</span> I clean my room on Sunday. — ينظف غرفته"},{"r":"listen to music","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ˈlɪsn tə ˈmjuːzɪk/</span> I listen to music at home. — يستمع إلى الموسيقى"},{"r":"play the guitar","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/pleɪ ðə ɡɪˈtɑː/</span> My brother plays the guitar. — يعزف على الغيتار"},{"r":"walk in the garden","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/wɔːk/</span> We walk in the garden. — يتمشى في الحديقة"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong> — time: 25 minutes at home.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8) — Vocabulary.</strong> Complete with <em>play</em>, <em>do</em>, <em>go to</em>, <em>help</em>, <em>watch</em>, <em>visit</em>, <em>read</em>, <em>clean</em> (one verb per sentence, 1 pt each).</p>\n<ol><li>I ____ my homework every evening. 2. We ____ football on Saturday. 3. I ____ the library on Wednesday. 4. She ____ TV at 8 o'clock. 5. I ____ my mother in the kitchen. 6. They ____ their grandparents on Friday. 7. I ____ a book before bed. 8. I ____ my room on Sunday.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6) — Sentence building.</strong> Write three correct sentences with the words given (2 pts each: 1 pt word order, 1 pt spelling).</p>\n<ol><li>after school / I / the club / go to</li><li>my bike / ride / I / in the park</li><li>music / listen to / I / in the evening</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6) — Writing.</strong> Write a short paragraph (40–50 words) about your afternoon. Barème: 5 activity phrases correct (3 pts) + 2 time expressions (1 pt) + capital letters and full stops (1 pt) + handwriting and length (1 pt).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. do 2. play 3. go to 4. watches (accept <em>watch</em>) 5. help 6. visit 7. read 8. clean. B. 1. After school, I go to the club. 2. I ride my bike in the park. 3. I listen to music in the evening. C. Sample answer (44 words): <em>After school, I go home. I help my mother, then I do my homework in my room. I read a book about football. In the evening, I watch TV with my family. On Friday, I visit my grandparents in Settat.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en27","domaine":"anglais","unite":27,"theme":"Unit 6 — Let's play after school!","tag":"EN27","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 6 - Grammar (I visit ... / I don't have ...)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Form</strong> affirmative present simple sentences with <em>I, you, we, they</em> + base verb.","<strong>Form</strong> the negative with <strong>don't</strong> + base verb.","<strong>Ask</strong> <em>yes/no</em> questions with <strong>Do you …?</strong> and answer <em>Yes, I do. / No, I don't.</em>","<strong>Distinguish</strong> the verb <em>be</em> (<em>I am happy</em>) from an action verb (<em>I play football</em>).","<strong>Use</strong> the frequency words <em>every day, always, sometimes, never, on Monday</em>.","<strong>Write</strong> six correct sentences about a real afternoon, three affirmative and three negative."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Two friends in Marrakech write to an English pen-friend: <em>\"We play football every day. We don't play tennis. Do you play football in England?\"</em> Three sentences, three forms: <strong>affirmative, negative, question</strong>. This lesson gives learners the machine that produces them.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> On the board the teacher writes two sentences taken from EN26 and asks the class to spot the difference: <em>I am happy.</em> — <em>I play football.</em> Learners notice that the first has <em>am</em> and an <strong>adjective</strong>, the second has an <strong>action verb</strong> and no <em>am</em>. Rule discovered: <strong>one verb per sentence</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — Affirmative (I / you / we / they)</strong> <strong>Subject + base verb + complement</strong> <em>I visit my grandparents on Friday.</em> · <em>You do your homework.</em> · <em>We play basketball.</em> · <em>They watch TV.</em> No <em>s</em>, no <em>am/is/are</em>, no <em>do</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — Negative</strong> <strong>Subject + don't + base verb</strong>  (<em>don't</em> = <em>do not</em>) <em>I don't have a bike.</em> · <em>We don't play tennis.</em> · <em>They don't watch TV in the morning.</em> ⚠️ After <em>don't</em> the verb never changes: <em>don't <strong>have</strong></em>, not <em>don't has</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — Question (yes/no)</strong> <strong>Do + subject + base verb …?</strong> <em>Do you play football?</em> → <em>Yes, I do. / No, I don't.</em> <em>Do they live in Fez?</em> → <em>Yes, they do. / No, they don't.</em> Short answers never repeat the main verb: ✗ <em>Yes, I play.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — Word order and frequency</strong> Time and frequency words go <strong>before the verb</strong> (<em>always, sometimes, never</em>) or <strong>at the end / beginning</strong> of the sentence (<em>every day, on Friday, after school</em>). <em>I <strong>always</strong> help my mother.</em> · <em>I help my mother <strong>every day</strong>.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas.</strong> To build any sentence, use the four-box method:</p>\n<p>``<code> [ SUBJECT ] [ don't ? ] [ BASE VERB ] [ COMPLEMENT + TIME ] I           —            play        football on Saturday We        don't          watch       TV in the morning Do you        —            visit       your grandparents ? </code>``</p>\n<ol><li>Choose the subject: <em>I, you, we, they</em> (or a plural noun: <em>my friends, the pupils</em>).</li><li>Decide: affirmative (nothing), negative (<em>don't</em>), question (<em>Do</em> at the front).</li><li>Write the verb <strong>in the base form</strong>, always the same.</li><li>Add the complement and, if you want, a time expression.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Make the sentence <em>I have a computer</em> negative. Step 1: subject <em>I</em>. Step 2: add <em>don't</em>. Step 3: keep the base verb <em>have</em>. → <em>I <strong>don't have</strong> a computer.</em> ⚠️ Not <em>I haven't a computer</em> (that belongs to <em>have got</em>: <em>I haven't got a computer</em>), and not <em>I don't has</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Turn <em>You play the guitar</em> into a question and answer it. <em>Do you play the guitar?</em> — <em>No, I don't. I play football.</em> The auxiliary <em>Do</em> jumps to the front; the verb stays in the base form.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> A short pen-friend paragraph, checked box by box: <em>My friends and I live in Agadir. We go to school at 8 o'clock. We don't go to school on Sunday. After school, we play football on the beach. We don't watch TV in the afternoon. Do you play football in your town?</em> Each sentence has exactly one verb; every negative uses <em>don't</em> + base verb; the question begins with <em>Do</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Good practice content: <em>We don't go to school on Sunday. We eat couscous on Friday. I speak Arabic and Amazigh. I don't speak Spanish. In Ramadan, we don't eat in the afternoon.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Find someone who…\" cards: each learner receives three prompts (<em>play chess, ride a bike, help at home</em>) and asks three classmates <em>Do you …?</em>, noting <em>Yes, I do</em> / <em>No, I don't</em>. Then they report: <em>Two pupils play chess. Three pupils don't ride a bike.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Structure</th><th>Example</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Affirmative</td><td>I/you/we/they + verb</td><td><em>We play football.</em></td><td>نلعب كرة القدم</td></tr><tr><td>Negative</td><td>+ <strong>don't</strong> + verb</td><td><em>We don't play football.</em></td><td>لا نلعب كرة القدم</td></tr><tr><td>Yes/no question</td><td><strong>Do</strong> + subject + verb…?</td><td><em>Do you play football?</em></td><td>هل تلعب كرة القدم؟</td></tr><tr><td>Short answers</td><td><em>Yes, I do. / No, I don't.</em></td><td>—</td><td>نعم / لا</td></tr><tr><td>Wh- question</td><td><strong>What do</strong> you do…?</td><td><em>What do you do after school?</em></td><td>ماذا تفعل بعد المدرسة؟</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Frequency words</strong>: <em>always</em> /ˈɔːlweɪz/, <em>usually</em> /ˈjuːʒuəli/, <em>sometimes</em> /ˈsʌmtaɪmz/, <em>never</em> /ˈnevə/, <em>every day</em>, <em>once a week</em>. <strong>Pronunciation</strong>: <em>don't</em> /dəʊnt/ (silent <em>o</em>+<em>n</em> nasal? no — one syllable, long /əʊ/); <em>Do you</em> is often linked: /djuː/.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Two verbs.</strong> ✗ <em>I am play football.</em> → ✓ <em>I play football.</em></li><li>⚠️ **Verb after <em>don't</em> changed.** ✗ <em>They don't plays / don't has.</em> → ✓ <em>They don't play / don't have.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>No auxiliary in the question.</strong> ✗ <em>You play football?</em> → ✓ <em>Do you play football?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Full answer instead of short answer.</strong> ✗ <em>Yes, I play.</em> → ✓ <em>Yes, I do.</em></li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>I (play / plays) football. 2. We (don't / doesn't) watch TV. 3. (Do / Are) you help your mother? 4. They (go / goes) to the club. 5. I (am / —) read a book.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>I have a bike. 2. We play tennis. 3. They watch TV in the morning. 4. You do your homework at school.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>You play basketball. 2. They live in Oujda. 3. You visit your grandparents on Friday.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>Do you play chess? (No) 2. Do they go to school on Sunday? (No) 3. Do you speak Arabic? (Yes)</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>**Exercice 5 — Complete with <em>do</em> or <em>don't</em>.**</p>\n<ol><li>____ you like football? 2. I ____ have a computer. 3. We ____ go to the club on Monday. 4. What ____ you do after school?</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>I am play football. 2. She don't has a bike. 3. You play tennis? 4. Yes, I play.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>don't / we / on Sunday / go / to school</li><li>do / what / after school / you / do / ?</li><li>always / my mother / I / help</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>with the verbs: <em>live, go, play, don't watch, help</em>. <em>My friends and I ____ in Tetouan. We ____ to school at 8 o'clock. After school, we ____ football. We ____ TV in the afternoon. In the evening, I ____ my little sister with her homework.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>For each sentence write the three forms.</p>\n<ol><li>You ride a bike. 2. They read magazines.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>: three with <em>I</em> + verb, three with <em>I don't</em> + verb.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Put the frequency word in the right place.</p>\n<ol><li>I help my father. (always) 2. We play cards. (sometimes) 3. They go to the cinema. (never) 4. I do my homework. (every day)</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write four <em>Do you…?</em> questions, then write the report sentences for a partner who answers <em>Yes / No / Yes / No</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>Write to an English pen-friend about your week: use at least two affirmative sentences, two negatives and one question.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>One sentence in each group is grammatically wrong; find it and correct it. a. <em>I don't play tennis. / We go to school. / They doesn't watch TV.</em> b. <em>Do you like music? / What do you do? / You do live in Fez?</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Complete with <em>do</em>, <em>don't</em> or nothing (—). 1 pt each.</p>\n<ol><li>I ____ play football on Saturday. 2. We ____ go to school on Sunday. 3. ____ you have a bike? 4. What ____ you do after school? 5. They ____ watch TV in the morning. 6. You ____ live in Rabat. 7. ____ they speak English? 8. I ____ have a computer at home.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Rewrite in the negative, then as a question (1 pt + 1 pt each).</p>\n<ol><li>You play chess. 2. They visit their grandparents. 3. We read magazines.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6).</strong> Write an e-mail of 50–60 words to a pen-friend about your week. Barème: 2 affirmative sentences (2 pts), 2 negatives with <em>don't</em> (2 pts), 1 question with <em>Do</em> (1 pt), punctuation and length (1 pt).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. — 2. don't 3. Do 4. do 5. don't 6. — 7. Do 8. don't. B. 1. <em>You don't play chess. / Do you play chess?</em> 2. <em>They don't visit their grandparents. / Do they visit their grandparents?</em> 3. <em>We don't read magazines. / Do we read magazines?</em> C. Sample (54 words): <em>Hi Sara, I live in Beni Mellal. I go to school from Monday to Saturday. I don't go to school on Sunday. After school, I help my father in his shop and I do my homework. I don't play video games. On Friday, we eat couscous. Do you eat couscous in England?</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en28","domaine":"anglais","unite":28,"theme":"Unit 6 — Let's play after school!","tag":"EN28","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 6 - Phonics (consonant blends: fl pl bl)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Identify</strong> the blends /fl/, /pl/, /bl/ at the beginning of a word.","<strong>Blend</strong> the two consonants without inserting a vowel between them.","<strong>Read</strong> aloud twelve <em>fl / pl / bl</em> words with correct pronunciation.","<strong>Write</strong> words correctly after dictation.","<strong>Sort</strong> words into three sound families."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The teacher says three words very slowly: <em>flag — plane — blue</em>. Then he writes on the board what learners often say: <em>\"filag\", \"pilane\", \"bilue\"</em>. Arabic and Moroccan Darija do not usually begin a word with two consonants, so learners add a small vowel. This lesson trains the mouth to say <strong>two consonants in one movement</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> Learners look at three groups of words and find what is common: <em>flag, flower, floor</em> — <em>plane, plant, play</em> — <em>blue, black, blackboard</em>. They discover that each group starts with <strong>two letters, two sounds, one movement</strong>: f+l, p+l, b+l. The second letter is always <strong>l</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — a consonant blend</strong> A <strong>blend</strong> is two consonants pronounced together, each keeping its own sound: <strong>fl</strong> = /f/ + /l/, <strong>pl</strong> = /p/ + /l/, <strong>bl</strong> = /b/ + /l/. A blend is different from a <strong>digraph</strong> (<em>sh, ch, th</em>), where two letters make <strong>one new sound</strong>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The three blends of Unit 6</strong> <strong>fl</strong> /fl/ → <em>flag, flower, floor, fly, flat</em> <strong>pl</strong> /pl/ → <em>plane, plant, play, plate, please</em> <strong>bl</strong> /bl/ → <em>blue, black, blackboard, blanket, block</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — \"no vowel inside\".</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Say the /l/ alone: put the tip of the tongue behind the top teeth: <em>l… l… l…</em>.</li><li>Say the first sound alone: /f/ (air between teeth and lip), /p/ (lips pop), /b/ (lips pop with voice).</li><li>Join them <strong>without opening the mouth for a vowel</strong>: /f/+/l/ → /fl/. Practise with a mirror.</li><li>Add the rest of the word: /fl/ + /æɡ/ → <em>flag</em> /flæɡ/.</li><li>Check: if you can hear \"fi-lag\", the mouth stopped between the two consonants; start again faster.</li></ol>\n<p>``<code> f + l -> fl      p + l -> pl      b + l -> bl /f//l/   /fl/     /p//l/  /pl/     /b//l/  /bl/ flag             plane            blue </code>``</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Read <em>blackboard</em> /ˈblækbɔːd/. Cut it: <em>black</em> + <em>board</em>. First blend /bl/, then /æk/, then /bɔːd/. The stress is on the first part: <strong>BLACK</strong>board.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Dictation of <em>plant</em>: the learner hears /plɑːnt/ (British) or /plænt/. He writes p-l-a-n-t. The frequent error is <em>palant</em>; the method is to write the two consonants first, then the vowel.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Minimal-pair discrimination: <em>play</em> /pleɪ/ vs <em>pray</em> /preɪ/, <em>flat</em> /flæt/ vs <em>frat</em>, <em>blue</em> /bluː/ vs <em>brew</em>. The tongue touches the teeth for /l/; it curls back for /r/. Learners hold a finger under the chin to feel the difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Useful classroom and local words with these blends: <em>blackboard, blue (the blue city of Chefchaouen), flag (the Moroccan flag is red with a green star), plane (Mohammed V airport), plate (a plate of couscous), plant, please</em>. The sentence <em>Please clean the blackboard</em> is a real classroom instruction; <em>The flag is red</em> describes a real object in the school yard.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> Learner A reads a word from a list of 12; learner B points at it and repeats. Then they swap. Second round: A says a sentence (<em>The blue plate is on the floor</em>), B repeats it, and both count the blends they hear.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Blend</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>flag</td><td>/flæɡ/</td><td>fl</td><td>علم</td></tr><tr><td>flower</td><td>/ˈflaʊə/</td><td>fl</td><td>زهرة</td></tr><tr><td>floor</td><td>/flɔː/</td><td>fl</td><td>أرضية</td></tr><tr><td>fly</td><td>/flaɪ/</td><td>fl</td><td>يطير</td></tr><tr><td>plane</td><td>/pleɪn/</td><td>pl</td><td>طائرة</td></tr><tr><td>plant</td><td>/plɑːnt/</td><td>pl</td><td>نبات</td></tr><tr><td>play</td><td>/pleɪ/</td><td>pl</td><td>يلعب</td></tr><tr><td>plate</td><td>/pleɪt/</td><td>pl</td><td>طبق</td></tr><tr><td>please</td><td>/pliːz/</td><td>pl</td><td>من فضلك</td></tr><tr><td>blue</td><td>/bluː/</td><td>bl</td><td>أزرق</td></tr><tr><td>black</td><td>/blæk/</td><td>bl</td><td>أسود</td></tr><tr><td>blackboard</td><td>/ˈblækbɔːd/</td><td>bl</td><td>سبورة</td></tr><tr><td>blanket</td><td>/ˈblæŋkɪt/</td><td>bl</td><td>غطاء</td></tr><tr><td>block</td><td>/blɒk/</td><td>bl</td><td>كتلة / عمارة</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Adding a vowel inside the blend.</strong> ✗ /fiˈlæɡ/ <em>filag</em> → ✓ /flæɡ/ <em>flag</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Confusing /l/ and /r/.</strong> ✗ <em>pray</em> for <em>play</em> → keep the tongue tip on the teeth ridge for /l/.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Confusing /b/ and /p/.</strong> <em>blue</em> (voice) vs <em>plue</em> (no voice): put a hand on the throat to feel the vibration of /b/.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Spelling the blend backwards.</strong> ✗ <em>lfag, lpate</em> → always write the letter <em>l</em> <strong>second</strong>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>you hear at the beginning: 1. flag (fl / pl / bl) 2. blue (fl / pl / bl) 3. plate (fl / pl / bl) 4. floor (fl / pl / bl) 5. black (fl / pl / bl)</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Complete with <em>fl</em>, <em>pl</em> or <em>bl</em>.**</p>\n<ol><li>____ower 2. ____ane 3. ____ackboard 4. ____oor 5. ____ate 6. ____anket</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>into three columns (fl / pl / bl): <em>fly, block, please, flat, blue, play</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>How many sounds are in each word? 1. flag 2. play 3. blue</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>and tick the word your partner says: 1. play / pray 2. blue / brew 3. flat / frat</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Write the words your teacher says: <em>flag, plane, blackboard, flower, plate, blue</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>flag / flower / plane 2. blue / black / plate 3. play / please / floor</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>with words from the lesson.</p>\n<ol><li>The Moroccan ____ is red and green. 2. Please clean the ____. 3. My schoolbag is ____. 4. The ____ is on the table.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>from the IPA: 1. /pleɪ/ 2. /flɔː/ 3. /blæk/ 4. /pliːz/</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Copy and read three times: <em>Blue plates and black flags fly on the floor.</em> Underline every blend.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>in the spelling: 1. lfower 2. palane 3. bulack 4. pilease</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Find in your English book three more words with <em>fl</em>, <em>pl</em> or <em>bl</em> and write them with their blend.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>, each containing at least two words with the blends of the lesson.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Copy this table and add two words of your own in each column. ``<code> fl        | pl        | bl flag      | plane     | blue </code>``</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Complete with <em>fl</em>, <em>pl</em> or <em>bl</em> (1 pt each): 1. ____ag 2. ____ate 3. ____ue 4. ____ower 5. ____ay 6. ____ack 7. ____oor 8. ____ease.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Write the word for each transcription (1.5 pt each): 1. /ˈblækbɔːd/ 2. /pleɪn/ 3. /flaɪ/ 4. /blæŋkɪt/.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6).</strong> Write three sentences with at least two blend words in each (2 pts each: 1 pt for the blends, 1 pt for grammar and punctuation).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. flag 2. plate 3. blue 4. flower 5. play 6. black 7. floor 8. please. B. 1. blackboard 2. plane 3. fly 4. blanket. C. Sample: <em>The blue flag is next to the blackboard. Please put the black plate on the floor. I play with a plane and a flower.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en29","domaine":"anglais","unite":29,"theme":"Unit 6 — Let's play after school!","tag":"EN29","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 6 - Reading (information texts)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Identify</strong> the type of a short information text (title, sub-titles, paragraphs, key facts).","<strong>Scan</strong> the text to find precise information (name, age, town, activity, day).","<strong>Answer</strong> true/false and Wh- questions in complete sentences.","<strong>Guess</strong> the meaning of two new words from the context.","<strong>Transfer</strong> the information into a table.","<strong>Speak</strong> in pairs about one club they would like to join."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The school of a small town near Beni Mellal puts a poster on the wall: three after-school clubs are open on Wednesday afternoon. To choose one, a learner has to <strong>read for information</strong>, not word by word, but looking for the facts he needs: which club, which day, what time, what price.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> Before reading, the teacher asks the class to look only at the <strong>title</strong> and the <strong>three sub-titles</strong> and to predict the content. This is called <strong>pre-reading</strong>: the eyes travel over the page and the brain prepares the vocabulary. Then learners read once for the general idea (<em>skimming</em>) and a second time to find details (<em>scanning</em>).</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method — reading an information text in three steps</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Before reading</strong>: look at the title, the sub-titles, the numbers. Predict.</li><li><strong>First reading (skimming)</strong>: read fast; answer <em>What is the text about?</em></li><li><strong>Second reading (scanning)</strong>: look for one piece of information at a time; underline it with a pencil.</li></ol></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Original reading text (written for this document).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>AFTER-SCHOOL CLUBS AT IBN BATTOUTA MIDDLE SCHOOL</strong> <strong>The Sports Club</strong> The Sports Club is open on Monday and Wednesday from 3 p.m. to 5 p.m. The pupils play football, basketball and volleyball in the school yard. Mr Rachid is the teacher. He says: \"We don't play in the rain. In winter we do exercises in the big hall.\" Thirty pupils come to the club every week. It is free. <strong>The English Club</strong> The English Club is in room 12. It is open on Wednesday afternoon, from 3 p.m. to 4.30 p.m. The pupils read easy stories, sing songs and play word games. Mrs Nadia is the teacher. The members write a small newspaper in English. It has four pages and a picture of the school on the first page. The club has twenty-two members this year. <strong>The Garden Club</strong> The Garden Club is small: only twelve pupils. They meet on Friday at 3 p.m. They plant flowers and small trees near the gate. They don't use plastic bottles; they use old cans for the young plants. Water is important in our region, so the pupils water the garden in the evening, not at midday. The club has a blue box for seeds.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Comprehension work step by step.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Global question</strong>: <em>What is the text about?</em> → Three after-school clubs in a Moroccan middle school.</li><li><strong>Scanning practice</strong>: to find <em>when the English Club is open</em>, do not read everything: look for the word <em>Wednesday</em> or a number with <em>p.m.</em></li><li><strong>Answering in a full sentence</strong>: question <em>When is the Garden Club open?</em> → not <em>Friday</em> alone, but <em>The Garden Club is open on Friday at 3 p.m.</em></li><li><strong>Guessing from context</strong>: <em>members</em> appears in \"twenty-two members this year\" next to <em>pupils who come to the club</em>; so <em>members</em> = the people who belong to the club. <em>Seeds</em> appears with <em>plant</em> and <em>box</em>, so it means what you put in the soil to grow a plant.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> <em>True or false: The Sports Club is free.</em> — The text says \"It is free.\" → <strong>True</strong>. Always quote the sentence that proves the answer; this is the habit tested in the <em>devoir de synthèse</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> <em>How many pupils are in the Garden Club?</em> — Scan for a number near the word <em>Garden</em>: \"only twelve pupils\". Answer: <em>There are twelve pupils in the Garden Club.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Transfer to a table: ``<code> Club        | Day(s)            | Time            | Teacher    | Members Sports      | Monday, Wednesday | 3 p.m. - 5 p.m. | Mr Rachid  | 30 English     | Wednesday         | 3 p.m. - 4.30   | Mrs Nadia  | 22 Garden      | Friday            | 3 p.m.          | -          | 12 </code>``</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> The text mirrors real Moroccan school life: clubs (<em>نوادي</em>) for sport, environment and languages, the importance of water in a semi-arid climate, and re-using cans instead of plastic. Learners can compare with the clubs of their own school.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> A asks: <em>Which club do you like?</em> B answers: <em>I like the English Club because I read stories.</em> Each pair then writes one sentence for the class report.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>club</td><td>/klʌb/</td><td>نادٍ</td></tr><tr><td>member</td><td>/ˈmembə/</td><td>عضو</td></tr><tr><td>free (no money)</td><td>/friː/</td><td>مجاني</td></tr><tr><td>hall</td><td>/hɔːl/</td><td>قاعة</td></tr><tr><td>yard</td><td>/jɑːd/</td><td>ساحة</td></tr><tr><td>seed</td><td>/siːd/</td><td>بذرة</td></tr><tr><td>plant (verb)</td><td>/plɑːnt/</td><td>يغرس</td></tr><tr><td>water (verb)</td><td>/ˈwɔːtə/</td><td>يسقي</td></tr><tr><td>meet</td><td>/miːt/</td><td>يلتقي</td></tr><tr><td>newspaper</td><td>/ˈnjuːzpeɪpə/</td><td>جريدة</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Reading skills words</strong>: <em>title, sub-title, paragraph, skim, scan, true / false, justify</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Reading every word.</strong> For an information text, scanning is faster and enough; reading word by word wastes the exam time.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>One-word answers.</strong> ✗ <em>Friday.</em> → ✓ <em>The Garden Club is open on Friday.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Answering from personal knowledge</strong> instead of the text. The answer must be in the text; justify with a quotation.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Copying a whole paragraph</strong> as an answer. Copy only the useful sentence.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Read only the title and sub-titles. What is the text about? Choose: a. a sport match b. after-school clubs c. a school timetable.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>The Sports Club is open on Friday. 2. The English Club is in room 12. 3. The Garden Club has twelve pupils. 4. The Sports Club costs 50 dirhams.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>Sports Club 2. English Club — a. Mrs Nadia b. Mr Rachid</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>How many members: 1. in the Sports Club? 2. in the English Club? 3. in the Garden Club?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>Which club plants flowers? 2. Which club writes a newspaper? 3. Which club uses the big hall in winter?</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>When is the English Club open? 2. Where do the pupils of the Sports Club play? 3. What do the members of the Garden Club plant?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Copy the sentence from the text that proves: 1. The Sports Club is free. 2. The Garden Club is small.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>of <em>members</em> and <em>seeds</em> from the context, and write your own definition in English (one short sentence each).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>``<code> Club | Day(s) | Time | Members </code>``</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<ol><li>The English Club is open on Monday. 2. Mr Rachid is the teacher of the Garden Club. 3. The pupils water the garden at midday.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write the question for each answer. 1. ____ ? — In room 12. 2. ____ ? — Twenty-two. 3. ____ ? — On Friday.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Which club do you want to join? Why?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>for a new club in your school (name, day, time, activities).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Write two sentences comparing the Garden Club and the Sports Club (number of pupils, day).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6) — True or false + justification.</strong> 1. The English Club has twenty-two members. 2. The Sports Club plays in the rain. 3. The Garden Club uses plastic bottles. (1 pt for T/F, 1 pt for the proof.)</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/8) — Answer in complete sentences (2 pts each).</strong> 1. Where is the English Club? 2. When does the Sports Club meet? 3. Who is the teacher of the Sports Club? 4. Why do the pupils water the garden in the evening?</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6) — Writing.</strong> Write a poster of 40–50 words for a club in your school. Barème: name and day/time (2 pts), two activities (2 pts), correct sentences and punctuation (2 pts).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. T — \"The club has twenty-two members this year.\" 2. F — \"We don't play in the rain.\" 3. F — \"They don't use plastic bottles.\" B. 1. The English Club is in room 12. 2. The Sports Club meets on Monday and Wednesday from 3 p.m. to 5 p.m. 3. Mr Rachid is the teacher of the Sports Club. 4. Because water is important in the region, so they water the garden in the evening, not at midday. C. Sample (46 words): <em>THE READING CLUB. Our club is in the library. It is open on Thursday from 3 p.m. to 4 p.m. We read stories about Morocco and we write short reviews. Mrs Salma is the teacher. The club is free. All pupils are welcome!</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en30","domaine":"anglais","unite":30,"theme":"Unit 6 — Let's play after school!","tag":"EN30","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 6 - Writing (verbs, writing about what I do after school)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Recognise</strong> the verb in a sentence and underline it.","<strong>Choose</strong> the correct verb for each activity phrase.","<strong>Order</strong> three to five sentences into a short paragraph with time linkers.","<strong>Apply</strong> the rules of capital letters, full stops and <em>and</em>.","<strong>Write</strong> a paragraph of 45–60 words about his or her own afternoon.","<strong>Self-assess</strong> the paragraph with a simple rubric."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The English Club of the school (see EN29) prepares its little newspaper. Each pupil must give a paragraph entitled <em>\"My afternoon\"</em>. Salma writes: <em>\"after school i go home i help my mother i do my homework\"</em>. Everything is true, but nothing is readable: no capital letters, no full stops, no linkers. This lesson turns a list of ideas into a <strong>paragraph</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes Salma's line on the board and asks the class to repair it. Learners find, one after the other, the four repairs:</p>\n<ol><li>a <strong>capital letter</strong> at the beginning of each sentence and for <em>I</em>;</li><li>a <strong>full stop</strong> at the end of each sentence;</li><li>a <strong>verb</strong> in every sentence;</li><li><strong>linkers</strong> (<em>then</em>, <em>and</em>, <em>after that</em>) so that the sentences follow each other.</li></ol>\n<p>Result: <em>After school, I go home. I help my mother, and then I do my homework.</em></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — Every English sentence needs a verb</strong> A sentence without a verb is not a sentence: ✗ <em>I football after school.</em> → ✓ <em>I <strong>play</strong> football after school.</em> Underline the verb before you write the full stop: it is the safety check.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — Capital letters and full stops</strong> A capital letter: at the beginning of a sentence, for the pronoun <strong>I</strong> (always), for names of people, towns, countries, languages, days and months (<em>Salma, Rabat, Morocco, English, Monday, June</em>). A full stop <strong>.</strong> at the end of every statement; a question mark <strong>?</strong> at the end of every question.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — Linkers for a paragraph</strong> <strong>and</strong> (two activities): <em>I do my homework <strong>and</strong> I watch TV.</em> <strong>then / after that</strong> (order): <em>First I go home. <strong>Then</strong> I do my homework.</em> <strong>but</strong> (contrast): <em>I play football, <strong>but</strong> I don't play tennis.</em> <strong>because</strong> (reason): <em>I go to the library <strong>because</strong> I have a test.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — The shape of an A1 paragraph</strong> 1 sentence to open (where you go after school) → 2 or 3 sentences of activities with time words → 1 sentence to close (what you like best).</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Brainstorm</strong>: write four activity phrases in your notebook (from EN26).</li><li><strong>Order</strong> them with time words: <em>after school / first / then / in the evening</em>.</li><li><strong>Write</strong> one sentence per activity; check the verb.</li><li><strong>Join</strong> two sentences with <em>and</em> or <em>but</em>.</li><li><strong>Check</strong> the four Cs: <strong>C</strong>apital letters, <strong>C</strong>omplete sentences, <strong>C</strong>orrect verbs, <strong>C</strong>losing sentence.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> From notes to paragraph. Notes: <em>club — homework — TV — Friday grandparents</em>. Paragraph: <em>After school, I go to the youth centre. Then I do my homework at home. In the evening, I watch TV with my brother. On Friday, I visit my grandparents. I like Friday afternoon best.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Correcting a learner's text. ✗ <em>i am play basketball then i am go home i dont watch tv</em> ✓ <em>I play basketball. Then I go home. I don't watch TV.</em> Three corrections: remove <em>am</em> before the action verb, add capital letters and full stops, write the apostrophe in <em>don't</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Using <em>but</em> and <em>because</em>: <em>I play football every day, but I don't play on Sunday. On Sunday I stay at home because I help my father in the shop.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Encourage real content: the <em>دار الشباب</em>, the neighbourhood football pitch, the family shop, Friday couscous, the medina, homework by the light of the courtyard. Prices in dirhams (<em>My ball costs 70 dirhams</em>), towns (<em>Nador, Safi, Errachidia</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Writing rubric (used for every writing task of the unit) — /10</strong></p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Task and length</td><td>45–60 words, on topic, at least 4 activities</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Vocabulary</td><td>correct activity phrases (verb + noun)</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Grammar</td><td>present simple, <em>don't</em>, no double verb</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Linkers and organisation</td><td><em>and, then, but, because</em>; logical order</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanics</td><td>capital letters, full stops, spelling, handwriting</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Speaking before writing (pair work).</strong> Learner A tells his afternoon orally in four sentences; learner B counts the verbs on his fingers and says the number. Speaking first makes the writing much faster and reduces the number of missing verbs.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule / example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Verb</td><td>every sentence has one: <em>I <strong>help</strong> my mother.</em></td></tr><tr><td>Capital <em>I</em></td><td>always: <em>my friends and <strong>I</strong></em></td></tr><tr><td>Days / languages</td><td><em>Monday, Friday, English, Arabic</em> → capital letter</td></tr><tr><td>Full stop / question mark</td><td><em>I play football. / Do you play football?</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>and</em></td><td>joins two activities</td></tr><tr><td><em>then / after that</em></td><td>shows the order</td></tr><tr><td><em>but</em></td><td>shows contrast</td></tr><tr><td><em>because</em></td><td>gives a reason</td></tr><tr><td>Apostrophe</td><td><em>don't</em> = do not; <em>I'm</em> = I am</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ **No capital letter for <em>I</em>.** ✗ <em>i play football</em> → ✓ <em>I play football.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Sentences with no full stop</strong> (one long line). Cut after each verb group.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Double verb.</strong> ✗ <em>I am go home</em> → ✓ <em>I go home.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Missing apostrophe.</strong> ✗ <em>dont / I dont</em> → ✓ <em>don't</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>in each sentence. 1. I play basketball after school. 2. We don't watch TV in the morning. 3. My friends visit their grandparents. 4. I read a book in my room.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>i live in agadir. 2. on friday i visit my uncle. 3. my friend and i speak english and arabic.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>I go to the club after school 2. Do you play football 3. I don't watch TV in the afternoon</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>(<em>and / but / then / because</em>). 1. I play football ____ I don't play tennis. 2. First I go home. ____ I do my homework. 3. I do my homework ____ I watch TV. 4. I go to the library ____ I have a test.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>I ____ my homework at 6 o'clock. 2. We ____ to the club on Wednesday. 3. They ____ TV in the evening.</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>(5 mistakes): <em>after school i am go home. i help my mother then i dont watch tv</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>to make a paragraph. a. Then I do my homework. b. After school, I go home. c. I like the evening best. d. In the evening, I watch TV with my family.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>with <em>and</em>, <em>but</em> or <em>because</em>. 1. I play football. I don't play basketball. 2. I do my homework. I read a book. 3. I go to bed early. I am tired.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>about your afternoon (four activity phrases only, no full sentences).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>of 45–60 words.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Use the rubric and give yourself a mark out of 10 for Exercice 10; write one sentence about what you must improve.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>(45–60 words), using <em>he</em> or <em>she</em> (the teacher accepts the <em>-s</em> form: <em>he plays</em>).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>Write a short article of 50–60 words for the school newspaper: \"What pupils of 1AC do after school\". Use <em>some pupils…</em>, <em>other pupils…</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Exchange notebooks with your partner and correct three mistakes in his or her paragraph with the four-C check.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Rewrite with capital letters, full stops and apostrophes (2 pts each): 1. i play football on saturday 2. my friend and i go to the club 3. i dont watch tv in the morning</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/4).</strong> Join with <em>and</em>, <em>but</em>, <em>then</em> or <em>because</em> (1 pt each): 1. I do my homework. I watch TV. 2. I play football. I don't play tennis. 3. First I go home. I help my mother. 4. I go to the library. I have an English test.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/10) — Writing.</strong> Write a paragraph of 45–60 words: <em>\"What I do after school\"</em>. Marked with the rubric (task and length 2, vocabulary 2, grammar 2, linkers 2, mechanics 2).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. <em>I play football on Saturday.</em> 2. <em>My friend and I go to the club.</em> 3. <em>I don't watch TV in the morning.</em> B. 1. and 2. but 3. Then 4. because. C. Sample (55 words): <em>After school, I walk home with my cousin. First I eat lunch and I help my mother. Then I do my homework in my room, because I have a test on Friday. At five o'clock, I play football in the street with my friends. In the evening, I read a book. I like reading best.</em></p>\n<p>## Unit 7 — Let's buy presents!</p>\n<p>Unit 7 moves from <em>I / we / they</em> to the <strong>third person singular</strong> of the present simple: <em>he likes</em>, <em>she doesn't like</em>, <em>What does he like?</em> The theme — special days and presents — is built around Moroccan celebrations (Eid, Aid Al Mawlid, birthdays, the school prize day) and around real prices in dirhams.</p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en31","domaine":"anglais","unite":31,"theme":"Unit 7 — Let's buy presents!","tag":"EN31","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 7 - Words (special days)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Name</strong> the main special days and celebration words in English.","<strong>Name</strong> at least ten presents with their prices in dirhams.","<strong>Use</strong> the expressions <em>Happy birthday! / Congratulations! / Best wishes!</em>","<strong>Ask and answer</strong> in pairs: <em>What do you buy for your mother? — I buy her a scarf.</em>","<strong>Classify</strong> words into <em>days / presents / actions</em>.","<strong>Write</strong> three sentences about a special day in his or her family."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> In a shop in the medina of Fez, three pupils are choosing presents. It is the last week before Eid, and their little sister has a birthday on the same week. To buy, to ask the price, to write a card, they need the vocabulary of <strong>special days and presents</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher draws a simple calendar of the school year on the board and asks: <em>Which days are special for you?</em> Learners answer in Darija or French; the teacher gives the English words: <em>birthday, Eid, national holiday, the first day of school, prize day, Mother's Day, wedding</em>. Then he adds three columns: DAY / PRESENT / ACTION.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — a special day</strong> A <strong>special day</strong> is a day that a family or a country celebrates: a <em>birthday</em>, a religious feast (<em>Eid</em>), a <em>national holiday</em>, a <em>wedding</em>, a <em>prize day</em> at school. The verb is <strong>to celebrate</strong>: <em>We celebrate Eid with our family.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The three word families of the unit</strong> <strong>Days</strong>: birthday, Eid, holiday, wedding, prize day, Mother's Day, New Year. <strong>Presents</strong>: a book, a scarf, a watch, a game, a ball, flowers, sweets, a T-shirt, a bag, a pen, a card, dates, a doll. <strong>Actions</strong>: buy, give, get / receive, wrap, celebrate, invite, thank, make a card, sing, eat.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — the \"present sentence\".</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Choose the person: <em>for my mother / for my little brother / for my friend</em>.</li><li>Choose the present with its article: <strong>a</strong> scarf, <strong>a</strong> watch, <strong>an</strong> orange cake, — flowers (plural, no article), — sweets.</li><li>Choose the verb: <em>buy</em> (with money), <em>give</em> (to a person), <em>make</em> (with your hands).</li><li>Add the price in dirhams if you want: <em>A scarf costs sixty dirhams.</em></li></ol>\n<p>Result: <em>I buy a scarf for my mother. It costs sixty dirhams.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Wrong article: ✗ <em>I buy a flowers.</em> → ✓ <em>I buy flowers</em> (plural, no <em>a</em>) or ✓ <em>I buy a flower</em> (one only).</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Prices dialogue in the shop: — <em>How much is this watch?</em> — <em>It's ninety dirhams.</em> — <em>And how much are these sweets?</em> — <em>They're twelve dirhams.</em> Note <em>is</em> for one object, <em>are</em> for a plural; <em>How much…?</em> asks the price.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> A card for Eid: <em>Happy Eid, Grandma! Best wishes from Salma.</em> Three parts: the greeting, the name of the person, the signature.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Real celebrations: <strong>Eid Al Fitr</strong> and <strong>Eid Al Adha</strong>, <strong>Aid Al Mawlid</strong>, <strong>Achoura</strong> (children get toys and sweets), the <strong>national holidays</strong> (Throne Day on 30 July, Independence Manifesto Day on 11 January, Green March on 6 November), a <em>wedding</em> in the family, the <strong>prize day</strong> (حفل نهاية السنة) in the school. Typical presents: dates, sweets, a <em>djellaba</em>, a scarf, a football, a book; typical prices: <em>a ball: 70 dirhams, a book: 45 dirhams, a scarf: 60 dirhams, sweets: 15 dirhams</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> Shop role-play. A is the shopkeeper, B is the customer: <em>Hello. How much is this bag? — It's eighty dirhams. — Here you are. Thank you.</em> Then swap roles with another present.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>birthday</td><td>/ˈbɜːθdeɪ/</td><td>عيد ميلاد</td></tr><tr><td>present / gift</td><td>/ˈprezənt/</td><td>هدية</td></tr><tr><td>holiday</td><td>/ˈhɒlədeɪ/</td><td>عطلة / عيد وطني</td></tr><tr><td>wedding</td><td>/ˈwedɪŋ/</td><td>حفل زفاف</td></tr><tr><td>celebrate</td><td>/ˈselɪbreɪt/</td><td>يحتفل</td></tr><tr><td>buy</td><td>/baɪ/</td><td>يشتري</td></tr><tr><td>give</td><td>/ɡɪv/</td><td>يعطي</td></tr><tr><td>card</td><td>/kɑːd/</td><td>بطاقة</td></tr><tr><td>scarf</td><td>/skɑːf/</td><td>وشاح</td></tr><tr><td>watch</td><td>/wɒtʃ/</td><td>ساعة يد</td></tr><tr><td>sweets</td><td>/swiːts/</td><td>حلويات</td></tr><tr><td>dates</td><td>/deɪts/</td><td>تمر</td></tr><tr><td>flowers</td><td>/ˈflaʊəz/</td><td>أزهار</td></tr><tr><td>invite</td><td>/ɪnˈvaɪt/</td><td>يدعو</td></tr><tr><td>thank</td><td>/θæŋk/</td><td>يشكر</td></tr><tr><td>How much…?</td><td>/haʊ mʌtʃ/</td><td>بكم؟</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Fixed phrases</strong>: <em>Happy birthday! Happy Eid! Congratulations! Best wishes! Thank you very much! Here you are.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Article before a plural.</strong> ✗ <em>a flowers, a sweets</em> → ✓ <em>flowers, sweets</em>.</li><li>⚠️ **<em>buy</em> / <em>give</em> confusion.** ✗ <em>I give a book in the shop.</em> → ✓ <em>I <strong>buy</strong> a book in the shop and I <strong>give</strong> it to my sister.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Price question.</strong> ✗ <em>How much cost this bag?</em> → ✓ <em>How much <strong>is</strong> this bag?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Wrong greeting.</strong> ✗ <em>Happy congratulations</em> → ✓ <em>Happy birthday! / Congratulations!</em></li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>birthday 2. wedding 3. card 4. sweets 5. celebrate — a. حلويات b. عيد ميلاد c. يحتفل d. بطاقة e. حفل زفاف</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Complete with <em>a</em>, <em>an</em> or nothing.** 1. ____ scarf 2. ____ flowers 3. ____ orange 4. ____ sweets 5. ____ watch</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>**Exercice 3 — <em>buy</em>, <em>give</em> or <em>make</em>?** 1. I ____ a card with paper and colours. 2. I ____ a ball in the shop. 3. I ____ the present to my sister.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Write the sentence: 1. ball / 70 dh 2. book / 45 dh 3. scarf / 60 dh</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>Your friend is 13 today. 2. Your cousin passes his exam. 3. It is the first day of Eid.</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>into DAYS / PRESENTS / ACTIONS: <em>wedding, watch, invite, dates, birthday, wrap</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>A: Hello. ____ much is this bag? B: ____ eighty dirhams. A: Here ____ are. B: ____ you.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<ol><li>scarf / watch / Eid 2. buy / give / birthday 3. sweets / dates / card</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>from the IPA: 1. /ˈbɜːθdeɪ/ 2. /kɑːd/ 3. /swiːts/ 4. /ɪnˈvaɪt/</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<ol><li>When is your birthday? 2. What present do you want? 3. Who do you invite?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<ol><li>I buy a flowers for my mother. 2. How much cost this watch? 3. I give a ball in the shop.</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>(3 lines) for your grandmother for Eid.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>You have 200 dirhams. Choose three presents for three people and write three sentences with the prices; then write the total.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>\"A special day in my family\": which day, who comes, what you eat, what presents you give.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Complete with <em>a</em>, <em>an</em> or nothing, then translate the item into Arabic (0.5 + 0.5 each): 1. ____ watch 2. ____ dates 3. ____ orange cake 4. ____ card 5. ____ flowers 6. ____ scarf.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Complete the shop dialogue (1 pt per gap). A: Hello. How ____ is this ball? B: ____ seventy dirhams. A: And how much ____ these sweets? B: ____ fifteen dirhams. A: Here you ____ . B: ____ you very much.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/8) — Writing.</strong> Write a paragraph of 45–55 words about a special day in your family. Barème: special day named and dated (2), two presents with prices in dirhams (2), correct verbs <em>buy/give/celebrate</em> (2), mechanics and length (2).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. a (ساعة يد) 2. — (تمر) 3. an (كعكة بالبرتقال) 4. a (بطاقة) 5. — (أزهار) 6. a (وشاح). B. much — It's — are — They're — are — Thank. C. Sample (52 words): <em>My little sister's birthday is on 5 May. We celebrate it at home in Kenitra. My mother makes a big cake and my aunts come with their children. I buy a doll for my sister. It costs eighty dirhams. My father gives her a book. We sing and we eat sweets.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en32","domaine":"anglais","unite":32,"theme":"Unit 7 — Let's buy presents!","tag":"EN32","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 7 - Grammar (what does he like? he likes / doesn't like ...)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Add</strong> the third-person <em>-s</em> / <em>-es</em> / <em>-ies</em> correctly to verbs.","<strong>Form</strong> negatives with <strong>doesn't</strong> + base verb.","<strong>Ask</strong> questions with <strong>Does he …?</strong> and <strong>What does she like?</strong> and give short answers.","<strong>Contrast</strong> <em>do / does</em> according to the subject.","<strong>Describe</strong> the tastes of a third person: <em>My father likes tea. He doesn't like coffee.</em>","<strong>Write</strong> five correct sentences about the likes and dislikes of a family member."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Salma wants to buy presents. She knows her own tastes, but she must talk about <strong>other people</strong>: <em>My mother likes tea. My brother doesn't like books. What does my father like?</em> In English, when the subject is <em>he</em>, <em>she</em> or <em>it</em>, the verb changes. This little <em>-s</em> is the most tested point of the whole year.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes two columns on the board: <em>I like tea. / You play football. / We watch TV.</em> — <em>He likes tea. / She plays football. / It watches the door.</em> Learners compare and discover the rule alone: with <strong>he / she / it</strong>, the verb takes an <strong>-s</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — Affirmative with he / she / it</strong> <strong>Subject + verb + -s</strong>: <em>He like<strong>s</strong> football. She play<strong>s</strong> the guitar. My sister watch<strong>es</strong> TV.</em> Spelling: add <strong>-es</strong> after <em>o, s, sh, ch, x</em> (<em>go → goes, watch → watches, wash → washes</em>); change <em>-y</em> to <strong>-ies</strong> after a consonant (<em>study → studies, fly → flies</em>), but keep <em>-ys</em> after a vowel (<em>play → plays</em>). Irregular: <em>have → <strong>has</strong></em>, <em>do → <strong>does</strong></em>, <em>go → <strong>goes</strong></em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — Negative with he / she / it</strong> <strong>Subject + doesn't + base verb</strong>: <em>He <strong>doesn't like</strong> coffee. She <strong>doesn't have</strong> a bike.</em> ⚠️ The <em>-s</em> moves into <em>doesn't</em>, so the main verb loses it: ✗ <em>He doesn't likes.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — Questions with he / she / it</strong> <strong>Does + subject + base verb …?</strong> <em>Does he like tea?</em> → <em>Yes, he does. / No, he doesn't.</em> <strong>Wh- + does + subject + base verb …?</strong> <em>What does he like? Where does she live? When does he play football?</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — do or does?</strong> <em>I, you, we, they</em> → <strong>do / don't</strong>. <em>He, she, it</em> (and one person's name: <em>Salma, my father</em>) → <strong>does / doesn't</strong>.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — the three-question test.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Who is the subject? If it is <em>he/she/it</em> or one single person or thing → the <em>-s</em> family.</li><li>Is the sentence affirmative, negative or a question?</li></ol>\n<ul><li>affirmative → put the <em>-s</em> <strong>on the verb</strong>;</li><li>negative → put the <em>-s</em> <strong>in doesn't</strong>, verb naked;</li><li>question → put the <em>-s</em> <strong>in Does</strong>, verb naked.</li></ul>\n<ol><li>Check the spelling of the <em>-s</em> form (<em>-s / -es / -ies</em>).</li></ol>\n<p>``<code> AFFIRMATIVE        NEGATIVE                QUESTION he      He likes tea.      He doesn't like tea.    Does he like tea? they    They like tea.     They don't like tea.    Do they like tea? </code>``</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Make <em>She (study) English every day</em> correct. Subject <em>she</em> → <em>-s</em> family; affirmative; <em>study</em> ends in consonant + <em>y</em> → <em>studies</em>. → <em>She studies English every day.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Make <em>My brother likes sweets</em> negative. Subject = one person → <em>doesn't</em>; the verb goes back to the base form. → <em>My brother doesn't like sweets.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Build a question for the answer <em>He likes dates.</em> The information asked is the thing he likes → question word <em>What</em>; subject <em>he</em> → <em>does</em>; base verb <em>like</em>. → <em>What does he like?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Practice sentences with real content: <em>My grandmother likes mint tea. My father works in Casablanca and goes to Rabat on Monday. My sister doesn't like fish. Amine watches the football match on Saturday.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Present detective\": A thinks of a family member; B asks five questions (<em>Does he like books? Does she like sweets? What does he like?</em>) and then proposes a present: <em>Buy him a book!</em> Roles are swapped.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>he / she / it form</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>like</td><td>likes</td><td>+ s</td></tr><tr><td>play</td><td>plays</td><td>vowel + y → + s</td></tr><tr><td>watch</td><td>watches</td><td>ch → + es</td></tr><tr><td>go</td><td>goes</td><td>o → + es</td></tr><tr><td>wash</td><td>washes</td><td>sh → + es</td></tr><tr><td>study</td><td>studies</td><td>consonant + y → ies</td></tr><tr><td>fly</td><td>flies</td><td>consonant + y → ies</td></tr><tr><td>have</td><td>has</td><td>irregular</td></tr><tr><td>do</td><td>does</td><td>irregular</td></tr><tr><td>be</td><td>is</td><td>irregular</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Question words</strong>: <em>what, where, when, who, how, how much, how many, why</em>. <strong>Pronunciation of the final -s</strong>: /s/ after /p, t, k, f/ (<em>likes</em> /laɪks/), /z/ after voiced sounds (<em>plays</em> /pleɪz/), /ɪz/ after /s, z, ʃ, tʃ, dʒ/ (<em>watches</em> /ˈwɒtʃɪz/).</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Double marking.</strong> ✗ <em>He doesn't likes tea.</em> / ✗ <em>Does she likes tea?</em> → ✓ <em>He doesn't like tea. / Does she like tea?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Forgotten -s.</strong> ✗ <em>My father work in Rabat.</em> → ✓ <em>My father work<strong>s</strong> in Rabat.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>do instead of does.</strong> ✗ <em>Do he like football?</em> → ✓ <em>Does he like football?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Spelling.</strong> ✗ <em>studys, gos, watchs</em> → ✓ <em>studies, goes, watches</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>**Exercice 1 — Write the <em>he</em> form.** 1. like 2. play 3. watch 4. go 5. study 6. have</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>She (like / likes) sweets. 2. My father (work / works) in Fez. 3. They (watch / watches) TV. 4. He (don't / doesn't) like coffee. 5. (Do / Does) your sister play volleyball?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>**Exercice 3 — Complete with <em>do</em> or <em>does</em>.** 1. ____ he like tea? 2. ____ they live in Oujda? 3. What ____ she want? 4. ____ you help your mother?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>He likes fish. 2. She has a bike. 3. My brother watches TV.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>Does he like dates? (Yes) 2. Does she play the guitar? (No) 3. Do they celebrate Eid? (Yes)</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>He doesn't likes tea. 2. Does she likes books? 3. My mother go to the market. 4. Do he play football?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>He likes football. (Does…?) 2. She lives in Tangier. (Where…?) 3. He wants a watch. (What…?)</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p><em>My uncle ____ (live) in Safi. He ____ (work) in a shop. He ____ (not like) television, but he ____ (read) a lot. On Friday, he ____ (visit) my grandmother.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Sort into /s/, /z/, /ɪz/: <em>likes, plays, watches, works, goes, washes</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>about one member of your family: two affirmative, two negative, one question.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Rewrite the paragraph changing <em>I</em> to <em>my sister</em>: <em>I get up at seven. I go to school. I study English. I don't watch TV. I help my mother.</em></p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write five questions to discover what a person likes, then write the present you choose and why (<em>She likes flowers, so I buy her a plant.</em>)</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>\"My best friend and his/her presents\": what he/she likes, doesn't like, what you buy.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Find and correct the five mistakes: <em>My father like tea. He don't like coffee. Does he likes sweets? My mother watchs TV and she studys English.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Put the verb in the correct form (1 pt each): 1. He ____ (like) dates. 2. She ____ (go) to the market on Sunday. 3. My brother ____ (not / like) fish. 4. ____ your father ____ (work) in Rabat? 5. She ____ (study) English and French. 6. My cousin ____ (have) a new bike. 7. They ____ (not / celebrate) New Year. 8. What ____ she ____ (want) for her birthday?</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Write the question for each answer (2 pts each): 1. ____ ? — He likes football. 2. ____ ? — She lives in Nador. 3. ____ ? — No, he doesn't.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6) — Writing.</strong> Write five sentences (50 words maximum) about what a person of your family likes and doesn't like, and the present you buy for that person. Barème: three correct <em>-s</em> forms (3), one correct negative with <em>doesn't</em> (1), present + price (1), mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. likes 2. goes 3. doesn't like 4. Does … work 5. studies 6. has 7. don't celebrate 8. does … want. B. 1. What does he like? 2. Where does she live? 3. Does he like … ? (any <em>Does he/she …?</em> question). C. Sample (48 words): <em>My mother likes mint tea and she likes flowers. She works at home and she goes to the market on Sunday. She doesn't like coffee. For Mother's Day, I buy her a red scarf. It costs sixty dirhams. She says thank you and she is very happy.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en33","domaine":"anglais","unite":33,"theme":"Unit 7 — Let's buy presents!","tag":"EN33","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 7 - Phonics (consonant blends: cl gl sl)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Identify</strong> /kl/, /ɡl/, /sl/ at the beginning of a word.","<strong>Pronounce</strong> the three blends without an added vowel.","<strong>Read</strong> fourteen words containing these blends.","<strong>Spell</strong> them correctly in a dictation.","<strong>Discriminate</strong> the pairs /kl/–/ɡl/ (voiceless / voiced) and /sl/–/ʃl/."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The classroom itself is full of these sounds: the <strong>cl</strong>ock on the wall, the <strong>gl</strong>ass of the window, the pupils who <strong>cl</strong>ean the board, the pen that <strong>sl</strong>ides on the paper. Learners already met <em>fl, pl, bl</em> in Unit 6; the new blends keep the same second letter <strong>l</strong>, so the same method works.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> Three word groups on the board: <em>clock, class, clean, clever</em> — <em>glass, glue, globe, gloves</em> — <em>sleep, slow, slide, slipper</em>. Learners answer two questions: <em>Which letter is always second?</em> (<strong>l</strong>) <em>Which sounds change?</em> (the first one: /k/, /ɡ/, /s/).</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition</strong> <strong>cl</strong> = /k/ + /l/ → <em>clock</em> /klɒk/ · <strong>gl</strong> = /ɡ/ + /l/ → <em>glass</em> /ɡlɑːs/ · <strong>sl</strong> = /s/ + /l/ → <em>sleep</em> /sliːp/. /k/ and /ɡ/ are made in the same place at the back of the mouth: /k/ <strong>without</strong> voice, /ɡ/ <strong>with</strong> voice. Put a hand on the throat: <em>class</em> (no vibration) / <em>glass</em> (vibration).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Nine key words to master</strong> <em>clock, class, clean, clever, close</em> — <em>glass, glue, gloves, globe</em> — <em>sleep, slow, slide, slipper, slim</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Start from /l/ (tongue tip on the ridge behind the top teeth).</li><li>Put /k/, /ɡ/ or /s/ in front, with <strong>no vowel</strong> between: /k/+/l/ → /kl/.</li><li>Add the vowel and the end: /kl/ + /ɒk/ = <em>clock</em>.</li><li>Feel the voice: hand on the throat for the /ɡl/ words.</li><li>Say a minimal pair to check: <em>class</em> / <em>glass</em>; <em>clue</em> / <em>glue</em>.</li></ol>\n<p>``<code> cl /kl/   ->  clock  class  clean  clever  close gl /gl/   ->  glass  glue   gloves globe sl /sl/   ->  sleep  slow   slide  slipper </code>``</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> <em>gloves</em> /ɡlʌvz/. Cut: /ɡl/ + /ʌ/ + /vz/. The final <em>-es</em> is pronounced /z/ because <em>glove</em> ends with a voiced /v/.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Discriminating <em>class</em> /klɑːs/ and <em>glass</em> /ɡlɑːs/: only the first sound differs. The teacher says one of them; learners raise their left hand for <em>class</em> and their right hand for <em>glass</em> — a technique that works with 45 pupils at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> <em>sleep</em> /sliːp/ vs <em>slip</em> /slɪp/: the vowel changes (long /iː/ vs short /ɪ/), not the blend. <em>I sleep at ten o'clock</em> / <em>Be careful, you slip on the floor.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Sentences using school reality: <em>Clean the blackboard, please. The clock says three o'clock. Our class has forty-five pupils. I close the window. My grandmother wears gloves in winter in Ifrane, because it is cold and it snows.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Sound bingo\": each learner draws a 3×3 grid and writes nine words from the list; the teacher reads words at random; the first pair with a line says <em>Bingo!</em> and must read its three words aloud correctly.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Blend</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>clock</td><td>/klɒk/</td><td>cl</td><td>ساعة حائط</td></tr><tr><td>class</td><td>/klɑːs/</td><td>cl</td><td>قسم</td></tr><tr><td>clean</td><td>/kliːn/</td><td>cl</td><td>ينظف / نظيف</td></tr><tr><td>clever</td><td>/ˈklevə/</td><td>cl</td><td>ذكي</td></tr><tr><td>close</td><td>/kləʊz/</td><td>cl</td><td>يغلق</td></tr><tr><td>glass</td><td>/ɡlɑːs/</td><td>gl</td><td>كأس / زجاج</td></tr><tr><td>glue</td><td>/ɡluː/</td><td>gl</td><td>لاصق</td></tr><tr><td>gloves</td><td>/ɡlʌvz/</td><td>gl</td><td>قفازات</td></tr><tr><td>globe</td><td>/ɡləʊb/</td><td>gl</td><td>كرة أرضية</td></tr><tr><td>sleep</td><td>/sliːp/</td><td>sl</td><td>ينام</td></tr><tr><td>slow</td><td>/sləʊ/</td><td>sl</td><td>بطيء</td></tr><tr><td>slide</td><td>/slaɪd/</td><td>sl</td><td>ينزلق</td></tr><tr><td>slipper</td><td>/ˈslɪpə/</td><td>sl</td><td>نعل / بابوش</td></tr><tr><td>slim</td><td>/slɪm/</td><td>sl</td><td>نحيف</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Vowel inside the blend.</strong> ✗ /kiˈlɒk/ <em>kilock</em> → ✓ /klɒk/ <em>clock</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>/kl/ – /ɡl/ confusion.</strong> ✗ <em>glass</em> for <em>class</em> → use the hand on the throat: /ɡ/ vibrates.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>/s/ before /l/ turned into /ʃ/.</strong> ✗ <em>shleep</em> → ✓ <em>sleep</em> /sliːp/.</li><li>⚠️ **Spelling <em>k</em> instead of <em>c</em>.** ✗ <em>klock, klass</em> → ✓ <em>clock, class</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>clock (cl/gl/sl) 2. glue (cl/gl/sl) 3. sleep (cl/gl/sl) 4. clean (cl/gl/sl) 5. gloves (cl/gl/sl)</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Complete with <em>cl</em>, <em>gl</em> or <em>sl</em>.** 1. ____ass (a room) 2. ____ass (for water) 3. ____eep 4. ____ock 5. ____obe 6. ____ipper</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>into three columns: <em>close, slow, glue, clever, slide, globe</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Put a hand on your throat and write the word you hear (teacher's dictation, 4 items).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>clock 2. glue 3. sleep</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>of eight words: <em>clock, class, clean, glass, glue, gloves, sleep, slow</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>Look at the ____: it is three o'clock. 2. Please ____ the blackboard. 3. I wear ____ in winter. 4. I ____ at ten o'clock. 5. Give me the ____ for my card.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<ol><li>clock / clean / glass 2. glue / globe / slide 3. sleep / slow / close</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>from the IPA: 1. /ˈklevə/ 2. /ɡlʌvz/ 3. /slaɪd/ 4. /kləʊz/</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<ol><li>klock 2. gelas 3. sileep 4. kilean</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p><em>Clever classes clean the glass with slow gloves.</em> Copy it and underline the blends.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Find three more <em>cl / gl / sl</em> words in your book and transcribe them.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>using <em>clean, close, look at the clock</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Write one sentence for each word of the pairs <em>class / glass</em> and <em>sleep / slip</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Complete with <em>cl</em>, <em>gl</em> or <em>sl</em> (1 pt each): 1. ____ock 2. ____ue 3. ____eep 4. ____ever 5. ____oves 6. ____ow 7. ____ean 8. ____obe.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Write the words (1.5 pt each): 1. /klɑːs/ 2. /ɡlɑːs/ 3. /ˈslɪpə/ 4. /kləʊz/.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6).</strong> Write three sentences, each with two words from the lesson (2 pts each).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. clock 2. glue 3. sleep 4. clever 5. gloves 6. slow 7. clean 8. globe. B. 1. class 2. glass 3. slipper 4. close. C. Sample: <em>Please clean the glass. My class has a big clock. I close the door and I sleep.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en34","domaine":"anglais","unite":34,"theme":"Unit 7 — Let's buy presents!","tag":"EN34","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 7 - Reading (instructions on making a card)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Recognise</strong> an instruction text (list of materials + numbered steps + imperative verbs).","<strong>Order</strong> the steps of a process correctly.","<strong>Understand</strong> and use sequence words (<em>first, then, next, after that, finally</em>).","<strong>Follow</strong> the instructions to make a real Eid or birthday card.","<strong>Write</strong> a short set of instructions of his or her own."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The English Club is preparing cards for Eid Al Fitr. On the wall, a poster explains how to make one with paper, scissors and glue. An instruction text is not read like a story: it is read <strong>while doing</strong>, step by step, with the eyes going back to the page after each action.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher shows a finished card and asks: <em>How do we make this?</em> Learners give ideas in Darija/French; the teacher writes only the English verbs on the board: <em>take, fold, cut, draw, write, colour, glue, give</em>. All are <strong>imperatives</strong>: the base form with no subject.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>How an instruction text works</strong></p>\n<ol><li>A <strong>title</strong>: <em>How to make an Eid card</em>.</li><li>A list of <strong>materials</strong>: <em>You need …</em></li><li><strong>Numbered steps</strong> with <strong>imperative verbs</strong>: <em>Fold the paper. Cut a star.</em></li><li><strong>Sequence words</strong>: <em>First, Then, Next, After that, Finally</em>.</li></ol></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The imperative</strong> Base verb, no subject: <em>Take a sheet of paper.</em> Negative: <em>Don't use too much glue.</em> Polite: <em>Please cut carefully.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Original reading text (written for this document).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>HOW TO MAKE AN EID CARD — English Club, Ibn Battouta Middle School</strong> <strong>You need</strong>: one sheet of blue paper (A4), a pencil, scissors, glue, a green pen, small pieces of white paper and a ruler. <strong>Steps</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>First</strong>, put the blue paper on your table. Take the ruler and the pencil.</li><li><strong>Then</strong> fold the paper in the middle. Now you have a card with two pages.</li><li><strong>Next</strong>, draw a big star and a small moon on the white paper. Cut them with the scissors. Be careful with the scissors!</li><li><strong>After that</strong>, glue the star and the moon on the front page of the card. Don't use too much glue: the paper is thin.</li><li><strong>Finally</strong>, open the card and write your message with the green pen: <em>\"Happy Eid! Best wishes from your friend.\"</em> Write your name at the bottom.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Tip</strong>: You can use old paper from your copybook. Don't buy expensive paper — a beautiful card costs zero dirhams!</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Comprehension work step by step.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Skim</strong>: what is the text about? → making a card, in five steps.</li><li><strong>Find the materials</strong>: they are always in a separate list; here there are seven items.</li><li><strong>Follow the order</strong>: the sequence words are the signposts. <em>First</em> = step 1, <em>Finally</em> = last step.</li><li><strong>Spot the negative instructions</strong>: <em>Don't use too much glue</em> — instructions often warn as well as tell.</li><li><strong>Check with a picture in your head</strong>: after step 2 the paper is folded; after step 4 the star is on the front page.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Question: <em>What do you write on the inside of the card?</em> — Scan for <em>open the card and write</em>: the answer is the message <em>\"Happy Eid! Best wishes from your friend.\"</em> and your name.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Ordering exercise: put <em>glue the star / fold the paper / write the message</em> in order → fold (2), glue (4), write (5). The sequence words in the text prove the order.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Rewriting an instruction as a full sentence: <em>Fold the paper in the middle</em> → <em>You fold the paper in the middle.</em> The imperative has no subject; the statement needs <em>you</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Cards for <strong>Eid Al Fitr</strong>, <strong>Eid Al Adha</strong>, <strong>Aid Al Mawlid</strong>, a birthday, or Mother's Day; the star and the crescent moon; re-using paper from an old copybook, which respects both the family budget and the environment. Learners can write the message in English and add a word in Arabic.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> A gives the five instructions orally, in the right order, using <em>First / Then / Next / After that / Finally</em>; B mimes the actions. Then they swap. Finally each pair makes one real card during the last ten minutes or at home.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>sheet of paper</td><td>/ʃiːt/</td><td>ورقة</td></tr><tr><td>fold</td><td>/fəʊld/</td><td>يثني / يطوي</td></tr><tr><td>cut</td><td>/kʌt/</td><td>يقص</td></tr><tr><td>scissors</td><td>/ˈsɪzəz/</td><td>مقص</td></tr><tr><td>glue</td><td>/ɡluː/</td><td>لاصق</td></tr><tr><td>draw</td><td>/drɔː/</td><td>يرسم</td></tr><tr><td>colour</td><td>/ˈkʌlə/</td><td>يلوّن</td></tr><tr><td>ruler</td><td>/ˈruːlə/</td><td>مسطرة</td></tr><tr><td>message</td><td>/ˈmesɪdʒ/</td><td>رسالة</td></tr><tr><td>star / moon</td><td>/stɑː/ /muːn/</td><td>نجمة / قمر</td></tr><tr><td>careful</td><td>/ˈkeəfl/</td><td>حذر</td></tr><tr><td>tip (advice)</td><td>/tɪp/</td><td>نصيحة</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Sequence words</strong>: <em>first, then, next, after that, finally</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Adding a subject to the imperative.</strong> ✗ <em>You fold the paper</em> (as an instruction) → ✓ <em>Fold the paper.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Wrong negative.</strong> ✗ <em>No use glue.</em> → ✓ <em>Don't use glue.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Ignoring the sequence words</strong> and mixing the steps.</li><li>⚠️ <em><strong>scissors</strong></em> is always plural: ✗ <em>a scissor</em> → ✓ <em>scissors / a pair of scissors</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>a. a recipe b. how to make a card c. a story about Eid</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Tick the things you need: <em>blue paper / a computer / scissors / glue / a football / a green pen / a ruler</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>You need a red pen. 2. You fold the paper in the middle. 3. You draw a star and a moon. 4. The card is expensive.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>a. Glue the star. b. Fold the paper. c. Write the message. d. Cut the star and the moon.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>in step 3 of the text.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>____ , take the paper. ____ fold it. ____ , draw a star. ____ , write your message.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>What colour is the paper? 2. What do you draw on the white paper? 3. What do you write inside the card?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>in the text and copy them.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<ol><li>you / fold / the paper 2. you / not / use / too much glue 3. you / write / your name</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Copy the sentence from the text that proves: 1. The card is not expensive. 2. Scissors can be dangerous.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Match: 1. scissors 2. glue 3. ruler 4. sheet — a. مسطرة b. مقص c. ورقة d. لاصق</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>: \"How to make a birthday card for my sister\".</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>(4 steps, imperatives + sequence words). Materials: a tray, glasses, a teapot, mint, sugar.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Design (in words) a poster of six instructions to keep the classroom clean, using imperatives and <em>Don't</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> True or false + justification from the text (1 + 1 each): 1. You need a ruler. 2. You write the message with a pencil. 3. You must use a lot of glue.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Order these steps and add a sequence word to each (1 pt each): <em>write the message / fold the paper / cut the star</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/8) — Writing.</strong> Write five instructions to make a present for your mother (a card, a box or a flower pot). Barème: five imperative verbs (3), sequence words (2), materials listed (2), mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. T — \"a ruler\" in the list of materials. 2. F — \"write your message with the green pen\". 3. F — \"Don't use too much glue\". B. <em>First, fold the paper. Then cut the star. Finally, write the message.</em> C. Sample: <em>You need paper, glue, colours and old magazines. First, cut a big heart in the paper. Then draw flowers on it. Next, glue small pieces of magazine around the heart. After that, write \"Thank you, Mum\". Finally, give the card to your mother on Mother's Day.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en35","domaine":"anglais","unite":35,"theme":"Unit 7 — Let's buy presents!","tag":"EN35","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 7 - Writing (long and short forms, writing about presents for my family)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Transform</strong> long forms into short forms and vice versa (<em>is not → isn't</em>, <em>does not → doesn't</em>).","<strong>Place</strong> the apostrophe correctly.","<strong>Choose</strong> the right register: short forms in messages and speech, long forms in formal writing.","<strong>Write</strong> a paragraph of 50–60 words about the presents he or she buys for the family.","<strong>Self-assess</strong> the paragraph with the writing rubric."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Two texts about the same family. Text A: <em>\"My father does not like coffee. He is not at home on Friday.\"</em> Text B: <em>\"My father doesn't like coffee. He isn't at home on Friday.\"</em> Both are correct English. Text A uses <strong>long forms</strong>, text B uses <strong>short forms</strong> (contractions). Learners must be able to write both — and, above all, to put the apostrophe in the right place.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes five pairs on the board and asks the class to find what disappears in the short form: <em>I am → I'm</em> (the <em>a</em>), <em>is not → isn't</em> (the <em>o</em>), <em>do not → don't</em> (the <em>o</em>), <em>does not → doesn't</em> (the <em>o</em>), <em>has not → hasn't</em> (the <em>o</em>). Discovery: the <strong>apostrophe replaces the missing letter(s)</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — The apostrophe replaces the letters you remove</strong> <em>I am → I'<strong>m</strong></em> · <em>she is → she'<strong>s</strong></em> · <em>they are → they'<strong>re</strong></em> · <em>do not → do<strong>n't</strong></em> · <em>does not → doe<strong>sn't</strong></em> · <em>have not → have<strong>n't</strong></em> · <em>has not → ha<strong>sn't</strong></em> · <em>cannot → ca<strong>n't</strong></em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — Where the apostrophe goes</strong> In <em>n't</em> forms, the apostrophe stands between <strong>n</strong> and <strong>t</strong>: <em>doesn't, don't, isn't, aren't, hasn't, haven't, can't</em>. ⚠️ Never <em>does'nt</em>, never <em>dont</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — Two different 's</strong> <em>He's</em> = <em>he is</em> (verb) — <em>my brother's watch</em> = possession (the watch <strong>of</strong> my brother). Look at the word after: a verb/adjective after <em>he's</em> (<em>He's happy</em>), a noun after a possessive (<em>my brother's watch</em>).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — When to use which</strong> <strong>Short forms</strong>: speech, dialogues, messages, e-mails to friends, and most A1 writing. <strong>Long forms</strong>: formal letters, official notices, and when you want to insist: <em>I do <strong>not</strong> like it.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — writing the \"presents paragraph\".</strong></p>\n<ol><li>List three people: <em>my mother, my father, my little brother</em>.</li><li>For each, write one sentence about their tastes with the third person <em>-s</em>: <em>My mother likes flowers.</em></li><li>Add one negative with a short form: <em>She doesn't like sweets.</em></li><li>Add the present and its price: <em>I buy her a plant. It costs forty dirhams.</em></li><li>Close with a sentence about the special day: <em>We celebrate Eid on Sunday.</em></li><li>Check: apostrophes, capital letters, full stops, the <em>-s</em> of the third person.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Long → short: <em>My sister does not have a watch, and she is not happy.</em> → <em>My sister doesn't have a watch, and she isn't happy.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Short → long (for a formal notice): <em>The shop isn't open on Sunday and it doesn't sell books.</em> → <em>The shop is not open on Sunday and it does not sell books.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Model paragraph (58 words): <em>Next week we celebrate Eid Al Fitr. My mother likes flowers, so I buy her a small plant. It costs forty dirhams. My father doesn't like sweets, but he likes tea, so I buy him a nice glass. My little brother's present is a blue ball. It costs seventy dirhams. I don't have a lot of money, but my family is happy.</em> Note <em>my little brother's present</em> (possession) and the two short forms.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Prices in dirhams, presents from the <em>souk</em> or the medina, Eid, Mother's Day, a wedding in the family, the <em>dar chabab</em> prize day.</p>\n<p><strong>Writing rubric — /10</strong></p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Task and length</td><td>50–60 words, three people, three presents</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Grammar</td><td>third-person <em>-s</em>, <em>doesn't</em>, correct short forms</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Vocabulary</td><td>presents, prices in dirhams, special day</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>linkers <em>and, but, so</em>; logical order</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanics</td><td>apostrophes, capital letters, full stops, spelling</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Speaking before writing (pair work).</strong> A says one sentence about a family member's tastes; B repeats it with a short form and adds a present idea. This oral rehearsal fixes both the <em>-s</em> and the apostrophe forms.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Long form</th><th>Short form</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I am</td><td>I'm</td><td>apostrophe replaces <em>a</em></td></tr><tr><td>he is / she is</td><td>he's / she's</td><td>also possession: <em>Ali's book</em></td></tr><tr><td>they are</td><td>they're</td><td>not <em>there</em></td></tr><tr><td>do not</td><td>don't</td><td>one apostrophe only</td></tr><tr><td>does not</td><td>doesn't</td><td>never <em>does'nt</em></td></tr><tr><td>have not</td><td>haven't</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>has not</td><td>hasn't</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>cannot</td><td>can't</td><td>one word in the long form</td></tr><tr><td>it is</td><td>it's</td><td><em>its</em> (no apostrophe) = possession</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Apostrophe in the wrong place.</strong> ✗ <em>does'nt, do'nt</em> → ✓ <em>doesn't, don't</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>No apostrophe at all.</strong> ✗ <em>dont, isnt, cant</em> → ✓ <em>don't, isn't, can't</em>.</li><li>⚠️ **<em>it's</em> vs <em>its</em>.** <em>It's cold</em> (= it is) / <em>The dog and its ball</em> (possession).</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Short forms with the -s kept.</strong> ✗ <em>He doesn't likes</em> → ✓ <em>He doesn't like</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>does not 2. do not 3. is not 4. are not 5. has not 6. cannot</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>doesn't 2. I'm 3. they're 4. hasn't 5. can't</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>dont 2. doesnt 3. isnt 4. cant</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>**Exercice 4 — <em>it's</em> or <em>its</em>?** 1. ____ cold today. 2. The cat plays with ____ ball. 3. ____ my birthday!</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>My father (doesn't / does'nt) like coffee. 2. She (isnt / isn't) at home. 3. They (dont / don't) celebrate New Year.</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p><em>My sister does not like fish. She is not happy today. My parents do not have a car.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>(formal notice): <em>The library isn't open on Sunday. It doesn't have English books. Pupils can't take books home.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Say what <em>'s</em> means. 1. He's my cousin. 2. My mother's scarf is red. 3. It's ten o'clock. 4. Ali's ball is new.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<ol><li>My brother ____ like sweets. 2. I ____ have a watch. 3. She ____ at school today. 4. We ____ buy expensive presents.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>about your family with <em>likes / doesn't like</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p><em>My mother dont like coffee. My father does'nt likes sweets. Its her birthday tomorrow. I buy she a scarf.</em></p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>\"Presents for my family\": three people, their tastes, the presents and the prices.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>(35–45 words) using at least four short forms: tell him what you buy for Eid.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Mark your Exercice 12 with the rubric and write two sentences: what is good, what you must improve.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Write the short forms (1 pt each): 1. does not 2. do not 3. is not 4. cannot 5. has not 6. they are.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/4).</strong> <em>It's</em> or <em>its</em>? (1 pt each) 1. ____ Eid tomorrow. 2. The school and ____ garden are new. 3. ____ very cold in Ifrane. 4. The cat drinks ____ milk.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/10) — Writing.</strong> Write a paragraph of 50–60 words: \"Presents for my family\". Marked with the rubric (task and length 2, grammar 3, vocabulary 2, organisation 1, mechanics 2).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. doesn't 2. don't 3. isn't 4. can't 5. hasn't 6. they're. B. 1. It's 2. its 3. It's 4. its. C. Sample (55 words): <em>Next Sunday is my mother's birthday. She likes flowers and she doesn't like sweets, so I buy her a plant for thirty-five dirhams. My father likes tea, so my sister buys him a beautiful glass. My little brother wants a ball. It isn't expensive: it costs sixty dirhams. We celebrate at home with a big cake.</em></p>\n<p>## Unit 8 — What's the time?</p>\n<p>Unit 8 gives learners the tools to talk about <strong>time</strong>: the parts of the day, the clock, and the daily routine. It combines the vocabulary of times of the day, the question <em>What's the time?</em>, the blends <em>sm sn st sk</em>, an information text about a Moroccan pupil's day, and the identification of Wh- questions in writing.</p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en36","domaine":"anglais","unite":36,"theme":"Unit 8 — What's the time?","tag":"EN36","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 8 - Words (times of the day)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Name</strong> the parts of the day: <em>morning, noon, afternoon, evening, night, midnight</em>.","<strong>Use</strong> the prepositions <em>in the / at</em> correctly with times of the day.","<strong>Name</strong> ten routine actions: <em>get up, have breakfast, go to school, have lunch…</em>","<strong>Match</strong> an action with a time of the day.","<strong>Ask and answer</strong> in pairs: <em>When do you have breakfast? — At seven o'clock, in the morning.</em>","<strong>Write</strong> five sentences about his or her own day, in order."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> A day in the life of Hamza, a 1AC pupil in Taza: he gets up at half past six, has breakfast at seven, goes to school at half past seven, has lunch at midday, comes home at half past five and sleeps at ten. To tell this story in English you need two things: the <strong>parts of the day</strong> and the <strong>prepositions</strong> that go with them.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher draws a long line on the board from 0 to 24 and asks learners to place five moments: <em>breakfast, school, lunch, homework, sleep</em>. Then he writes the English names of the periods above the line. Learners see immediately that the day is cut into blocks.</p>\n<p>``<code> 0h ----- 6h --------- 12h --------- 18h --------- 24h night | morning     | afternoon   | evening     | night ^ get up      ^ noon/midday               ^ midnight </code>``</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — parts of the day</strong> <strong>morning</strong> /ˈmɔːnɪŋ/ = from sunrise to 12; <strong>noon / midday</strong> = 12; <strong>afternoon</strong> = 12 to about 18; <strong>evening</strong> = 18 to bedtime; <strong>night</strong> = when you sleep; <strong>midnight</strong> = 12 at night.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — prepositions of time</strong> <strong>in the</strong> morning / afternoon / evening → <em>I study in the evening.</em> <strong>at</strong> night, <strong>at</strong> noon, <strong>at</strong> midnight, <strong>at</strong> + a clock time → <em>at seven o'clock, at night.</em> <strong>on</strong> + a day → <em>on Monday.</em> No preposition with <em>today, tomorrow, every day</em>. ⚠️ <em>in the night</em> exists but the normal A1 form is <strong>at night</strong>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Ten routine verbs</strong> <em>get up, wash, have breakfast, go to school, study, have lunch, go home, do my homework, have dinner, go to bed / sleep.</em> Note: <strong>have</strong> breakfast / lunch / dinner (no article), <strong>go to</strong> school / bed (no article).</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — building a routine sentence.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Choose the action: <em>have breakfast</em>.</li><li>Add the clock time with <strong>at</strong>: <em>at seven o'clock</em>.</li><li>Add the part of the day with <strong>in the</strong> (or <em>at night</em>): <em>in the morning</em>.</li><li>Result: <em>I have breakfast at seven o'clock in the morning.</em></li><li>For a third person, remember the <em>-s</em>: <em>He has breakfast at seven.</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> ✗ <em>I go to school in seven o'clock.</em> → ✓ <em>I go to school <strong>at</strong> seven o'clock.</em> Clock times take <strong>at</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> ✗ <em>I do my homework in the night.</em> → ✓ <em>I do my homework <strong>in the evening</strong></em> (or <em>at night</em> if it is really late).</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Hamza's day, five sentences: <em>Hamza gets up at half past six in the morning. He has breakfast at seven. He goes to school at half past seven. He has lunch at midday. He goes to bed at ten o'clock at night.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Moroccan school life gives natural examples: school starts at 8 a.m. and often has a long lunch break, so pupils go home <em>at midday</em> and come back <em>at 2 p.m.</em>; the call to prayer marks the parts of the day; in Ramadan, families eat <em>at sunset</em> and children study <em>at night</em>. Learners can also mention the <em>souk</em> day of their town.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"My day, your day\": A asks four questions (<em>When do you get up? When do you have lunch? When do you do your homework? When do you go to bed?</em>), B answers with <em>at</em> + time + part of the day. Then B reports two facts about A to the class using the third person: <em>Karim gets up at six o'clock.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>morning</td><td>/ˈmɔːnɪŋ/</td><td>صباح</td></tr><tr><td>noon / midday</td><td>/nuːn/ /ˈmɪddeɪ/</td><td>زوال</td></tr><tr><td>afternoon</td><td>/ˌɑːftəˈnuːn/</td><td>بعد الزوال</td></tr><tr><td>evening</td><td>/ˈiːvnɪŋ/</td><td>مساء</td></tr><tr><td>night</td><td>/naɪt/</td><td>ليل</td></tr><tr><td>midnight</td><td>/ˈmɪdnaɪt/</td><td>منتصف الليل</td></tr><tr><td>get up</td><td>/ˌɡet ˈʌp/</td><td>يستيقظ</td></tr><tr><td>have breakfast</td><td>/ˈbrekfəst/</td><td>يتناول الفطور</td></tr><tr><td>have lunch</td><td>/lʌntʃ/</td><td>يتغدى</td></tr><tr><td>have dinner</td><td>/ˈdɪnə/</td><td>يتعشى</td></tr><tr><td>go to bed</td><td>/ɡəʊ tə bed/</td><td>ينام / يذهب إلى النوم</td></tr><tr><td>early / late</td><td>/ˈɜːli/ /leɪt/</td><td>مبكرا / متأخرا</td></tr><tr><td>a.m. / p.m.</td><td>/eɪ ˈem/ /piː ˈem/</td><td>صباحا / بعد الزوال</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Wrong preposition with a clock time.</strong> ✗ <em>in seven o'clock</em> → ✓ <em>at seven o'clock</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <em><strong>in the night</strong></em> instead of <em>at night</em> in A1 sentences.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Article with meals.</strong> ✗ <em>I have a breakfast</em> → ✓ <em>I have breakfast</em>.</li><li>⚠️ **Article with <em>bed</em> / <em>school</em>.** ✗ <em>go to the bed / go to the school</em> (as a routine) → ✓ <em>go to bed / go to school</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>morning 2. afternoon 3. evening 4. night 5. midnight — a. ليل b. صباح c. منتصف الليل d. بعد الزوال e. مساء</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — <em>in the</em>, <em>at</em> or <em>on</em>?** 1. ____ the morning 2. ____ night 3. ____ Monday 4. ____ eight o'clock 5. ____ the evening 6. ____ midday</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>of a normal day: <em>have lunch, get up, go to bed, go to school, have breakfast</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>I ____ up at six o'clock. 2. I ____ breakfast at seven. 3. I ____ to school at half past seven. 4. I ____ to bed at ten.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Write T or F. 1. I get up at five o'clock. 2. I have lunch at midday. 3. I study at night.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>I go to school in eight o'clock. 2. I do my homework in the night. 3. I have a breakfast at seven. 4. I go to the bed at ten.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>(action + at + time + part of the day). 1. get up / 6.30 2. have lunch / 12 3. watch TV / 20.00</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Rewrite for <em>Hamza</em>: <em>I get up at six. I have breakfast at seven. I go to school at half past seven. I study in the afternoon.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Write the question. 1. ____ ? — At seven o'clock. 2. ____ ? — In the evening. 3. ____ ? — At ten at night.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>for your own day: <em>get up / breakfast / school / lunch / homework / dinner / bed</em>, with the time for each.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>**Exercice 11 — <em>early</em> or <em>late</em>?** 1. I get up at five o'clock: I get up ____ . 2. He goes to bed at midnight: he goes to bed ____ .</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>\"My school day\", in chronological order, with five times.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>Write four sentences comparing your Monday and your Sunday (<em>On Monday I get up at six, but on Sunday I get up at nine.</em>)</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>(four questions and four answers) about his or her day, and write the answers in the third person.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Complete with <em>in the</em>, <em>at</em> or <em>on</em> (1 pt each): 1. ____ morning 2. ____ night 3. ____ Friday 4. ____ half past seven 5. ____ afternoon 6. ____ midnight.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Rewrite in the third person for <em>Salma</em> (2 pts each): 1. I get up at six o'clock. 2. I have lunch at midday. 3. I study in the evening.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/8) — Writing.</strong> Write a paragraph of 50–60 words about your school day, in chronological order, with at least five times. Barème: chronological order (2), five times with correct prepositions (3), routine verbs (2), mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. in the 2. at 3. on 4. at 5. in the 6. at. B. 1. <em>Salma gets up at six o'clock.</em> 2. <em>Salma has lunch at midday.</em> 3. <em>Salma studies in the evening.</em> C. Sample (54 words): <em>I get up at half past six in the morning and I wash my face. I have breakfast at seven with my family. I go to school at half past seven. At midday, I have lunch at home. In the afternoon, I do my homework. I go to bed at ten at night.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en37","domaine":"anglais","unite":37,"theme":"Unit 8 — What's the time?","tag":"EN37","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 8 - Grammar (what's the time? It's ... o'clock.)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Ask</strong> the time in two ways: <em>What's the time? / What time is it?</em>","<strong>Tell</strong> the time with <em>o'clock, half past, quarter past, quarter to</em> and minutes.","<strong>Use</strong> the digital way of telling the time (<em>It's seven forty-five</em>).","<strong>Add</strong> <em>a.m.</em> and <em>p.m.</em> correctly.","<strong>Ask and answer</strong> <em>What time do you …?</em> about a daily routine.","<strong>Draw and read</strong> clock faces in pairs."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The school bell rings at 8.00, the break starts at 9.55, the English lesson begins at 10.10 and the bus to Sidi Kacem leaves at 1.30 p.m. Every one of these moments needs a different way of saying the time in English.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher moves the hands of a paper clock and asks <em>What's the time?</em> Learners answer with the numbers they know. He then writes four models on the board and lets the class match each one with a clock face: <em>It's three o'clock. / It's half past three. / It's a quarter past three. / It's a quarter to four.</em></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — The question</strong> <strong>What's the time?</strong> = <strong>What time is it?</strong> Answer: <strong>It's</strong> + time.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — The four basic patterns</strong> <strong>o'clock</strong> for the exact hour: <em>3.00 → It's three o'clock.</em> <strong>half past</strong> for 30 minutes: <em>3.30 → It's half past three.</em> <strong>a quarter past</strong> for 15 minutes: <em>3.15 → It's a quarter past three.</em> <strong>a quarter to</strong> for 45 minutes: <em>3.45 → It's a quarter to four</em> (we look at the <strong>next</strong> hour).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — Minutes</strong> Minutes 1–29: <strong>past</strong> + the hour → <em>3.10 → It's ten past three.</em> <em>3.25 → It's twenty-five past three.</em> Minutes 31–59: <strong>to</strong> + the <strong>next</strong> hour → <em>3.50 → It's ten to four.</em> <em>3.40 → It's twenty to four.</em> Digital style (very common): <em>It's three ten. It's three fifty. It's seven forty-five.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — a.m., p.m. and the 24-hour clock</strong> <strong>a.m.</strong> = from midnight to noon; <strong>p.m.</strong> = from noon to midnight: <em>School starts at 8 a.m. and finishes at 6 p.m.</em> In Morocco timetables are often written with the 24-hour clock (<em>14 h 00</em>); in English we say <em>two o'clock in the afternoon</em> or <em>2 p.m.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — reading a clock.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Look at the <strong>big hand</strong> (minutes). Is it on 12, on 6, on 3, on 9, or elsewhere?</li><li>On 12 → <em>o'clock</em>; on 6 → <em>half past</em>; on 3 → <em>a quarter past</em>; on 9 → <em>a quarter to</em>.</li><li>If it is elsewhere: are the minutes under 30 (<strong>past</strong>) or over 30 (<strong>to</strong>)?</li><li>Look at the <strong>small hand</strong> (hour). With <em>past</em>, use the hour it has passed; with <em>to</em>, use the <strong>next</strong> hour.</li><li>Say the whole sentence: <em>It's twenty past nine.</em></li></ol>\n<p>``<code> 12   ->  o'clock          e.g. 9.00  It's nine o'clock. 3   ->  a quarter past   e.g. 9.15  It's a quarter past nine. 6   ->  half past        e.g. 9.30  It's half past nine. 9   ->  a quarter to     e.g. 9.45  It's a quarter to ten. </code>``</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> 7.40. Minutes = 40 > 30, so <strong>to</strong>; 60 − 40 = 20; next hour = 8. → <em>It's twenty to eight.</em> (Digital: <em>It's seven forty.</em>)</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> 12.15 p.m. → <em>It's a quarter past twelve</em> / <em>It's twelve fifteen</em>, in the afternoon → <em>a quarter past twelve p.m.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Routine question: <em>What time do you have lunch? — At half past twelve.</em> Note the <strong>at</strong> in the answer; <em>What time…?</em> needs <em>do</em> (or <em>does</em>): <em>What time does the lesson start? — At ten past ten.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Real timetable of a 1AC class: <em>English on Monday at 8 a.m.; the break at five to ten; lunch at half past twelve; the last lesson finishes at half past five.</em> Trains and buses: <em>The train to Rabat leaves at a quarter past six.</em> Prayer times, sunset in Ramadan, and shop opening hours (<em>The shop opens at nine and closes at ten p.m.</em>) are all good practice contexts.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Clock dictation\": A draws a clock face in his notebook without showing it and says the time; B draws what he hears; then they compare. Second round: B says a time in digital form and A says it in the <em>past / to</em> form.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>English (traditional)</th><th>English (digital)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8.00</td><td>It's eight o'clock.</td><td>It's eight.</td></tr><tr><td>8.05</td><td>It's five past eight.</td><td>It's eight oh five.</td></tr><tr><td>8.15</td><td>It's a quarter past eight.</td><td>It's eight fifteen.</td></tr><tr><td>8.30</td><td>It's half past eight.</td><td>It's eight thirty.</td></tr><tr><td>8.45</td><td>It's a quarter to nine.</td><td>It's eight forty-five.</td></tr><tr><td>8.50</td><td>It's ten to nine.</td><td>It's eight fifty.</td></tr><tr><td>12.00 (day)</td><td>It's twelve o'clock / midday.</td><td>It's noon.</td></tr><tr><td>00.00</td><td>It's midnight.</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Useful phrases</strong>: <em>What's the time, please? / Excuse me, what time is it? / My watch is slow. / The bell rings at eight. / I'm late! / We're early.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ **Wrong hour with <em>to</em>.** ✗ <em>It's a quarter to three</em> for 3.45 → ✓ <em>It's a quarter to <strong>four</strong></em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Mixing the two systems.</strong> ✗ <em>It's half past eight thirty.</em> → ✓ <em>It's half past eight.</em> / <em>It's eight thirty.</em></li><li>⚠️ **Missing <em>at</em> in answers.** ✗ <em>I have lunch half past twelve.</em> → ✓ <em>I have lunch <strong>at</strong> half past twelve.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>French-style \"half\".</strong> ✗ <em>It's eight and half.</em> → ✓ <em>It's half past eight.</em></li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>(traditional form). 1. 4.00 2. 6.30 3. 9.15 4. 2.45</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>7.30 2. 7.15 3. 7.45 4. 7.00 — a. a quarter to eight b. seven o'clock c. half past seven d. a quarter past seven</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>**Exercice 3 — <em>past</em> or <em>to</em>?** 1. 5.20 2. 5.50 3. 5.10 4. 5.40</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>It's six fifteen. 2. It's nine thirty. 3. It's ten forty-five.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>**Exercice 5 — <em>a.m.</em> or <em>p.m.</em>?** 1. School starts at 8 ____ . 2. I have dinner at 8 ____ . 3. I get up at 6 ____ .</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>It's a quarter to three. (3.45) 2. It's eight and half. 3. I have lunch half past twelve. 4. It's half past seven thirty.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>What time does school start? 2. What time is the break? 3. What time do you go home?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<ol><li>11.20 2. 1.55 3. 12.00 (day) 4. 00.00</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Write the question. 1. ____ ? — It's half past nine. 2. ____ ? — At seven o'clock. 3. ____ ? — At a quarter past eight.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p><em>What time ____ your father ____ (leave) home? — He ____ (leave) at half past six.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write one sentence for each line. ``<code> Monday 8.00 English | Monday 9.55 break | Monday 12.30 lunch | Monday 17.30 end </code>``</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>In the street: a tourist asks a pupil the time and the time of the next bus to Marrakech.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>\"My Monday\", with six times, using both systems at least once each.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Write five times, exchange with your partner, and check each other's clocks.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Write the time in the traditional form (1 pt each): 1. 5.00 2. 5.15 3. 5.30 4. 5.45 5. 6.10 6. 6.50 7. 12.00 (day) 8. 00.00.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Answer with <em>at</em> + time (2 pts each): 1. What time do you get up? 2. What time does school start? 3. What time do you go to bed?</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6) — Writing.</strong> Write a paragraph of 45–55 words about your Monday with five times. Barème: five correct times (3), correct prepositions (1), routine verbs (1), mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. It's five o'clock. 2. It's a quarter past five. 3. It's half past five. 4. It's a quarter to six. 5. It's ten past six. 6. It's ten to seven. 7. It's twelve o'clock / midday. 8. It's midnight. B. Sample: 1. <em>At half past six.</em> 2. <em>At eight o'clock.</em> 3. <em>At ten o'clock.</em> C. Sample (48 words): <em>On Monday I get up at a quarter to seven. I have breakfast at seven o'clock and I go to school at half past seven. Our first lesson starts at eight. I have lunch at half past twelve. In the evening, I do my homework at six o'clock.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en38","domaine":"anglais","unite":38,"theme":"Unit 8 — What's the time?","tag":"EN38","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 8 - Phonics (consonant blends: sm sn st sk)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Identify</strong> /sm/, /sn/, /st/, /sk/ at the beginning of a word.","<strong>Pronounce</strong> an initial /s/ + consonant without an added vowel.","<strong>Read</strong> sixteen <em>s</em>-blend words.","<strong>Spell</strong> them correctly in a dictation.","<strong>Notice</strong> that <em>sk</em> can be written <em>sc</em> (<em>school</em> /skuːl/) and <em>k</em> (<em>skirt</em>)."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Four words of the school day: <strong>st</strong>art, <strong>sk</strong>y, <strong>sn</strong>ack, <strong>sm</strong>all. Learners already know blends with <em>l</em>; now the <strong>first</strong> letter is always <strong>s</strong>, and the second changes: <em>m, n, t, k</em>. This family is very frequent in English: <em>stop, school, student, small, snow</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes four columns and reads eight words; learners place each word in the right column and find the common letter (<strong>s</strong> at the beginning).</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The four blends</strong> <strong>sm</strong> /sm/ → <em>small, smile, smell, smart</em> <strong>sn</strong> /sn/ → <em>snow, snack, snake, sneeze</em> <strong>st</strong> /st/ → <em>stop, start, student, star, stand, street</em> <strong>sk</strong> /sk/ → <em>sky, skirt, skate, school</em> (written <em>sch</em>), <em>scarf</em> (written <em>sc</em>)</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Warning — spelling of /sk/</strong> The sound /sk/ has three spellings: <strong>sk</strong> (<em>sky, skirt</em>), <strong>sc</strong> (<em>scarf, score</em>) and <strong>sch</strong> (<em>school, scheme</em>). Learn each word with its spelling.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Make a long /s/ alone: <em>sssss</em> (air between the tongue and the teeth).</li><li>Cut the /s/ short and immediately add the second sound: /s/+/m/ → /sm/.</li><li>Add the vowel: /sm/ + /ɔːl/ = <em>small</em>.</li><li>Do not put a vowel in front: ✗ /esˈmɔːl/ \"esmall\" is a very common first-language habit and must be removed.</li><li>Test with the word <em>school</em> /skuːl/: two sounds /s/+/k/, then /uːl/ — never \"iskool\".</li></ol>\n<p>``<code> s + m -> sm : small  smile  smell s + n -> sn : snow   snack  snake s + t -> st : stop   start  student  street s + k -> sk : sky    skirt  school(sch)  scarf(sc) </code>``</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> <em>street</em> /striːt/ has <strong>three</strong> consonants at the beginning: /s/+/t/+/r/. Build it in two moves: /st/ then add /r/. Same for <em>strong</em>, <em>string</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> <em>student</em> /ˈstjuːdənt/ — the stress is on the first syllable: <strong>STU</strong>dent. Learners often stress the second syllable; clap the rhythm to correct it.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Minimal pairs: <em>sky / ski</em> (both /sk/ but different vowels), <em>snow / no</em> (with and without the /s/), <em>stop / top</em>. Removing the /s/ changes the word, so the /s/ must be heard clearly.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Real sentences: <em>Our school starts at eight o'clock. It snows in Ifrane and in the Atlas Mountains in winter. The sky is blue in Zagora. My sister wears a blue skirt. I buy a scarf in the souk. Stop at the red light in the street.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Snowball reading\": A reads a word, B repeats it and adds a new one from the list; they continue until one of them forgets. Then both read the tongue twister <em>Small students stop and smile in the snow.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Blend (spelling)</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>small</td><td>/smɔːl/</td><td>sm</td><td>صغير</td></tr><tr><td>smile</td><td>/smaɪl/</td><td>sm</td><td>يبتسم</td></tr><tr><td>smell</td><td>/smel/</td><td>sm</td><td>يشم / رائحة</td></tr><tr><td>smart</td><td>/smɑːt/</td><td>sm</td><td>أنيق / ذكي</td></tr><tr><td>snow</td><td>/snəʊ/</td><td>sn</td><td>ثلج</td></tr><tr><td>snack</td><td>/snæk/</td><td>sn</td><td>وجبة خفيفة</td></tr><tr><td>snake</td><td>/sneɪk/</td><td>sn</td><td>ثعبان</td></tr><tr><td>stop</td><td>/stɒp/</td><td>st</td><td>يتوقف</td></tr><tr><td>start</td><td>/stɑːt/</td><td>st</td><td>يبدأ</td></tr><tr><td>student</td><td>/ˈstjuːdənt/</td><td>st</td><td>تلميذ / طالب</td></tr><tr><td>star</td><td>/stɑː/</td><td>st</td><td>نجمة</td></tr><tr><td>street</td><td>/striːt/</td><td>str</td><td>شارع</td></tr><tr><td>sky</td><td>/skaɪ/</td><td>sk</td><td>سماء</td></tr><tr><td>skirt</td><td>/skɜːt/</td><td>sk</td><td>تنورة</td></tr><tr><td>school</td><td>/skuːl/</td><td>sk (sch)</td><td>مدرسة</td></tr><tr><td>scarf</td><td>/skɑːf/</td><td>sk (sc)</td><td>وشاح</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Vowel added before the /s/.</strong> ✗ \"iskool\", \"esmall\" → ✓ /skuːl/, /smɔːl/.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Vowel inside the blend.</strong> ✗ \"sitop\" → ✓ /stɒp/.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Spelling /sk/ wrongly.</strong> ✗ <em>skool, skarf</em> → ✓ <em>school, scarf</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Wrong stress.</strong> ✗ stu<strong>DENT</strong> → ✓ <strong>STU</strong>dent.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>school (sm/sn/st/sk) 2. small (sm/sn/st/sk) 3. snow (sm/sn/st/sk) 4. start (sm/sn/st/sk)</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Complete with <em>sm</em>, <em>sn</em>, <em>st</em> or <em>sk</em>.** 1. ____ile 2. ____ake 3. ____op 4. ____irt 5. ____all 6. ____udent</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>into four columns: <em>smell, snack, star, sky, smart, street</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>stop 2. sky 3. small</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>: <em>stop, start, student, street, school</em>. Which one has three consonants at the beginning?</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>of eight words: <em>small, smile, snow, snack, stop, student, sky, school</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>Our ____ starts at eight o'clock. 2. It ____ in the Atlas Mountains in winter. 3. The ____ is blue today. 4. My sister wears a blue ____ . 5. Please ____ talking!</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<ol><li>small / smile / snow 2. star / start / sky 3. school / scarf / snack</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>from the IPA: 1. /ˈstjuːdənt/ 2. /skɑːf/ 3. /snəʊ/ 4. /smaɪl/</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<ol><li>skool 2. sitop 3. esmall 4. skarf</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Underline the stressed syllable: <em>student, smiling, snowball, October</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Find three more <em>s</em>-blend words in your book and transcribe them.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>about your school day, each with one <em>s</em>-blend word.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Write your own tongue twister with four <em>s</em>-blend words and read it three times.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Complete with <em>sm</em>, <em>sn</em>, <em>st</em> or <em>sk</em> (1 pt each): 1. ____ow 2. ____art 3. ____ile 4. ____y 5. ____ack 6. ____udent 7. ____ell 8. ____irt.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Write the words (1.5 pt each): 1. /skuːl/ 2. /skɑːf/ 3. /striːt/ 4. /smɔːl/.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6).</strong> Write three sentences about your school day, each with two <em>s</em>-blend words (2 pts each).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. snow 2. start 3. smile 4. sky 5. snack 6. student 7. smell 8. skirt. B. 1. school 2. scarf 3. street 4. small. C. Sample: <em>My school is small and it starts at eight. I walk in the street with a snack in my bag. In winter, the sky is grey and it snows.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en39","domaine":"anglais","unite":39,"theme":"Unit 8 — What's the time?","tag":"EN39","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 8 - Reading (information texts)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Read</strong> an information text about daily routines and find times.","<strong>Complete</strong> a timetable from the text.","<strong>Answer</strong> true/false and Wh- questions with justification.","<strong>Compare</strong> two people's days.","<strong>Infer</strong> one simple fact that is not written directly.","<strong>Speak</strong> in pairs about differences between his or her day and the text."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> A school magazine publishes two short profiles: a pupil in a village near Azilal and a pupil in Casablanca. Their days are different because of distance, transport and family work. Reading these two profiles trains the learner to <strong>find times</strong> and to <strong>compare</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> Before reading, the teacher asks: <em>What is difficult for a pupil who lives far from school?</em> Learners answer (walking, the bus, getting up early). The vocabulary needed (<em>far, near, walk, bus, market, help</em>) is written on the board.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Reading strategy — the \"number hunt\"</strong> In a text about routines, the information is carried by <strong>numbers</strong> and by <strong>time expressions</strong>. Circle every number and every <em>at / in the / o'clock</em> before you answer the questions: the answers are almost always next to them.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Original reading text (written for this document).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>TWO PUPILS, TWO DAYS</strong> <strong>Nawal, 12, Aït Bouguemez (near Azilal)</strong> Nawal gets up at half past five. It is dark and cold in the morning, so she puts on her big scarf. She has bread and tea for breakfast at six. Then she walks to school with her brother and two friends. The road is long: they walk for fifty minutes. School starts at half past seven. At midday, Nawal eats her lunch at school because her house is far. In the afternoon, she has two lessons. She walks home at four o'clock. She helps her mother with the animals, and she does her homework at seven in the evening. She goes to bed at half past nine. On Friday, she doesn't go to school in the afternoon: she goes to the weekly market with her father. <strong>Yassine, 12, Casablanca</strong> Yassine gets up at seven o'clock. His school is near his flat: it is a five-minute walk. He has milk and biscuits at a quarter past seven, and he starts school at eight. He has lunch at home at half past twelve because his mother is at home. He goes back to school at two o'clock and finishes at half past five. In the evening, he does his homework at six and he watches TV at eight. He goes to bed at half past ten. On Saturday morning, he goes to the swimming pool with his cousin.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Comprehension work step by step.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Skim</strong>: two pupils, two daily routines, in two different places.</li><li><strong>Number hunt</strong>: Nawal — 5.30, 6, 50 minutes, 7.30, 12, 4, 7, 9.30. Yassine — 7, 5 minutes, 7.15, 8, 12.30, 2, 5.30, 6, 8, 10.30.</li><li><strong>Table</strong>: put the two routines side by side; the comparison then writes itself.</li><li><strong>Inference</strong>: <em>Why does Nawal eat at school?</em> The text says \"because her house is far\" — the reason is written. But <em>Why does she put on a big scarf?</em> The text says it is \"dark and cold\": the learner must connect two ideas.</li><li><strong>Justification</strong>: every answer is followed by a quotation.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> <em>True or false: Yassine walks fifty minutes to school.</em> → <strong>False</strong>: \"His school is near his flat: it is a five-minute walk.\" It is Nawal who walks fifty minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> <em>What time does Nawal get up? Why so early?</em> → <em>She gets up at half past five because the road to school is long: she walks for fifty minutes.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Comparison sentence: <em>Nawal gets up at half past five, but Yassine gets up at seven o'clock.</em> The linker <strong>but</strong> is the key to comparing.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> The text reflects real contrasts of the Moroccan school map: long walks in mountain douars, midday meals at school, the weekly <em>souk</em>, and city routines with a flat near the school. It is a natural place to talk about the <em>ramassage scolaire</em> (school transport) and the <em>cantine</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> A says one sentence about Nawal's day; B says the corresponding sentence about his or her own day, using <em>but</em>: <em>Nawal gets up at half past five, but I get up at seven.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>far / near</td><td>/fɑː/ /nɪə/</td><td>بعيد / قريب</td></tr><tr><td>dark</td><td>/dɑːk/</td><td>مظلم</td></tr><tr><td>road</td><td>/rəʊd/</td><td>طريق</td></tr><tr><td>walk (n. and v.)</td><td>/wɔːk/</td><td>يمشي / مشي</td></tr><tr><td>flat</td><td>/flæt/</td><td>شقة</td></tr><tr><td>animals</td><td>/ˈænɪmlz/</td><td>حيوانات</td></tr><tr><td>weekly market</td><td>/ˈwiːkli/</td><td>السوق الأسبوعي</td></tr><tr><td>swimming pool</td><td>/ˈswɪmɪŋ puːl/</td><td>مسبح</td></tr><tr><td>biscuits</td><td>/ˈbɪskɪts/</td><td>بسكويت</td></tr><tr><td>back (go back)</td><td>/bæk/</td><td>يرجع</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Mixing the two people.</strong> Always check the name before you answer.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Answering without the time.</strong> <em>When does she get up?</em> → the answer must contain a time.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>No justification</strong> in true/false questions.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Copying a whole paragraph</strong> instead of the useful sentence.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Write <em>Nawal</em> or <em>Yassine</em>. 1. He lives in Casablanca. 2. She walks for fifty minutes. 3. He goes to the swimming pool. 4. She goes to the weekly market.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>Nawal gets up at seven. 2. Yassine has lunch at home. 3. Nawal goes to bed at half past nine. 4. Yassine's school is far.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>Nawal's breakfast 2. Yassine's breakfast 3. Nawal's bedtime 4. Yassine's bedtime</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>far 2. near 3. dark 4. flat — a. شقة b. بعيد c. مظلم d. قريب</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>Who walks to school with her brother? 2. Who watches TV at eight?</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>What time does Nawal get up? 2. Why does she eat lunch at school? 3. What does Yassine do on Saturday morning?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Copy the proof: 1. Nawal's road is long. 2. Yassine's school is near.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>```</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>get up</th><th>breakfast</th><th>school starts</th><th>bed</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table>\n<p>Nawal       |        |           |               | Yassine     |        |           |               | ```</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>**Exercice 9 — Compare with <em>but</em>.** Write three sentences comparing Nawal and Yassine.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Why does Nawal put on a big scarf in the morning? Answer and explain.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write the question. 1. ____ ? — At half past twelve. 2. ____ ? — Because her house is far. 3. ____ ? — For fifty minutes.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Compare your day with Nawal's day, using <em>but</em> three times.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>Write a short profile (45–55 words) of a pupil in your town with six times.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Whose day is more difficult? Why?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> True or false + justification (1 + 1 each): 1. Nawal walks to school alone. 2. Yassine has lunch at school. 3. Nawal goes to the market on Friday.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/8).</strong> Answer in complete sentences (2 pts each): 1. What time does Nawal have breakfast? 2. Why does she get up early? 3. What time does Yassine finish school? 4. What does Nawal do at seven in the evening?</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6) — Writing.</strong> Compare your day with Yassine's day in 45–55 words, using <em>but</em> at least twice. Barème: three comparisons (3), <em>but</em> used correctly (1), times with correct prepositions (1), mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. F — \"she walks to school with her brother and two friends\". 2. F — \"He has lunch at home at half past twelve\". 3. T — \"she goes to the weekly market with her father\". B. 1. She has breakfast at six o'clock. 2. Because the road is long and she walks for fifty minutes to school. 3. He finishes school at half past five. 4. She does her homework. C. Sample (50 words): <em>Yassine gets up at seven o'clock and I get up at half past six. His school is near his flat, but my school is twenty minutes from my house. He has lunch at home and I have lunch at home too. He goes to bed at half past ten, but I go to bed at ten.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en40","domaine":"anglais","unite":40,"theme":"Unit 8 — What's the time?","tag":"EN40","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 8 - Writing (identifying Wh- questions, writing about my day)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Identify</strong> the seven main Wh- words and what each one asks about.","<strong>Build</strong> correct Wh- questions with <em>do / does / is / are</em>.","<strong>Match</strong> a question with the type of answer expected.","<strong>Write</strong> an interview of five questions and answers.","<strong>Write</strong> a paragraph of 50–65 words about his or her day.","<strong>Punctuate</strong> questions and statements correctly."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The school magazine wants an interview: five questions to a classmate about his day, and then a written paragraph. To write good questions you must know <strong>which Wh- word asks which information</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes five answers on the board and asks the class to find the question: <em>— At seven o'clock. — In Meknes. — My cousin. — Because I like it. — By bus.</em> Learners discover that each answer \"calls\" a different Wh- word: <em>When? Where? Who? Why? How?</em></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The seven Wh- words</strong> <strong>What</strong> = thing / action → <em>What do you do after school?</em> <strong>Where</strong> = place → <em>Where do you live?</em> <strong>When / What time</strong> = time → <em>When do you get up? What time does school start?</em> <strong>Who</strong> = person → <em>Who helps you?</em> <strong>Why</strong> = reason (answer with <em>because</em>) → <em>Why do you get up early?</em> <strong>How</strong> = manner → <em>How do you go to school? — By bus. / On foot.</em> <strong>How many / How much</strong> = quantity / price → <em>How many lessons have you got? How much is the ticket?</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — word order of a Wh- question</strong> <strong>Wh- word + auxiliary + subject + base verb + …?</strong> <em>What time <strong>does</strong> he <strong>start</strong> school?</em> · <em>Where <strong>do</strong> you <strong>live</strong>?</em> With the verb <em>be</em>, there is no <em>do</em>: <em>Where <strong>is</strong> your school? Who <strong>is</strong> your teacher? What <strong>is</strong> the time?</em> ⚠️ Never two auxiliaries and never the <em>-s</em> on the main verb: ✗ <em>What does he starts?</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — punctuation</strong> Every question ends with a <strong>question mark ?</strong>; every statement ends with a <strong>full stop .</strong> Direct speech in an interview uses a dash or quotation marks: <em>— What time do you get up? — At six o'clock.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — writing an interview.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Choose five pieces of information you want: time of getting up, transport, lunch, homework, bedtime.</li><li>For each one, choose the Wh- word: <em>What time / How / Where / When / What time</em>.</li><li>Add the auxiliary that matches the subject: <em>do you</em> / <em>does he</em>.</li><li>Put the verb in the base form and finish with a question mark.</li><li>Write the answer under each question, with <strong>at</strong> for a time and <strong>because</strong> for a reason.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Information wanted: the way he travels. Wh- word <em>How</em>; subject <em>he</em> → <em>does</em>; base verb <em>go</em>. → <em>How does he go to school? — He goes by bus.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Correcting a faulty question: ✗ <em>Where you live?</em> → the auxiliary is missing → ✓ <em>Where <strong>do</strong> you live?</em> ✗ <em>What time does school starts?</em> → the <em>-s</em> is doubled → ✓ <em>What time does school <strong>start</strong>?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Model interview (short): <em>— What time do you get up? — I get up at half past six. — How do you go to school? — I go on foot; my school is near. — Where do you have lunch? — I have lunch at home. — When do you do your homework? — At six o'clock in the evening. — Why do you like Wednesday? — Because we have English and sport.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Transport words for the <em>How</em> question: <em>on foot, by bus, by taxi (grand taxi), by bike, by tramway</em> (Casablanca, Rabat). Places for <em>Where</em>: <em>at home, at school, in the canteen, at my grandmother's house</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Writing rubric — /10</strong></p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Task and length</td><td>50–65 words / 5 questions + answers</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Wh- questions</td><td>correct word and correct order</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Grammar</td><td><em>do / does</em>, base verb, <em>-s</em> in answers</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Times and linkers</td><td><em>at / in the / on</em>, <em>then, because</em></td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanics</td><td>question marks, capital letters, spelling</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Speaking before writing (pair work).</strong> A interviews B orally with five Wh- questions; B answers; then they change roles. Only after the oral phase do they write. This halves the number of word-order mistakes.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Wh- word</th><th>Asks about</th><th>Typical answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What</td><td>thing / action</td><td><em>I play football.</em></td></tr><tr><td>What time / When</td><td>time</td><td><em>At seven o'clock.</em></td></tr><tr><td>Where</td><td>place</td><td><em>In Fez. / At home.</em></td></tr><tr><td>Who</td><td>person</td><td><em>My sister.</em></td></tr><tr><td>Why</td><td>reason</td><td><em>Because I like English.</em></td></tr><tr><td>How</td><td>manner</td><td><em>By bus. / On foot.</em></td></tr><tr><td>How many</td><td>quantity</td><td><em>Four lessons.</em></td></tr><tr><td>How much</td><td>price</td><td><em>Twenty dirhams.</em></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Transport phrases</strong>: <em>on foot, by bus, by taxi, by bike, by car, by train, by tramway</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Missing auxiliary.</strong> ✗ <em>Where you live?</em> → ✓ <em>Where do you live?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Double marking.</strong> ✗ <em>What time does he starts?</em> → ✓ <em>What time does he start?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Missing question mark.</strong> ✗ <em>Where do you live.</em> → ✓ <em>Where do you live?</em></li><li>⚠️ **<em>Why</em> answered without <em>because</em>**. ✗ <em>Why do you like it? — I like English.</em> → ✓ <em>Because I like English.</em></li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>____ do you live? — In Salé. 2. ____ time do you get up? — At six. 3. ____ helps you? — My sister. 4. ____ do you like English? — Because it is useful.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>Where do you have lunch? 2. How do you go to school? 3. How many lessons have you got? 4. How much is the bus ticket? — a. Four. b. At home. c. Four dirhams. d. By bus.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>Where ____ you live? 2. What time ____ he start school? 3. When ____ they play football?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>What time do you get up 2. I get up at six o'clock 3. Where does she live</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>Why (do / does) your sister study at night? 2. What time (do / does) the lessons start? 3. Where (do / does) you have lunch?</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>Where you live? 2. What time does he starts? 3. Why do you like it. 4. How you go to school?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>for each answer. 1. ____ ? — At half past six. 2. ____ ? — By bus. 3. ____ ? — In Ouarzazate. 4. ____ ? — Because I like sport.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Rewrite these questions for <em>your brother</em>: 1. What time do you get up? 2. Where do you have lunch? 3. How do you go to school?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>for an interview about a school day.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>to your five questions (about yourself).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Turn this interview into a paragraph in the third person: <em>— What time do you get up? — At seven. — How do you go to school? — On foot. — When do you do your homework? — At six.</em></p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>\"My day\", with five times, one <em>because</em> and one transport phrase.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>and write six questions and answers.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Exchange notebooks and check three things in your partner's questions: the Wh- word, the auxiliary, the question mark.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Complete with the right Wh- word (1 pt each): 1. ____ do you live? 2. ____ time does school start? 3. ____ is your English teacher? 4. ____ do you go to school? — By bus. 5. ____ do you like sport? 6. ____ many lessons have you got on Monday?</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Write the questions for these answers (1.5 pt each): 1. — At half past six. 2. — In Errachidia. 3. — On foot. 4. — Because I like English.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/8) — Writing.</strong> Write a paragraph of 50–65 words about your day. Barème: five times with correct prepositions (3), one <em>because</em> (1), one transport phrase (1), order and linkers (1), mechanics (2).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. Where 2. What 3. Who 4. How 5. Why 6. How. B. 1. <em>What time do you get up?</em> 2. <em>Where do you live?</em> 3. <em>How do you go to school?</em> 4. <em>Why do you like English?</em> C. Sample (57 words): <em>I get up at half past six and I wash my face. I have breakfast at seven o'clock with my sister. I go to school on foot, because my school is near my house. Lessons start at eight. I have lunch at home at half past twelve. I do my homework at six and I go to bed at ten.</em></p>\n<p>## Unit 9 — Where does she work?</p>\n<p>Unit 9 links places and jobs. Learners consolidate the third-person present simple in <em>Wh-</em> questions (<em>Where does he work? He works in a hospital.</em>), meet the long vowel <em>a + magic e</em>, read a magazine interview and learn to punctuate lists with commas and <em>and</em>.</p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en41","domaine":"anglais","unite":41,"theme":"Unit 9 — Where does she work?","tag":"EN41","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 9 - Words (places)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Name</strong> at least fifteen places of a Moroccan town.","<strong>Match</strong> a place with the job done there and with an action.","<strong>Use</strong> the prepositions <em>in / at / near / next to / opposite</em>.","<strong>Ask and answer</strong> <em>Where do you buy bread? — At the bakery.</em>","<strong>Describe</strong> the place where a family member works.","<strong>Draw and label</strong> a simple map of his or her neighbourhood."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> A new pupil arrives in the neighbourhood and asks: <em>Where is the post office? Where do you buy bread? Where does the doctor work?</em> To answer, you need the <strong>names of places</strong> and the <strong>prepositions</strong> that locate them.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher draws a simple street on the board with five buildings and asks learners to name them in Darija or French; he writes the English names: <em>the bakery, the pharmacy, the post office, the school, the mosque</em>. Then he adds a job for each: <em>baker, chemist, postman, teacher</em>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Places and the people who work there</strong> hospital → <em>doctor, nurse</em> · school → <em>teacher</em> · bakery → <em>baker</em> · shop → <em>shopkeeper</em> · farm → <em>farmer</em> · police station → <em>police officer</em> · post office → <em>postman</em> · restaurant → <em>cook / waiter</em> · bank → <em>bank clerk</em> · garage → <em>mechanic</em> · office → <em>secretary, engineer</em> · market / souk → <em>seller</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — prepositions of place</strong> <strong>in</strong> a building or a town: <em>in a hospital, in Fez, in the classroom</em>. <strong>at</strong> a point or an institution seen as a function: <em>at school, at home, at the bakery, at work</em>. <strong>near / next to / opposite / behind / in front of / between</strong>: <em>The pharmacy is <strong>next to</strong> the bakery. The school is <strong>opposite</strong> the mosque.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Fifteen useful places</strong> <em>school, hospital, pharmacy, bakery, shop, market / souk, mosque, post office, bank, police station, library, restaurant, café, farm, garage, factory, stadium, train station, bus station, swimming pool.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — locating a place.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Name the place with <strong>the</strong>: <em>the pharmacy</em>.</li><li>Choose the preposition: <em>next to, opposite, near, between</em>.</li><li>Name the second place: <em>the bakery</em>.</li><li>Say the sentence: <em>The pharmacy is next to the bakery.</em></li><li>To speak about a job, use <em>work in / at</em>: <em>My uncle works in a garage. My mother works at home.</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> ✗ <em>My father works in the farmer.</em> → ✓ <em>My father works <strong>on a farm</strong>; he is a <strong>farmer</strong>.</em> Do not confuse the <strong>place</strong> and the <strong>person</strong>. (Note the fixed preposition: <em>on</em> a farm.)</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> <em>Where do you buy medicine? — At the pharmacy.</em> The answer to <em>Where…?</em> about a service is usually <em>at the</em> + place.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Small map description: ``<code> [ school ] --- [ mosque ] --- [ bakery ] --- [ pharmacy ] </code>`` <em>The mosque is between the school and the bakery. The pharmacy is next to the bakery. The school is opposite my house.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Places of a Moroccan town: <em>the souk, the hammam, the dar chabab (youth centre), the kissaria, the medina, the taxi station, the moqata'a (local administration), the health centre</em>. Learners describe their own <em>derb</em> or <em>douar</em>, and can add prices: <em>A loaf of bread costs one dirham and twenty centimes at the bakery.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Where do you…?\" A asks five questions (<em>Where do you buy bread / medicine / vegetables? Where do you send a letter? Where do you play football?</em>), B answers with <em>At the…</em> / <em>In the…</em>. Then B draws A's neighbourhood map from A's oral description.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>hospital</td><td>/ˈhɒspɪtl/</td><td>مستشفى</td></tr><tr><td>pharmacy</td><td>/ˈfɑːməsi/</td><td>صيدلية</td></tr><tr><td>bakery</td><td>/ˈbeɪkəri/</td><td>مخبزة</td></tr><tr><td>market / souk</td><td>/ˈmɑːkɪt/</td><td>سوق</td></tr><tr><td>mosque</td><td>/mɒsk/</td><td>مسجد</td></tr><tr><td>post office</td><td>/ˈpəʊst ˌɒfɪs/</td><td>مكتب البريد</td></tr><tr><td>bank</td><td>/bæŋk/</td><td>بنك</td></tr><tr><td>police station</td><td>/pəˈliːs ˌsteɪʃn/</td><td>مركز الشرطة</td></tr><tr><td>library</td><td>/ˈlaɪbrəri/</td><td>مكتبة</td></tr><tr><td>restaurant</td><td>/ˈrestrɒnt/</td><td>مطعم</td></tr><tr><td>farm</td><td>/fɑːm/</td><td>مزرعة</td></tr><tr><td>garage</td><td>/ˈɡærɑːʒ/</td><td>ميكانيكي / مرآب</td></tr><tr><td>factory</td><td>/ˈfæktəri/</td><td>معمل</td></tr><tr><td>stadium</td><td>/ˈsteɪdiəm/</td><td>ملعب</td></tr><tr><td>train station</td><td>/ˈtreɪn ˌsteɪʃn/</td><td>محطة القطار</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Prepositions</strong>: <em>in, at, on (a farm), near, next to, opposite, behind, in front of, between</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Place / person confusion.</strong> ✗ <em>He works in a baker.</em> → ✓ <em>He works in a <strong>bakery</strong>; he is a <strong>baker</strong>.</em></li><li>⚠️ **Wrong preposition with <em>farm</em>.** ✗ <em>in a farm</em> → ✓ <em>on a farm</em>.</li><li>⚠️ **Article with <em>school</em> / <em>home</em>.** ✗ <em>at the home</em> → ✓ <em>at home</em>; <em>at school</em> (as an institution).</li><li>⚠️ <em><strong>library</strong></em> ≠ <strong>bookshop</strong>: a <em>library</em> lends books; a <em>bookshop</em> sells them.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>hospital 2. bakery 3. school 4. farm 5. garage — a. teacher b. mechanic c. doctor d. baker e. farmer</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>bread 2. medicine 3. vegetables 4. stamps</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>**Exercice 3 — <em>in</em>, <em>at</em> or <em>on</em>?** 1. ____ a hospital 2. ____ school 3. ____ a farm 4. ____ home 5. ____ Casablanca</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>mosque 2. library 3. stadium 4. factory — a. معمل b. مسجد c. ملعب d. مكتبة</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>The doctor works in a ____ . 2. The baker works in a ____ . 3. The farmer works on a ____ .</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>My father works in a baker. 2. My uncle works in a farm. 3. I am at the home. 4. I borrow books in a bookshop.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>with <em>next to</em>, <em>opposite</em>, <em>between</em>. ``<code> [ school ] [ mosque ] [ bakery ] [ pharmacy ] </code>``</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<ol><li>Where is your school? 2. What is next to your house? 3. Where do you buy bread?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>from the IPA: 1. /ˈhɒspɪtl/ 2. /ˈbeɪkəri/ 3. /mɒsk/ 4. /ˈlaɪbrəri/</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<ol><li>bakery / pharmacy / doctor 2. farm / factory / farmer 3. mosque / market / mechanic</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a sentence for each: 1. Ahmed / nurse 2. Salma / teacher 3. Omar / mechanic</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Describe five places near your house with prepositions.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>a map of your street with six English labels, then write three sentences about it.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Write four riddles: <em>You buy bread there. What is it? — A bakery.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Match the place and the person, then write a sentence for two of them (1 pt matching + 2 pts sentences): <em>hospital, bakery, school, garage / mechanic, doctor, baker, teacher</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Complete with <em>in</em>, <em>at</em> or <em>on</em> (1 pt each): 1. My mother works ____ a hospital. 2. I am ____ home. 3. My cousin works ____ a farm. 4. I study ____ school. 5. He lives ____ Marrakech. 6. She works ____ a bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/8) — Writing.</strong> Describe your neighbourhood in 50–60 words with five places and four prepositions. Barème: five places (3), four prepositions (2), sentences with <em>there is / is next to</em> (2), mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. hospital–doctor, bakery–baker, school–teacher, garage–mechanic. Sample sentences: <em>The doctor works in a hospital. The mechanic works in a garage.</em> B. 1. in 2. at 3. on 4. at 5. in 6. in. C. Sample (56 words): <em>My house is in a small street in Taroudant. The bakery is next to my house and the pharmacy is opposite. My school is near the market: I walk for ten minutes. Behind my house, there is a big mosque. Between the school and the mosque, there is a café where my father drinks tea.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en42","domaine":"anglais","unite":42,"theme":"Unit 9 — Where does she work?","tag":"EN42","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 9 - Grammar (where does he work? he works in a ...)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Build</strong> Wh- questions in the third person: <em>Where does he work? What does she do?</em>","<strong>Answer</strong> with the third-person <em>-s</em> and the right preposition.","<strong>Contrast</strong> <em>Where does…?</em> (place) and <em>What does… do?</em> (job).","<strong>Use</strong> <em>a / an</em> before a job: <em>He is <strong>a</strong> teacher. She is <strong>an</strong> engineer.</em>","<strong>Write</strong> a short profile of a working person.","<strong>Interview</strong> a partner about a family member's job."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The school magazine prepares a page called <em>\"Jobs in our families\"</em>. Each pupil must write three lines: <em>My aunt is a nurse. She works in the health centre in Sefrou. She starts at eight o'clock.</em> Three sentences, three grammar points: <em>a</em> + job, the <em>-s</em> of the third person, and the preposition of place.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes an answer on the board — <em>He works in a garage</em> — and asks the class to find two possible questions: <em>Where does he work?</em> (place) and <em>What does he do?</em> (job). Learners see that the Wh- word decides the information.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — Wh- questions in the third person</strong> <strong>Wh- + does + subject + base verb …?</strong> <em>Where <strong>does</strong> she <strong>work</strong>? — She works in a hospital.</em> <em>What <strong>does</strong> he <strong>do</strong>? — He is a mechanic.</em> (fixed expression for asking about a job) <em>When <strong>does</strong> he <strong>start</strong>? — At eight o'clock.</em> ⚠️ The <em>-s</em> is in <em>does</em>, so the main verb has none: ✗ <em>Where does she works?</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — The answer</strong> <strong>Subject + verb + -s + preposition + place</strong>: <em>He work<strong>s</strong> <strong>in</strong> a garage. She teach<strong>es</strong> <strong>in</strong> a school. My uncle work<strong>s</strong> <strong>on</strong> a farm.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule 3 — <em>a / an</em> before a job** In English a job always takes an article in the singular: <em>He is <strong>a</strong> teacher. She is <strong>an</strong> engineer. My mother is <strong>a</strong> nurse.</em> ✗ <em>He is teacher</em> is wrong (this is a very frequent transfer from Arabic and French). <strong>an</strong> before a vowel sound: <em>an engineer, an artist, an office worker</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — Useful jobs</strong> <em>teacher, doctor, nurse, baker, farmer, mechanic, engineer, shopkeeper, driver, cook, police officer, postman, hairdresser, secretary, tailor, carpenter, fisherman</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — writing a job profile.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Name the person and the job with <em>a / an</em>: <em>My aunt is a nurse.</em></li><li>Give the place with <em>in / at / on</em>: <em>She works in the health centre.</em></li><li>Give the town: <em>in Sefrou.</em></li><li>Give a time with <em>at</em>: <em>She starts at eight o'clock.</em></li><li>Add one thing she does or does not do: <em>She helps sick people. She doesn't work on Sunday.</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Build the question for <em>She works in a bank.</em> → information asked = the place → <em>Where does she work?</em> Build the question for <em>He is a driver.</em> → information asked = the job → <em>What does he do?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Correcting a paragraph: ✗ <em>My father is teacher. He work in a school. Where he works?</em> → ✓ <em>My father is <strong>a</strong> teacher. He work<strong>s</strong> in a school. Where <strong>does</strong> he work?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Full profile (three sentences): <em>My neighbour is a fisherman. He works in Essaouira, near the port. He gets up at four o'clock in the morning and he doesn't work when the sea is dangerous.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Jobs that learners really know: <em>farmer</em> in the Gharb, <em>fisherman</em> in Essaouira or Agadir, <em>tailor</em> in the medina of Fez, <em>tour guide</em> in Marrakech, <em>nurse</em> in a health centre (<em>centre de santé</em>), <em>teacher</em>, <em>taxi driver</em>, <em>shopkeeper</em> in the <em>hanout</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Job interview\": A chooses a job card and answers B's questions (<em>What do you do? Where do you work? What time do you start? Do you like your job?</em>) in role. Then B reports to the class in the third person: <em>He is a nurse. He works in a hospital. He starts at seven o'clock.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What does he do?</td><td>He is a mechanic.</td></tr><tr><td>Where does he work?</td><td>He works in a garage.</td></tr><tr><td>What time does he start?</td><td>He starts at eight o'clock.</td></tr><tr><td>Does she work on Sunday?</td><td>No, she doesn't.</td></tr><tr><td>How does she go to work?</td><td>She goes by bus.</td></tr><tr><td>Why does he like his job?</td><td>Because he helps people.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Jobs and their places</strong>: <em>teacher – school; doctor / nurse – hospital, health centre; baker – bakery; farmer – farm; mechanic – garage; engineer – factory, office; shopkeeper – shop; driver – bus, taxi; cook – restaurant; postman – post office; hairdresser – salon; tailor – shop in the medina; fisherman – port</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ *<em>Double </em>-s*.** ✗ <em>Where does she works?</em> → ✓ <em>Where does she work?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>No article before the job.</strong> ✗ <em>My father is teacher.</em> → ✓ <em>My father is a teacher.</em></li><li>⚠️ <em><strong>Where</strong></em> used to ask the job. ✗ <em>Where does he do?</em> → ✓ <em>What does he do?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Missing preposition.</strong> ✗ <em>He works a garage.</em> → ✓ <em>He works <strong>in</strong> a garage.</em></li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>He is a baker. (What does he do? / Where does he work?) 2. She works in a bank. (What does she do? / Where does she work?)</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Complete with <em>does</em> or <em>do</em>.** 1. Where ____ your mother work? 2. What ____ you do after school? 3. What time ____ he start? 4. Where ____ they live?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>**Exercice 3 — Add <em>a</em> or <em>an</em>.** 1. He is ____ teacher. 2. She is ____ engineer. 3. My uncle is ____ farmer. 4. My aunt is ____ nurse.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>cook 2. postman 3. nurse 4. mechanic — a. hospital b. garage c. restaurant d. post office</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>*<em>Exercice 5 — Complete with the </em>-s* form.** 1. He ____ (work) in a shop. 2. She ____ (teach) English. 3. My father ____ (go) to work at six.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>Where does she works? 2. My father is teacher. 3. Where does he do? 4. He works a garage.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>____ ? — She works in a hospital. 2. ____ ? — He is a driver. 3. ____ ? — At seven o'clock. 4. ____ ? — By bus.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p><em>My aunt ____ (be) a nurse. She ____ (work) in the health centre in Sefrou. She ____ (start) at eight o'clock and she ____ (not / work) on Sunday.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<ol><li>What does your father do? 2. Where does your mother work? 3. What time does he/she start?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>for these people: 1. Karim / mechanic / garage 2. Latifa / teacher / school 3. Hassan / fisherman / port</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write a negative sentence for each: 1. My mother / work / at night 2. My uncle / like / his job 3. My sister / work / on Friday</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write about a person in your family: job, place, town, times, one thing he/she does not do.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>Write six questions and answers in role (you are a taxi driver in Casablanca).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Turn your interview into a third-person paragraph.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Complete (1 pt each): 1. Where ____ your father work? 2. My mother is ____ nurse. 3. He ____ (work) in a bank. 4. What ____ she do? 5. She is ____ engineer. 6. He ____ (not / work) on Sunday. 7. What time ____ he start? 8. She ____ (teach) French.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Write the question (1.5 pt each): 1. — He is a farmer. 2. — She works in a pharmacy. 3. — At seven o'clock. 4. — By taxi.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6) — Writing.</strong> Write a profile of 50–60 words about a working person you know. Barème: job with article (1), place with preposition (1), two <em>-s</em> forms (2), one negative (1), mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. does 2. a 3. works 4. does 5. an 6. doesn't work 7. does 8. teaches. B. 1. What does he do? 2. Where does she work? 3. What time does he/she start? 4. How does he/she go to work? C. Sample (55 words): <em>My neighbour Fatima is a hairdresser. She works in a small salon near the market in Berkane. She opens at nine o'clock and she closes at seven in the evening. She doesn't work on Monday. She cuts the hair of many women before Eid, so she is very busy in the week of the feast.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en43","domaine":"anglais","unite":43,"theme":"Unit 9 — Where does she work?","tag":"EN43","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 9 - Phonics (long vowels: a + magic e)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Explain</strong> the \"magic e\" rule for the letter <em>a</em>.","<strong>Contrast</strong> short /æ/ and long /eɪ/ in minimal pairs (<em>hat / hate</em>).","<strong>Read</strong> sixteen <em>a + magic e</em> words.","<strong>Spell</strong> them correctly in a dictation.","<strong>Apply</strong> the rule to new words met in the unit (<em>bake, place, late</em>)."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Two words that differ by one silent letter: <em>tap</em> and <em>tape</em>, <em>hat</em> and <em>hate</em>, <em>man</em> and <em>mane</em>, <em>plan</em> and <em>plane</em>. The final <strong>e</strong> is not pronounced, but it changes the vowel: this is the famous <strong>magic e</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes four pairs on the board and reads them: <em>cap / cape, mad / made, pan / pane, at / ate</em>. Learners answer: <em>Which letter is added? Is it pronounced? What changes?</em> Conclusion: the <em>e</em> is silent and makes the <em>a</em> say its <strong>name</strong>, /eɪ/, exactly as in the alphabet.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The magic e rule</strong> In a word with the pattern <strong>a + consonant + e</strong>, the final <em>e</em> is silent and the letter <em>a</em> is pronounced /eɪ/ — its alphabet name. <em>cake</em> /keɪk/, <em>name</em> /neɪm/, <em>late</em> /leɪt/, <em>game</em> /ɡeɪm/, <em>place</em> /pleɪs/. Without the <em>e</em>, <em>a</em> is short /æ/: <em>cat</em> /kæt/, <em>hat</em> /hæt/, <em>plan</em> /plæn/.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Sixteen key words</strong> <em>name, game, cake, make, take, late, gate, plate, place, face, race, snake, plane, date, same, page.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — decoding a new word.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Look at the end of the word: is there a silent <strong>e</strong>?</li><li>Count the letters between <em>a</em> and <em>e</em>: if there is <strong>one consonant</strong>, the magic e works: <em>ca-k-e</em> → /keɪk/.</li><li>Say the alphabet name of <em>a</em>: /eɪ/.</li><li>Add the consonants: /k/ + /eɪ/ + /k/ = <em>cake</em>.</li><li>Check with the short partner: <em>cake</em> /keɪk/ vs <em>back</em> /bæk/.</li></ol>\n<p>``<code> short a  /æ/          |  a + magic e  /eɪ/ cat  hat  plan  tap   |  cake  hate  plane  tape mad  at   man   fat   |  made  ate   mane   fate </code>``</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> <em>plate</em> /pleɪt/: a blend /pl/ + magic-e <em>a</em> /eɪ/ + /t/. Two rules of the year in one word.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> <em>snake</em> /sneɪk/: /sn/ blend from Unit 8 + magic e. Learners who say \"sanak\" must rebuild it: /sn/ … /eɪ/ … /k/.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Minimal-pair sentences to fix the contrast: <em>I have a <strong>hat</strong></em> /hæt/ vs <em>I <strong>hate</strong> fish</em> /heɪt/; <em>Give me the <strong>tape</strong></em> vs <em>Turn off the <strong>tap</strong></em>; <em>This is my <strong>plan</strong></em> vs <em>The <strong>plane</strong> is late</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Sentences with local content: <em>My name is Aya. We make a cake for Eid. The plate of couscous is on the table. I take the bus to Rabat. Don't be late for the English lesson. The gate of our school is blue.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Long or short?\" A reads a word from a mixed list; B says <em>long</em> (/eɪ/) or <em>short</em> (/æ/) and repeats the word. Then both read the sentence pairs aloud, exaggerating the difference.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Short partner</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>name</td><td>/neɪm/</td><td>—</td><td>اسم</td></tr><tr><td>game</td><td>/ɡeɪm/</td><td>gam-</td><td>لعبة</td></tr><tr><td>cake</td><td>/keɪk/</td><td>back /bæk/</td><td>كعكة</td></tr><tr><td>make</td><td>/meɪk/</td><td>mad /mæd/</td><td>يصنع</td></tr><tr><td>take</td><td>/teɪk/</td><td>tack /tæk/</td><td>يأخذ</td></tr><tr><td>late</td><td>/leɪt/</td><td>lat-</td><td>متأخر</td></tr><tr><td>gate</td><td>/ɡeɪt/</td><td>gat-</td><td>بوابة</td></tr><tr><td>plate</td><td>/pleɪt/</td><td>plan /plæn/</td><td>طبق</td></tr><tr><td>place</td><td>/pleɪs/</td><td>—</td><td>مكان</td></tr><tr><td>face</td><td>/feɪs/</td><td>fat /fæt/</td><td>وجه</td></tr><tr><td>race</td><td>/reɪs/</td><td>rat /ræt/</td><td>سباق</td></tr><tr><td>snake</td><td>/sneɪk/</td><td>snack /snæk/</td><td>ثعبان</td></tr><tr><td>plane</td><td>/pleɪn/</td><td>plan /plæn/</td><td>طائرة</td></tr><tr><td>date</td><td>/deɪt/</td><td>—</td><td>تاريخ / تمر</td></tr><tr><td>same</td><td>/seɪm/</td><td>Sam /sæm/</td><td>نفسه</td></tr><tr><td>page</td><td>/peɪdʒ/</td><td>—</td><td>صفحة</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Pronouncing the final e.</strong> ✗ /ˈkeɪkə/ → ✓ /keɪk/. The <em>e</em> is silent.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Reading the magic-e word with a short /æ/.</strong> ✗ /plæn/ for <em>plane</em> → ✓ /pleɪn/.</li><li>⚠️ **Forgetting the <em>e</em> in writing.** ✗ <em>cak, plat, snak</em> → ✓ <em>cake, plate, snake</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Applying the rule with two consonants.</strong> <em>dance</em> /dɑːns/, <em>chance</em>: with two consonants before the <em>e</em>, the vowel is not /eɪ/.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>cat 2. cake 3. plan 4. plane 5. snake 6. snack</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>and write the new word: 1. tap 2. hat 3. mad 4. plan</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>into two columns (/æ/ and /eɪ/): <em>late, back, name, snack, plate, fat</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>I eat a piece of ____ (cake / back). 2. The ____ is on the table (plate / plan). 3. Don't be ____ ! (late / lat)</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>cake 2. plate 3. name</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>of eight words: <em>name, cake, plate, late, snake, place, face, page</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Write the two words: 1. /hæt/ – /heɪt/ 2. /plæn/ – /pleɪn/ 3. /tæp/ – /teɪp/</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<ol><li>cak 2. plat 3. snak 4. nam</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<ol><li>My ____ is Aya. 2. We ____ a cake for Eid. 3. The school ____ is blue. 4. Open your book at ____ ten.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>from the IPA: 1. /pleɪs/ 2. /sneɪk/ 3. /ɡeɪm/ 4. /teɪk/</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>(the word with a short /æ/): 1. cake / snack / plate 2. name / face / plan 3. late / back / gate</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Find four <em>a + magic e</em> words in your book and transcribe them.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>, each with one long /eɪ/ word and one short /æ/ word.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Explain the magic e rule in two sentences, with one example of each type.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"flash":[{"r":"name","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/neɪm/</span> اسم — —"},{"r":"game","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ɡeɪm/</span> لعبة — gam-"},{"r":"cake","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/keɪk/</span> كعكة — back /bæk/"},{"r":"make","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/meɪk/</span> يصنع — mad /mæd/"},{"r":"take","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/teɪk/</span> يأخذ — tack /tæk/"},{"r":"late","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/leɪt/</span> متأخر — lat-"},{"r":"gate","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/ɡeɪt/</span> بوابة — gat-"},{"r":"plate","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/pleɪt/</span> طبق — plan /plæn/"},{"r":"place","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/pleɪs/</span> مكان — —"},{"r":"face","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/feɪs/</span> وجه — fat /fæt/"},{"r":"race","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/reɪs/</span> سباق — rat /ræt/"},{"r":"snake","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/sneɪk/</span> ثعبان — snack /snæk/"},{"r":"plane","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/pleɪn/</span> طائرة — plan /plæn/"},{"r":"date","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/deɪt/</span> تاريخ / تمر — —"},{"r":"same","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/seɪm/</span> نفسه — Sam /sæm/"},{"r":"page","v":"<span dir=\"ltr\" class=\"ipa\">/peɪdʒ/</span> صفحة — —"}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Long /eɪ/ or short /æ/? Write L or S (1 pt each): 1. plate 2. snack 3. name 4. back 5. late 6. plan 7. face 8. hat.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Add the magic e and write the new word (1.5 pt each): 1. tap 2. mad 3. hat 4. plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6).</strong> Write three sentences, each with one long and one short <em>a</em> word (2 pts each).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. L 2. S 3. L 4. S 5. L 6. S 7. L 8. S. B. 1. tape 2. made 3. hate 4. plane. C. Sample: <em>I take my black hat. The cake is on a small plate. My plan is to take the plane to Madrid.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en44","domaine":"anglais","unite":44,"theme":"Unit 9 — Where does she work?","tag":"EN44","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 9 - Reading (a magazine interview)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Recognise</strong> the layout of an interview (introduction, questions, answers, dashes).","<strong>Match</strong> questions and answers in a mixed interview.","<strong>Answer</strong> comprehension questions with justification.","<strong>Transform</strong> interview answers into a third-person report.","<strong>Write</strong> two extra questions for the interviewee.","<strong>Role-play</strong> the interview in pairs."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> The school magazine of a middle school in Agadir interviews a young woman engineer who works in a fish factory. An interview is a text with a special shape: a short <strong>introduction</strong>, then a series of <strong>questions and answers</strong>, marked by dashes or by the initials of the speakers.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher shows the text with the questions hidden and asks: <em>What is the question for this answer?</em> Learners reconstruct two or three questions, then read the real ones and compare. This activity trains both reading and question formation.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The shape of an interview</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Introduction</strong>: who the person is, what she does, where.</li><li><strong>Q / A pairs</strong>: the interviewer's questions are Wh- questions or <em>yes/no</em> questions; the answers are complete sentences in the first person (<em>I work…</em>).</li><li><strong>Closing</strong>: thanks, sometimes advice for young readers.</li></ol>\n<p>To <strong>report</strong> an interview, change <em>I</em> to <em>he/she</em> and add the <em>-s</em>: <em>\"I work in a factory.\"</em> → <em>She works in a factory.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Original reading text (written for this document).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>\"I LOVE MY JOB\" — Our interview with Rim Boulaich, engineer</strong> Rim Boulaich is twenty-eight. She is an engineer in a fish factory in Agadir. She studied in Rabat and she comes from Tiznit. Our club met her on Wednesday afternoon. — <strong>Good afternoon, Rim. What do you do exactly?</strong> — I am a food engineer. I check the fish and the machines in the factory. I work with a team of twelve people. — <strong>Where do you work?</strong> — I work in a big factory near the port of Agadir. There are three hundred workers in the factory. — <strong>What time do you start?</strong> — I start at seven o'clock in the morning and I finish at four. On Friday I finish at one o'clock. — <strong>How do you go to work?</strong> — I go by bus with my colleagues. The factory bus comes at half past six. — <strong>Do you like your job?</strong> — Yes, I do. I love it, because fish from Agadir goes to many countries. But it is cold in the factory, so I wear a big coat and gloves. — <strong>What do you say to young girls who want to be engineers?</strong> — Study maths and English! English is very important: I read documents in English every day. — Thank you very much, Rim. — You're welcome. Good luck with your magazine!</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Comprehension work step by step.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Read the introduction</strong> first: it usually answers <em>who, what, where</em>.</li><li><strong>Read the questions</strong> only: they give the plan of the text (job, place, time, transport, opinion, advice).</li><li><strong>Scan</strong> each answer for the detail asked.</li><li><strong>Report</strong>: change the pronouns and add the <em>-s</em>. <em>\"I start at seven.\"</em> → <em>She starts at seven.</em></li><li><strong>Infer</strong>: <em>Why does she wear gloves?</em> The text does not say \"because her hands are cold\" in those words; the learner joins \"it is cold in the factory\" and \"I wear a big coat and gloves\".</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> <em>How many people work in the factory?</em> — Careful: two numbers appear, <em>twelve</em> (her team) and <em>three hundred</em> (the factory). The right answer is <em>There are three hundred workers in the factory.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Reporting: <em>\"I go by bus with my colleagues.\"</em> → <em>She goes to work by bus with her colleagues.</em> Two changes: <em>I → she</em>, <em>go → goes</em>, and <em>my → her</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Justified answer: <em>Does she like her job? Justify.</em> → <em>Yes, she does: \"I love it, because fish from Agadir goes to many countries.\"</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> The text uses a real economic reality: the fish industry of Agadir, one of the country's major sectors, and the importance of English for technical documents. The interviewee comes from Tiznit and studied in Rabat, which shows internal mobility for studies.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> A is the journalist, B is Rim; they read the interview aloud, then B answers two new questions invented by A (<em>Do you work on Sunday? How many hours do you work?</em>) and improvises an answer.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>engineer</td><td>/ˌendʒɪˈnɪə/</td><td>مهندس</td></tr><tr><td>factory</td><td>/ˈfæktəri/</td><td>معمل</td></tr><tr><td>worker</td><td>/ˈwɜːkə/</td><td>عامل</td></tr><tr><td>team</td><td>/tiːm/</td><td>فريق</td></tr><tr><td>colleague</td><td>/ˈkɒliːɡ/</td><td>زميل في العمل</td></tr><tr><td>check</td><td>/tʃek/</td><td>يراقب / يتحقق</td></tr><tr><td>machine</td><td>/məˈʃiːn/</td><td>آلة</td></tr><tr><td>port</td><td>/pɔːt/</td><td>ميناء</td></tr><tr><td>coat</td><td>/kəʊt/</td><td>معطف</td></tr><tr><td>advice</td><td>/ədˈvaɪs/</td><td>نصيحة</td></tr><tr><td>document</td><td>/ˈdɒkjumənt/</td><td>وثيقة</td></tr><tr><td>You're welcome.</td><td>—</td><td>لا شكر على واجب</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ **Copying the answer with <em>I</em>** in a report. ✗ <em>She: I work in a factory.</em> → ✓ <em>She works in a factory.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Taking the wrong number</strong> (12 vs 300): read the whole sentence around the number.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Answers without justification</strong> in a true/false task.</li><li>⚠️ **Confusing <em>factory</em> and <em>farm</em>**, or <em>worker</em> and <em>work</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p>Complete: name, age, job, town of work, home town.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>Rim is a doctor. 2. She works near the port. 3. She starts at eight o'clock. 4. She goes to work by bus.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>her age 2. people in her team 3. workers in the factory 4. the time of the factory bus</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>Where do you work? 2. What time do you start? 3. How do you go to work? — a. By bus. b. At seven o'clock. c. In a factory near the port.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<ol><li>Which city? 2. Which industry (fish or clothes)? 3. Does she like her job?</li></ol>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>What does Rim do exactly? 2. What time does she finish on Friday? 3. Why does she wear a coat and gloves?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Copy the proof: 1. She likes her job. 2. English is important for her.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Rewrite in the third person: 1. \"I work with a team of twelve people.\" 2. \"I start at seven o'clock.\" 3. \"I read documents in English every day.\"</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>for these answers from the text. 1. ____ ? — I am a food engineer. 2. ____ ? — By bus. 3. ____ ? — Study maths and English!</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<ol><li>Rim works in a hospital. 2. She finishes at six o'clock. 3. There are twelve workers in the factory.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Match: 1. factory 2. worker 3. team 4. advice — a. نصيحة b. معمل c. فريق d. عامل</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Write two questions you would like to ask Rim, and imagine her answers.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>in the third person.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Interview a working person you know and write six Q/A lines in the same layout.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> True or false + justification (1 + 1 each): 1. Rim comes from Agadir. 2. She works with twelve people. 3. She finishes at four o'clock on Friday.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Report in the third person (2 pts each): 1. \"I check the fish and the machines.\" 2. \"I go by bus with my colleagues.\" 3. \"I read documents in English every day.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/8) — Writing.</strong> Write an interview of six lines (three questions and three answers) with a working person of your choice, plus a two-line introduction. Barème: layout (2), three correct Wh- questions (3), three complete answers (2), mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. F — \"she comes from Tiznit\". 2. T — \"I work with a team of twelve people\". 3. F — \"On Friday I finish at one o'clock\". B. 1. <em>She checks the fish and the machines.</em> 2. <em>She goes by bus with her colleagues.</em> 3. <em>She reads documents in English every day.</em> C. Sample: <em>Mr Brahim is a taxi driver in Fez. He is forty-five. — What time do you start? — I start at six o'clock in the morning. — Where do you work? — I work in the new town and in the medina. — Do you like your job? — Yes, I do, because I meet many people every day.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en45","domaine":"anglais","unite":45,"theme":"Unit 9 — Where does she work?","tag":"EN45","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 9 - Writing (using commas with and in lists, writing about where people work)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Punctuate</strong> a list correctly with commas and <em>and</em>.","<strong>Write</strong> sentences with three or four items in a list.","<strong>Combine</strong> lists with the third-person present simple.","<strong>Write</strong> a paragraph of 55–70 words about jobs in his or her family or neighbourhood.","<strong>Check</strong> his or her own text with a punctuation checklist."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> A pupil writes: <em>\"In my family my father is a farmer my uncle is a mechanic my aunt is a nurse and my sister is a student.\"</em> The information is good, but the reader is lost: there are no commas. English has a simple rule for lists, and it is worth marks in every exam.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes the same sentence twice, once without punctuation and once with commas, and asks the class which one is easier to read. Then he counts with them: three or more items, so we need commas between the items and <strong>and</strong> before the last one.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — Commas in a list</strong> With <strong>three items or more</strong>, put a comma between the items and <strong>and</strong> before the last one: <em>We study English, French, maths and history.</em> With <strong>two items only</strong>, no comma: <em>We study English and maths.</em> ⚠️ Never a comma directly before <em>and</em> in the simple A1 rule taught here: <em>bread, milk and eggs</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — Lists of people and jobs</strong> <em>My father is a farmer, my uncle is a mechanic, my aunt is a nurse and my sister is a student.</em> Each part has its own subject and verb; the commas separate the parts.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — Lists after a verb</strong> <em>In our town there are a hospital, two schools, a post office and a big souk.</em> <em>She checks the fish, the machines and the workers' clothes.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — Punctuation checklist for the paragraph</strong> capital letter at the beginning · comma between list items · <strong>and</strong> before the last item · full stop at the end · capital letters for names of people and towns · <em>a / an</em> before a job.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — writing the \"jobs paragraph\".</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Write a first sentence to open: <em>There are many different jobs in my family.</em></li><li>Make a list sentence of three or four people with commas: <em>My father is a farmer, my mother is a teacher and my uncle is a mechanic.</em></li><li>Add two sentences with the third-person <em>-s</em> and places: <em>My father works on a farm near Sidi Slimane. My mother teaches in a primary school.</em></li><li>Add a time or a detail with a list: <em>She starts at eight o'clock, she finishes at four and she doesn't work on Saturday.</em></li><li>Close with an opinion: <em>I want to be an engineer.</em></li><li>Run the punctuation checklist.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> Punctuate: <em>In the souk you can buy vegetables fruit fish and bread.</em> → <em>In the souk you can buy vegetables, fruit, fish and bread.</em> Four items → three separators: two commas and one <em>and</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> Two items only: ✗ <em>I study English, and maths.</em> → ✓ <em>I study English and maths.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Model paragraph (66 words): <em>There are many different jobs in my family. My father is a farmer, my mother is a teacher and my uncle is a mechanic. My father works on a farm near Sidi Slimane: he grows wheat, oranges and vegetables. My mother teaches in a primary school; she starts at eight o'clock and finishes at four. My uncle repairs cars, buses and tractors in a garage. I want to be a doctor.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Lists that make sense locally: <em>wheat, oranges and vegetables</em> (Gharb), <em>fish, salt and sardines</em> (Safi, Agadir), <em>carpets, leather and pottery</em> (Fez, Marrakech), <em>mint, sugar and tea</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Writing rubric — /10</strong></p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Task and length</td><td>55–70 words, three jobs, three places</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Punctuation of lists</td><td>commas + <em>and</em> before the last item</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Grammar</td><td>third-person <em>-s</em>, <em>a / an</em> + job, prepositions</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Vocabulary</td><td>jobs, places, Moroccan details</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanics</td><td>capital letters, full stops, spelling</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Speaking before writing (pair work).</strong> A says a list of four school subjects, B repeats it and says where the comma goes (\"comma, comma, and\"). Then A lists four jobs in his family and B writes them down with the punctuation; they compare notebooks.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 items</td><td><em>bread and milk</em> (no comma)</td></tr><tr><td>3 items</td><td><em>bread, milk and eggs</em></td></tr><tr><td>4 items</td><td><em>bread, milk, eggs and tea</em></td></tr><tr><td>list of clauses</td><td><em>He works, he studies and he helps his father.</em></td></tr><tr><td>list of places</td><td><em>There are a school, a mosque and a bakery.</em></td></tr><tr><td>enumeration + job</td><td><em>My father is a farmer, my uncle is a driver and my aunt is a nurse.</em></td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>No commas at all</strong> in a list of three or more items.</li><li>⚠️ **Comma before <em>and</em>** in the simple A1 list: ✗ <em>bread, milk, and eggs</em> → ✓ <em>bread, milk and eggs</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Comma with two items only</strong>: ✗ <em>English, and maths</em> → ✓ <em>English and maths</em>.</li><li>⚠️ **Missing <em>a / an</em> before jobs** inside the list: ✗ <em>my uncle is mechanic</em> → ✓ <em>my uncle is a mechanic</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>I buy bread milk and eggs. 2. We study English French and maths. 3. In my street there is a bakery a pharmacy and a café.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>bread ____ milk 2. bread ____ milk ____ eggs 3. English ____ maths</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>**Exercice 3 — Complete with <em>and</em>.** 1. I like football, basketball ____ volleyball. 2. My sister studies maths, physics ____ English.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>**Exercice 4 — Add <em>a</em> or <em>an</em>.** 1. My uncle is ____ mechanic. 2. My aunt is ____ engineer. 3. My cousin is ____ nurse.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>with four school subjects.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>I buy bread, and milk. 2. We study English French maths. 3. My father is farmer, my uncle is a driver.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p><em>My father is a farmer. My mother is a teacher. My uncle is a mechanic.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>about: 1. what you buy in the souk 2. the places in your street 3. what you do after school</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>*<em>Exercice 9 — Complete with the </em>-s* form and punctuate.** <em>My mother ____ (teach) in a primary school, she ____ (start) at eight o'clock ____ she ____ (finish) at four.</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Copy this text and correct the six mistakes: <em>in my family my father is farmer my mother is a teacher and my uncle is a mechanic they work in sidi slimane</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write three list sentences: 1. the Gharb (wheat, oranges, vegetables) 2. Agadir (fish, sardines, tomatoes) 3. Fez (carpets, leather, pottery)</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>\"Jobs in my family\": three jobs, three places, one list sentence with four items.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>with two list sentences and three prepositions of place.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Exchange notebooks and check three points: commas, <em>and</em> before the last item, <em>a / an</em> before jobs.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Punctuate the lists (2 pts each): 1. I buy bread milk eggs and tea. 2. We study English French maths and history. 3. In my street there is a bakery a pharmacy and a café.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/4).</strong> Correct the mistakes (1 pt each): 1. I study English, and maths. 2. My uncle is mechanic. 3. My mother work in a school. 4. bread, milk, and eggs.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/10) — Writing.</strong> Write a paragraph of 55–70 words: \"Jobs in my family\". Marked with the rubric (task and length 2, list punctuation 3, grammar 3, vocabulary 1, mechanics 1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. <em>bread, milk, eggs and tea.</em> 2. <em>English, French, maths and history.</em> 3. <em>a bakery, a pharmacy and a café.</em> B. 1. <em>I study English and maths.</em> 2. <em>My uncle is a mechanic.</em> 3. <em>My mother works in a school.</em> 4. <em>bread, milk and eggs.</em> C. Sample (63 words): <em>There are three different jobs in my family. My father is a baker, my mother is a housewife and my sister is a student. My father works in a small bakery in the medina of Meknes: he starts at four o'clock, he finishes at midday and he sells bread, cakes and biscuits. My sister studies maths, physics and English at the university. I want to be a teacher.</em></p>\n<p>## Unit 10 — It's hot today!</p>\n<p>The last unit of the year deals with the <strong>weather</strong> and the activities that go with it. It reinvests everything learnt before — present simple, times, places, adjectives — in the very concrete field of Moroccan climate: hot summers in Marrakech, snow in the Atlas, fog on the Atlantic coast, wind in Essaouira.</p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en46","domaine":"anglais","unite":46,"theme":"Unit 10 — It's hot today!","tag":"EN46","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 10 - Words (weather activities)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Name</strong> eight weather conditions and four seasons.","<strong>Match</strong> a weather condition with a suitable activity and with clothes.","<strong>Use</strong> temperature vocabulary in degrees Celsius.","<strong>Ask and answer</strong> in pairs: <em>What's the weather like in Ifrane in winter? — It's cold and it snows.</em>","<strong>Classify</strong> activities into <em>indoor / outdoor</em>.","<strong>Write</strong> four sentences linking weather and activity with <em>so</em> and <em>because</em>."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Four postcards arrive at the school: from Marrakech in July (<em>It's very hot, 43 degrees</em>), from Ifrane in January (<em>It's cold and it snows</em>), from Essaouira in April (<em>It's windy, perfect for surfing</em>), from Tangier in November (<em>It's rainy and cloudy</em>). Four climates in one country: the weather decides what people do.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher draws four simple weather symbols on the board (a sun, a cloud, rain, snow) and asks learners to name them. He then adds a fifth and a sixth (wind, fog) and writes the sentence pattern above: <em>It's sunny. It's raining.</em></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Definition — weather words</strong> Adjectives with <em>It's</em>: <em>sunny, hot, warm, cold, cool, cloudy, windy, rainy, foggy, dry, wet, stormy</em>. Verbs with <em>It's + -ing</em>: <em>It's raining. It's snowing.</em> Nouns: <em>sun, cloud, rain, snow, wind, fog, storm</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The four seasons</strong> <em>spring</em> /sprɪŋ/ (March–May), <em>summer</em> /ˈsʌmə/ (June–August), <em>autumn</em> /ˈɔːtəm/ (September–November), <em>winter</em> /ˈwɪntə/ (December–February).</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Temperature</strong> <em>It's twenty-five degrees.</em> / <em>It's minus two degrees.</em> / <em>What's the temperature? — Thirty degrees.</em> Useful scale for Morocco: <em>0–10 = cold, 10–20 = cool, 20–30 = warm, 30–45 = hot / very hot</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Weather and activities</strong> hot and sunny → <em>swim, play in the park, go to the beach, drink water, wear a cap</em> rainy → <em>stay at home, read a book, watch TV, wear a raincoat, take an umbrella</em> snowy → <em>wear a coat and gloves, play in the snow, drink hot tea</em> windy → <em>fly a kite, surf, close the windows</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — the weather + activity sentence.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>State the weather: <em>It's hot today.</em></li><li>Link with <strong>so</strong> (consequence) or <strong>because</strong> (reason): <em>It's hot today, <strong>so</strong> I drink a lot of water.</em> / <em>I stay at home <strong>because</strong> it's raining.</em></li><li>Add the place and the time: <em>In Marrakech, it's very hot in July.</em></li><li>For a habit, use the present simple: <em>In winter, it snows in the Atlas Mountains.</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> ✗ <em>Today is hot.</em> is possible but the standard A1 pattern is ✓ <em>It's hot today.</em> Use the impersonal <strong>it</strong> for weather, temperature and time.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> ✗ <em>It's rain.</em> → ✓ <em>It's rainy</em> (adjective) or ✓ <em>It's raining</em> (verb, now) or ✓ <em>It rains a lot in winter</em> (habit).</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Four linked sentences: <em>In summer, it's very hot in Zagora, so people stay at home in the afternoon. In winter, it snows in Ifrane, so children play in the snow. In Essaouira, it's windy all year, so many tourists surf there. On the Atlantic coast, it's often foggy in the morning, so drivers are careful.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Real climate facts to exploit: snow in the Middle and High Atlas (Ifrane, Michlifen, Oukaïmeden), the <em>chergui</em> (hot dry wind), the fog of the Atlantic coast, the very hot summer of the south-east (Zagora, Errachidia), the rainy season from November to March, drought and water saving. Learners can compare their own town with two others.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Weather map\": A chooses a Moroccan city and describes the weather (<em>It's cold and cloudy, ten degrees</em>); B guesses the city and the season, then proposes an activity (<em>You stay at home and drink tea!</em>). Swap after three turns.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>sunny</td><td>/ˈsʌni/</td><td>مشمس</td></tr><tr><td>hot / warm</td><td>/hɒt/ /wɔːm/</td><td>حار / دافئ</td></tr><tr><td>cold / cool</td><td>/kəʊld/ /kuːl/</td><td>بارد / منعش</td></tr><tr><td>cloudy</td><td>/ˈklaʊdi/</td><td>غائم</td></tr><tr><td>windy</td><td>/ˈwɪndi/</td><td>عاصف / فيه ريح</td></tr><tr><td>rainy / to rain</td><td>/ˈreɪni/</td><td>ممطر / يمطر</td></tr><tr><td>snowy / to snow</td><td>/ˈsnəʊi/</td><td>مثلج / يتساقط الثلج</td></tr><tr><td>foggy</td><td>/ˈfɒɡi/</td><td>ضبابي</td></tr><tr><td>dry / wet</td><td>/draɪ/ /wet/</td><td>جاف / رطب</td></tr><tr><td>storm</td><td>/stɔːm/</td><td>عاصفة</td></tr><tr><td>spring / summer</td><td>/sprɪŋ/ /ˈsʌmə/</td><td>ربيع / صيف</td></tr><tr><td>autumn / winter</td><td>/ˈɔːtəm/ /ˈwɪntə/</td><td>خريف / شتاء</td></tr><tr><td>degree</td><td>/dɪˈɡriː/</td><td>درجة</td></tr><tr><td>umbrella</td><td>/ʌmˈbrelə/</td><td>مظلة</td></tr><tr><td>coat / gloves</td><td>/kəʊt/ /ɡlʌvz/</td><td>معطف / قفازات</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ **Missing <em>it</em>.** ✗ <em>Is hot today.</em> → ✓ <em>It's hot today.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Noun instead of adjective.</strong> ✗ <em>It's rain / It's sun.</em> → ✓ <em>It's rainy / It's sunny.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Wrong verb form.</strong> ✗ <em>It's snow.</em> → ✓ <em>It's snowing / It snows in winter.</em></li><li>⚠️ **Season without <em>in</em>.** ✗ <em>Summer it's hot.</em> → ✓ <em><strong>In</strong> summer, it's hot.</em></li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>sunny 2. rainy 3. snowy 4. windy — a. عاصف b. مشمس c. مثلج d. ممطر</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Complete with <em>It's</em>.** 1. ____ hot today. 2. ____ raining. 3. ____ twenty degrees. 4. ____ cloudy in Tangier.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>Choose: 1. It's (sun / sunny). 2. It's (rain / rainy). 3. There is a lot of (wind / windy).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Put the months in the right season: <em>January, April, July, October</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>What do you wear? 1. It's cold and it snows. 2. It's hot and sunny. 3. It's raining.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>Is hot today. 2. It's rain. 3. Summer it's hot in Marrakech. 4. It's snow in Ifrane.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>**Exercice 7 — Link with <em>so</em> or <em>because</em>.** 1. It's very hot. I drink a lot of water. 2. I stay at home. It's raining. 3. It's windy in Essaouira. Many people surf.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Classify: <em>read a book, go to the beach, play football, watch TV, fly a kite, drink hot tea</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>Write a sentence for each: 1. Zagora / 43°C / August 2. Ifrane / −2°C / January 3. Rabat / 22°C / April</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p><em>Dear Sara, I am in Ifrane. ____ very cold and ____ snowing. The temperature is ____ two degrees. I wear a big ____ and ____ . See you soon!</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write the question. 1. ____ ? — It's sunny and warm. 2. ____ ? — Thirty degrees. 3. ____ ? — In winter.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>Describe the four seasons in your town with temperatures and activities.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>Write three sentences comparing the weather in Ifrane, Marrakech and Essaouira in January.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Write a postcard from a Moroccan city: weather, temperature, clothes, one activity.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Complete with the right weather word (1 pt each): 1. It's ____ : I take my umbrella. 2. It's ____ : I wear a cap and I drink water. 3. It's ____ : the sky is grey but it doesn't rain. 4. It's ____ : the kite flies high. 5. It's ____ : I wear a coat and gloves. 6. It's ____ : the drivers can't see the road.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Link with <em>so</em> or <em>because</em> (2 pts each): 1. It's very hot in Zagora. People stay at home in the afternoon. 2. I don't go to the beach. It's cloudy and cold. 3. It snows in the Atlas. Children play in the snow.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/8) — Writing.</strong> Write a postcard of 45–55 words from a Moroccan city: weather, temperature, clothes, one activity. Barème: weather words (2), temperature (1), clothes (1), activity with <em>so / because</em> (2), postcard layout and mechanics (2).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. raining / rainy 2. hot / sunny 3. cloudy 4. windy 5. cold / snowy 6. foggy. B. 1. <em>It's very hot in Zagora, so people stay at home in the afternoon.</em> 2. <em>I don't go to the beach because it's cloudy and cold.</em> 3. <em>It snows in the Atlas, so children play in the snow.</em> C. Sample (50 words): <em>Dear Yassine, I am in Ifrane with my uncle. It's very cold today: minus one degree! It's snowing, so I wear a big coat, a scarf and gloves. This morning we played in the snow and we drank hot tea. The cedar forest is beautiful. See you next week! Salma.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en47","domaine":"anglais","unite":47,"theme":"Unit 10 — It's hot today!","tag":"EN47","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 10 - Grammar (what's the weather like? It's ...)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Ask</strong> <em>What's the weather like (in …)?</em> and answer with <em>It's …</em>","<strong>Use</strong> the impersonal subject <em>it</em> for weather, temperature and time.","<strong>Distinguish</strong> <em>It's raining</em> (now) from <em>It rains</em> (habit).","<strong>Ask and answer</strong> <em>What's the temperature? / Is it cold in …?</em>","<strong>Combine</strong> weather and activity with <em>so</em>, <em>because</em>, <em>but</em>.","<strong>Write</strong> a weather dialogue of six lines."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> A cousin phones from Spain: <em>What's the weather like in Morocco?</em> The answer must fit the place and the season: <em>In Marrakech it's hot and dry, but in Chefchaouen it's rainy today.</em> This lesson gives the exact question form and the answer patterns.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes three questions on the board and asks the class to match them with three answers: <em>What's the weather like? / What's the temperature? / Is it cold?</em> — <em>It's sunny. / Twenty-five degrees. / No, it isn't.</em> Learners notice that all the answers begin with <em>it</em>, even when there is no visible subject in Arabic or French.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — The key question</strong> <strong>What's the weather like (in + place) (in + season)?</strong> <em>What's the weather like in Agadir in summer? — It's warm and sunny.</em> Note the word <strong>like</strong> at the end: it means \"how is it?\", not the verb <em>to like</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule 2 — The impersonal <em>it</em><strong> English needs a subject in every sentence. For weather, temperature, time and distance, this subject is </strong>it**: <em>It's cold. It's twenty degrees. It's five o'clock. It's ten kilometres to the beach.</em> ⚠️ Never omit it: ✗ <em>Is cold today.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p>**Rule 3 — <em>It's raining</em> or <em>It rains</em>?<strong> </strong>It's raining / It's snowing<strong> = now, at this moment. </strong>It rains / It snows** = a habit, a general fact: <em>It rains a lot in Tangier in winter. It snows in the Atlas every year.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — Yes/no questions and short answers</strong> <em>Is it hot in Marrakech in July? — Yes, it is.</em> <em>Is it cold in Agadir in winter? — No, it isn't. It's mild.</em> <em>Does it snow in Casablanca? — No, it doesn't.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — building a weather exchange.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Ask with the full formula: <em>What's the weather like in Fez in January?</em></li><li>Answer with <em>It's</em> + one or two adjectives: <em>It's cold and rainy.</em></li><li>Add the temperature: <em>It's about eight degrees.</em></li><li>Add an activity with <em>so</em>: <em>…so people drink hot tea and stay at home.</em></li><li>If you speak about right now, use <em>-ing</em>: <em>It's raining at the moment.</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> ✗ <em>How is the weather?</em> is understood but the standard A1 form is ✓ <em>What's the weather like?</em> Both exist; the textbook form is the second.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> <em>Does it snow in Marrakech? — No, it doesn't. But it snows in the High Atlas, near Oukaïmeden, and you can see the white mountains from the city.</em> Habit → present simple with <em>does</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Six-line dialogue: <em>— Hello, Karim! What's the weather like in Dakhla? — It's windy and sunny. It's twenty-four degrees. — Is it cold in the evening? — No, it isn't. It's cool, about eighteen degrees. — Does it rain there? — No, it doesn't. It almost never rains in Dakhla.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Contrasts to exploit: <em>It's very hot in Errachidia in July (45 degrees), but it's cool in Ifrane (25 degrees). It rains a lot in Tangier and Chefchaouen. It almost never rains in Dakhla and Zagora. It snows in the Atlas in January and February.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Phone call\": A phones B from another Moroccan city. B asks three weather questions (<em>What's the weather like? What's the temperature? Is it raining?</em>) and A answers. Then they swap cities and roles.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What's the weather like?</td><td>It's sunny and warm.</td></tr><tr><td>What's the weather like in Fez in winter?</td><td>It's cold and rainy.</td></tr><tr><td>What's the temperature?</td><td>It's twenty-two degrees.</td></tr><tr><td>Is it hot today?</td><td>Yes, it is. / No, it isn't.</td></tr><tr><td>Does it snow in your town?</td><td>Yes, it does. / No, it doesn't.</td></tr><tr><td>Is it raining now?</td><td>Yes, it is. / No, it isn't.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Adverbs of frequency for weather</strong>: <em>always, usually, often, sometimes, rarely, never</em>: <em>It never snows in Agadir. It often rains in Tangier in December.</em></p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ **Omitting <em>it</em>.** ✗ <em>Is hot today.</em> → ✓ <em>It's hot today.</em></li><li>⚠️ **Forgetting <em>like</em>.** ✗ <em>What's the weather?</em> → ✓ <em>What's the weather like?</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Confusing now and habit.</strong> ✗ <em>It rains now</em> (for this moment) → ✓ <em>It's raining now.</em></li><li>⚠️ <strong>Wrong short answer.</strong> ✗ <em>Yes, it's.</em> → ✓ <em>Yes, it is.</em> (No contraction in a positive short answer.)</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>What's the weather ____ ? 2. What's the ____ ? — Twenty degrees. 3. ____ it cold today?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Answer with <em>It's</em>.** 1. What's the weather like today? 2. What's the temperature? 3. What time is it?</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>Is it hot in Marrakech in July? (Yes) 2. Does it snow in Agadir? (No) 3. Is it raining now? (No)</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>**Exercice 4 — <em>It's raining</em> or <em>It rains</em>?** 1. Look out of the window: ____ ! 2. ____ a lot in Tangier in winter. 3. ____ in the Atlas every January.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>**Exercice 5 — Add <em>it</em>.** 1. ____ is windy today. 2. ____ 's twenty-five degrees. 3. ____ snows in Ifrane.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>Is hot today. 2. What's the weather? 3. Yes, it's. 4. It rains now, look!</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>____ ? — It's cloudy and cool. 2. ____ ? — Thirty-eight degrees. 3. ____ ? — No, it doesn't.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Put the adverb in the right place. 1. It rains in Tangier. (often) 2. It snows in Agadir. (never) 3. It is hot in Zagora. (always)</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p><em>— What's the weather ____ in Dakhla? — ____ windy and sunny. — ____ it cold in the evening? — No, ____ isn't. — ____ it rain there? — No, it ____ .</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>Write three sentences with <em>so</em>: 1. hot / drink water 2. rainy / stay at home 3. windy / fly a kite</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Write four sentences comparing the weather in your town and in Ifrane in January, using <em>but</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>A phone call between a pupil in Tangier and a pupil in Zagora in July.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>\"The weather in my region\", with temperatures, two frequency adverbs and two activities.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Write five <em>Is it…? / Does it…?</em> questions about Moroccan cities, with the answers.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/8).</strong> Complete (1 pt each): 1. What's the weather ____ in Fez? 2. ____ cold and rainy. 3. ____ the temperature? — Nine degrees. 4. Is it hot in Zagora? — Yes, ____ ____ . 5. Does it snow in Rabat? — No, ____ ____ . 6. Look! ____ ____ (rain) now.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Write the questions (2 pts each): 1. — It's sunny and warm. 2. — Twenty-nine degrees. 3. — No, it doesn't. It never snows here.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/6) — Writing.</strong> Write a dialogue of six lines between two pupils in two different Moroccan cities. Barème: three correct questions (3), three correct answers with <em>it</em> (2), layout and mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. like 2. It's 3. What's 4. it is 5. it doesn't 6. It's raining. B. 1. <em>What's the weather like?</em> 2. <em>What's the temperature?</em> 3. <em>Does it snow in your town?</em> C. Sample: <em>— Hello Nadia! What's the weather like in Agadir? — It's sunny and warm, twenty-six degrees. — Is it windy? — Yes, it is, but the beach is beautiful. — Does it rain in April? — No, it doesn't. And in Fez? — Here it's cool and cloudy, seventeen degrees.</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en48","domaine":"anglais","unite":48,"theme":"Unit 10 — It's hot today!","tag":"EN48","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 10 - Phonics (long vowels: i + magic e)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Distinguish</strong> short <em>i</em> /ɪ/ from long <em>i</em> /aɪ/ in minimal pairs.","<strong>Apply</strong> the magic <em>e</em> rule to the letter <em>i</em>.","<strong>Read aloud</strong> twenty words with the two sounds without hesitation.","<strong>Write</strong> words dictated with the correct final <em>e</em>.","<strong>Sort</strong> words into two sound columns.","<strong>Read</strong> a short rhyming text with correct vowel length."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Two words, one letter of difference: <em>bit</em> /bɪt/ and <em>bite</em> /baɪt/, <em>fin</em> /fɪn/ and <em>fine</em> /faɪn/. The final <strong>e</strong> is silent, but it changes the vowel completely — and it changes the meaning. After the <em>a + magic e</em> rule of EN43, the same magic works with <em>i</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes <em>kit</em> and <em>kite</em> on the board, says the two words twice and asks: <em>Which one is a toy in the sky?</em> Learners hear that the second vowel is long and that it says the <strong>name</strong> of the letter <em>i</em>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule — i + magic e</strong> <strong>i + consonant + e</strong> at the end of a word → the <em>i</em> is <strong>long</strong> and says its own name /aɪ/; the final <em>e</em> is <strong>silent</strong>. <em>bit → bite · fin → fine · kit → kite · rid → ride · Tim → time · win → wine · dim → dime · slid → slide</em> Without the magic <em>e</em>, the <em>i</em> is <strong>short</strong> /ɪ/: <em>big, sit, milk, fish, wind, six, this</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The two sounds</strong> <strong>/ɪ/ short</strong>: <em>it, in, big, sit, six, fish, milk, wind, this, city, minute</em> — a quick, relaxed sound. <strong>/aɪ/ long</strong>: <em>I, my, fly, why, five, nine, time, like, white, night, right, ride, kite, smile, write</em> — a long sound that glides from /a/ to /ɪ/.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Other spellings of /aɪ/</strong> <em>y</em> at the end: <em>my, why, fly, sky, dry, try</em> <em>igh</em>: <em>night, right, light, high</em> <em>i</em> alone before two consonants sometimes: <em>kind, mind, child, find</em> ⚠️ Exceptions with a short <em>i</em> despite the <em>e</em>: <em>give</em> /ɡɪv/, <em>live</em> (verb) /lɪv/, <em>have</em> → the rule is very regular but not absolute.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — reading an unknown word.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Look at the end: is there a final <em>e</em> after <em>consonant</em>?</li><li>If yes and the vowel is <em>i</em>, say the long sound /aɪ/ and do not pronounce the <em>e</em>: <em>smile</em> /smaɪl/.</li><li>If no, say the short sound /ɪ/: <em>smil…</em> does not exist, but <em>milk</em> /mɪlk/ does.</li><li>Check with a known word: <em>nine</em> like <em>fine</em>, <em>win</em> like <em>in</em>.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> <em>This is a nice white kite.</em> → /ðɪs ɪz ə naɪs waɪt kaɪt/. Three long sounds (<em>nice, white, kite</em>) and two short ones (<em>this, is</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> ✗ <em>tim</em> for <em>time</em>: a pupil who does not see the magic <em>e</em> says /tɪm/ (the boy's name Tim) instead of /taɪm/. The final <em>e</em> is the signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Dictation logic: the teacher says /faɪv/; the learner hears a long <em>i</em>, so he writes <strong>five</strong> with a final <em>e</em>, not <em>fiv</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Rhyming text (written for this document).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>NINE WHITE KITES</strong> It's nine o'clock. The sky is white. The wind is high, so kites can fly. Five white kites and nine red kites, They fly, they slide, they shine at night. \"It's time to smile,\" says little Nadia. \"It's fine, it's fine, this windy day!\"</p></blockquote>\n<p>Learners read it twice: once slowly, marking the long <em>i</em> sounds, once at normal speed.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Names and words useful locally with the long sound: <em>Nadia, Amine</em> (careful: /əˈmiːn/ has a long <em>e</em> sound), <em>nine, five, time, white, kite, rice, nice</em>. In Essaouira the wind makes kite-flying and kitesurfing a real activity, which links this phonics lesson with the unit theme.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"Short or long?\": A reads a word from a list of twenty, B says \"short\" or \"long\" and repeats the word; three points per correct answer, then they swap. Second round: A says a minimal pair (<em>fin / fine</em>), B points to the right word on the board.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Short /ɪ/</th><th>Long /aɪ/</th><th>Meaning of the long word</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>bit /bɪt/</td><td>bite /baɪt/</td><td>يعض</td></tr><tr><td>fin /fɪn/</td><td>fine /faɪn/</td><td>جيد / بخير</td></tr><tr><td>kit /kɪt/</td><td>kite /kaɪt/</td><td>طائرة ورقية</td></tr><tr><td>rid /rɪd/</td><td>ride /raɪd/</td><td>يركب</td></tr><tr><td>Tim /tɪm/</td><td>time /taɪm/</td><td>وقت</td></tr><tr><td>dim /dɪm/</td><td>dime /daɪm/</td><td>عشرة سنتات</td></tr><tr><td>slid /slɪd/</td><td>slide /slaɪd/</td><td>ينزلق</td></tr><tr><td>win /wɪn/</td><td>wine /waɪn/</td><td>(drink)</td></tr><tr><td>sit /sɪt/</td><td>site /saɪt/</td><td>موقع</td></tr><tr><td>pin /pɪn/</td><td>pine /paɪn/</td><td>شجرة الصنوبر</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Other /aɪ/ words</strong>: <em>my, why, fly, sky, dry, night, right, light, high, five, nine, nice, white, smile, write, child, kind, find</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ **Pronouncing the final <em>e</em>**: ✗ /kaɪtə/ → ✓ /kaɪt/.</li><li>⚠️ **Short sound with the magic <em>e</em>**: ✗ /tɪm/ for <em>time</em> → ✓ /taɪm/.</li><li>⚠️ **Writing without the final <em>e</em>**: ✗ <em>fiv, nin, whit</em> → ✓ <em>five, nine, white</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Applying the rule to the exceptions</strong>: <em>give</em> and <em>live</em> (verb) keep the short /ɪ/.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p><em>big, bike, six, five, fish, white, milk, time</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>**Exercice 2 — Add the magic <em>e</em> and read.** 1. bit → ____ 2. fin → ____ 3. kit → ____ 4. rid → ____ 5. slid → ____</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<p>**Exercice 3 — Circle the long <em>i</em> words.** <em>this, nine, wind, smile, sit, night, fish, kite</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>milk 2. write 3. six 4. fly</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>and mark the long sounds: <em>This is a nice white kite.</em></p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<p>Which word do you hear? (teacher reads one of the two) 1. Tim / time 2. win / wine 3. sit / site 4. pin / pine</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Write the words: /faɪv/, /naɪn/, /waɪt/, /taɪm/, /kaɪt/, /smaɪl/.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>Sort: <em>city, night, wind, fine, this, why, minute, child, big, right</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<ol><li>fiv 2. nin 3. whit 4. tim 5. smil</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>In this list, which words do NOT follow the rule? <em>five, give, live (verb), nine, drive</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Read and count the /aɪ/ sounds: <em>At nine o'clock the white kite flies high in the dry sky.</em></p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>each containing two long <em>i</em> words.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>the rhyming text \"Nine white kites\"; mark every /aɪ/.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Find ten words with the /aɪ/ sound in the previous lessons of this document and classify them by spelling (<em>i+e</em>, <em>y</em>, <em>igh</em>).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Short /ɪ/ or long /aɪ/? (1 pt each): 1. wind 2. white 3. six 4. smile 5. this 6. night</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> Add the magic <em>e</em> and give the meaning in Arabic (1 pt each): 1. bit 2. fin 3. kit 4. rid 5. Tim 6. slid</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/4).</strong> Correct the spelling (1 pt each): 1. fiv 2. nin 3. whit 4. smil</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice D (/4) — Reading.</strong> Copy this sentence and underline the six long <em>i</em> sounds: <em>At nine o'clock, my white kite flies high in the dry sky and I smile.</em></p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. /ɪ/ 2. /aɪ/ 3. /ɪ/ 4. /aɪ/ 5. /ɪ/ 6. /aɪ/. B. 1. bite (يعض) 2. fine (بخير) 3. kite (طائرة ورقية) 4. ride (يركب) 5. time (وقت) 6. slide (ينزلق). C. 1. five 2. nine 3. white 4. smile. D. Underlined: <em>nine, my, white, kite, high, dry, sky, I, smile</em> (any six of these; the sentence in fact contains nine).</p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en49","domaine":"anglais","unite":49,"theme":"Unit 10 — It's hot today!","tag":"EN49","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 10 - Reading (a weather report)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Identify</strong> the structure of a weather report (region, weather, temperature, advice).","<strong>Read</strong> a table of temperatures and answer questions about it.","<strong>Find</strong> the coldest, the hottest, the windiest place in a text.","<strong>Answer</strong> true/false with justification and Wh- questions.","<strong>Transfer</strong> the information into a simple table or map.","<strong>Present</strong> the report orally as a TV weather presenter."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Every evening the television gives the weather for tomorrow, region by region. A weather report is a very useful text type at A1 level: short sentences, all in <em>it's</em> + adjective, plus numbers. Reading it means learning to find precise information quickly.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes four city names on the board and reads the report aloud once; learners write one weather word next to each city. Then they read the text and check.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The structure of a weather report</strong></p>\n<ol><li>A general sentence: <em>Good evening. Here is the weather for tomorrow.</em></li><li>Region by region, from north to south: <em>In the north… In the centre… In the south…</em></li><li>Weather + temperature: <em>It's cloudy, with 16 degrees.</em></li><li>Advice or warning: <em>Take your umbrella. Drink a lot of water.</em></li><li>Closing: <em>Have a nice evening!</em></li></ol></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Original reading text (written for this document).</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>TOMORROW'S WEATHER IN MOROCCO</strong> Good evening and welcome to the weather report for Sunday. <strong>In the north</strong>, in Tangier and Tetouan, it's cloudy and rainy. The temperature is fifteen degrees in the morning and eighteen degrees in the afternoon. Take your umbrella! <strong>In the Atlas Mountains</strong>, near Ifrane and Midelt, it's very cold. It snows on the high roads and the temperature is minus one degree in the morning. Drivers must be careful. <strong>On the Atlantic coast</strong>, in Rabat and Casablanca, it's foggy in the morning, but the afternoon is sunny and pleasant: twenty-two degrees. It's a good day for a walk. <strong>In Essaouira</strong>, it's windy again, with twenty degrees. The kitesurfers are happy! <strong>In the south</strong>, in Marrakech, Zagora and Errachidia, it's hot and dry. In Zagora, the temperature is thirty-eight degrees in the afternoon. Stay at home between one and four o'clock and drink a lot of water. <strong>In the Sahara</strong>, near Dakhla, it's sunny and windy: twenty-six degrees, and the sea is cool. That's all for today. Have a nice evening and see you tomorrow!</p></blockquote>\n<table><thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Weather</th><th>Temperature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tangier</td><td>rainy</td><td>15–18 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Ifrane</td><td>snow</td><td>−1 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Casablanca</td><td>fog then sun</td><td>22 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Essaouira</td><td>windy</td><td>20 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Zagora</td><td>hot and dry</td><td>38 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Dakhla</td><td>sunny and windy</td><td>26 °C</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Reading strategies step by step.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Skim</strong> the region names in bold: they give the plan.</li><li><strong>Scan</strong> for numbers when the question asks for a temperature.</li><li><strong>Compare</strong>: to find the hottest place, look at all the numbers, not only the first.</li><li><strong>Locate the advice</strong>: each advice sentence goes with one weather condition (<em>Take your umbrella</em> ↔ rain).</li><li><strong>Transfer</strong> into the table: it forces you to read every line.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> <em>Where is it the coldest? — In the Atlas Mountains, near Ifrane and Midelt: minus one degree.</em> Note the superlative form used receptively; production is not required at A1.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> <em>Why must drivers be careful? — Because it snows on the high roads.</em> The answer joins the advice and the weather sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Difference of temperature: <em>38 − (−1) = 39 degrees</em> between Zagora and Ifrane on the same day — a good cross-curricular link with maths.</p>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> The report follows real Moroccan geography: the rainy north, the snowy Atlas, the foggy Atlantic coast, the windy Essaouira and Dakhla, the hot south-east. It is also a chance to speak about water saving and about protecting oneself from the sun.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> \"TV weather presenter\": each pair chooses two regions, prepares three sentences (weather, temperature, advice) and presents them to the class or to a neighbouring pair, standing next to the map drawn on the board.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>IPA</th><th>Arabic gloss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>weather report</td><td>/ˈweðə rɪˈpɔːt/</td><td>نشرة الأحوال الجوية</td></tr><tr><td>the north / the south</td><td>/nɔːθ/ /saʊθ/</td><td>الشمال / الجنوب</td></tr><tr><td>coast</td><td>/kəʊst/</td><td>ساحل</td></tr><tr><td>mountains</td><td>/ˈmaʊntɪnz/</td><td>جبال</td></tr><tr><td>careful</td><td>/ˈkeəfl/</td><td>حذر</td></tr><tr><td>pleasant</td><td>/ˈpleznt/</td><td>لطيف</td></tr><tr><td>temperature</td><td>/ˈtemprətʃə/</td><td>درجة الحرارة</td></tr><tr><td>minus</td><td>/ˈmaɪnəs/</td><td>ناقص / تحت الصفر</td></tr><tr><td>advice</td><td>/ədˈvaɪs/</td><td>نصيحة</td></tr><tr><td>Have a nice evening!</td><td>—</td><td>أمسية سعيدة</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Taking the first number found</strong> instead of the one asked for (morning vs afternoon).</li><li>⚠️ **Forgetting <em>minus</em>** when reading a temperature below zero.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Answering without the region</strong>: <em>It's hot</em> → ✓ <em>It's hot in the south, in Zagora.</em></li><li>⚠️ **Confusing <em>north</em> and <em>south</em>** on the map.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<ol><li>Tangier 2. Ifrane 3. Essaouira 4. Zagora — a. hot and dry b. snow c. rainy d. windy</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<p>Give the temperature of: 1. Ifrane 2. Casablanca 3. Zagora 4. Dakhla</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>It rains in the south. 2. It snows in the Atlas. 3. Casablanca is foggy in the afternoon. 4. Essaouira is windy.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<p>Which advice goes with which weather? 1. Take your umbrella. 2. Drink a lot of water. 3. Drivers must be careful.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>Classify: <em>Tangier, Zagora, Tetouan, Errachidia</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>What's the weather like in Tangier? 2. Where is it the coldest? 3. Why are the kitesurfers happy?</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<p>Copy the proof: 1. It's dangerous on the mountain roads. 2. The afternoon is nice in Rabat.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>with two more columns: <em>clothes</em> and <em>activity</em> for each city.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>What is the difference of temperature between Zagora and Ifrane? Write the answer in a sentence.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<ol><li>It's twenty-two degrees in Zagora. 2. It's sunny all day in Casablanca. 3. It rains in Dakhla.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>for three cities using <em>it's</em> and one piece of advice each.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>for four Moroccan regions.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>Prepare and present two regions as a TV weather presenter (six sentences).</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>Write four sentences comparing today's real weather in your town with the report above, using <em>but</em>.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Total: /20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice A (/6).</strong> Answer with the text (2 pts each): 1. What's the weather like in the Atlas Mountains? 2. What is the temperature in Zagora in the afternoon? 3. Why is the afternoon good for a walk in Rabat?</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice B (/6).</strong> True or false + justification (1 + 1 each): 1. It rains in Dakhla. 2. It's windy in Essaouira. 3. It's cold in the south.</p>\n<p><strong>Devoir — Exercice C (/8) — Writing.</strong> Write a weather report of 60–75 words for four regions of Morocco, with the temperature and one piece of advice for each. Barème: structure and greetings (2), four regions with weather (2), four temperatures (2), advice and mechanics (2).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A. 1. <em>It's very cold and it snows on the high roads.</em> 2. <em>It's thirty-eight degrees.</em> 3. <em>Because it's sunny and pleasant, twenty-two degrees.</em> B. 1. F — \"it's sunny and windy: twenty-six degrees\". 2. T — \"it's windy again, with twenty degrees\". 3. F — \"it's hot and dry\". C. Sample (70 words): <em>Good evening. Here is the weather for tomorrow. In the north, in Tetouan, it's cloudy and rainy: seventeen degrees. Take your raincoat. In the Atlas, it's very cold, two degrees, and it snows in Michlifen. On the coast, in Agadir, it's sunny and warm, twenty-five degrees: a good day for the beach. In the south, in Errachidia, it's very hot, thirty-nine degrees. Drink a lot of water. Have a nice evening!</em></p>"}},
{"id":"anglais-en50","domaine":"anglais","unite":50,"theme":"Unit 10 — It's hot today!","tag":"EN50","langue":"en","titre":"Unit 10 - Writing (verbs and adjectives, writing about the weather)","duree":"","niveau":"1re année collège · Maroc","objectifs":["<strong>Distinguish</strong> a verb from an adjective in a simple sentence.","<strong>Place</strong> the adjective correctly (before the noun, after <em>be</em>).","<strong>Use</strong> the weather verbs <em>rain, snow, shine, blow</em> correctly.","<strong>Write</strong> a weather paragraph of 60–80 words about the four seasons.","<strong>Self-assess</strong> with the writing rubric of the unit.","<strong>Prepare</strong> the final revision for the end-of-year test."],"cours":[{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"📘 Cours","x":"<p><strong>Situation d'entrée.</strong> Two sentences, two grammatical categories: <em>The sun <strong>shines</strong>.</em> (verb) and <em>The day is <strong>sunny</strong>.</em> (adjective). The same idea, two ways of saying it. Knowing which one to use, and where to put it, is the last writing skill of the year.</p>\n<p><strong>Activité de découverte.</strong> The teacher writes six words on the board — <em>rain, rainy, snow, snowy, shine, sunny</em> — and asks learners to sort them into two lists: \"words that describe\" and \"words that say what happens\". They then produce one sentence with each pair.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 1 — Verb or adjective?</strong> A <strong>verb</strong> says what happens: <em>It rains. The sun shines. The wind blows.</em> An <strong>adjective</strong> describes: <em>a rainy day, a sunny morning, a windy afternoon.</em> Many weather words exist in both forms: <em>rain / rainy · snow / snowy · sun / sunny · wind / windy · cloud / cloudy · fog / foggy · storm / stormy</em>. The adjective is usually the noun + <strong>-y</strong>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 2 — Where does the adjective go?</strong> <strong>Before the noun</strong>: <em>a <strong>cold</strong> morning, a <strong>hot</strong> day, <strong>white</strong> snow, a <strong>strong</strong> wind.</em> **After the verb <em>be</em>**: <em>The morning <strong>is cold</strong>. The day <strong>is hot</strong>.</em> ⚠️ Never after the noun: ✗ <em>a day hot</em> → ✓ <em>a hot day</em>. The adjective never takes an <em>-s</em>: ✗ <em>hots days</em> → ✓ <em>hot days</em>.</p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 3 — Two adjectives together</strong> Join them with <strong>and</strong> (<em>It's cold and rainy</em>) or <strong>but</strong> (<em>It's sunny but cold</em>). With three or more, use the list punctuation of EN45: <em>It's cool, cloudy and windy.</em></p></blockquote>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule 4 — Weather verbs</strong> <em>It rains / It's raining · It snows / It's snowing · The sun shines · The wind blows.</em> Do not mix: ✗ <em>It's rain</em> / ✗ <em>It's sun.</em> → ✓ <em>It's raining / The sun is shining.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Méthode pas à pas — the four-season paragraph.</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Opening: <em>I live in ____ , in the ____ of Morocco.</em></li><li>One sentence per season, with the season + <em>in</em>, one or two adjectives and a temperature: <em>In winter, it's cold and rainy: about twelve degrees.</em></li><li>Add one activity per season with <em>so</em>: <em>…so we stay at home and drink hot tea.</em></li><li>Use at least one verb sentence: <em>In summer, the sun shines all day.</em></li><li>Closing: <em>My favourite season is spring, because the fields are green.</em></li><li>Check with the rubric and the punctuation checklist.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 1.</strong> ✗ <em>In winter is cold.</em> → ✓ <em>In winter, <strong>it</strong> is cold.</em> The impersonal <em>it</em> of EN47 is compulsory.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 2.</strong> ✗ <em>It's a day rainy.</em> → ✓ <em>It's a <strong>rainy</strong> day.</em> The adjective comes before the noun.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemple résolu 3.</strong> Model paragraph (76 words): <em>I live in Khemisset, in the centre of Morocco. In winter, it's cold and rainy: about eleven degrees, so we stay at home and drink hot tea. In spring, it's cool and pleasant and the fields are green. In summer, the sun shines all day and it's very hot, thirty-six degrees, so we swim in the river. In autumn, it's windy and it sometimes rains. My favourite season is spring, because the weather is perfect.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Writing rubric — /10</strong></p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Task and length</td><td>60–80 words, four seasons, one activity each</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Weather language</td><td><em>it's</em> + adjectives, temperatures, one verb sentence</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Grammar</td><td>adjective position, no <em>-s</em> on adjectives, <em>in</em> + season</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>one sentence per season, linkers <em>so, because, but</em></td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanics</td><td>capitals, commas, full stops, spelling</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Ancrage marocain.</strong> Each learner writes about his or her own region, which makes 45 different and authentic paragraphs: the rainy Rif, the snowy Atlas, the mild Atlantic coast, the hot Draa valley, the windy Souss. A wall display of the paragraphs makes a class weather map of Morocco for the end of the year.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking (pair work).</strong> A says a sentence with a verb (<em>The sun shines in July</em>), B transforms it into an adjective sentence (<em>July is sunny / It's sunny in July</em>). Six turns each, then they read their paragraphs to each other and check the rubric together.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"🎯 Points clés","x":"<table><thead><tr><th>Noun</th><th>Adjective</th><th>Verb</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>rain /reɪn/</td><td>rainy /ˈreɪni/</td><td>to rain</td></tr><tr><td>snow /snəʊ/</td><td>snowy /ˈsnəʊi/</td><td>to snow</td></tr><tr><td>sun /sʌn/</td><td>sunny /ˈsʌni/</td><td>to shine /ʃaɪn/</td></tr><tr><td>wind /wɪnd/</td><td>windy /ˈwɪndi/</td><td>to blow /bləʊ/</td></tr><tr><td>cloud /klaʊd/</td><td>cloudy /ˈklaʊdi/</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>fog /fɒɡ/</td><td>foggy /ˈfɒɡi/</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>storm /stɔːm/</td><td>stormy /ˈstɔːmi/</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Other useful adjectives</strong>: <em>hot, warm, cool, cold, dry, wet, mild, pleasant, perfect, terrible, green</em>.</p>"},{"t":"htmlcard","titre":"⚠️ Erreurs fréquentes","x":"<ul><li>⚠️ <strong>Adjective after the noun</strong>: ✗ <em>a day hot</em> → ✓ <em>a hot day</em>.</li><li>⚠️ **<em>-s</em> on the adjective**: ✗ <em>cold days are longs</em> → ✓ <em>cold days are long</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>Noun instead of adjective</strong>: ✗ <em>It's sun today</em> → ✓ <em>It's sunny today</em>.</li><li>⚠️ <strong>No subject</strong>: ✗ <em>In summer is hot</em> → ✓ <em>In summer, it is hot</em>.</li></ul>"}],"exercices":[{"type":"ouverte","badge":1,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 1","q":"<p><em>rainy, snow, shine, windy, blow, cloudy</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":2,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 2","q":"<ol><li>rain → ____ 2. snow → ____ 3. wind → ____ 4. cloud → ____ 5. fog → ____ 6. storm → ____</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":3,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 3","q":"<ol><li>a day (hot) 2. a morning (cold) 3. the snow (white)</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":4,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 4","q":"<ol><li>It's ____ in Zagora in July. 2. It's ____ in Ifrane in January. 3. It's ____ in Essaouira.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":5,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 5","q":"<p>**Exercice 5 — Add <em>it</em>.** 1. In winter, ____ is cold. 2. In summer, ____ 's hot. 3. Today ____ 's cloudy.</p>\n<p><em>Niveau 2 — Entraînement (6 to 11)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":6,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 6","q":"<ol><li>It's a day rainy. 2. Hots days are long. 3. It's sun today. 4. In summer is very hot.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":7,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 7","q":"<ol><li>The sun shines in July. 2. It rains in December. 3. The wind blows in Essaouira.</li></ol>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":8,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 8","q":"<p>**Exercice 8 — Join with <em>and</em> or <em>but</em>.** 1. It's sunny. It's cold. 2. It's cloudy. It's windy. 3. It's hot. It's dry.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":9,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 9","q":"<p>about your town, with a temperature.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":10,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 10","q":"<p>**Exercice 10 — Add an activity with <em>so</em>** to each sentence of Exercice 9.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":11,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 11","q":"<p>Read this paragraph and give a mark out of 10 with the rubric: <em>In my town winter is cold. Summer is hot. I like summer.</em></p>\n<p><em>Niveau 3 — Approfondissement (12 to 14)</em></p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":12,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 12","q":"<p>\"The weather in my region\", four seasons, one activity per season, at least one verb sentence.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":13,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 13","q":"<p>to a foreign pen-friend describing the weather of your town today and your favourite season.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]},{"type":"ouverte","badge":14,"diff":"","consigne":"Exercice 14","q":"<p>With your group, write four short paragraphs for four Moroccan regions and display them around a map.</p>","modele":"<em>Corrige avec ton manuel ou ton professeur.</em>","criteres":["Compare ta réponse avec la correction","Rédige sur ton cahier"]}],"devoir":{"titre":"📝 Devoir","consigne":"<p><strong>Nom / Name</strong>: …………………… <strong>Classe / Class</strong>: 1AC …… <strong>Durée / Time</strong>: 1 h <strong>Note</strong>: …… / 20</p>\n<p><strong>I. Reading comprehension (/6)</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>WEATHER AND WEEKEND</strong> Good evening. Here is the weather for Saturday. In the north, in Tangier, it's cloudy and rainy: seventeen degrees. In the Atlas Mountains, it's very cold and it snows above two thousand metres: three degrees in Ifrane. On the Atlantic coast, in Agadir, it's sunny and warm, twenty-six degrees: a perfect day for the beach. In the south, in Zagora, it's very hot and dry, forty degrees. Drink a lot of water and stay at home between one and four o'clock. Amine lives in Agadir. He is happy, because he plays football on the beach with his cousins every Saturday morning. His sister Salma doesn't like the beach: she stays at home and reads. Their mother says: \"It's hot today, so take your caps and a bottle of water!\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tâche A (/2).</strong> Answer: 1. What's the weather like in Tangier? 2. What is the temperature in Zagora? 3. Where does it snow? 4. What does Amine do on Saturday morning?</p>\n<p><strong>Tâche B (/2).</strong> True or false + justification: 1. It's cold in Agadir. 2. Salma plays football with her brother. 3. Their mother gives them advice.</p>\n<p><strong>Tâche C (/2).</strong> Complete the table: <em>city · weather · temperature</em> for Tangier, Ifrane, Agadir and Zagora.</p>\n<p><strong>II. Language and communication (/8)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tâche D (/2).</strong> Complete: 1. What's the weather ____ in Fez? 2. ____ raining. 3. Is it hot in Zagora? — Yes, ____ ____ . 4. Does it snow in Rabat? — No, ____ ____ .</p>\n<p><strong>Tâche E (/2).</strong> Make the adjective and use it in a sentence: 1. rain 2. wind 3. cloud 4. sun</p>\n<p><strong>Tâche F (/2).</strong> What time is it? Write in words: 1. 7:00 2. 8:15 3. 10:30 4. 5:45</p>\n<p><strong>Tâche G (/2).</strong> Sounds — short /ɪ/ or long /aɪ/? <em>big, five, white, six, milk, night</em></p>\n<p><strong>III. Writing (/6)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tâche H (/6).</strong> Write a paragraph of 60–75 words: \"The weather in my town and my favourite season\". Include two seasons at least, one temperature, one activity with <em>so</em>, one sentence with <em>because</em>. <em>Barème</em>: content and length (2) · <em>it's</em> + adjectives and temperatures (2) · linkers and adjective position (1) · mechanics (1).</p>","criteres":["Respecte le barème","Montre toutes les étapes","Relis-toi avant de voir la correction"],"modele":"<p>A (0.5 each). 1. <em>It's cloudy and rainy.</em> 2. <em>Forty degrees.</em> 3. <em>In the Atlas Mountains, above two thousand metres.</em> 4. <em>He plays football on the beach with his cousins.</em> B. 1. F — \"it's sunny and warm, twenty-six degrees\". 2. F — \"she stays at home and reads\". 3. T — \"take your caps and a bottle of water!\" C (0.5 each). Tangier: cloudy and rainy, 17 °C · Ifrane: snow, 3 °C · Agadir: sunny and warm, 26 °C · Zagora: hot and dry, 40 °C. D (0.5 each). 1. like 2. It's 3. it is 4. it doesn't. E (0.5 each). 1. rainy — <em>It's a rainy day.</em> 2. windy — <em>It's windy in Essaouira.</em> 3. cloudy — <em>The sky is cloudy.</em> 4. sunny — <em>It's sunny today.</em> F (0.5 each). 1. <em>It's seven o'clock.</em> 2. <em>It's a quarter past eight.</em> 3. <em>It's half past ten.</em> 4. <em>It's a quarter to six.</em> G (0.33 each). /ɪ/: big, six, milk — /aɪ/: five, white, night. H. Sample (68 words): <em>I live in Oujda, in the east of Morocco. In winter, it's cold and windy: about nine degrees, so we stay at home and drink hot tea. In summer, the sun shines all day and it's very hot, thirty-eight degrees, so we go out in the evening. My favourite season is spring, because it's warm and pleasant and the fields are green. I play football with my friends every afternoon.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Grille de conversion / mark bands</strong> (for all four assessments): 0–7 <em>insufficient, remediation needed</em> · 8–11 <em>in progress</em> · 12–15 <em>satisfactory</em> · 16–20 <em>good to excellent</em>.</p>\n<p>### B. Lexique</p>\n<p>Active A1 word list for the whole year, organised by theme. Each entry gives the English word, its IPA transcription in British Received Pronunciation, and an Arabic gloss. The list matches the vocabulary used in the fifty lessons of this document and is compatible with the A1 core lexis of the Cambridge Young Learners word lists.</p>"}});
